HT_DATA.load("detail", {"ar9WRwCiSr0":{"wy":"Praised as a brilliant, deadpan and inventive hardware hack, and a good starting point for Tom7's videos.","q":{"x":"are a must watch.","i":30974434,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":45699619,"author":"pinkmuffinere","month":"2025-10","story":"First convex polyhedron found that can't pass through itself","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite Tom7 video, hilarious with technical depth and whimsy","quote":"Tom7 is one of my favorite people, he is hilarious, has an amazing technical depth, and so much whimsy to go along with it.","text":"Tom7 is one of my favorite people, he is hilarious, has an amazing technical depth, and so much whimsy to go along with it. I'll proselytize for him all day! relevant video: [LINK] less relevant, but I think my favorite: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31730202,"author":"anyfoo","month":"2022-06","story":"The Floppotron 3.0","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good starting point among fantastic videos by Tom7","quote":"though really, they are all fantastic","text":"You will love Tom7/suckerpinch's stuff then, if you don't know him already. Here's a good one for the start, though really, they are all fantastic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30974434,"author":"GuestHNUser","month":"2022-04","story":"Harder Drive: Hard drives we didn't want or need [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deadpan, ridiculously elaborate projects that inspire","quote":"His dead pan delivery coupled with the ridiculous amount of effort he puts into things that don't _really_ matter is honestly inspiring.","text":"Tom7 is brilliant for anyone that hasn't seen him before. His Reverse Emulation video ( [VIDEO] ) and Weird Chess Algorithms ( [LINK] ) are a must watch. His dead pan delivery coupled with the ridiculous amount of effort he puts into things that don't _really_ matter is honestly inspiring."},{"id":25432462,"author":"sgrove","month":"2020-12","story":"SuperRT – Realtime Raytracing on the SNES","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hacks a real NES cartridge with a Raspberry Pi emulator","quote":"As usual for his videos, it's absolutely wonderful.","text":"In that case, you might like one of tom7's many wonnderful videos [0], \"Reverse emulating the NES to give it SUPER POWERS!\" where he hacks a real NES cartridge with a raspberry pi with an emulator, then figures out how to map all of the data into NES sprites, etc. As usual for his videos, it's absolutely wonderful. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21096404,"author":"sdenton4","month":"2019-09","story":"SNES Cartridges and Enhancement Chips","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reverse emulation uses a Raspberry Pi as an NES coprocessor","quote":"The talk is brilliant","text":"Along these lines, Tom7 invented* 'reverse emulation' which effectively uses a Raspberry Pi as a co-processor, allowing him to run [spoilers ahoy!]an SNES emulator on an NES.[/spoilers] The talk is brilliant: [VIDEO] * - as far as I know..."},{"id":36810543,"author":"iamevn","month":"2023-07","story":"GradIEEEnt half decent: The hidden power of imprecise lines [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Among the commenter's favorite videos by the channel","quote":"Anything that catches your eye is good but my favorites are","text":"Anything that catches your eye is good but my favorites are: 30 Weird Chess Algorithms: ELO World [LINK] Reverse Emulating the NES to give it SUPER POWERS! [VIDEO]"},{"id":34172964,"author":"NotYourLawyer","month":"2022-12","story":"I ran the length of every street in Pittsburgh: PAC TOM [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Raspberry Pi in an NES cartridge doing crazy things","quote":"Great YouTube channel.","text":"Great YouTube channel. Here’s a fun video where he integrates a raspberry pi into a NES cartridge to do all kinds of crazy things using the NES’s native hardware. Spoiler alert: full-motion video of the rickroll video is not the craziest thing he manages to pull off. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32947282,"author":"jabbany","month":"2022-09","story":"Pcmcia Pico W Card","stance":"rec","why":"Uses a modern device to give old hardware extra capabilities","quote":"it reminds me of this gem","text":"Heh, it reminds me of this gem: [VIDEO] which does something similar... using a modern device to give old devices essentially capabilities beyond what they should be able to do."},{"id":30817267,"author":"bla3","month":"2022-03","story":"What made the NES so interesting?","stance":"rec","why":"Fun to watch without knowing the punchline in advance","quote":"If you liked this, you'll likely also enjoy","text":"What a great article. If you liked this, you'll likely also enjoy [VIDEO] I'm not giving a summary since that video is most fun to watch if you don't know the punchline in advance. (No relationship to the video other than that I liked it.)"},{"id":27707193,"author":"jerf","month":"2021-07","story":"Installing Linux into a 286 laptop from the year 1989","stance":"rec","why":"Best watched without spoilers, worth it","quote":"still worth it.","text":"Well, then do I have the video for you: [VIDEO] Bit of a spoiler to post it in this context, but still worth it."},{"id":22844397,"author":"progval","month":"2020-04","story":"RaSCSI: SCSI Interface for Raspberry Pi (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Reverse-emulated NES with a Raspberry Pi, jokes can be skipped","quote":"you can skip to 3:35 if you're not interested in the jokes","text":"This reminds me of this video of a \"reverse-emulated\" NES using a raspberry-pi: [VIDEO] (you can skip to 3:35 if you're not interested in the jokes). The author further explains how he did it in this \"making of\" video: [LINK]"},{"id":18741306,"author":"rrherr","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among best talks of 2018, Deconstruct conference","quote":"","text":"From Deconstruct: Nabil Hassein: Computing, Climate Change, and All Our Relationships [LINK] Tom 7: Reverse emulating the NES to give it SUPER POWERS! [VIDEO]"},{"id":43085588,"author":"Dwedit","month":"2025-02","story":"NES86 – IBM PC Emulator for the NES","stance":"reference","why":"Example of cartridges containing full computers feeding the PPU","quote":"","text":"\"Cartridge that works on a stock NES\" is muddied waters now, since a cartridge can contain a full computer that feeds data directly to the PPU bus, while the main NES CPU mostly runs idle. See \"DOOM on NES\": [LINK] or Reverse emulating the NES to give it SUPER POWERS [VIDEO]"},{"id":38843991,"author":"mrguyorama","month":"2024-01","story":"Video playback on low-end MS-DOS machines","stance":"reference","why":"Compared to reverse emulation of the NES","quote":"","text":">It uses a 16mips pic (64hz 4-cycle) driving 1K of an 8k dual port ram. This is much closer to the \"Reverse Emulation\" of the NES that [VIDEO] did than playing a movie on the actual device. Using the original hardware as nothing more than an RF output interface is clearly not the same work, though just like in the reverse emulation of the NES, it is still a neat thing. The reason video on original IBM 5150s is so neat to most people is that it could have reasonably been done in it's heyday if we could take knowledge and algorithms back in time, which the reverse emulation versions could not."},{"id":38931783,"author":"derefr","month":"2024-01","story":"Non-PC compatible x86 computers (2007)","stance":"reference","why":"Link appears incidental to the legacy x86 discussion","quote":"","text":"That you can plug legacy x86 extension hardware into the board, and have it find a bug-for-bug-compatible x86 system to talk to on the other end. A major use-case being embedded systems — e.g. industrial control systems. These systems use a combination of 1. custom hardware — usually in the form of peripheral cards — built to speak old legacy x86-only bus protocols like ISA; and 2. old custom software, designed to run on an x86 CPU (usually in DOS), and to speak directly to that hardware over that bus. You can't just find some USB adapter for the old hardware's bus, because the software won't take it. And you can't just replace the old software, because the new software (whether a newer version of the old software, or software from another vendor) won't control the old card; and the card speaks an even lower -level (probably analogue!) protocol specific to the industrial system. Often, on these old systems, the custom peripheral card is still working due to it being solid-state (and thank god for that; the card's vendor is probably long-gone!) but the old x86 box the card lived in for the last 40 years is dying of various old-computer maladies — or just can't be made to fit into a new form-factor forced by a newer generation of the industrial system being controlled. In such a case, you ideally want to find a new embedded host system, made of new parts that will last you the next 40 years, but which can nevertheless still talk in very specific, custom, low-level ways to your "},{"id":38438004,"author":"silverlyra","month":"2023-11","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote link, loosely tied to the hierarchy of foundational tools","quote":"","text":"When seeing Prettier as a software tool, to consider Prettier more “foundational” than (e.g.) TypeScript or V8 does seem like an inversion of hierarchy [1] – but viewed as history, as a practice, Prettier’s accomplishment of making automatic code formatting (à la `go fmt` or Python `black`) widespread in JavaScript, the lingua franca of programming, is foundational. All that time spent staring at code that could have been more readable, or writing style guides and reformatting code, is now spent doing what we were trying to do in the first place. And now, speeding up the time we wait to apply formatting or check it in CI by rewriting it in Rust™ gives us a bit more of that time back. :) [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29581782,"author":"roywiggins","month":"2021-12","story":"There oughta be a WiFi Game Boy cartridge","stance":"reference","why":"Prior example of adding a modern coprocessor to old console hardware","quote":"","text":"Well, that's been done also: [VIDEO] Also, commercial 3D SNES games sometimes had to bring their own coprocessors, so it's fully in line with tradition: [LINK]"},{"id":21547696,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-11","story":"NaN Gates and Flip FLOPS","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes the cartridge-as-dual-of-the-console line, calls it creative","quote":"a very creative hack","text":"LOL... from \"Reverse emulating the NES to give it SUPER POWERS!\"[1] (which is insa^Wbizza^Wa very creative hack): \"The cartridge is the dual of the Nintendo.\" [1] [VIDEO] edit: for a very impressive variation on the SMW \"joke\" in [1], I recommend this[2] video of TASbot streaming Skype realtime to [2xNES, SNES+SuperGameBoy] through the controller ports . [2] [LINK]"},{"id":20976530,"author":"mrob","month":"2019-09","story":"Tutorial series for simulating NES Graphics (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Raspberry Pi cartridge feeding data to the NES PPU","quote":"Tom Murphy implemented this with a Raspberry Pi","text":"Note that some of these features require special hardware support in the cartridge. The NES cartridge connector exposes CPU and PPU (picture processing unit) pins, and the simplest games (\"NROM\") connect ROM chips directly to them. More advanced designs add an additional memory management controller (commonly called a \"mapper\"): [LINK] The animated backgrounds are accomplished by the mapper bank switching the character (tile) memory. The parallax scrolling is accomplished by the mapper counting scanlines and generating an interrupt when the correct scanline is reached, so the CPU can change the PPU scroll registers mid-frame with the correct timing. Later mappers like the the MMC5 could precisely time mid-frame bank changes to support more than 256 tiles on screen at once, and to support 8x8 attribute blocks instead of 16x16. The ultimate mapper feeds arbitrary data to the PPU to simulate having a bitmapped display with 8x1 attribute blocks (the PPU only reads attribute memory once every 8 pixels, so that's a hard limit). Tom Murphy implemented this with a Raspberry Pi (which isn't ideally suited to the task, so there are timing imperfections causing graphical glitches, but theoretically it could be done without them): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19870140,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2019-05","story":"All Chromebooks will also be Linux laptops going forward","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Raspberry Pi making an NES play SNES games","quote":"even a guy who use a Raspberry Pi to emulate an NES cartridge and get his unmodified NES to play SNES games","text":"People still run Commodore 64s. There are demo scenes, old hardware enthusiasts that squeeze crazy performance out of old hardware, even a guy who use a Raspberry Pi to emulate an NES cartridge and get his unmodified NES to play SNES games: [VIDEO] Old hardware can often be repaired. If you told a C64 user back in the day his machine would break in two years, he or she wouldn't shell out the $$ on it, but today people buy $400~$800 cellphones that turn into garbage."}],"r":[["DpXy041BIlA",2],["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1]]},"wSF82AwSDiU":{"wy":"HN commenters frequently recommend it as an eye-opening case against heavy porn use, though it is contested science.","q":{"x":"Here's an enlightening video on the detrimental effects of porn on the brain.","i":17167113,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":30443539,"author":"PKop","month":"2022-02","story":"Inside Pornhub","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Very informative on physiological effects of porn","quote":"This presentation [0] on the physiological effects of porn consumption is very informative.","text":"Midwest US, mid 30's. This sort of view is strongly held by the intellectual and political circles I support and follow. I think it is a very insidious and corrupting addiction, and the modern incarnation of it is orders of magnitude more stimulating than in prior eras. Infinite access to a stimulus that simulates in male brains \"mating\" with ever varied women, which is hijacking a strong biological imperative. As another comment mentioned a similar problem with \"advertising\" in general, I think sexualization and over stimulus of the population weakens people, and not to say there is some conspiracy purposefully pushing this (it may just emergent from technology and human instinct) I do think the corrupting influence weakens a population and makes them more easily dominated by strong forces... media, business, government, etc. All to say that, in terms of \"what is best to cultivate a strong society and people\" porn is extremely antithetical to this. A stronger society is one that would be avoiding, however necessary, consumption and production of pornography. This presentation [0] on the physiological effects of porn consumption is very informative. Observations mirror what strong opiates do to the brain A good overview of the public health problem of porn[1] The psychologically destabilizing and weakening effects of porn, and how they breakdown willpower to oppose stronger forces are illustrated by the time when the Israeli military broadcast pornography onto the televisions"},{"id":18497716,"author":"sizzle","month":"2018-11","story":"Ask HN: Resources for introverted devs to learn workplace politics?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Neural reward system effects of porn","quote":"Great Ted talk on subject","text":"Also, an often overlooked topic of modern times, high speed internet pornography + chronic masturbation and its numbing effects on our neural reward system which makes things like eye contact and social activities unbearable and highly disconcerting. This addiction isn't talked about much because it is almost a universal coping mechanism for guys beginning in adolescence, but you have nothing to lose in trying to abstain and see if your social anxiety melts away. At worst you just determine that it's something else like depression and can go seek out a proper medical diagnosis. The crazy thing about the social anxiety inducing effects of chronic masturbation is that it is not taught in medical school and doctors do not diagnose or advise that it can be detrimental to people's lives, granted you are oblivious to the hormonal imbalances it causes w.r.t. neuroscience (orgasm induced prolactin release suppressing dopamine in neural pleasure/reward system). Great Ted talk on subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12742607,"author":"cyberpunk","month":"2016-10","story":"UK Government proposes porn-viewing ID","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eye-opening and worrying points about porn","quote":"This really opened my eyes to this problem for me..","text":"[VIDEO] ^^ This really opened my eyes to this problem for me.. It's really rather worrying, and I guess my generation (early 30s) were the last to avoid this so it's kind of invisible to us because of how awkward it is to talk about such things. We need to do something, perhaps better education and getting more comfortable talking about sex and relationships with young people is a better approach than failed-from-the-outset restrictions... Either way, the points in that video are imo, valid, and should worry everyone."},{"id":6979411,"author":"Theodores","month":"2013-12","story":"The UK \"Porn\" Filter Blocks Kids' Access To Tech, Civil Liberties Websites","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Plausible science-based reason to avoid porn","quote":"Why did it take me decades to find someone able to present that reasonably plausible reason","text":"Here is the truth - watch too much porn and you get erectile dysfunction: [VIDEO] Why did it take me decades to find someone able to present that reasonably plausible reason as to why I should definitely not want to watch porn under any circumstances whatsoever? The argument (with science) in the above TED talk is that if you do watch a lot of porn then you are not going to aroused by real women as much as you should, leading to unsatisfactory bedroom performances. Yep, porn is actually that dangerous. I might not care for the traditional 'don't watch porn because it is porn'/'think of the children'/'porn exploits women' arguments, however, I do have enlightened self interest and loss of libido is so not what I want to have. If I was in the UK government I would want to get this message out to kids in schools, not to scare them away from porn for the sake of it, but because they deserve to have 'normal', happy sexual health. By censoring porn they are not going to get this message, in fact they will be further away from it than they are now, doing what they can to get the 'forbidden fruit' instead of knowing why they just should not bother. Instead of censoring they could have demanded that there be a banner on the pages of porn sites warning that use of porn leads to erectile dysfunction. This would be a proactive move, no harder to get into law than that stupid 'this site uses cookies' directive. They could get the ISPs to do it so porn from outer Mongolia would be suitably"},{"id":36380588,"author":"nashashmi","month":"2023-06","story":"Falcon LLM – A 40B Model","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as academic knowledge in a debate","quote":"Try a little bit of academic knowledge here","text":"And you know this because you’re the expert of knowing what is morality? Decades of people telling you it is immoral, yet somehow you come along to say the opposite and hope people believe it? The only justification you can give is an ad hominem attack accusing the other person of not knowing what they are talking about. Try a little bit of academic knowledge here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26911650,"author":"Korpocalypse","month":"2021-04","story":"Ask HN: How Do You Optimize Your Intake of Information?","stance":"rec","why":"Related talk on dopamine, internet and novelty seeking","quote":"related ted talk on dopamine, the internet, and novelty seeking","text":"related ted talk on dopamine, the internet, and novelty seeking [VIDEO] also related: the modern diet contains alot of calorically dense food, which means less stomach and intenstinal stretching, which means more ghrelin, which activates these novelty-seeking dopaminergic systems in the brain as for the original question: a) as far as optimizing, I think that approaching the optimal state is less important than making improvements to a consistent performance ethic. Better to do something a little better every day than to miss days trying to do it better than everybody else. b) with information, I tend to focus on information that relates to a particular goal relevant to job performance or general wellbeing and then take notes on any new information and review prior notes relevant to the newest information in order to build an intuitive mental logical structure of the problem/goal and all of its moving parts. But since so few things are scientifically provable, I try to \"bet\" on certain things based on how more or less likely they are and an informal cost-benefit analysis"},{"id":25419175,"author":"trident1000","month":"2020-12","story":"Pornhub just purged all unverified content from the platform","stance":"rec","why":"Good TED talks on societal issues from porn","quote":"Theres some good TED talks on this which are easy to find.","text":"Ive watched porn all my life (like most). I dont have a moral vendetta against it. But it seems to be a no-go topic for discussing the serious societal issues from porn. As I've grown older ive realized its really very toxic and probably the most widespread addiction in the US causing real issues. When you watch porn all day or even just a few times per week, your testosterone and hormonal balance as a man changes. You do not go after women and start a family. You are more likely to be depressed. It causes brain fog and mood disorders. Etc. Theres some good TED talks on this which are easy to find. This is never talked about and should be. Little off topic maybe but just wanted to state this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17167113,"author":"PKop","month":"2018-05","story":"Pornography and the Butterfly Effect","stance":"rec","why":"Enlightening on detrimental brain effects of porn","quote":"Here's an enlightening video on the detrimental effects of porn on the brain.","text":"Here's an enlightening video on the detrimental effects of porn on the brain. Porn addiction shows similar effects as opiates. [VIDEO] Related: \"NoFap\" [LINK]"},{"id":7443028,"author":"diadara","month":"2014-03","story":"Hexagon 16384","stance":"rec","why":"Addiction effects on the brain like hard drugs","quote":"Have you seen this TED talk about porn addiction","text":"[VIDEO] Have you seen this TED talk about porn addiction and how it has similar effects like some sort of hard drug addiction on the brain. Everything uses the same pipeline."},{"id":5379890,"author":"argonz","month":"2013-03","story":"Ask HN: Maybe not the best place to seek life advice but I trust you guys","stance":"rec","why":"Suggests resetting dopamine circuits to regain motivation","quote":"By withdrawing and resensitizing your dopamine circuits you might jumpstart your motivation","text":"Porn can create/maintain different thinking patterns, underhabituate you for normal real-life cues. By withdrawing and resensitizing your dopamine circuits you might jumpstart your motivation, getting more fun and getting out of the rut. Shouldn't expect a miracle but may worth a try. [VIDEO] good luck!"},{"id":5052423,"author":"msluyter","month":"2013-01","story":"Did porn warp me forever?","stance":"rec","why":"Empirical grounding for article's anecdote","quote":"which provides some empirical grounding for the anecdote in the article","text":"On the same topic, \"The Great Porn Experiment\" TED talk, which provides some empirical grounding for the anecdote in the article: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23285117,"author":"PKop","month":"2020-05","story":"The transformation of the porn industry under coronavirus","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of harm from porn","quote":"There is plenty of evidence of harm from porn usage, it hijacks the brain in a similar manner as hard core drugs.","text":"There is plenty of evidence of harm from porn usage, it hijacks the brain in a similar manner as hard core drugs. [0] The social consequences are pretty obvious, satisfying fundemental bioligical needs through artifical manner degrades what those desires are meant to achieve: family formation, marriages, which it inhibits forming or ruins those that already exist. Go spend some time on the no-fap sub-reddit to see the massive positive change that occurs when men quit consuming porn. Porn is a tool for social control, pacification and weakening culture [1], it is not empowering for either side of the equation. If this wasn't true, it wouldn't have been used as a form of psychological warfare like it was in this situation [2]. Did they do this to \"empower\" their enemy? [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":6373326,"author":"scrrr","month":"2013-09","story":"Teenagers and the Internet","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as proof too much porn is bad","quote":"Apparently there's some proof that too much pornography is bad for you.","text":"Apparently there's some proof that too much pornography is bad for you. [1] Not too mention all the multitasking. Other than that the Internet is like every other thing. It has good and bad sides. I, for one, have faith in teenagers. They'll turn out alright. It's usually the old folks that make the bigger/worse mistakes. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4025686,"author":"zerostar07","month":"2012-05","story":"'The Demise of Guys': How video games and porn are ruining a generation ","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that porn is addictive","quote":"it has all the requirements to become destructively addictive","text":"Agree that the article is crap but at least regarding the internet porn part, it has all the requirements to become destructively addictive. And all stats show that men watch most porn so it's not unjustified sexism. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2U9WMftV40c",1],["T2IaJwkqgPk",1],["8455KEDitpU",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["2V1FtfBDsLU",1],["Rybpaqhg5Qg",1],["PlJkgQZb0VU",1]]},"sWblpsLZ-O8":{"wy":"It is HN's favorite example of extreme code compression, a compelling song and visuals from a program smaller than a tweet.","q":{"x":"not only because it is 256 bytes, but because it is a compelling song too.","i":16854026,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":36396794,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2023-06","story":"Platform Hopping","stance":"strong_rec","why":"256-byte C64 demo with music","quote":"Mind blowing stuff","text":"By now when I see \"linusakesson.net\" I upvote without reading, then I read TFA and watch the vid (or listen to the music in this case). I'm never disappointed. Speaking of which I plan to do something really cool: I've got a \"time capsule\" C64 (well C128 but close enough) and it still works. It's never been \"polluted\" by anything modern: no \"gotek\" SD reader, no nothing: just a real time capsule from the early to mid eighties. And I plan to encode, manually, the 256 bytes (yes, bytes) of linusakesson's \"A mind is born\" C64 demo. I'll then run it (with the C128 hooked to an old CRT, of course), thinking that technically I could have encoded and ran that in the eighties. Mind blowing stuff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27226937,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2021-05","story":"Before the iPhone, I worked on a few games for what were called \"feature phones\"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"256 bytes including music","quote":"The craziest ever, to me, is the following in 256... bytes.","text":"> fwiw, this made me spend a few minutes in youtube looking at old c64 and apple and Atari demos. what people ended up doing with those devices - years after they were mainstream - is still crazy to me. The craziest ever, to me, is the following in 256... bytes. Bytes (including the music ofc, which is the whole point). [VIDEO]"},{"id":23004463,"author":"wgx","month":"2020-04","story":"Memories – 256 bytes demo winner of Revision 2020","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Astonishing music in a 256 byte C64 demo","quote":"my favourite 256 byte demo is for the C64","text":"Not MS-DOS, but my favourite 256 byte demo is for the C64: \"A Mind Is Born\", check it out: [VIDEO] that music is astonishing."},{"id":22638574,"author":"twic","month":"2020-03","story":"A Mysterious Song on the Internet (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Whole demo is only 256 bytes","quote":"There's an amazing demo called 'A Mind Is Born'","text":"There's an amazing demo called 'A Mind Is Born': [VIDEO] Amazing principally because the whole thing is 256 bytes: [LINK] I never meet anyone who has even heard of the demoscene, let alone come seen this particular work. I was at a film festival watching a programme of short films, one of which was the well-made AI-box escape romp 'Watch Room': [LINK] And similarly found my interface with my seat disrupted by the closing credits using the music from 'A Mind Is Born'. This being a festival, the director was there, so i could tell him how much i appreciated the reference!"},{"id":16854026,"author":"kwoff","month":"2018-04","story":"A dive into the making of a 64K Intro","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compelling song in only 256 bytes","quote":"not only because it is 256 bytes, but because it is a compelling song too.","text":"I still play [VIDEO] over and over, just for the song, not only because it is 256 bytes, but because it is a compelling song too."},{"id":46044175,"author":"yetihehe","month":"2025-11","story":"Human brains are preconfigured with instructions for understanding the world","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates Kolmogorov complexity with 256 bytes of code","quote":"It's only 256 bytes, but can generate over 2 minutes of complex music and video.","text":"For a demonstration of Kolmogorov complexity, it's good to watch \"A mind is born\"[0] by lftkryo. It's only 256 bytes, but can generate over 2 minutes of complex music and video. Also, the name is appropriate for this topic :D [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39803001,"author":"voiceblue","month":"2024-03","story":"Game of Life, simulating itself, infinitely zoomable","stance":"rec","why":"Emergent behavior from a 256 byte program","quote":"You might enjoy watching the emergent behavior in the execution of this 256 byte program","text":"You might enjoy watching the emergent behavior in the execution of this 256 byte program - a mind is born [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29818096,"author":"anitil","month":"2022-01","story":"Sonic 3D: Director's Cut (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"256-byte demo built on LFSRs","quote":"I really like 'A Mind is Born'","text":"I really like 'A Mind is Born' - [VIDEO] The summary of it's development could be summarised with \"It's LFSRs all the way down\""},{"id":15579521,"author":"bane","month":"2017-10","story":"Procedurally generated electronic music","stance":"rec","why":"Winning 256-byte C64 demo","quote":"Here's a winning demoscene production, 256 bytes, on a Commodore 64.","text":"Here's a winning demoscene production, 256 bytes, on a Commodore 64. [VIDEO] Technical discussion here: [LINK]"},{"id":46342120,"author":"__patchbit__","month":"2025-12","story":"Maximizing Compression of Apple II Hi-Res Images","stance":"reference","why":"Example of extreme code size compression","quote":"The 256 bytes that encode C64's `A mind is born' demo","text":"The 256 bytes that encode C64's `A mind is born' demo [VIDEO] or the `quad core' demo [LINK] should those techniques pack AI model simulator images for hexapod robotics down at the supervisor binary interface layer, do the demoscene folks get to work catergories in AI and Robotics for `new' competition?"},{"id":31313874,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2022-05","story":"An Afterlife User's Guide to the C64","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter plans to hand-enter the 256-byte demo on a C128","quote":"manually input the 256 bytes making this insane C64 demo","text":"During the first covid lockdown, I was by \"chance\" stuck in the home I grew up in. And I did found my old C128 in attic (which I was only ever using in C64 mode, except to copy disks). It's a time capsule C128 that hasn't been \"polluted\" by anything made in the last 33 years so (I switched to the Amiga in 1989 or so). So one day I plan to manually input the 256 bytes making this insane C64 demo (posted here on HN already), keeping my time capsule C128 intact: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30881997,"author":"pflanze","month":"2022-04","story":"Infinite Mac: An Instant-Booting Quadra in the Browser","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> and it is extremely complex and does much more Who's to say? The interesting bit in this discussion is why the code size is large, and looking in detail what the programs do would be a start. Of course, one could suppose that using a high-level language makes it easier to write code, hence, more code is going to be written. Or you or I could really conclude anything we want, if we're willing to pretend to know. It would be interesting to know, but that takes work to find out. And I'm a lazy bastard. PS. not to argue any more :), but I have checked the size of rsync 3.2.3 now, it's 51 Kloc of C code (including the 6300 loc of the included copy of zlib). You'd need to add a cryptographic library, a GUI library, and GUI code to make it a viable replacement for Dropbox, without comparing features. Git annex 8.20210223 is 70 Kloc of Haskell and 9100 loc of JavaScript, but it depends on Git to do its work, which (version 2.30.2) is 327 Kloc of C code and 225 Kloc of sh and Perl code. Thunar 4.16.8 is 73 Kloc of C code, sshfs is 4700 loc and fuse is 22 Kloc of C, openssh is 113 Kloc of C, so maybe you could build a Dropbox replacement in 212 Kloc of C without looking through the feature set. Maybe that's still 4-5 times smaller than the Dropbox client, but your number of 50 Kloc is again off by a factor of 4. :) (I guess one could say that the truth is \"somewhere in the middle\", looking at those number on a logarithmic scale...) I'm sure you could also come up with a much smaller "},{"id":29773414,"author":"yuchi","month":"2022-01","story":"Monti on the ‘Bin – By Iftkryo","stance":"reference","why":"Known as the author's famous C64 demo","quote":"C64 Demo fame","text":"Execution of Minto’s Csárdás on a breadbin Commodore 64 by Linus Ákesson of “A Mind Is Born”[0] C64 Demo fame. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28563965,"author":"yetihehe","month":"2021-09","story":"Algorithmic symphonies from one line of code (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Realized 256-byte audiovisual demo with synced music","quote":"6 years later","text":"From article: > This would make it possible, for example, for a 256-byte demoscene production to have an interesting and varying audiovisual structure with a strong, inherent synchronization between the effects and the music. 6 years later: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26339810,"author":"verytrivial","month":"2021-03","story":"Dialog – domain-specific language for interactive fiction","stance":"reference","why":"Work by Linus Akesson on C64","quote":"Linus has some extreme coding cred in my book!","text":"I am fascinated by these IF engines for some reason. What sets the Dialog apart is, IMHO, the performance it gets on 6502 in combination with the Å-machine VM by the same author. It's a domain specific language + optimized compiler + runtime for a platform (C64) that hasn't been made for decades. There are some excellent Z-machine impls for C64, but basically anything using the Inform7 runtime (a bunch of convenience parsing and world tricks) runs like a dog. Dialog + Å-machines gets a respectable and growing amount of the way there but cutting out the middle man. Linus has some extreme coding cred in my book! [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25789077,"author":"tiborsaas","month":"2021-01","story":"Apple M1 Assembly Language Hello World","stance":"reference","why":"Example of cool demos written in assembly","quote":"you can write cool demos like this","text":"Besides understanding better what your language of choice is doing under the hood (vaguely) you can write cool demos like this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18568224,"author":"yetihehe","month":"2018-11","story":"Mathematical Simplicity May Drive Evolution's Speed","stance":"reference","why":"Rich-looking structure from tiny nudged random data","quote":"it's just nudged/selected random data","text":"> DNA is extremely important, but it makes no sense if [it is] not in a cell, in an organism, in an ecosystem. Other interactions are at play, and this application of algorithmic information cannot capture the extent of that complexity. It reminds me of demoscene, specifically \"A mind is born\"[1], 256-byte demo which \"looks\" complicated and with rich structure, but it's just nudged/selected random data. Author said that he played around with some random generated content until he found something nice. Just by itself that 256 bytes is still not enough to replicate behaviour, it must be run on hardware, which is like \"environment\" for DNA. Without several supporting chips which are easy to access, that code would mean nothing and would not generate such complexity. Moreover, those \"patterns\" in demo are patterns only because WE interpret them as meaningful. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35561617,"author":"phoe-krk","month":"2023-04","story":"What Kind of Mind Does ChatGPT Have?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"how is a mind born?","text":"> we should think more about what makes a 'mind'. Unrelated, but the first thing that came to me: how is a mind born? [0][1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["q0RX59G2jaA",1],["ouAG4vXFORc",1],["S4gU2Tsv6hY",1],["iGbC5j4pG9w",1],["ZXJWO2FQ16c",1],["jB0vBmiTr6o",1],["x1bX3F7uTrg",1],["uHQ4WCU1WQc",1],["LA_DrBwkiJA",1],["r3snVCRo_bI",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["Erp8IAUouus",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["aSEQfqNYNAc",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["PQmSUHhP3ug",1],["_YWMGuh15nE",1],["fwcl17Q0bpk",1]]},"fU9hR3kiOK0":{"wy":"Commenters repeatedly cite it as the talk that reframed how they think about caching, derived data and event sourcing.","q":{"x":"This talk on Apache Samza completely changed how I think about caching and derived data in general","i":45084830,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45084830,"author":"chamomeal","month":"2025-08","story":"Replacing a cache service with a database","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed how commenter thinks about caching and derived data","quote":"This talk on Apache Samza completely changed how I think about caching and derived data in general","text":"Idk I think caching is a crucial part of many well-designed systems. There’s a lot of very cache-able data out there. If invalidating events are well defined or the data is fine being stale (week/month level dashboards, for example), that’s a fantastic reason to use a cache. I’d much rather just stuff those values in a cache than figure out any other more complicated solution. I also just think it’s a necessary evil of big systems. Sometimes you need derived data. You can even think about databases as a kind of cache: the “real” data is the stream of every event that ever updated data in the database! (Yes this stretching the meaning of cache lol) However I agree that caching is often an easy bandaid for a bad architecture. This talk on Apache Samza completely changed how I think about caching and derived data in general: [VIDEO] And this interview has some interesting insights on the problems that caching faces at super large scale systems (twitter specifically): [LINK]"},{"id":45087464,"author":"chamomeal","month":"2025-08","story":"Replacing a cache service with a database","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Event stream as source of truth gives auto-updating views","quote":"I loved this talk and I think you should check it out!","text":"Hey OP you may have seen this already, but in case you didn’t see my other comment, you should definitely check out this talk by Martin Kleppman. [VIDEO] It details Apache samza, which I didn’t totally grasp but it seems similar to what you’re talking about here. He talks about how if you could essentially use an event stream as your source of truth instead of a database, and you had a sufficiently powerful stream processor, you could define views on that data by consuming stream events. The end result is kind of like an auto-updating cache with no invalidation issues or race conditions. Need a new view on the data? Just define it and run the entire event stream through it. Once the stream is processed, that source of data is perpetually accurate and up-to-date. I’m not a database guy and most of this stuff is over my head, but I loved this talk and I think you should check it out! It’s the first thing I thought of when I read your post."},{"id":19905066,"author":"btown","month":"2019-05","story":"Ask HN: What overlooked class of tools should a self-taught programmer look into","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pedagogical intro to streaming architecture versus relational databases","quote":"is a fantastic intro to these dynamics, and it's something I'll always recommend to both experienced and inexperienced data modelers and backend developers","text":"The talk on \"Turning the database inside-out\" [0][1] by the author, Martin Kleppmann, is a fantastic intro to these dynamics, and it's something I'll always recommend to both experienced and inexperienced data modelers and backend developers. It goes pedagogically through the way things are typically done in a relational database in such a clear way that word-for-word it's one of the best tutorials I've seen... but it also weaves a narrative of \"how can this be done better/more scalably/more reliably/more flexibly-to-business-needs\" in pointing to a streaming/event-sourcing architecture. You may or not need the latter right away, but it's a fantastic tool to have in your toolbox to be able to say \"ah, this new requirement feels like it would benefit hugely from this architecture.\" Especially for OP who's starting to think about the \"why\" of messaging queues, this could be a fantastically valuable first step. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":17130800,"author":"lbradstreet","month":"2018-05","story":"GCP and Confluent partner to deliver a managed Apache Kafka service","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the log as the core of databases","quote":"I can highly recommend Martin Kleppmann","text":"Yes, I like to think of it as as the transaction log without any of the logic. If you’re interested, I can highly recommend Martin Kleppmann’s talk “Turning the database inside out” [VIDEO]"},{"id":9455670,"author":"btown","month":"2015-04","story":"How Sift Science Rebuilt Its App on React and DropWizard","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Simplifying database-cache interactions by thinking in streams of derived data","quote":"Here's an excellent talk that provides some context to the Confluent article","text":"Since this is taking off a bit: Here's an excellent talk that provides some context to the Confluent article, and takes us through the thought process of simplifying convoluted database-cache-user interactions by thinking in terms of streams of derived data: [VIDEO] . Also highly recommended."},{"id":35500573,"author":"avinassh","month":"2023-04","story":"Ask HN: Append-Only SQL Schema?","stance":"rec","why":"Log as persistence with materialised views for querying","quote":"I think you would love this talk by Martin Kleppmann","text":"I think you would love this talk by Martin Kleppmann - Turning the database inside out with Apache Samza: [VIDEO] He basically explores similar idea, you have a log to persist and materialised views to query."},{"id":28702975,"author":"dmitryminkovsky","month":"2021-09","story":"The Reactive Monolith – How to Move from CRUD to Event Sourcing","stance":"rec","why":"Inspired the commenter's adoption of event sourcing","quote":"Martin's talk validated many of my obsessive-compulsive insecurities about data.","text":"I second this wholeheartedly because this happened to me. I'm the kind of person who learns by my making my own mistakes (as opposed to learning from others', which is what smart people do)—and boy, did I learn a lot from the mistake of trying to build a general purpose application with event sourcing. Theoretically, event sourcing is the way all applications should be built. I was first taken by this methodology by Martin Kleppmann's talk \"Turning the Database Inside Out\" (2014)[0]. Looking back, I think I had always subconsciously felt like there was something wrong with mutating state in place. You lose information. You lose accountability. After all, \"accountants don't use erasers,\" and Martin's talk validated many of my obsessive-compulsive insecurities about data. As Charity Majors points out on Twitter [1]: > ...while storing raw events may be \"expensive\" compared to any single optimization, all optimizations can be derived from raw events. The reverse is impossible. And that's true. The table-stream duality [2][3] means that if you store events, you can later project those events into the exact perfect table for any use-case. But if you transform those events into a projection and store that projection, well, whatever table you store is data you have. You wind up having just an aggregate. Unfortunately, the technical costs you incur when you attempt to store events instead of tabular data are tremendous . The problems and questions that arise are basically endless. To"},{"id":25349678,"author":"je42","month":"2020-12","story":"Kafka Is Not a Database","stance":"rec","why":"Starter for using a streaming store like Kafka as a database","quote":"a good starter to for knowing how to use a Streaming Store like Kafka as DB","text":"Indeed. Known the tools and apply the one that has least cons and most pros ;). Also, a good starter to for knowing how to use a Streaming Store like Kafka as DB is the video Database inside-out. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11967214,"author":"z1mm32m4n","month":"2016-06","story":"Alan Kay on the misunderstanding of OOP (1998)","stance":"rec","why":"Stream processing as a programming architecture linked to messaging","quote":"I think this video[1] does a nice job of talking about stream processing as a programming architecture.","text":"Hearing the way Alan Kay talks about \"messaging\" always reminds me of two technologies: the Flux/Redux architecture and Apache Samza & Kafka. > The key in making great and growable systems is much more to design how its modules communicate rather than what their internal properties and behaviors should be. With React and Flux, this is exactly what's going on! You're defining view components, and when someone interacts with them, there's a very structured, discrete set of actions that can be fired. These actions are then dispatched and handled elsewhere, but somewhere at the core, there's a single source of truth for what happens upon any given message. And for Apache Samza, I think this video[1] does a nice job of talking about stream processing as a programming architecture. There's a connection to be made between processing streams of information and processing messages between modules, and that video goes a good way towards exposing it. [1]: [VIDEO] \"Turning the database inside out with Apache Samza\""},{"id":11465705,"author":"pramodliv1","month":"2016-04","story":"GraphQL-enabled event sourcing engine","stance":"rec","why":"Modern concrete example of event sourcing ideas","quote":"talk by Martin Kleppmann is pretty good too","text":"If you want a more modern and concrete example, the \"Turning your database inside out\" talk by Martin Kleppmann is pretty good too: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9370136,"author":"felipesabino","month":"2015-04","story":"Real-Time Full-Text Search with Luwak and Samza","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how useful stream processing is for data processing","quote":"Samza's author really opened my mind related to how useful stream process is to high performance data processing","text":"Samza's author really opened my mind related to how useful stream process is to high performance data processing [1] and I think Samza's only bummer (for me, personally, today) is it's lack of support for non-JMV languages [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":12844606,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2016-11","story":"Bottled Water – Real-Time Integration of PostgreSQL and Kafka","stance":"reference","why":"Author's video relating to Samza-style streaming","quote":"There's a decent video by the author knocking around somewhere.","text":"Previous discussion: [LINK] IIRC this works closely with Samza. There's a decent video by the author knocking around somewhere. EDIT: This is the video I was thinking of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11803843,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2016-05","story":"Andl, a relational language that is not SQL, is coming to Postgres","stance":"reference","why":"Samza ideas for scaling data replication","quote":"I think Apache Samza has some promising ideas in this area","text":"> \"The trouble with this is that the database is the hardest part to scale.\" Probably depends on the plan followed for data replication, as some approaches seem to make it easier to add new DB nodes as you scale. I've not tried it myself, but I think Apache Samza has some promising ideas in this area: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10973175,"author":"rapind","month":"2016-01","story":"Reactive GraphQL","stance":"reference","why":"Treating data as a stream","quote":"Data as a stream is pretty interesting too","text":"The idea of changefeeds / subscription cursors has been around for a while now, especially in Meteor. I doubt it'll be that crazy to add reactivity to graphql queries when compared to what Meteor is already doing w/ subscriptions and DDP. RethinkDB for example has changefeeds built in and is tackling this problem as well. See: [LINK] Data as a stream is pretty interesting too: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9888537,"author":"atrilumen","month":"2015-07","story":"Mostly Adequate Guide to Functional Programming in JavaScript","stance":"reference","why":"Immutability applied to the database layer","quote":"Immutability is useful everywhere in the stack, even the database.","text":"Immutability is useful everywhere in the stack, even the database. See \"Turning the database inside out with Apache Samza\" by Martin Kleppmann: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UEAMfLPZZhE",1],["cQP8WApzIQQ",1],["oeYBdghaIjc",1],["MFRWDuduuSw",1],["XehNK7UpZsc",1],["Qct6LKbneKQ",1],["7JUs4c99-mo",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["UQuIVsNzqDk",1],["ML4tw_UzqZE",1],["0bcZb-SsnrA",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["NcUJnmBqHTY",1],["PZRI1IfStY0",1],["fpbOEoRrHyU",1],["ARYP83yNAWk",1]]},"lJ8ydIuPFeU":{"wy":"The canonical recommendation whenever percentiles, tail latency, load testing or averages come up, described by some as life changing.","q":{"x":"This a life changing video by Gil Tene on this topic.","i":22797291,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":35782413,"author":"leetrout","month":"2023-05","story":"Grasshopper – An Open Source Python Library for Load Testing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic talk on load testing statistics","quote":"this talk by Gil Tene is fantastic if you're interested in load testing stats","text":"We use locust at work but I HIGHLY recommend wrk for a very robust yet simple load testing tool. [LINK] And of course, this talk by Gil Tene is fantastic if you're interested in load testing stats [VIDEO]"},{"id":22797291,"author":"wmfiv","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: Must Read from ACM Library?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"High percentiles matter because one customer request fans out to many","quote":"This a life changing video by Gil Tene on this topic.","text":"This a life changing video by Gil Tene on this topic. Bottom line is because of the number requests to support a single customer \"request\", the high percentiles are the ones that actually matter. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19558469,"author":"Rafuino","month":"2019-04","story":"Averages Can Be Misleading: Try a Percentile (2014)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk on why averages mislead for latency","quote":"this great talk from Gil Tene on how NOT to measure latencies (basically, don't use averages!).","text":"This topic always leads me to think about this great talk from Gil Tene on how NOT to measure latencies (basically, don't use averages!). [VIDEO] I'm also a huge fan of how Dormando showed latency distributions in one of his recent Memcached Extstore posts. The default is 95th percentile but you can change the percentile to what matters to you (i.e. 99th percentile if you ask me!). Scroll down to see what he did and play with it. [LINK]"},{"id":12241122,"author":"vikiomega9","month":"2016-08","story":"Throughput vs. Latency and Lock-Free vs. Wait-Free","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk on worst-case latency and high percentiles","quote":"Gil Tene has an excellent talk[1] on this.","text":"Doesn't this depend on how \"tuned\" your subcomponents are? If I had a system that's supposedly heavily optimized, I would simply start seeing the worst-case latency on a higher percentile, 99.9th and so forth. Gil Tene has an excellent talk[1] on this. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42998735,"author":"super_linear","month":"2025-02","story":"How Not to Measure Latency (The P99 Lie) by GIL Tene","stance":"rec","why":"Most page users experience the system's p99 latency","quote":"Core takeaway for me was that most users of a page will experience the p99 latency of the system","text":"Tried to link to the specific timestamp in the video, where the p99 lie is discussed. Core takeaway for me was that most users of a page will experience the p99 latency of the system: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23213863,"author":"agallego","month":"2020-05","story":"Kafka Removing Zookeeper Dependency","stance":"rec","why":"Argues tail percentiles are what customers actually experience","quote":"See Gil Tene's talk on how not to measure latency.","text":"who cares about lower percentage tiers in the world of big data? what does the p30 or 50 even matter at large scale? See Gil Tene's talk on how not to measure latency. [VIDEO] What matters is what most of your customers will get - p99, p999, p9999, p100."},{"id":22630356,"author":"allanrbo","month":"2020-03","story":"Lies, Damned Lies, and Averages: Perc50, Perc95 Explained for Programmers","stance":"rec","why":"Another really good explanation of percentile pitfalls","quote":"is another really good explanation of this topic","text":"The \"how not to measure latency\" talk by Gil Tene is another really good explanation of this topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18194507,"author":"latch","month":"2018-10","story":"We can do better than percentile latencies","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by the author of HdrHistogram on latency measurement","quote":"Worth watching is How NOT to Measure Latency","text":"Worth watching is How NOT to Measure Latency: [VIDEO] The speaker Gil Tene is also the author of the HdrHistogram which addresses this articles point: [LINK]"},{"id":13547228,"author":"telotortium","month":"2017-02","story":"Unlearning descriptive statistics","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why users often hit rare bad-case latencies across dependencies","quote":"Gil Tene has an entire talk about this point called \"How NOT to measure latency","text":"Gil Tene has an entire talk about this point called \"How NOT to measure latency\" ( [VIDEO] ). In many circumstances, a web service (either server or client) has large numbers of dependencies, so a user only has to hit 95th-percentile latency for one dependency to have their overall latency significantly hurt. In other words, users may encounter the \"rare\" bad case on a majority of requests. Thus, even higher percentiles should be tightly controlled in order to truly keep a web service's overall latency under control."},{"id":47577707,"author":"mrngm","month":"2026-03","story":"Queueing Requests Queues Your Capacity Problems, Too","stance":"reference","why":"Related Strange Loop talk on measuring latency","quote":"That reminds me of this talk[0] by Gil Tene","text":"That reminds me of this talk[0] by Gil Tene called \"How NOT to Measure Latency\" at the Strangeloop conference in 2015 (or read this blog post[1] that contains the most important points). [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":38282446,"author":"nerpderp82","month":"2023-11","story":"Four Kinds of Optimisation","stance":"reference","why":"Covers coordinated omission in latency measurement","quote":"the topic of Coordinated Omission","text":"Performance (Really) Matters by Emery Berger talks a lot about layout. [LINK] And the topic of Coordinated Omission, \"How NOT to Measure Latency\" by Gil Tene [VIDEO] Injection slowness into an application to measure the relative impact of that portion of the application has really worked well for me."},{"id":37689519,"author":"remram","month":"2023-09","story":"Your nines are not my nines (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about how percentiles lie","quote":"I remember seeing a talk years ago about percentiles and how they lie","text":"I remember seeing a talk years ago about percentiles and how they lie: [VIDEO] You should be exposing the maximum metric from your app, computing a percentile from an aggregated histogram is lossy. [edit: Found the link, \"How NOT to Measure Latency\" by Gil Tene]"},{"id":34169776,"author":"dikei","month":"2022-12","story":"Your load generator is probably lying to you","stance":"reference","why":"Original presentation the article summarizes","quote":"Here's the presentation.","text":"> I found this article hard to read; it felt like bulleted noted after watching a presentation and many of the statements lacked the context to back them up. Because it is. Here's the presentation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28484816,"author":"kryptiskt","month":"2021-09","story":"Suspicious Discontinuities","stance":"reference","why":"Explores the ideas under discussion","quote":"I don't know if it's the correct talk, but those ideas are explored in","text":"I don't know if it's the correct talk, but those ideas are explored in \"How NOT to Measure Latency by Gil Tene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25240114,"author":"atombender","month":"2020-11","story":"K6: Like unit testing, for performance","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for argument that monitoring tools mismeasure latency spikes","quote":"Gil Tene (Azul Systems) has argued convincingly","text":"Does K6 deal with the coordinated omission problem? Gil Tene (Azul Systems) has argued convincingly [1] (slides [2]) that monitoring tools get latency measurement wrong because sudden spikes aren't represented correctly in timings because of averaging and the wrong use of percentiles. He argues that percentiles simply aren't useful, because, statistically, most requests will experience >= 99.99-percentile response times. All percentiles lie; the only truly useful and realistic number is, in fact, the 100th percentile. He also argues that the most revealing way to show latency is with a complete histogram. I haven't used K6 yet, but I've been looking for a good load-testing tool, and intend to try it out. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":20964386,"author":"the8472","month":"2019-09","story":"Deterministic thinking: a problem in how we think, not just in how we act","stance":"reference","why":"Example of using CDFs instead of thresholds","quote":"How to NOT measure latency , looks at CDFs instead of slow/fast thresholds.","text":"Some additional examples of people fixing instances of this problem Dissolving the fermi paradox [0], convolves distributions instead of multiplying point estimates of life/no life. How to NOT measure latency , looks at CDFs instead of slow/fast thresholds. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12716402,"author":"sitkack","month":"2016-10","story":"Sanic – Python 3.5+ web server that's written to go fast","stance":"reference","why":"Further reading on why averages mislead for latency","quote":"See also, Gil Tene","text":"You almost never want to use average as your metric when dealing with time. For time, the 95 or 99 percentile latency is going to be measure of how many requests out of a hundred a less than your metric. Esp when the thing you are measuring is a piece of larger system, which is the crux, because of how a resource in turn requests k-more resources. Each request has an equal chance of being over the latency percentile. Even at 10 requests, only 90% of sessions will be under the 99% latency. See also, Gil Tene [VIDEO] A nice overview, [LINK] See also, [LINK]"},{"id":10804839,"author":"hackercomplex","month":"2015-12","story":"Tech-Hub Housing Costs","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on why averages hide tail latency","quote":"none of this Coordinated Omission bullshit.","text":"this is like.. average latency of 600ms.. sounds great when you tell your CEO but it still doesn't really tell the true story about how a huge portion of your customers are waiting 1.2s for their goddamn page to load. Max doesn't tell the whole story either, but it's pretty revealing. Show me that 99.9999 percentile baby! 4 point precision then we're talkin.. and none of this Coordinated Omission bullshit. [VIDEO] </rant>"},{"id":10334674,"author":"dang","month":"2015-10","story":"How Not to Measure Latency","stance":"reference","why":"Original talk that the submitted summary is based on","quote":"This is a summary of","text":"This is a summary of [VIDEO] . It looks like a good summary, so I guess we won't replace the URL above, even though HN prefers original sources. We did change the title to that of the talk though."},{"id":38090204,"author":"xxpor","month":"2023-10","story":"Firefox Got Faster for Real Users in 2023","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm sure internally folks are looking at more stuff, but blog post wise, it makes it look like you're intentionally omitting it (see [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["HlAXp0-M6SY",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1]]},"mPqWstVnRjQ":{"wy":"Commenters offer it as a fascinating, accessible introduction to the world of fungi whenever mushrooms or mycelium come up.","q":{"x":"made a nice introduction to the world of funghi- absolutely fascinating","i":15888731,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":26597289,"author":"samstave","month":"2021-03","story":"Buildings made with fungi could live, grow, and then biodegrade","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Stamets on mycelium's versatile applications.","quote":"a fantastic Joe Rogan Podcast","text":"\"There's been buzz lately about mycelium\" -- Well, Paul Stamets has been talking for decades. Did a TED talk, amazing other videos - a fantastic Joe Rogan Podcast etc. Mycelium is AMAZING - and I really hope that we start using it in the myriad ways it is versatile for so many different applications. --- Have you Seen these Paul Stamets vids: Joe rogan: [VIDEO] Ted (6 ways mushrooms can change the world: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":18139440,"author":"greenwireless","month":"2018-10","story":"Psychedelic Mushrooms Are Closer to Medicinal Use","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moving, discussion-provoking two-hour Stamets conversation.","quote":"Highly recommended.","text":"I generally dislike mushrooms but I was pretty moved by Joe Rogan's podcast #1035 with Paul Stamets. Despite being over 2 hours long I suggested it to and watched it in it's entirety with several different people and each time we end up discussing the content off and on for weeks or months afterwards. Highly recommended. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16372962,"author":"tmnvix","month":"2018-02","story":"New antibiotic family discovered in dirt","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating Stamets interview on fungi.","quote":"one of the most fascinating things I've come across in years.","text":"As a lay person, it's not clear to me whether these malacidins were found in mycelium (fungi). I'm inclined to think this is likely. If so, finding them in dirt is not really surprising (30% of dirt is comprised of mycelium - living and dead). It seems like the obvious place to look for new antibiotics. For anyone that's interested in fungi I can't recommend listening to Paul Stamets talk on the subject more highly. Like a lot of people here on HN I take in a lot of information and his recent interview with Joe Rogan[0] was one of the most fascinating things I've come across in years. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15888731,"author":"deepvibrations","month":"2017-12","story":"The largest living thing on Earth is a fungus (2014)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nice introduction to the world of fungi.","quote":"made a nice introduction to the world of funghi- absolutely fascinating","text":"I stumbled upon Paul Stamets on Joe Rogan's podcast, which made a nice introduction to the world of funghi- absolutely fascinating and exciting to think of what else we may discover in this field in the coming years! For anyone interested- [VIDEO]"},{"id":20342884,"author":"ThouYS","month":"2019-07","story":"Radiotrophic fungus","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the many things fungi can do.","quote":"I recommend Joe Rogans podcast with Paul Stamets","text":"If you're interested in funghi, I recommend Joe Rogans podcast with Paul Stamets, funghi seem to be able to do many things: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20041101,"author":"sakopov","month":"2019-05","story":"Complex life may only exist because of millions of years of groundwork by fungi","stance":"rec","why":"Well-known mycologist with interesting things to say.","quote":"I'd also recommend giving this podcast [1] with Paul Stamets a listen.","text":"It is indeed. I'd also recommend giving this podcast [1] with Paul Stamets a listen. He's a fairly well-known mycologist who has some very interesting things to say on the topic. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18073416,"author":"TheAceOfHearts","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: What podcasts do you listen to regularly?","stance":"rec","why":"Stamets on effects of mushrooms including psilocybin.","quote":"Another big favorite of mine was his discussion with Paul Stamets [4]","text":"Joe Rogan Experience [0]. I love that he sits down with people and actually has long in-depth conversations with them. Here's a few interesting ones: * Let's start with his Elon Musk discussion [1]. It came out 2 weeks ago and it already has 14m views. They basically discuss a bunch of his ideas along with things such as AI and the future of humanity. It's fascinating to get a peek into the kind of person he is. * His discussion with Matthew Walker has been one of the most enlightening ones for me [2]. He's a sleep expert, and in the podcast they go over different myths relating to that. There was recently a HN post [3] where some people were quoting him and his book. * Another big favorite of mine was his discussion with Paul Stamets [4], a mycologist (i.e. a mushroom expert). They discuss some of the effects of different kinds of mushrooms, including psilocybin mushrooms (colloquially known as magic mushrooms). * I loved his discussion with Gary Johnson [5], the Libertarian Party's presidential candidate for 2016. How many other presidential candidates would you imagine would be willing to sit down and answer hard-hitting questions for +2 hours? He also had a discussion with Larry Sharpe [6], the Libertarian Party's candidate for governor of New York in 2018. Full disclosure: I recently joined the Libertarian Party. Listening to these podcasts pushed me to learn more about their movement. * He had a discussion with Mikhaila Peterson [7], the daughter of Jordan Peterson, whi"},{"id":16840041,"author":"a_d","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Stamets on mycology, bioremediation and fungi, listed among favorite talks.","quote":"Paul Stamets on mycology, bioremediation and fungi- Joe Rogan Experience","text":"Bret Victor - Inventing on principle [LINK] Alan Kay - Power of Simplicity [LINK] Steve Jobs - Marketing (unveiling Think Different) [LINK] Robert Cialdini - On Influence [LINK] Peter Thiel - On Zero to One (Notes on Starts Ups or How to Build the Future) [LINK] Naval Ravikant on Reading, Happiness, Systems for Decision Making, Habits, Honesty and More (Farnam Street podcast) [LINK] Josh Wolfe - On \"This is who you are up against\" podcast [LINK] Richard Feynman - Fun to Imagine [LINK] Paul Graham - Startup school (2008) [LINK] James Burke - Connections (BBC documentary) <No link> Jim Al-Khalili tells the story of the Atom (BBC) [LINK] Elon Musk's 2003 Stanford University Entrepreneurial Thought Leaders Lecture [LINK] Ken Robinson - Do schools kill creativity? [LINK] Paul Stamets on mycology, bioremediation and fungi- Joe Rogan Experience [VIDEO]"},{"id":16863194,"author":"BadassFractal","month":"2018-04","story":"Mylo: A leather-like material made from mycelium","stance":"rec","why":"Super interesting conversation about mushrooms' potential.","quote":"I found the conversation between Paul Stamets and Joe Rogan to be super interesting","text":"I found the conversation between Paul Stamets and Joe Rogan to be super interesting: [VIDEO] . Never really thought much about mushrooms until that episode. This proof of concept seems like a great taste of how much untapped potential fungi have for changing everyday life if we invest more into its research."},{"id":15661856,"author":"OzzyB","month":"2017-11","story":"A fungus with over 20k sexes","stance":"rec","why":"Paul Stamets on fungi and their benefits.","quote":"talks at great length about fungi and it's benefits -- truly fascinating stuff.","text":"OT: The Joe Rogan podcast recently had Paul Stamets on who talks at great length about fungi and it's benefits -- truly fascinating stuff. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42580632,"author":"sshine","month":"2025-01","story":"Are there more trees on Earth than stars in the Milky Way? (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Discussion at 1h53m on fungus consuming another and FDA cordyceps rulings.","quote":"It's episode #1035 at around 1h53m","text":"I have no idea, I'm not a mycologist. But what I understood was that one fungus was trying to consume another. It's episode #1035 at around 1h53m: [VIDEO] [LINK] It starts a discussion about how the FDA is incompetent to make rulings about cordyceps mushrooms because the thousands of peer-reviewed articles about them predate a more precise sampling method that uncovers that there are multiple species (or anamorph strands of the same species, I'm unsure)."},{"id":29765609,"author":"wsc981","month":"2022-01","story":"Safest mushrooms to forage and eat","stance":"reference","why":"Stamets hints portobellos may be carcinogenic unless cooked well.","quote":"Portabello seem to be more dangerous than the general public realizes","text":"Portabello seem to be more dangerous than the general public realizes, though Paul Stamets doesn’t want to go into detail. Some suspect this type of mushroom is highly carcinogenic. Either way, cooking the portobellos at high temperatures should make them safe for consumpion. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22165429,"author":"exabyte","month":"2020-01","story":"Saving Florida’s Oranges Starts with Soil","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I studied engineering/physics and I've dipped into the agricultural field the past year having developed an interest in it, also observing the operations of a local organic farm. It's actually quite fascinating. I'm currently following a course created by Dr. Elaine Ingham, who is a microbiologist that studies soil biology and has done some really amazing research. She also worked at Colorado State University, which can't be a coincidence! Here's the lowdown of what happens, because we've been getting this wrong for centuries in agriculture. The nutrients (N, Ph, Mg, K, etc) are bound to materials in the soil ranging from rocks to pebbles, to sands, silts and clays. We have been destroying the soil by tilling practices and large machinery which are creating compaction layers that lead to anaerobic conditions causing the \"good\" biology in the soil to die and also preventing roots from growing to the depth they need. It turns out that the way that the nutrients become available to the roots of the plant is by means of the bacteria/fungi breaking their bonds from the previously mentioned materials (rocks, etc) and then, even more interestingly, they get eaten by higher trophic level predators who shit out the nutrients in plant available form! Some fun facts: - The roots release sugars (photosynthates) into the soil which attract particular bacteria/fungi based off its needs for nutrients AND pH requirements (bacteria release substances which raise pH and fungi acids which lower"},{"id":21289068,"author":"wsc981","month":"2019-10","story":"I Survived the “Destroying Angel” (2006)","stance":"reference","why":"Stamets advises against or thorough cooking of portobello mushrooms.","quote":"Paul Stamets recommends against eating Portobello mushrooms or -if you eat them- at least cook them very very well.","text":"Not directly related but mushroom-guru Paul Stamets recommends against eating Portobello mushrooms or -if you eat them- at least cook them very very well. Paul doesn't dare to go into too many details regarding this mushroom though: [VIDEO] Also, Enoki mushrooms should prevent some cancers as was seen in Japan: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17968862,"author":"nugga","month":"2018-09","story":"State of the World's Fungi","stance":"reference","why":"Stamets discussing Lion's Mane and magic mushrooms.","quote":"Paul Stamets on the Joe Rogan Experience talking about Lion's Mane","text":"Paul Stamets on the Joe Rogan Experience talking about Lion's Mane (Hericium erinaceus) and magic mushrooms: [VIDEO] Combining those two with Vitamin B3 (Niacin) supposedly does wonders for circulation or something along those lines. I can only attest that the niacin flush makes me want to rub and massage my body everywhere because it feels so nice and warm. (Note: long term daily extreme doses of Niacin may contribute to liver problems)"}],"r":[["pwaWilO_Pig",2],["gQnmP7UD_zk",1],["XI5frPV58tY",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["8b7erU_DOfE",1],["VI_riscmviI",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["W88Sact1kws",1],["YXehBx0Yc_w",1],["q7K0vRUKXKc",1],["NdSD07U5uBs",1],["zz4YVJ4aRfg",1],["64cEmjtwRgw",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["Cyya23MPoAI",1],["ycPr5-27vSI",1],["R3bo-s_OY4Q",1],["NMvwHDFVpCE",1],["Gatn5ameRr8",1]]},"pgoetgxecw8":{"wy":"HN returns to it constantly in software performance debates as the primer on non-pessimization and why modern software is slow.","q":{"x":"The video on Philosophies of Optimisation is a good primer on the tenets of the course.","i":42213459,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":29085974,"author":"dundarious","month":"2021-11","story":"Anatomy of a Terminal Emulator","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Refterm series on high-performance application design","quote":"Casey Muratori's refterm video series is a great example of how to do application design.","text":"Casey Muratori's refterm video series is a great example of how to do application design. It focuses on writing a terminal emulator for Windows, but almost all the concepts apply to nixes as well -- even down to the level of OS primitives like memory mappings to create a ring buffer, etc. He builds a nearly complete terminal that appears to better support unicode and escape sequences than windows-terminal, in drastically less code and with literally orders of magnitude better performance. In particular, I like his focus on experimentation to figure out a reasonable upper bound on performance (so you can measure success/failure), \"non-pessimisation\" (don't do things extrinsic to the actual task), understanding the actual machine and its capabilities (he doesn't use the term, but sometimes called \"mechanical sympathy\"), and tactics for isolating \"bad code\" that you probably have to use (at least to get started). [VIDEO]"},{"id":42213459,"author":"latexr","month":"2024-11","story":"Is Python That Slow?","stance":"rec","why":"Primer on the tenets of Casey's performance course","quote":"The video on Philosophies of Optimisation is a good primer on the tenets of the course.","text":"> Casey Muratori has a real in-depth course on why Python is slow and how to make it fast I don’t think that’s a fair characterisation of the course and that description does it a disservice. The course is not about Python, it’s about programming with performance in mind. The video on Philosophies of Optimisation is a good primer on the tenets of the course. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38555052,"author":"DeathArrow","month":"2023-12","story":"For processing strings, streams in C++ can be slow","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about software speed","quote":"Here's a great talk about software speed","text":"Anything object oriented is slower than anything procedural. Boxing and unboxing is expensive, running constructors and destructors is expensive, copying objects is expensive, virtual tables are expensive. And stuff isn't aligned greatly in memory, we can expect lots of cache misses and branch mispredictions. The fastest possible way is to have C strings in an array and run a function over it. If there are advantages to OOP, speed is not one of them. Here's a great talk about software speed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35741639,"author":"Hikikomori","month":"2023-04","story":"Performance Excuses Debunked","stance":"rec","why":"What non-pessimization means in practice","quote":"If you want to understand what he means with this watch the refterm series.","text":"He isn't saying that you should spend time on optimising your code, rather that you should write performant code from the beginning by not giving the cpu more work than it needs to do to perform the task, typically by avoiding OO paradigms, followings things like SOLID, etc. Then if needed, real optimisation can handle the hotspots in your code and is typically something that only a few of an orgs best engineers can do properly, but if everything is slow then its an insurmountable task for these few engineers. If you want to understand what he means with this watch the refterm series. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34792032,"author":"AnIdiotOnTheNet","month":"2023-02","story":"Maybe people do care about performance and reliability","stance":"rec","why":"Computers are fast; de-pessimization over optimization","quote":"Computers are so ludicrously fast these days that in the majority of cases you do not need optimization at all.","text":"I'm going to pre-emptively link Casey Muratori's Refterm Lecture Part 1[0], because invariably when this topic comes up people are all \"but optimization takes time and we gotta push the new features right now or the market will eat us.\" Computers are so ludicrously fast these days that in the majority of cases you do not need optimization at all. You do need de-pessimization though. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34976512,"author":"loup-vaillant","month":"2023-02","story":"“Clean” Code, Horrible Performance","stance":"rec","why":"Free lecture explaining the broad concept with an example","quote":"Thankfully there's a free lecture that explains the broad concept with an example.","text":"Agreed, but most people here haven't paid for the rest of the course, and Casey sometimes forgot that here he's addressing a wider audience. For instance he fails to explain why he didn't bother addressing the tail of his unrolled loop. He does in the course, but here he's just assuming it's irrelevant, and doesn't address again potential criticism like \"he doesn't even bother to write correct code, look at that lazy unrolling!\" . Thankfully there's a free lecture that explains the broad concept with an example. It's this lecture that convinced me to try out his course, where I hope he'll go into more details: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33606510,"author":"DeathArrow","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Do you recall any book or course that made a topic finally click?","stance":"rec","why":"Reasons software is built the wrong way","quote":"This video from Casey Muratori helped me point to some reasons","text":"I under the impression that we are building software the wrong way. This video from Casey Muratori helped me point to some reasons: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31743893,"author":"infogulch","month":"2022-06","story":"Making Code Faster","stance":"rec","why":"Three modes of speeding up programs","quote":"which is simply avoiding adding extra unnecessary work for the cpu","text":"I like Casey Miratory's \"non-pessimization\" approach in Philosophies of Optimization [1] [2]. He breaks \"making a program go faster\" into 3 modes: (1) \"optimization\" which includes counting expected cycles or memory bandwidth, careful benchmarking, designing custom algorithms, etc which can be fun but is expensive and is only rarely applied; (2) \"non-pessimization\" which is simply avoiding adding extra unnecessary work for the cpu, which should be done 100% of the time but isn't in the industry today; (3) and \"fake optimization\" which is shallowly repeating 'optimization tips' and conclusions from unrelated cases of (1) without actually measuring it. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":31770381,"author":"AnIdiotOnTheNet","month":"2022-06","story":"The computers are fast, but you don't know it","stance":"rec","why":"Non-pessimization over optimization","quote":"you don't even know how ludicrously fast computers are because so much stuff is so insanely slow","text":"But perfect performance isn't even the benchmark, it's \"not ridiculously slow\". This is what is meant by \"Computers are fast, but you don't know it\", you don't even know how ludicrously fast computers are because so much stuff is so insanely slow. They're so fast that, in the vast majority of cases, you don't even need optimization, you just need non-pessimization: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30878142,"author":"AnIdiotOnTheNet","month":"2022-04","story":"Infinite Mac: An Instant-Booting Quadra in the Browser","stance":"rec","why":"Avoiding needless waste instead of optimizing","quote":"See Refterm Lecture Part 1 - Philosophies of Optimization by Casey Muratori","text":"Honestly you don't even have to think about optimization to make your software not horrifically slow on modern hardware, instead you have to think about non-pessimization. Simply put, avoid needless complexity and gratuitous waste in the first place. See Refterm Lecture Part 1 - Philosophies of Optimization by Casey Muratori: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30120545,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2022-01","story":"Reclaiming the lost art of Linux server administration","stance":"rec","why":"Casey explains why efficiency matters","quote":"Listen to Casey explain it","text":"I’ve found this to be an unsuccessful approach in practice. Performance is a complex, many-faceted thing. It has hidden costs that are hard to quantify. Customers leave in disgust because the site is slow. No amount of “throwing more cores at it” will help if there’s a single threaded bottleneck somewhere. Superlinear algorithms will get progressively worse, easily outpacing processor speed improvements. Notably this is a recent thing — single threaded throughout was improving exponentially for decades so many admins internalised the concept that simply moving an app with a “merely quadratic” scaling problem to new hardware will always fix the problem. Now… this does nothing. I’ve turned up at many sites as a consultant at eyewatering daily rates to fix slow apps. Invariably they were missing trivial things like database indexes or caching. Not Redis or anything fancy like that! Just cache control headers on static content. Invariably, doing the right thing from the beginning would have been cheaper. Listen to Casey explain it: [VIDEO] You need to have efficiency in your heart and soul or you can’t honestly call yourself an engineer. Learn your craft properly so you can do more with less — including less developer time!"},{"id":29228427,"author":"hsn915","month":"2021-11","story":"Selecting a programming language can be a form of premature optimization","stance":"rec","why":"Explains non-pessimization versus premature optimization","quote":"If you are interested in an expansion on this idea, checkout Casey Muratori's lecture about philosophies of optimization","text":"The fallacy of premature optimization rears its ugly head again. When Knuth wrote that quote, he meant something very specific with the word optimization: low level micro optimizations. Spending a lot of time trying to get every little ounce of performance from every little CPU instruction. High level reasonable design decisions are not \"optimizations\" in this sense at all. You should think of them as non-pessimization. Choosing Python or another slow language is premature pessimization. You just make your program slower for no reason. So choosing a language based on how programs written in it perform is simply non-pessimization. If you are interested in an expansion on this idea, checkout Casey Muratori's lecture about philosophies of optimization: [VIDEO] The article makes claims about software being \"fast enough\" and not needing further optimization. But what you call \"fast enough\" is probably 10000 times slower than what it could be. I'm not joking. People who program in slow languages have a really skewed perception about performance. If a page takes 5 seconds to load, they don't see that as a problem. If a server takes 500ms to respond to a request, they don't see that as a problem. This is simply unacceptable."},{"id":29035521,"author":"grayrest","month":"2021-10","story":"55 GiB/s FizzBuzz","stance":"rec","why":"Casey's intro to non-pessimization","quote":"Casey's most recent intro to non-pessimization","text":"> The overwhelming majority of slow code isn't slow because it's failed to do this kind of microoptimization, it's slow because it used the wrong algorithm. I disagree. I believe the majority of code is slow because it's written without any consideration at all to performance. I like Casey Muratori's philosophy of non-pessimization where true optmization (measuring, working hot spots) is rare and rarely necessary but massive speedups compared to the general state of the art are achievable by simply not writing code using patterns that are inherently slow. This isn't deep algorithmic stuff it's just avoiding copies and/or pointer chasing. Edit: [VIDEO] <- Casey's most recent intro to non-pessimization > For a realistic-sized problem, if you write solutions in C and Python with the same amount of effort from equally skilled programmers, the Python version will almost certainly be faster The Advent of Code runs every year and I'm not sure about C (not something I track) but there are plenty of Rust submissions and while the Rust submissions take longer to come in, it's not THAT much longer. From memory it's like 2x on a timescale of minutes. The Python programs are not faster."},{"id":48597664,"author":"andai","month":"2026-06","story":"Fable Converted Pylint to Rust","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">You can't get 2000x faster without actually eliminating some large percentage of the work Yeah, exactly. That's how optimization works! Recognizing that in many cases, the work is that was being done is not necessary to produce the result you actually need, that it could have been in a different way. This can sometimes involve adding complexity (by means of a better data structure or algorithm, e.g. a quadtree), and sometimes involve removing it (non-pessimization[0]). That being said, it's certainly also possible to achieve a speedup by breaking things, which you are suggesting is the case here. I can't comment on that part. [0] Casey Muratori - Refterm Lecture Part 1 - Philosophies of Optimization [VIDEO]"},{"id":48682143,"author":"andai","month":"2026-06","story":"Overfitted a 900KB Transformer to Compress a 100MB CSV into 7MB","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was working on a multiplayer game a while ago, and one of the iterations of the netcode was \"thin client\" where clients just sent input, server simulated the game, and it dumped world state onto the pipe at 60hz. I didn't ship that version but I estimated a $3000 bandwidth bill with that approach! I started looking into diffing the state, compression, etc... until I realized, wait a minute! My player movement is linear so I only need a packet for start and stop! And so I achieved near infinite efficiency improvement :) I think the word is... a specialized solution can beat a general one. Also, \"remembering what the program actually needs to do, and just making it do that\"... I de-pessimized the netcode: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35141107,"author":"sllabres","month":"2023-03","story":"WezTerm is a GPU-accelerated cross-platform terminal emulator written in Rust","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Probably inspired by the performance problems with the windows terminal [1] and the accelerated terminal [2] developed by Molly Rocket as 'answer'? A series of videos presenting the poc [3] [1] [LINK] (It takes a PhD to develop that) [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] , [VIDEO]"},{"id":35384556,"author":"latexr","month":"2023-03","story":"AI-enhanced development makes me more ambitious with my projects","stance":"reference","why":"Concept of fake optimization","quote":"","text":"> For example they can write some code and say, \"adjust for best practice\". And they’ll get code which isn’t good, just a parroting of bad ideas widely disseminated. A bit like Casey Muratori’s concept of fake optimisation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34976080,"author":"loup-vaillant","month":"2023-02","story":"“Clean” Code, Horrible Performance","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"But first, we need to make sure our programs aren't as slow as molasses.","text":"I realised when I implemented EdDSA for Monocypher that optimisations compound. When I got rid of a bottleneck, I noticed that another part of the code was the new bottleneck, and some of the optimisations compounded multiplicatively . It took many changes before I finally started to hit diminishing returns and stop. All while restricting myself to standard C99, and trying fairly hard not to spend too many lines of code on this. My point being, if most of the program is slowed down by a slew of wasted CPU cycles (costly abstractions, slow interpreted language…), there's a good chance what should have been obvious bottlenecks get drowned in a see of underperformance. They're harder to spot, and fixing them doesn't change much. So before you even get to actual optimisation, your program should be fast enough that actual optimisations have a real impact. And yes, actual optimisation should be done quite rarely. But first, we need to make sure our programs aren't as slow as molasses. See [VIDEO]"},{"id":31285335,"author":"andai","month":"2022-05","story":"When I tell the Windows Terminal team something is simple, I am \"misguided\"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">most of the code in the parser/renderer part of the terminal is unnecessary and just slows things down. What this code needs to do is extremely simple and it seems like it has been massively overcomplicated He means exactly what he says. Notably, refterm had almost no optimization applied to it.[0] The massive performance increase was entirely due to non-pessimization.[1] >refterm actually isn't very fast. Despite being several orders of magnitude faster than Windows Terminal, refterm is largely unoptimized and is much slower than it could be. It is nothing more than a straightforward implementation of a tile renderer, with a very simple cache to ensure that glyph generation only gets called when new glyphs are seen. It is all very, very simple. A more complex codebase that parsed Unicode and rendered glyphs itself would likely be much faster than refterm for many important metrics.[0] [0] - [LINK] [1] - Refterm Lecture Part 1 - Philosophies of Optimization - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30661383,"author":"DeathArrow","month":"2022-03","story":".NET Myths Dispelled","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think this is a valid critique. But then, most of the development (C aside) has been a victim to OOP. I have begun to think that OOP creates more problems than it solves. Added complexity and performance loss being the biggest. It's insane if you peek into a big projects where many developers came and went and each of them read an article about what it seemed an interesting design pattern and tried to implement them. You can have some kind of procedural programming if you use classes just to store data and use static methods. But the frameworks are OOP and there is no way around that unless you write your own frameworks. I am a big fan of Mike Acton's Data Oriented Architecture speeches, but I didn't find a way to implement something similar in C#. For those interested in something better than OOP, here's a few links: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hxM8QmyZXtg",2],["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1]]},"_tpB-B8BXk0":{"wy":"HN people with ADHD or ADHD-affected relatives share it as an authoritative, practical talk that helped some seek diagnosis.","q":{"x":"It's a talk by Dr. Russell Barkley on ADHD and it's what made me finally schedule a full analysis","i":33450345,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":22133966,"author":"ncfausti","month":"2020-01","story":"Why I Procrastinate (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Barkley's talk describes the daily ADHD experience","quote":"halfway through, it brought me to tears.","text":"As a 33 year old who was just diagnosed with ADHD, a few minutes into reading this I knew exactly where it was going. For me, it was when I watched Dr. Russell Barkley's talk[1] and halfway through, it brought me to tears. No one before then had described exactly the way I felt on a daily basis. Since going through a full day of neuropsychological testing and being diagnosed with ADHD-I and starting medication, I feel like the me I think I am, naturally curious, optimistic, and actually following through with the things that interest me. Planning and grad school assignments just click so effortlessly now, and my stress is way down, as I am completing things in a timely manner and not fighting myself at every step of the way. If any of this resonates with you, please talk with a psychiatrist to see if this is something you're unknowingly (and unnecessarily) struggling with. Also,if you're like me and like listening to audio books at 2 or 3x speed, check out Speechify, it allows you to listen to any kind of text including rough scans of books/PDFs. It's been incredibly helpful for required readings in classes. edit: wrong video url [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19990425,"author":"azeirah","month":"2019-05","story":"Ask HN: Developers with ADHD/ADD, how do you cope?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Barkley as the key ADHD authority","quote":"This man is literally ADHD Jesus.","text":"Lists, logs, writing, post-its, Todo lists etcetcetc. I tend to forget stuff even while I'm doing it, so it helps a million if what I'm doing is written on a Post-It right in front of me. Also, to keep track of tasks, problems, todos etc, I have a huge text file that is bound to the ctrl-` keybinding (autohotkey script opens a file in sublime text). I think the most important thing is to externalize your mind into your environment, you might forget, but your table or text files won't. Also, make sure to watch Russel Barkley. This man is literally ADHD Jesus. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42606022,"author":"lathiat","month":"2025-01","story":"Ask HN: My 12 year-old nephew just got diagnosed with ADHD, any advice?","stance":"rec","why":"Engaging, informative ADHD talk from a respected researcher and clinician","quote":"He’s a very well known and respected ADHD researcher and clinician that has some very engaging and informative videos.","text":"Medicate and try not to stop. Don’t avoid it, it’s life changing and the most effective intervention by far. None of the other stuff is nearly as effective. It is very likely turn their life around. As someone who was not medicated in his teenage years (Briefly in childhood and now all through adult hood. But was nissing all through school and it sucked). It’s a real life changer. Look up Russ Barkley on YouTube. He’s a very well known and respected ADHD researcher and clinician that has some very engaging and informative videos. Here’s a taster: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36993836,"author":"code_biologist","month":"2023-08","story":"Cognitive AI for ADHD","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley argues ADHD is about executive function rather than attention","quote":"Great video I came across that expresses similar ideas about it being executive function that's the challenge, not attention.","text":"Great video I came across that expresses similar ideas about it being executive function that's the challenge, not attention. \"This is how you treat ADHD based off science, Dr Russell Barkley part of 2012 Burnett Lecture\" [VIDEO] Edit: here's a newer one by the same speaker \"ADHD as Motivation Deficit Disorder\" [LINK]"},{"id":37211411,"author":"j33zusjuice","month":"2023-08","story":"Anxious brains redirect emotion regulation","stance":"rec","why":"Describes ADHD patterns of motivation that the commenter recognized in themselves","quote":"Does this all feel familiar?","text":"Does this all feel familiar? [VIDEO] I don’t know if it’s always this way, but I create emergencies and catastrophes because I otherwise have no motivation to do anything. My back has to be against the wall to get me to act. It’s been a lifelong issue, I’ve tried to kill myself several times because of it, and I learned it was ADHD in the last month. 100% of my issues are explained by this. I never would have guessed."},{"id":33960406,"author":"prometheus76","month":"2022-12","story":"The silent struggles of workers with ADHD","stance":"rec","why":"Useful ADHD advice for people with ADHD and their managers","quote":"Here's a video with some actual useful advice for ADHD people and their managers","text":"Knowing something or knowing how to use something is not the same as using it. This is a classic struggle between those without ADHD and those with it. People without ADHD think it's just a training issue. It's really not. This is an especially difficult dynamic between non-adhd parents and adhd kids. Ask me how I know. In other words, I know how to use Outlook, and it can help. I know how to use Post-it notes. And it helps a little, but if I don't have a chance to write out a post-it note and put it in a prominent place (with the other 250 prominent post-it notes in a prominent place) it's going to get lost and/or I'm going to forget. This isn't just a \"you're just not organized\" problem. One of the bigger challenges is categorizing things. As you can see just in this post, my brain started sprawling out to all the issues that confront someone with ADHD and the scope started to spread and I started talking about other things besides your post-it notes idea. This isn't a training issue. Largely, the issue is with impulse control and a dysfunctional intrinsic rewards system. Here's a video with some actual useful advice for ADHD people and their managers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33450345,"author":"ncfausti","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: How to deal with burnout and its consequences?","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley talk on ADHD that prompted the commenter to seek a full diagnosis","quote":"It's a talk by Dr. Russell Barkley on ADHD and it's what made me finally schedule a full analysis","text":"I have ADHD-I and I'm feeling almost exactly the same way, right up to a falling out with a company I had significant ownership in/was part of the founding team. How are your daily routines? Are you taking breaks and pursuing the things you actually enjoy doing? Have you thought much about the kind of experiences that have brought you the most joy/satisfaction in life (both at work and outside of work)? Maybe watch this video and see if it resonates with you. It's a talk by Dr. Russell Barkley on ADHD and it's what made me finally schedule a full analysis (after literally decades of putting it off). [VIDEO] If you'd like to talk I'm here to listen."},{"id":22126704,"author":"rl3","month":"2020-01","story":"Procrastination is about managing emotions, not time","stance":"rec","why":"Informative Barkley talk on ADHD","quote":"It was quite informative.[0]","text":"> Some people argue that what we're today are calling ADHD should be referred to as emotional impulsiveness. The argument is that lack of emotional control causes all kinds of symptoms ... While that's certainly a major component, I believe sensory stimulation also plays a role. Certain video games for example tend to yield considerably higher dopamine rewards in part due to their elevated degree of stimuli relative to other, less stimulating activities. It's why I can play certain games to a world-class level, and then by the same token attempt to read technical literature but find myself re-reading the same sentence a dozen times over. On that front, I've found that if I really attempt to immerse myself in a topic in every way I can, while simultaneously convincing myself on the emotional front that learning whatever I'm attempting to learn will be fantastic-it tends to result in better attention. It's as if my mind has a minimum RPM requirement to operate, and the primary challenge is just achieving a minimum speed that gets the blades moving. > I highly recommend looking it up, you should be able to find some of his talks on YouTube. Just watched a (partial?) talk of his, thank you. It was quite informative.[0] He mentioned a steady stream of fast-metabolized glucose [to the frontal lobe] helping tremendously. That's in weird contrast to my experience, as I tend to avoid virtually all refined sugars. In fact, I also fast for 16 hours every day, finding that my mental clar"},{"id":22133154,"author":"andai","month":"2020-01","story":"Procrastination is about managing emotions, not time","stance":"rec","why":"How to use the environment and habits to compensate for ADHD","quote":"explains how to use the environment to compensate for ADHD","text":"I thought you might appreciate this clip from a lecture by Dr. Russell Barkley which explains how to use the environment to compensate for ADHD (externalize everything - time, to-do's, motivation, rewards, feedback) and how to keep your willpower reserves high (frequent breaks, positive self-talk and positive mental pictures, meditation and exercise). [VIDEO]"},{"id":17775446,"author":"azeirah","month":"2018-08","story":"The Akrasia Effect: Why We Don’t Follow Through on What We Set Out to Do","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley's advice on breaks and ADHD","quote":"I think you'd want be interested in watching this video","text":"According to Russel Barkley (well known and central figure in the ADHD scientific community), you should definitely take short breaks every so many minutes. He recommends 3 mins for every 10 minutes of work, but that's for children. I think you'd want be interested in watching this video [VIDEO] Definitely check out his other work as well, listening to his talks is like reading my own diary... except translated to scientific language"},{"id":16610298,"author":"burntrelish1273","month":"2018-03","story":"In Praise of ADHD","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley lecture on treating ADHD, listed among worthwhile viewing","quote":"Worth watching","text":"Praise? :/ Just diagnosed as an adult partially due to parents not \"believing in it,\" refused testing then and I was blind to it until now. Currently friendless, jobless, broke, homeless and a bazillion unfinished projects not exactly the picture of praiseworthy. ADHD is a misnomer, it's an executive function disorder, but the name will never change because it's embedded in disability laws. Medicating for emotional regulation, continuity of thought, completing tasks and not coming-off as weird are lifesaving... don't avoid medication out of some antivaxx nonsense. Worth watching: - ADHD Child vs. Non-ADHD Child Interview [LINK] - How To Know If You Have ADHD [LINK] - This is how you treat ADHD based off science, Dr Russell Barkley part of 2012 Burnett Lecture [VIDEO] - Dr Russell Barkley on ADHD Meds and how they all work differently from each other [LINK] - The Neuroanatomy of ADHD and thus how to treat ADHD - CADDAC - Dr Russel Barkley part 1c [LINK]"},{"id":9480739,"author":"joshuak","month":"2015-05","story":"Entrepreneurial Tendencies Among People with ADHD","stance":"rec","why":"Shorter summary of Barkley's ADHD lecture","quote":"This 13 min video [1] also by Dr Russell Barkley is something of a summery","text":"This 13 min video [1] also by Dr Russell Barkley is something of a summery, but the longer one is absolutely worth watching. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34577579,"author":"novok","month":"2023-01","story":"Ejaculation Frequency and Prostate Cancer","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I got diagnosed in middle age after a very long assessment that cost me a lot of money, because I wanted accuracy. If it was depression, neglect or trauma I would've been fine with that assessment too. I had no idea going in that I was anything close to ADHD, just extremely frustrated at my career & life stagnation. If I known years ago I'd would've been about 7 or 8 years ahead in my career. Yes ADHD is a different mind structure, it's also being called neurodiverse. But just watch a kiddo or a completely dysfunctional adult a with severe ADHD / autism and you quickly dissuade yourself that it isn't a problem and isn't a dysfunctional disability, and to expect a society to totally reconfigure itself beyond the equivalent of a few wheelchair ramps to match a forever minority with a lack of various mental abilities is economically naive. Just like how we don't mandate zero-flicker lightbulbs for autstic people with sensory issues for the entire country because they consume more power with a higher power factor. But it doesn't matter much either way in the end, and seeing how conspiratorial you are, I don't think I'd get much of anywhere trying to convince you otherwise. Sadly or happily, reality is way more mundane than you think it is. Also many freak out about stimulants, but they really are not that big of a deal IMO. If ADHD didn't involve stimulants, one of the most effective mental health medications for any mental condition out there BTW, I don't think you'd get this de"},{"id":33960151,"author":"kurikuri","month":"2022-12","story":"The silent struggles of workers with ADHD","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why knowing about tools doesn't fix ADHD executive function problems","quote":"a person with ADHD can know about the tools and the thought simply won't enter their mind to keep up with the tool itself","text":"> Folks, learn how to use the tools that are available to you in order to schedule your workday. And if you are a manager then you have an obligation to train your staff in the use of tracking tools, which can be something as simple as post-it notes. Learning tools doesn't solve the problem, a person with ADHD can know about the tools and the thought simply won't enter their mind to keep up with the tool itself [1]. Many ADHD medications can be relatively cheaply had (generics are available for Adderall, Straterra, Ritalin, etc.), but the stigma associated with those medications are still quite high. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33803405,"author":"adolph","month":"2022-11","story":"Tell HN: If you have trouble focusing on projects/meetings, see a psychatrist","stance":"reference","why":"Barkley on treating ADHD with medication plus compensating environments","quote":"to design prosthetic environments around the individual to compensate for their EF [Executive Function] deficits.","text":"Barkley considers meds necessary but not sufficient. The second part is \"to design prosthetic environments around the individual to compensate for their EF [Executive Function] deficits.\" [0] For those not following the ADHD conversation, Executive Function refers to a set of: Nonverbal working memory, Internalization of Speech (verbal working memory), Self-regulation of affect/motivation/arousal, Reconstitution (planning and generativity). [1] 0. This is how you treat ADHD based off science, Dr Russell Barkley part of 2012 Burnett Lecture: [VIDEO] 1. [LINK]"},{"id":31568264,"author":"nerdyadventurer","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: I am lost in life. How do I fix this?","stance":"reference","why":"Personal ADHD story, video linked as background","quote":"","text":"I also had similar experience, my undiagnosed ADHD was unknowingly ruining my life close to a decade. Problem with ADHD is it become late to diagnosed if you are smart, do well in school. My ADHD story goes something like this I was diagnosed was OCD at 16, even though I had symptoms from 14 or so. Before 16 I had symptom like difficulty in brushing teeth according to order, difficult to stay still and walk around the house thinking. But these things were not bothering my life that much. I did well on school exams, but starting from 16 with distractibility small task started to take hours, failed exams, anyway I attended to private college here and get through it. After college, I did not apply to any jobs, because I want to do something of my own, I tried different things some failed, but I had constant lingering of project I idea, I worked on it time to time while procrastinating but not close to at least releasing prototype. Like this five years passed away (up to today), I am still not any closer to finish it. As procrastination, I was helping relations, friends browsing social media, watching TV. Also did small freelance dev gigs. Over this time I have read about science of doing work, learning, but none of these helped me to work constantly. I muster my will power for several days and then fall off the wagon, but I keep trying, keep falling. I was feeling envy like OP because my peers were rising the ladder of success/life over these five years, while I was remaining st"},{"id":28238364,"author":"jw433","month":"2021-08","story":"The thing standing between procrastination and daily progress is ritual","stance":"reference","why":"Supports ADHD being highly treatable","quote":"ADHD is the most treatable mental disorder out there, even if medication is only option being considered [1].","text":"Disagree. Executive function deficiencies have been proven to be an essential symptom of ADHD. ADHD is the most treatable mental disorder out there, even if medication is only option being considered [1]. If you have had long-term problems with executive functioning and you feel like you've tried everything, then you should get medically evaluated for ADHD. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["LnS0PfNyj4U",1],["42QuXLucH3Q",1],["R13BD8qKeTg",1],["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1]]},"YEBfamv-_do":{"wy":"It is the go-to beginner explanation of Diffie-Hellman, recommended because the paint analogy makes the idea click without prior cryptography knowledge.","q":{"x":"I found this video, Public key cryptography - Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange (full version), to be completely enlightening","i":13093159,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":35860989,"author":"throw1234651234","month":"2023-05","story":"Public Key Crypto Explained in 0 words","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Concise, clear explanation of public key crypto concepts","quote":"This is the only video that ever explained it concisely and clearly","text":"This is the only video that ever explained it concisely and clearly, IF you don't care about trying to actually implement it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23558372,"author":"ndesaulniers","month":"2020-06","story":"Postbank to replace 12M bank cards after employees steal 'master key'","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite explanation of Diffie-Hellman key exchange","quote":"This is my favorite video on Diffie-Hellman key exchange","text":"Interesting, thanks for sharing (I too was curious). This is my favorite video on Diffie-Hellman key exchange which is a slightly different problem (sharing a secret, without the constraint of dividing the key): [VIDEO] (oh, hey, same channel! Guess I'll need to check out more videos from here!)"},{"id":13093159,"author":"zappo2938","month":"2016-12","story":"Crypto 101 – Introductory course on cryptography","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mixed-color analogy makes key exchange basics click","quote":"I found this video, Public key cryptography - Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange (full version), to be completely enlightening","text":"For idiots like myself, I found this video, Public key cryptography - Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange (full version), to be completely enlightening using mixed colors to explain the most basic features of a cryptography algorithm.[0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27024297,"author":"siparikh","month":"2021-05","story":"Diffie-Hellman for the Layman","stance":"rec","why":"Color-mixing analogy that aids understanding of key exchange","quote":"uses the same analogy that really helped me understand this better","text":"Here is another video from \"Art of the Problem\" that uses the same analogy that really helped me understand this better: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16243111,"author":"itsrajivshah","month":"2018-01","story":"Algorithms Explained: Diffie-Hellman","stance":"rec","why":"High-level paint-mixing metaphor for the DH exchange","quote":"I find it easier to visualise, through colours of paint, for a high-level metaphor of the DH key exchange process","text":"Interesting explanation - thanks for sharing. I find it easier to visualise, through colours of paint, for a high-level metaphor of the DH key exchange process, which may be useful - [VIDEO]"},{"id":15499868,"author":"gregmac","month":"2017-10","story":"Practical Public Key Cryptography","stance":"rec","why":"Paint-color visualization of public-key ideas for beginners","quote":"there's a pretty neat video that outlines how public-key cryptography works using paint colors","text":"If you haven't seen it, there's a pretty neat video that outlines how public-key cryptography works using paint colors [1]. It's focused on Diffie-Hellman key exchange, but it's a great intro for beginners to be able to visualize the concepts, and I think helps provide some context before jumping right into the GPG command line. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14613551,"author":"tialaramex","month":"2017-06","story":"Ask HN: Want to study SSL, HTTPS, and the works. Where to start?","stance":"rec","why":"Walks through Diffie-Hellman so anyone can follow","quote":"here's a video that walks through Diffie Hellman, so that anybody can follow","text":"I recommend beginning at the fundamentals. For example here's a video that walks through Diffie Hellman, so that anybody can follow, you can probably sprint through it, but by taking it slow they avoid accidentally forgetting anything important. [VIDEO] Grasping the fundamentals means that when it comes to policy decisions (e.g. in the management of certificates) you can see what the consequences of a particular decision are, rather than just hoping that whoever proposed that policy knew what they were doing. For example, I think a lot of people today use Certificate Signing Request (CSR) files without understanding them at all. But once you have a grounding in the underlying elements you can see at once what the CSR does, and why it's necessary without needing to have that spelled out separately. Or another example, understanding what was and was not risky as a result of the known weakness of SHA-1. I saw a lot of scare-mongering by security people who saw the SHA-1 weakness as somehow meaning impossible things were now likely, but it only affected an important but quite narrow type of usage, people who understood that could make better, more careful decisions without putting anybody at risk."},{"id":14643244,"author":"ivanr","month":"2017-06","story":"How HTTPS Handshake Happens","stance":"rec","why":"Simple, intuitive explanation of Diffie-Hellman key exchange","quote":"there's a great video that explains it here","text":"As others have already pointed out, this explanation focuses on the RSA key exchange, which has been deprecated. It's not recommended for use with the current line of protocols (TLS 1.2 and earlier) and it's been completely removed from TLS 1.3 (work in progress, but close to being finished). The key weakness of the RSA key exchange is that session encryption keys are transported over the network encrypted with the server's public key. When the server's private key is compromised in some way, the attacker can passively decrypt all traffic, they can also \"go back in time\" to decrypt all previous conversations they might have recorded and kept. For a more secure handshake approach, consider the Diffie-Hellman key exchange, which is a way for two parties to agree on a secret number (a very large number when used for encryption, of course) that third party can't discover from looking at the network traffic. It's a very simple and intuitive algorithm; there's a great video that explains it here: [VIDEO] Today, we generally use the ephemeral elliptic curve DH variant (ECDHE), which has better security characteristics and works much faster. However, DH is not sufficient, you must also authenticate the other party. Otherwise you could be negotiating your key exchange with the attacker and not the real recipient. This is where public cryptography comes in. After the key exchange, both parties use their private keys to sign the transcripts of the entire conversation up until that point"},{"id":13073735,"author":"jzl","month":"2016-11","story":"Ask HN: Is there a “ground-up” explanation of PGP/GnuPG?","stance":"rec","why":"Intuitive intro to Diffie-Hellman needing no prior cryptography knowledge","quote":"This is a great pair of videos that intuitively explain Diffie-Helman key exchange","text":"This is a great pair of videos that intuitively explain Diffie-Helman key exchange and then the RSA algorithm in a way that requires no previous knowledge of cryptography: \"Public key cryptography - Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange\" [VIDEO] \"Public Key Cryptography: RSA Encryption Algorithm\" [LINK] Good coverage of the fundamentals before attempting a deeper understanding of PGP specifically."},{"id":39884990,"author":"nurple","month":"2024-03","story":"Guess my RGB","stance":"reference","why":"Describes Diffie-Hellman as mixing color values like oil paint","quote":"a video[0] that describes Diffie-Hellman key exchange in terms of mixing color values","text":"Reminds me of a video[0] that describes Diffie-Hellman key exchange in terms of mixing color values (as globs of oil paint). I guess in this game you're guessing the mix of primary colors, so maybe it doesn't hold the same property of difficulty in deriving the constituents? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30966533,"author":"fauria","month":"2022-04","story":"Secret Colors: A Gentle Introduction to Cryptography","stance":"reference","why":"Diffie-Hellman explained with paint","quote":"(Diffie-Hellman key exchange explained using paint)","text":"When reading the title, this came to mind: [VIDEO] (Diffie-Hellman key exchange explained using paint). There is also this one by Computerphile: [LINK]"}],"r":[["JZE3_0qvrMg",1],["rAl-9HwD858",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["hpTCZ-hO6iI",1],["ZDR433b0HJY",1],["3QnD2c4Xovk",1],["8aGhZQkoFbQ",1],["pSm7BcQHWXk",1],["HQKy1KJpSVc",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["qMkYlIA7mgw",1],["pq1XqP4-qOo",1]]},"PFDu9oVAE-g":{"wy":"HN repeatedly names it the best, most intuitive explanation of eigenvalues, and a gateway to the whole linear algebra series.","q":{"x":"By far the best explanation of eigenvalues is found on 3blue1brown","i":33527453,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":43361311,"author":"mportela","month":"2025-03","story":"Statistical Formulas for Programmers (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made eigenvalues click; series is well produced.","quote":"His entire series on Linear Algebra is incredibly well produced.","text":"Then definitely what 3Blue1Brown's video on eigenvalues and eigenvectors. [1] That's when I clicked to me! His entire series on Linear Algebra is incredibly well produced. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33521929,"author":"raydiatian","month":"2022-11","story":"What is an eigenvalue?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Approachable primer on eigenvalues.","quote":"A far easier to digest primer on eigenvalues is available from 3Blue1Brown","text":"A far easier to digest primer on eigenvalues is available from 3Blue1Brown [+]. His presentation format is undeniably approachable, so much so that I think you could probably use it to teach linear algebra and eigenvectors to 9-year olds. [+] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33527453,"author":"scmitton","month":"2022-11","story":"What is an eigenvalue?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best explanation of eigenvalues.","quote":"By far the best explanation of eigenvalues is found on 3blue1brown","text":"By far the best explanation of eigenvalues is found on 3blue1brown: [VIDEO] Seriously, the course on the essence of linear algebra did more for me than my entire college linear algebra course. It's only a pity I discovered the videos at the end of my mechanical engineering degree and not sooner."},{"id":13622532,"author":"yamaneko","month":"2017-02","story":"The Essence of Linear Algebra [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Intuitive explanation of eigenvectors and eigenvalues.","quote":"3Blue1Brown explanation of eigenvectors and eigenvalues is very insightful and intuitive","text":"3Blue1Brown explanation of eigenvectors and eigenvalues is very insightful and intuitive [1]. One of my favorite videos from his channel is \"Who cares about topology? (Inscribed rectangle problem)\" [2]. They way a Torus and Möbius Strip come up as solution to the problem is so elegant. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":12665053,"author":"ivan_ah","month":"2016-10","story":"Meet the New Math, Unlike the Old Math","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visual introduction to eigenvalues.","quote":"this video is a very nice introduction","text":"On the topic of eigenvalues, this video is a very nice introduction: [VIDEO] (the whole playlist kicks ass; highly recommended for anyone interested in \"seeing\" what linear algebra is all about)"},{"id":17074405,"author":"steamer25","month":"2018-05","story":"Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Animated eigen-concepts built on visual foundations.","quote":"All of the videos in the series, including this later one on eigen-things, focus on animations","text":"3Blue1Brown has a good series on YouTube for building intuition in linear algebra: [LINK] In one of the last videos in the (relatively short) series, he discusses eigen-*: ~'eigen-stuffs are straight-forward but only make sense if you have a solid visual understanding of the pre-requisites (linear transformations, determinants, linear systems of equations, change of basis, etc.). Confusion about eigen-stuffs usually has more to do with a shaky foundation than the eigen-things themselves' [VIDEO] All of the videos in the series, including this later one on eigen-things, focus on animations to show what the number crunching is doing to the coordinate system."}],"r":[["tX4H_ctggYo",1],["t4vKPhjcMZg",1],["5v1Yg6XejyE",1],["eGKFTUuJppU",1],["GL77oOnrPzY",1],["AmgkSdhK4K8",1]]},"KZjFVdU8VLI":{"wy":"It is the canonical demo HN people cite for live interactive development and hot reloading, as proof the workflow is real and not smoke and mirrors.","q":{"x":"I still marvel at Bruce Hauman's demo","i":12170419,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12170419,"author":"nprescott","month":"2016-07","story":"Fun vs. Computer Science","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Impressive demo of interactive game development","quote":"I still marvel at Bruce Hauman's demo","text":"I don't disagree with the article, but I also don't know that pushing the boundaries of interactivity and \"fun\" are relegated to academia. Iterating on what makes things fun is (probably) highly unscientific and might be better left to people more focused on it directly, such as game programmers. As far as interactive development of games goes, I still marvel at Bruce Hauman's demo[1] of \"Interactive programming Flappy Bird in ClojureScript\". I think the best hope of achieving what the author is suggesting is in the development of something similar, either direct REPL access to the game environment or DSLs embedded in the game engines (which already exist I'm sure). Outside of the big name engines, I'm curious about what existing options are (leaving out anything browser based because JavaScript is an obvious example), I know of LÖVE[2] but I'm not familiar with how \"fun\" games made in it are. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":23934960,"author":"Nihilartikel","month":"2020-07","story":"Nubank acquires Cognitect","stance":"rec","why":"Interactive reimplementation of Flappy Bird showing hot reloading","quote":"Illustrated well in this video where the author is interactively re-implementing Flappy Bird","text":"From my standpoint as a one man show, it's invaluable to be able to write the web client and backend in the same language, to share code between them... and have that one language NOT be JS. Clojurescript with Rum/Citrus or Reagent/Reframe deliver on the promise of React/Redux with much less fuss and overhead. Editing JSX is a pain in comparison to the Hiccup syntax used more often in CLJS, e.g. [:div {:class shiny} \"some stuff\" [:button \"OK\"]] - with the structural editing in any good Clojure editor you can cut, paste, entire blocks of tags, or slurp a tag from outside your div into it, or spit one out, all with a few keystrokes, never stopping to find some closing </div> tag.. I guess some JSX IDEs might do this, but it's really nice that the HTML is represented as just another piece of data, not some weird chimerical syntax bolt on. With Figwheel or ShadowCLJS hot reloading is instantaneous and doesn't even interrupt your webapp. If they're written properly, functions and the app state are not conflated at all, so you can modify any function in your app's rendering or behavior and as long as they aren't incompatible with the runtime state, it will just pop right into your browser. Illustrated well in this video where the author is interactively re-implementing Flappy Bird: [VIDEO] I feel particularly strongly about this having started out doing front end work primarily in cljs but recently being falling into using React/Redux for a contract and being REALLY strung out by t"},{"id":21583149,"author":"LandR","month":"2019-11","story":"Learn ClojureScript: Functional Programming Concepts","stance":"rec","why":"Figwheel and realtime programming in action","quote":"One of the best things about ClojureScript is figwheel and realtime programming.","text":"One of the best things about ClojureScript is figwheel and realtime programming. You can see it in action here [VIDEO]"},{"id":14841749,"author":"freshhawk","month":"2017-07","story":"Show HN: Mesh IDE – a code editor that feels like a spreadsheet","stance":"rec","why":"Talks on GUI-centric Clojure tools with immediate feedback","quote":"Bruce Hauman has some good talks about his tools, which are GUI application-centric and a joy to use","text":"I'm not sure if the parent did, but I followed what you were looking for and the answer is still \"try clojure\", perhaps more specifically it is \"Try the Clojure libraries figwheel and devcards to do web stuff\" but the push for immediate feedback and \"interactive programming\" is central for everyone. It's a community that took Bret Victor's Inventing on Principle talk to heart, already agreeing due to the REPL centric workflows that most people used. Bruce Hauman has some good talks about his tools, which are GUI application-centric and a joy to use [1][2][3] If you aren't doing web or native mobile GUI stuff then the default tools have immediate unit test/linting/spec compliance feedback triggered every time you save a file. Unless you mean you want a very simplified language, in which case you should look elsewhere. Clojure has definitely chosen the high power side of that trade-off, but this also allows some truly first class tools to be developed. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] <- this one in particular is original and outstanding in my opinion"},{"id":10183385,"author":"base698","month":"2015-09","story":"Lisp is still a secret weapon","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the excellent ClojureScript tooling in a few minutes","quote":"Watch these talks to see what I mean about tooling","text":"If you're starting from from scratch it looks really awesome. No callback hell and in believable tooling. Watch these talks to see what I mean about tooling: Few minutes [VIDEO] Longer [LINK]"},{"id":9317792,"author":"dustingetz","month":"2015-04","story":"Myself – v1.0.3","stance":"rec","why":"Live interactive development demo, not smoke and mirrors","quote":"if you liked this, check these out","text":"This is called interactive development; if you liked this, check these out: * Interactive programming Flappy Bird in ClojureScript: [VIDEO] * Live-editing React app without refresh: [LINK] These demos aren't smoke and mirrors, I and many others do interactive development, 8 hours a day, writing regular web apps."},{"id":22136684,"author":"minikomi","month":"2020-01","story":"Ask HN: What change in your programming technique has been most transformative?","stance":"reference","why":"Canonical figwheel example of live game development","quote":"canonical figwheel example","text":"Sounds like you're remembering John Carmack developing VR using racket: [LINK] edit: Also check Arcadia for Clojure: [LINK] And developing flappy bird in clojurescript (canonical figwheel example): [VIDEO]"},{"id":18907661,"author":"Jach","month":"2019-01","story":"Porting 30K lines of code from Flow to TypeScript","stance":"reference","why":"Example of live interactive development in the browser","quote":"Example of what dynamic languages for the browser (ClojureScript) have been doing for 5 years now","text":"Does TypeScript let you build a program interactively as a living thing? Example of what dynamic languages for the browser (ClojureScript) have been doing for 5 years now: [VIDEO] Given that frontend work is so often an endless series of tweaks I don't know how people stand doing anything big for a long duration the classic way of edit -> compile (refresh) -> rebuild runtime state to look at what changed/play around/test -> repeat."},{"id":13410696,"author":"lambdadmitry","month":"2017-01","story":"Re-Frame:  a Reagent Framework for Writing SPAs, in ClojureScript","stance":"reference","why":"Shows figwheel hot-reload workflow","quote":"You can check out [2] if you are interested.","text":"Well, the CLJS->JS side of story is very polished, Figwheel [1] makes things work smoothly (in-browser errors and warnings, sourcemaps, hot reload all work from the box). You can check out [2] if you are interested. There are two areas that still can be improved in my opinion: * the asset story. Right now I use a gulp task that prepares my assets and outputs CSS into a folder known to Figwheel, which makes hotreloading work automagically. However, if you need a complicated interaction between JS, HTML and CSS (i.e. you use CSS Modules), it can be a bit more involved. * the NPM deps story. AFAIK it's gonna be solved in CLJS core soon, but right now your best bet is to pack all NPM deps using native JS tool (webpack, rollup or w/e) into one file and include it before CLJS-generated JS code. Ah, and in some cases you will need externs, too. It's actually way less complicated then it sounds, and if you need any help, feel free to reach me through mail (my nickname here at gmail.com). I would be happy to help. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["OINa46HeWg8",1],["CK62I-4cuSY",1],["j-kj2qwJa_E",1],["VMj-3S1tku0",1]]},"qd_hEja6bzE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a technically rich, beautifully made documentary on ambitious hardware engineering and failure.","q":{"x":"There's a beautiful feature film about the story of Makani. It's a great watch with plenty of technical details","i":42802986,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42802986,"author":"pveierland","month":"2025-01","story":"How shut-down Bay Area tech companies ditch their fancy gear","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Beautiful full-story film with plenty of technical detail","quote":"There's a beautiful feature film about the story of Makani. It's a great watch with plenty of technical details","text":"There's a beautiful feature film about the story of Makani. It's a great watch with plenty of technical details that follows the story from beginning to end. Available for free on YouTube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34270173,"author":"chrisdalke","month":"2023-01","story":"Caltech to Launch Space Solar Power Technology Demo into Orbit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documents Makani's ambition and repeated failures with extensive footage","quote":"One of my favorite engineering projects and documentaries, just because of the sheer ambition and scale","text":"Makani (Acquired by Google and shut down) built large airplane/kites which transmitted wind power to the ground via a tether. The engineering challenges were... intense. This was all documented extensively and this documentary summarizes all the footage they took: [VIDEO] One of my favorite engineering projects and documentaries, just because of the sheer ambition and scale, and the grit of the team to keep pushing through repeated failures. Sad the project was cancelled but it makes sense given the cost and complexity."},{"id":29107667,"author":"msadowski","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: What are some technically inspiring movies, TV shows or documentaries?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recommended as technically inspiring film about Makani","quote":"I highly recommend Pulling Power from the Sky: The Story of Makani [Feature Film] available on YouTube","text":"I highly recommend Pulling Power from the Sky: The Story of Makani [Feature Film] available on YouTube: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":35854318,"author":"userabchn","month":"2023-05","story":"TV series about startup failures, portraying greedy founders, and the damages","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary about failed X-funded wind startup Makani","quote":"an interesting documentary about Makani, a failed wind energy startup funded by X","text":"Not a TV series, but an interesting documentary about Makani, a failed wind energy startup funded by X: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29173291,"author":"gtpedrosa","month":"2021-11","story":"Kite Turbines","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the airborne wind problem in under two minutes","quote":"I think the Makani documentary does a good job explaining the problem in less than 2 min","text":"I think the Makani documentary does a good job explaining the problem in less than 2 min [1]. [1] [VIDEO] Regarding the size x quantity, I believe the reason lies in where the best wind is, which is usually high above the ground. The roughness of the terrain and obstacles generate shear and turbulence, which translates into more stress for the components. The higher wind has a more uniform distribution across the rotor and is higher in magnitude than in lower heights. So for small wind turbines to have access to the best wind, you would have to build expensive structures to reach there, making it infeasible. Hence kite approaches like the one posted and Makani (with different principles)."},{"id":27931808,"author":"Confiks","month":"2021-07","story":"Floating wind turbines could rise to great heights","stance":"rec","why":"Two-hour autodocumentary of Makani's engineering and shutdown","quote":"incredible engineering done by Makani, as told in this 2 hours long autodocumentary","text":"Reminds me of the incredible engineering done by Makani, as told in this 2 hours long autodocumentary [1]. They shut down in 2020 because they didn't see a path towards commercializing their airborne wind turbine. They made a non-assertion pledge on their patents, and open sourced much of their code (simulations, firmware) [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27935443,"author":"sangnoir","month":"2021-07","story":"Intrinsic, a new Alphabet company","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary focuses on hardware engineering of Makani","quote":"even the Makani documentary[2] was mostly about hardware (material science, mechanics, aerodynamics)","text":"I'd say it's very different with X. I looked at the large number of hardware design docs that were open-sourced[1] when they shut down Makani - hell, even the Makani documentary[2] was mostly about hardware (material science, mechanics, aerodynamics) with a some software sprinkled in. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24456984,"author":"bruckie","month":"2020-09","story":"Makani source code released","stance":"reference","why":"Feature film covering Makani's 13 years of work","quote":"a feature-length film that talks about Makani's work over the last 13 years","text":"There's also (quite surprisingly!) a feature-length film that talks about Makani's work over the last 13 years: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24460169,"author":"pas","month":"2020-09","story":"Makani source code released","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why kite turbines sweep larger areas with less material","quote":"since wind turbines extract power based on the area they sweep, their kite would be able to sweep a bigger area more easily","text":"In the film they explain that since wind turbines extract power based on the area they sweep, their kite would be able to sweep a bigger area more easily. (No need for so much material, no need for a 150m tall tower, etc.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":39043882,"author":"skyleradams","month":"2024-01","story":"Flying kites deliver container-sized power generation","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Compared to traditional wind turbines, this solution trades an enormous amount of complexity, maintenance, etc for small reductions in material cost.","text":"Not to be a HN naysayer, but wasn't this tried already with Makani Power?[1] They even made a documentary about it[2] Compared to traditional wind turbines, this solution trades an enormous amount of complexity, maintenance, etc for small reductions in material cost. We should be churning out traditional wind turbines for cheap instead. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rCMh7srOqvw",1],["huEtJw7pfLk",1],["SH3lR2GLgT0",1],["uqY3FMuMuRo",1],["Hzb37RyagCQ",1],["J3FcbFqSoQY",1],["tybOi4hjZFQ",1]]},"SrDEtSlqJC4":{"wy":"HN links it in right-to-repair and McFlurry-machine threads as the go-to in-depth explainer of the franchisee and Taylor arrangement.","q":{"x":"This video did a good indepth investigation.","i":29327467,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41949830,"author":"mcdow","month":"2024-10","story":"We can now fix McDonald's ice cream machines","stance":"rec","why":"Perverse incentives behind permanently broken ice cream machines","quote":"Here's a great YT video on why McDonald's ice cream machines are always broken","text":"Here's a great YT video on why McDonald's ice cream machines are always broken: [VIDEO] TL;DW: there are some perverse incentives to keep them broken. Basically the owner operators are forced to use a particular brand by corporate. Corporate McDonalds has a deal with a particular ice cream machine company. That particular company is the only company owner operators are allowed to buy from, and the only company allowed to service the machines. And it's no skin off of McDonald's back for these machines to always be broken, the cost falls on the owner-operators."},{"id":37312092,"author":"Aaronstotle","month":"2023-08","story":"iFixit Petitions Government for Right to Hack McDonald's Ice Cream Machine","stance":"rec","why":"Details how broken machines are a scheme on franchisees","quote":"For anyone interested in a good video about this","text":"For anyone interested in a good video about this, there is YT video that details how the Ice Cream Machine's being broken is essentially a scheme that McDonald's corporate runs on their franchisees. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37322814,"author":"synchronizing","month":"2023-08","story":"iFixit wants Congress to let it hack McDonald’s ice cream machines","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the exclusive McDonald's-Taylor partnership and costs to franchisees","quote":"Johnny Harris (filmmaker journalist) made a great video on topic","text":"Johnny Harris (filmmaker journalist) made a great video on topic: [VIDEO] In short, McDonald's continues to use these frequently malfunctioning ice cream machines due to their long-standing exclusive partnership with Taylor. The cost of repairs fall on the local franchise owners, not McD Corp."},{"id":29327446,"author":"shum1","month":"2021-11","story":"New emails released in the McDonald’s ice cream machine lawsuit","stance":"rec","why":"Context on the constantly broken McDonald's ice cream machines","quote":"Relevant link for those who want more context on the constantly broken McDonald icecream machine situation","text":"Relevant link for those who want more context on the constantly broken McDonald icecream machine situation: [VIDEO] I don't think the WIRED article isn't really doing justice to what Kytch is trying to show. The important argument is whether or not McDonald and Taylor conspired to prevent Kytch from selling / franchisees from purchasing this equipment, essentially a 'right to repair' argument. If Kytch wins, there could be ramifications for the tractor debate in the agricultural world as well."},{"id":29327467,"author":"fedreserved","month":"2021-11","story":"New emails released in the McDonald’s ice cream machine lawsuit","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth investigation of McDonald's and Taylor's ties","quote":"This video did a good indepth investigation.","text":"[VIDEO] This video did a good indepth investigation. From memory Taylors hq is within 5 miles of McDonald's HQ. To close for comfort when you see the type of shenanigans they go through to pad Taylors revenue"},{"id":28117647,"author":"dna_polymerase","month":"2021-08","story":"Why the McFlurry machine company just got hit with a restraining order","stance":"rec","why":"Mini-documentary covering Kytch and the ice cream machine saga","quote":"Worth watching, IMHO.","text":"Kytch was also mentioned in this excellent mini-documentary about the whole topic: [VIDEO] Worth watching, IMHO."},{"id":27420542,"author":"sackerhews","month":"2021-06","story":"Farmers Deserve the Right to Repair Their Tractors","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on profit from repair monopolies","quote":"This documentary is quite interesting","text":"Making something that only you can repair can be quite a lucrative business. This documentary is quite interesting: McDonalds Ice Cream Machines Are Always Broken: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27005755,"author":"mimsee","month":"2021-05","story":"Let's get Right to Repair passed","stance":"rec","why":"Why McDonald's ice cream machines fail: only one company may repair them","quote":"Check out the recent news on why McDonalds ice cream machines tend to fail.","text":"This. Check out the recent news on why McDonalds ice cream machines tend to fail. [VIDEO] Spoiler: it’s because the machine is broken and only one authorized company is allowed to fix it"},{"id":44259367,"author":"Pet_Ant","month":"2025-06","story":"Navy backs right to repair after $13B carrier goes half-fed","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There's a lot more to it than that","text":"There's a lot more to it than that: Johnny Harris \"The REAL Reason McDonalds Ice Cream Machines Are Always Broken\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":41949979,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-10","story":"We can now fix McDonald's ice cream machines","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Here's a great YT video on why McDonald's ice cream machines are always broken: [VIDEO] As habit or policy, can we all agree to get rid of the tracking information in Youtube links? * [VIDEO]"},{"id":41557533,"author":"chongli","month":"2024-09","story":"How to succeed in MrBeast production (Leaked PDF)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Johnny Harris did a long piece on the whole story [1].","text":"They being the third party repair companies. Johnny Harris did a long piece on the whole story [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39718247,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2024-03","story":"FTC and DOJ want to free McDonald's ice cream machines from DMCA repair rules","stance":"reference","why":"Conflicting relationship between McDonald's and Taylor hurting franchisees","quote":"Here [1] is a video on this topic by Johnny Harris from a couple years ago.","text":"Here [1] is a video on this topic by Johnny Harris from a couple years ago. It's been a while since I watched it but IIRC there is a conflicting relationship between specifically McDonalds the corporation and Taylor which benefits both of them but screws over the individual franchise owners. Here is an interactive map [2] that shows where all the broken machines are. [1] - [VIDEO] [video][30 mins] There is a sponsor ad from 06:49 to 08:47 [2] - [LINK]"},{"id":38659084,"author":"pengaru","month":"2023-12","story":"McDonald's Ice Cream Machine Hackers Say They Found 'Smoking Gun'","stance":"reference","why":"Covers McDonald's-specific machine problem","quote":"This video covered the situation fairly well IIRC","text":"One problem with your claims is this is pretty well documented as a uniquely McDonalds franchise problem at this point. Competing fast-food franchises serve similar frozen dairy products, in a similar staffing environment, without their machines constantly being out of service. This video covered the situation fairly well IIRC: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37321068,"author":"ixwt","month":"2023-08","story":"iFixit wants Congress to let it hack McDonald’s ice cream machines","stance":"reference","why":"Alleges franchisees must buy a specific Taylor machine","quote":"This video [0] alleges that McDonalds franchise are required to purchase a specific model of Taylor soft serve machine.","text":"This video [0] alleges that McDonalds franchise are required to purchase a specific model of Taylor soft serve machine. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37327140,"author":"overtomanu","month":"2023-08","story":"iFixit wants Congress to let it hack McDonald’s ice cream machines","stance":"reference","why":"Concludes corporate collusion profits at franchise owners' expense","quote":"There is a detailed video about it.","text":"There is a detailed video about it. Ultimately, conclusion seems like it is collusion between the two corporates to get profit at the expense of franchise owners The REAL Reason McDonalds Ice Cream Machines Are Always Broken by Johnny Harris [VIDEO]"},{"id":28389993,"author":"esseeayen","month":"2021-09","story":"The Feds Want to Know What the McFuck Is Going on with the McFlurry Machines","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I had a look at what machines they use here in Australia and they seem to be similar to Taylor machines that they showed being used in the USA (this video: [VIDEO] ). However, they almost always seem to be working without issue. If they are almost the same machine (would be for 240v) then I guess I’m glad that whatever is going on in the USA hasn’t reached here. Now the frozen drinks machines (slurpee, slushie, slush puppies) are down a lot more often but even then it isn’t down as much as the ice cream machines in the USA. But talking about McDonald’s McFlurry - in the USA do they still flurry it with the blending mixing spoon? They used to do that in Australia but now a McFlurry has no flurry and instead it’s just a plain sundae topped with Oreo crumbs or mini m&m’s."},{"id":28390946,"author":"chengiz","month":"2021-09","story":"The Feds Want to Know What the McFuck Is Going on with the McFlurry Machines","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Just pointing out this is a Johnny Harris, who put out a video [1].","text":"Just pointing out this is a Johnny Harris, who put out a video [1]. I was wondering what the vice president has to do with McDonalds icecream machines. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28119825,"author":"gopalv","month":"2021-08","story":"Why the McFlurry machine company just got hit with a restraining order","stance":"reference","why":"Deep-dive through manuals and sales projections on Taylor machines","quote":"There was a deep-dive into this from Johnny Harris[1]","text":"> The real problem is that McDonalds corporate forces franchisees to buy a crappy version of the machine that generates huge income for Taylor Wendys also uses the same vendor with a better machine, which is where this starts to look malicious rather than incompetent. The broken machine is one specifically made for McDonalds. In general, this is anti-consumer in two different ways - you don't give me ice-cream with the apple pie and whenever it breaks, you throw away a couple of gallons of the mixture to be safe. There was a deep-dive into this from Johnny Harris[1], which dug through the manuals for the machine, sales projections from Taylor and why McD franchisees are easier marks for this than others (the revenue volume is huge). [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":27339682,"author":"neonological","month":"2021-05","story":"Overkill objects for everyday life","stance":"reference","why":"Example of widespread repair restriction","quote":"This issue is much more widespread than you think","text":">So, the end result of having a soldered fuse was exactly the same as a socketed one: it was successfully repaired. I don’t see the justification for the extra BOM item. You joking? You realize houses and cars have socketed fuses that can be replaced almost turnkey. This is a simple solution to build into the product a UI that let's the user know a socket was blown and allow the user to replace a fuse like replacing a AA battery. FUSES are designed to fail and be replaced. Making those FUSES inaccessible is ALSO a design choice because it's contradictory. There was a point in time where every phone had a replaceable battery. You think that replaceable batteries disappeared because companies wanted to save costs? Sockets for batteries have been part of the design philosophy for consumer products for decades, the fact that these sockets are removed from phones is not a cost saving measure. This issue is much more widespread than you think: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26922817,"author":"Arbalest","month":"2021-04","story":"ArsTechnica has retracted the story about McDonald's Icecream","stance":"reference","why":"Investigation of McDonald's ice cream machine monopoly with Taylor","quote":"This is a youtube video on the same topic.","text":"This is a youtube video on the same topic. Inventor of this device tipped this youtuber off, and so the youtuber looks to confirm the reasoning behind the device. [VIDEO] Long story short, McDonalds created a monopoly for the icecream machine company, and then they abused it. This device comes into play, then McDonalds tells Franchisees not to use it."}],"r":[["IxuU5L2MEII",1]]},"cfyWvJdsDRI":{"wy":"HN cites it as the example of a better alternative to whiteboard and fizzbuzz-style interviews.","q":{"x":"please watch this alternative method of interviewing","i":18381001,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":38543013,"author":"andai","month":"2023-12","story":"NN-SVG","stance":"rec","why":"Shawn McGrath discusses efficient GPU rendering of vector graphics in N++","quote":"I found this interview (one relevant bit at 29:55) very interesting.","text":"This is great! I'm currently learning 2D graphics from scratch (making vector art software), can anyone recommend any related resources? I found this interview (one relevant bit at 29:55) very interesting. Shawn McGrath did the graphics code for the game N++ which is very vectory, so he found efficient ways to render it on a GPU. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32406545,"author":"PainfullyNormal","month":"2022-08","story":"Why is recruiting developers so difficult?","stance":"rec","why":"Way to assess coding ability without fizzbuzz","quote":"There are ways of discovering if somebody can code without making them do fizzbuzz. This is a good process","text":"There are ways of discovering if somebody can code without making them do fizzbuzz. This is a good process: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24460023,"author":"yakubin","month":"2020-09","story":"Ask HN: What is a better approach to interviewing?","stance":"rec","why":"Interview model showing how a candidate actually works","quote":"Casey Muratori showcased a new model of a programming interview here","text":"Casey Muratori showcased a new model of a programming interview here: [VIDEO] It shows to the interviewer how a given person works in a programming job, how they researched solutions to the problems they were faced with in the past etc.; as opposed to the usual exercises that have nothing to do with the job and won't really show you how well they will perform. It's also less confrontational which should put the interviewee at more ease."},{"id":18381001,"author":"zozbot123","month":"2018-11","story":"Google's interview system: it's not about solving the problem","stance":"rec","why":"Alternative method of interviewing programmers","quote":"please watch this alternative method of interviewing","text":"To any hiring managers that might be reading this, please watch this alternative method of interviewing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13815352,"author":"lj3","month":"2017-03","story":"Tech Interview Torture Chamber","stance":"rec","why":"Example of a better interview process","quote":"This is essentially what I suggest for an interview process and I'm not the only one.","text":"I don't know why you're being downvoted. This is essentially what I suggest for an interview process and I'm not the only one. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13476948,"author":"jblow","month":"2017-01","story":"Ask HN: What are good software architecture interview questions?","stance":"rec","why":"Example of a tailored candidate interview process","quote":"You want the questions to be tailored to the candidate. See this example","text":"It's not specific questions that are important so much as the process. You want the questions to be tailored to the candidate. See this example: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hgN8pCMLI2U",1],["ncHmEUmJZf4",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["oM6S7FEUfkU",1],["r9eQth4Q5jg",1],["V6VP-2aIcSc",1],["816VLQNdPMM",1],["BzhbAK1pdPM",1],["1faaOrtHJ-A",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["qv6UVOQ0F44",1],["eQ2OZKitRwc",1],["Z1qyvQsjK5Y",1]]},"kya_LXa_y1E":{"wy":"Recommended as an engaging critique of string theory hype and its effect on public trust in physics, and often cited alongside Peter Woit's writing.","q":{"x":"while the video is nearly an hour long, it feels much shorter and is well worth watching","i":39756012,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39756012,"author":"sockmeistr","month":"2024-03","story":"20 Years of \"Not Even Wrong\"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nearly an hour long but feels shorter.","quote":"while the video is nearly an hour long, it feels much shorter and is well worth watching","text":"I just wanted to second this recommendation - while the video is nearly an hour long, it feels much shorter and is well worth watching: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39224572,"author":"echelon","month":"2024-02","story":"In a 'Dark Dimension,' Physicists Search for the Universe's Missing Matter","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Refutation of string theory's share of research funding.","quote":"This [1] is a fantastic refutation of string theory","text":"This [1] is a fantastic refutation of string theory in that the string theorists have taken more than their fair share of research dollars and public consciousness and that it's time for other branches of physics to get the funding. With limited resources, if you don't context switch, you starve everything else. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46623825,"author":"carrozo","month":"2026-01","story":"The string theory hype machine will never die","stance":"rec","why":"Fun watch on string theory and science communication.","quote":"this video by Angela Collier is a fun watch","text":"not a physicist but this video by Angela Collier is a fun watch: “string theory lied to us and now science communication is hard. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41811412,"author":"Shawnecy","month":"2024-10","story":"Fundamental physics is dying? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Timeline of string theory and consequences for science communication.","quote":"I'm particularly fond of how Angela Collier laid out the timeline of string theory in her video on it[0]","text":"Exactly. Consistently untestable and unfalsifiable claims for decades has to be seriously questioned at some point, and I think we're well beyond that point. This is especially true for string theory. I'm particularly fond of how Angela Collier laid out the timeline of string theory in her video on it[0] as well as the consequences that science communication is now facing as a result. [0] = [VIDEO]"},{"id":41341678,"author":"johnkpaul","month":"2024-08","story":"The Elegant Universe: 25th Anniversary Edition","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful explainer on the current state of string theory.","quote":"I’ve found this explainer by Angela Collier super helpful in understanding the current state of string theory.","text":"I’ve found this explainer by Angela Collier super helpful in understanding the current state of string theory. [VIDEO] I was a gigantic pop science fan and read The Elegant Universe when I was approximately 13 and am excited it’s still being read even if it’s not necessarily true about our universe. It’s got the inspiration and excitement part that’s all I really needed as a kid."},{"id":39753872,"author":"seanhunter","month":"2024-03","story":"20 Years of \"Not Even Wrong\"","stance":"rec","why":"How string theory hype affects physicists and public perception.","quote":"I enjoyed the video \"String Theory Lied to Us and Now Science Communication is Hard\"[4]","text":"Agree but very much with your caveat. If you study the mathematics of some sort of abstract structure you are doing research in pure maths. That is a worthy and completely legitimate pursuit and a ton of stuff started life as pure abstract mathematical research and ended up having real-world benefits way down the line that noone could have forseen.[1] If on the other hand you say that abstract structure is a fundamental building block of the universe and, say, unifies the standard model with gravity etc but actually your theory isn't explaining anything observable I would say that isn't theoretical physics[2] but is just \"making shit up\". So far string theory has failed to produce any sort of verifiable prediction[3], so I just don't see how it's physics (you could say \"yet\" perhaps). I enjoyed the video \"String Theory Lied to Us and Now Science Communication is Hard\"[4] by Angela Collier, who is a computational astrophysicist[5], because it gives a sense for how these things impact other physicists and the popular perception of science. [1] And Ed Witten for example is without doubt a very impressive mathematician - he won the Fields medal. [2] Which is the science of matter, energy, space, time etc all the stuff which makes up the natural world. [3] In particular, the one that has been tried is supersymmetry and the LHC, as I understand it as a non-physicist, brought about an experimental result that called supersymmetric extensions of the standard model into serious doubt "},{"id":39758094,"author":"thaumaturgy","month":"2024-03","story":"20 Years of \"Not Even Wrong\"","stance":"rec","why":"Covers string theory critique; Woit linked to it.","quote":"Woit linked to her video at , so he at least seems to appreciate it.","text":"For those that have the time and interest, Angela Collier is a somewhat newer science communicator, and covers everything described in this reply (and more) at [VIDEO] . I don't have the knowledge needed to criticize or defend Dr. Collier's arguments, but Woit linked to her video at [LINK] , so he at least seems to appreciate it."},{"id":39760601,"author":"nick238","month":"2024-03","story":"The humiliating truth behind Harvard astronomer's alien spherules","stance":"rec","why":"History of string theory narrative and its effect on public perception.","quote":"Collier has a video on the history of string theory as a narrative","text":"Collier has a video on the history of string theory as a narrative and how it kinda tainted the public perception of high energy/particle physics: \"String theory lied to us and now science communication is hard\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":36968389,"author":"ttctciyf","month":"2023-08","story":"Oregon Decriminalized Hard Drugs","stance":"reference","why":"Physics postgrad criticism of string theory and public trust.","quote":"As non-crackpot physics profs and postgrads (e.g. [1], [2]) have pointed out","text":"Carroll's episode is worth listening to, not least for it's exceptionally clear exposition of the current physics consensus around QM, qft, standard model, etc., and how they are related, but the point you specifically mention is a weak spot for me. For one thing he hardly mentions the epic failure of string theory to make good on its initial promises nor the murky waters of anthropic claims and metaphysical notions of beauty, etc., used to keep it suspended like Wile E Coyote after running off the cliff of empirical support. As non-crackpot physics profs and postgrads (e.g. [1], [2]) have pointed out, this has not engendered public trust and is a key ingredient in whatever \"crisis\" the discipline is undergoing. Not that Carroll doesn't make plenty of good arguments to support his views, but his seeming equation of any criticism of the field with crackpotist heresies is a cheap trick for a philosophy prof. 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37004577,"author":"ep103","month":"2023-08","story":"Successful room temperature ambient-pressure magnetic levitation of LK-99","stance":"reference","why":"PhD candidate on string theory sentiment swinging back.","quote":"I stumbled across this youtube video of a phd candidate talking about string theory recently","text":"I'm far outside of these social circles nowadays, but I stumbled across this youtube video of a phd candidate talking about string theory recently ( [VIDEO] ), and it seemed to imply that the pendulum in those social circles is swinging back again."}],"r":[["Gj8IA6xOpSk",1],["kRlhlCWplqk",1],["lKhAd0Tyny0",1],["X8aWBcPVPMo",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["j2Mu8qfVb5I",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1],["lG2dXobAXLI",1],["NPOHf20slZg",1],["NQFqDKRAROI",1],["GVT3WUa-48Y",1],["Sf036fO-ZUk",1],["1hs451PfFzQ",1],["ISAKc9gpGjw",1]]},"mII9NZ8MMVM":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible animated primer on banking and the Fed, usually with the caveat that it is biased and dated.","q":{"x":"There's this amazing animated movie from 2011 that explains how banking works and the history of banks","i":35243448,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35243448,"author":"Jerry2","month":"2023-03","story":"Bank Failures Come in Waves","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animated film on how banking works and its history","quote":"There's this amazing animated movie from 2011 that explains how banking works and the history of banks","text":"Yes, that's how it works. There's this amazing animated movie from 2011 that explains how banking works and the history of banks: \"The Collapse of The American Dream Explained in Animation\" [1] It has almost 10 million views. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8792261,"author":"Fuxy","month":"2014-12","story":"The world is not falling apart: The trend lines","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animation explaining currency and banking better than anything else seen","quote":"a simple youtube animation that explains it better than anything i have seen.","text":"> Wealth Inequality - The gap is widening The US currency and most other currencies are working against the people and in favor of the bankers. Probably one of the best solutions that have presented themselves for this problem is bit-coin but since it is extremely volatile and still underused at the moment it is not ready yet. Here's [1] a simple youtube animation that explains it better than anything i have seen. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42871893,"author":"normalaccess","month":"2025-01","story":"Fed leaves rates unchanged, drops reference to inflation 'progress'","stance":"rec","why":"Cliff notes on how the Fed works, though old and corny","quote":"this video gives the cliff notes on how the FED works","text":"The fact that every dollar that is printed MUST be paid back to the Federal Reserve Bank with interest means that every dollar printed is worth less than it's face value. This is the original and primary source of inflation. All debt biassed currencies have this, not as a defect, but as a feature. If you own the bank you can do some money magic and get filthy rich. The Federal reserve Over the last 100 years the dollar has lost something like 96% of it's value. It's old and quite corny but this video gives the cliff notes on how the FED works [VIDEO]"},{"id":22575812,"author":"unixhero","month":"2020-03","story":"Ask HN: Book recommendations for understanding financial systems?","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining but biased clip on banking, to take with a grain of salt","quote":"take this biased Internet-clip with a grain of salt and enjoy","text":"Any book on International Finance, to start. And then maybe a Banking 101 to understand how banking works. Then Macro Economics, to know how economics of markets and countries work. There's no quick fix to learning this stuff. However you might still want to; so take this biased Internet-clip with a grain of salt and enjoy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7323855,"author":"icu","month":"2014-03","story":"Ask HN: What is really wrong with the US economy, really?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among resources on deleveraging and the Fed","quote":"For further information look to the following","text":"There are several levels to what is wrong. I have dedicated the last 5 years to discovering what is going on so I will try my best to condense it down. In terms of a 'shorter' business cycle view (less than 10 year perspective) to what is wrong is that we are not in a recession but in a deleverage. We haven't had one of those since WW2 so most people don't know what it is and think it is a recession. A deleverage is a once in a 100 year massive transfer of wealth event. I have put resources on what a deleverage is at the end of this reply. The long term problem is that the Fed is a private bank. This means that the US Government and American citizens do not control the Fed. Forget the façade that makes them look like a branch of Government, especially the whole Presidential appointment of the Fed Chairman. It is a private bank that gets to print money and sell it to the US Government that American citizens are forced to use. That means that the US Government and American citizens are slaves with no control over their future. Each dollar printed is a debt burden on the American people and a debasement of the current currency value. Essentially think of printing money as a way that the Fed transfers wealth from your pocket into theirs. Furthermore, I believe that the Fed manipulates the economic cycle to create wealth transfer events that they reap the benefits of. For further information look to the following: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["Gxd23UVID7k",1],["-bYAQ-ZZtEU",1],["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["rOzrJjdZDRQ",1],["iD9xk3SDSYc",1],["vYLEuQrvND0",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["-gGLvg0n-uY",1]]},"wM8Xli2KTzI":{"wy":"Cited repeatedly in e-bike threads as a well-argued safety counterpoint from someone who rides both.","q":{"x":"Fortnine made an excellent argument against the safety of ebikes, worth watching as a rebuttal to this article","i":29184637,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":44690391,"author":"Animats","month":"2025-07","story":"A list of changes to make it easier to build beautiful and walkable places","stance":"rec","why":"Good e-bike versus motorcycle discussion.","quote":"Good E-bike vs. Motorcycle discussion.[3]","text":"That's a US definition pushed by the E-bike industry.[1] The EU has a completely different classification system.[2] Anything above a US class 1 e-bike comes with more restrictions. Good E-bike vs. Motorcycle discussion.[3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32705057,"author":"Animats","month":"2022-09","story":"The E-bike Is a Monstrosity","stance":"rec","why":"Compares commuting on an e-bike versus a motorcycle.","quote":"Here's a good video on this by someone who has both an e-bike and a motorcycle.[1]","text":"\"E-bikes’ identity crisis might seem like the symptom of a transition\" The identity crisis is that they are electric motorcycles/mopeds pretending to be bicycles. Here's a good video on this by someone who has both an e-bike and a motorcycle.[1] He points out that for his commute in traffic, they get about the same performance. But on the motorcycle, he's more visible, has better protective gear, and has a lower center of gravity. The difference between a class I e-bike (20mph, no helmet required) and an electric motorcycle capable of 45 MPH is a switch. The trouble is, e-bikes with motorcycle speed capabilities lack motorcycle safety features. They're under-braked, under-tired, have crap suspensions, don't have full lights and turn signals, and the rider is up too high, so accidents are worse. There's a video of someone who put an e-bike motor hub in a small motorcycle frame, which got him something that's a better e-bike than most e-bikes, but is classified as a motorcycle. But low-end electric motorcycles have not caught on in the US. Bikers think they're uncool, and bicyclists think they're uncool. They're popular in parts of Asia. So, yes, there is an identity crisis. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31800074,"author":"thoms_a","month":"2022-06","story":"Reasons to tour by bicycle","stance":"rec","why":"Cited for the risks of cycling on shared roads.","quote":"But don't take my word for it","text":"Came here to say this. Bicycling is absolute madness to me in any location that doesn't have dedicated separate bike paths. You are maximizing all possible sources of risk by riding a bicycle on shared roads. I've ridden motorcycles since I was 16, and everyone always sees me as the daredevil. Yet, I still think bicycle commuters are the true madmen. Zero protection, can't keep up with traffic, your very presence is a psychological irritant to motorists which causes them to wish you harm, etc. But don't take my word for it: [VIDEO] My only advice to cyclists: put your bike on your car's bike rack, take to a bike path and enjoy. If you want to get on a road on two wheels, buy a motorcycle and wear every piece of protective gear you can afford. You (and your family) can thank me later."},{"id":31014215,"author":"porknubbins","month":"2022-04","story":"E-bike injuries on the rise even on well protected Dutch roads","stance":"rec","why":"Argues e-bike risks resemble motorcycles with less gear.","quote":"makes a pretty good argument that the risks of ebikes are similar to those of motorcycles","text":"The youtube motorcycle channel Fort Nine makes a pretty good argument that the risks of ebikes are similar to those of motorcycles, and the riders typically have less protective gear. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29184637,"author":"nickysielicki","month":"2021-11","story":"Reflections on E-Bikes","stance":"rec","why":"Argues against e-bike safety.","quote":"Fortnine made an excellent argument against the safety of ebikes, worth watching as a rebuttal to this article","text":"Fortnine made an excellent argument against the safety of ebikes, worth watching as a rebuttal to this article: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28269883,"author":"IgorPartola","month":"2021-08","story":"A DIY E-bike Conversion on the Cheap","stance":"rec","why":"Safety perspective check before riding e-bikes.","quote":"Before you get on one of these, it’s good to check your perspective on safety","text":"Before you get on one of these, it’s good to check your perspective on safety: [VIDEO] As a motorcyclist, electric bicycles make me very nervous."},{"id":37972079,"author":"avidiax","month":"2023-10","story":"The Reason You Should Get an E-bike: It'll make you happier","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that e-bikes are motorcycles lacking safety gear.","quote":"I think there's a solid argument that e-bikes are essentially motorcycles lacking a variety of safety gear.","text":"I think there's a solid argument that e-bikes are essentially motorcycles lacking a variety of safety gear. That may be an advantage where you can ride e-bikes on dedicated bike lanes, but is probably a disadvantage if you ride e-bikes on roads shared with cars. They lack the speed to match pace with cars, forcing passing, and they are more prone to being hit from the side, since e-bikes tend to ride on the right side of the lane and drivers underestimate their speed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31042913,"author":"Manuel_D","month":"2022-04","story":"The Colorado Safety Stop is the law of the land","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for e-bike commute speeds matching motorcycles.","quote":"In a typical commute, they average the same speeds as motorcycles","text":"> The bill defines “low speed conveyances” in Colorado law. These are small profile, low-speed vehicles that people use for transportation and recreation, including bicycles and electric bicycles, electric scooters (not including mopeds), and wheelchairs. I think including electric bicycles in this is unwise. In a typical commute, they average the same speeds as motorcycles: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28608359,"author":"Animats","month":"2021-09","story":"Trailerduck is a smart e-bike trailer","stance":"reference","why":"Hub motor on a motorcycle frame example.","quote":"Someone who is into both e-bikes and motorcycles has a good answer to this.","text":"Someone who is into both e-bikes and motorcycles has a good answer to this. He put an e-bike hub motor with a motorcycle-sized wheel on a motorcycle frame. The result has about the performance of a class 3 e-bike, but it's a better vehicle.[1] You sit lower, so the CG is lower, and if something goes wrong you tend to slide, not go over the handlebars. You have motorcycle-grade tires, brakes, and suspension, so the stopping distances are much shorter than with an e-bike. But you can't take the thing in a bike lane. And it requires a motorcycle license. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28258157,"author":"dharmab","month":"2021-08","story":"Benefits of Not Using an IDE","stance":"reference","why":"In-depth video on e-bike safety.","quote":"Motorcyling channel Fortnine did a video in depth on this topic","text":"Motorcyling channel Fortnine did a video in depth on this topic: [VIDEO] (They've also done some more motorized cycle and electric content lately. A recent video showed how to rebuild an old motorcycle's frame into an electric motorcycle using a kit.)"},{"id":31800297,"author":"cycomanic","month":"2022-06","story":"Reasons to tour by bicycle","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this video is absolute rubbish","text":"> Came here to say this. Bicycling is absolute madness to me in any location that doesn't have dedicated separate bike paths. You are maximizing all possible sources of risk by riding a bicycle on shared roads. > I've ridden motorcycles since I was 16, and everyone always sees me as the daredevil. Yet, I still think bicycle commuters are the true madmen. Zero protection, can't keep up with traffic, your very presence is a psychological irritant to motorists which causes them to wish you harm, etc. > But don't take my word for it: [VIDEO] Whatever one might think about the general premise, but this video is absolute rubbish. All the little example videos he puts in is of people riding on crazy tuned e-bikes or downhill bikes etc.. On the other hand the few instances of motorcycling videos show him just riding along in traffic, a bit disingenuous. > My only advice to cyclists: put your bike on your car's bike rack, take to a bike path and enjoy. If you want to get on a road on two wheels, buy a motorcycle and wear every piece of protective gear you can afford. You (and your family) can thank me later. Considering that the article points to bike paths off general roads your post is a bit redundant. I would still question your premise. You ride a motorbike beside the fact that motorcycle deaths and serious injuries outstrip cycling ones by quite a margin (it's right there in the video you posted). So I guess safety is not your main priority. Moreover, if we consider overall healt"}],"r":[["SEwTjZvy8vw",1],["o_AIw9bGogo",1],["SCAGc-rkIfo",1],["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["Uj-rK8V-rik",1],["3UxZDJ1HiPE",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["JOLtS4VUcvQ",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["nsJZ_kKjXcE",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1],["EOwxxsPaogY",1],["eqQBubilSXU",1]]},"BickMFHAZR0":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as one of Veritasium's best videos and one that has aged well.","q":{"x":"I recommend watching, I think it's one of the best veritasium Dereck has ever produced.","i":48783282,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48783282,"author":"bnegreve","month":"2026-07","story":"Giant trees have no trouble pumping water to top branches","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains tree water transport via negative pressure, not valves.","quote":"I recommend watching, I think it's one of the best veritasium Dereck has ever produced.","text":"> all it should take is a system of valves, That would work, but it's not how to works apparently. According to this veritasium video, it's because of \"negative pressure\" aka tension. [VIDEO] I recommend watching, I think it's one of the best veritasium Dereck has ever produced."},{"id":38134367,"author":"_nalply","month":"2023-11","story":"In a surprising finding, light can make water evaporate without heat","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Old video that aged well and rewards rewatching.","quote":"It's an old video, more than ten years ago, but it aged well IMHO.","text":"[VIDEO] It's an old video, more than ten years ago, but it aged well IMHO. I re-watched it and re-learned things I have forgotten."},{"id":20392098,"author":"linsomniac","month":"2019-07","story":"Water transport system inspired by trees","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How trees move water from roots to the top.","quote":"Just recently watched a Veritasium video on how trees get water from the roots to the top of the tree, and it's pretty awesome","text":"Just recently watched a Veritasium video on how trees get water from the roots to the top of the tree, and it's pretty awesome: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5085348,"author":"vacri","month":"2013-01","story":"Why trees can't grow taller than around 100 metres","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how trees pull water up with negative pressure.","quote":"The secret sauce is that in a liquid rather than a gas, you can have a negative pressure.","text":"This is an interesting video with an alternate explanation - the pressure required to get water to the top of the tree. Normally you can only suck a water column up 10 meters, and that's using a perfect vacuum - but trees can grow higher than that. The secret sauce is that in a liquid rather than a gas, you can have a negative pressure. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48786190,"author":"Arodex","month":"2026-07","story":"Giant trees have no trouble pumping water to top branches","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium coverage of the same topic.","quote":"See also Veritasium on that topic","text":"... May we have the solution to the questions, also? I went through the concours too but I am very rusty. See also Veritasium on that topic: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["zdkyGDqU7xA",1],["8AyHH9G9et0",1],["-k-V3ESHcfA",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1],["oVfHeWTKjag",1],["l9ZqXlHl65g",1]]},"PE4gwstWhmc":{"wy":"Recommended as a detailed, candid case study of scaling a real system from zero users to millions.","q":{"x":"I found the Dropbox lecture [1] at Stanford one of the most riveting things ever.","i":18070352,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18070352,"author":"fermienrico","month":"2018-09","story":"How we rolled out one of the largest Python 3 migrations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Riveting look at the technology behind Dropbox.","quote":"I found the Dropbox lecture [1] at Stanford one of the most riveting things ever.","text":"I found the Dropbox lecture [1] at Stanford one of the most riveting things ever. There is just so much technology behind Dropbox, it is staggering. There is a reason why it is so much better than iCloud sync, Google Drive, Box or OneDrive. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17290758,"author":"testb","month":"2018-06","story":"Ask HN: Most effective path to become a solid systems practitioner?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk going from zero users to millions.","quote":"Here's some of my favorite talks that go from 0 users to millions.","text":"imo the best way is to look at what other's have built. Here's some of my favorite talks that go from 0 users to millions. Dropbox - [VIDEO] Instagram - [LINK] Slack (bonus. not as applicable but good reminder of why initial architecture does matter) - [LINK]"},{"id":16669266,"author":"jesseendahl","month":"2018-03","story":"Congrats Dropbox","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Dropbox is far more than a GUI on rsync.","quote":"You might enjoy this talk","text":"Dropbox is a lot more than a GUI on top of rsync. Even purely from an engineering standpoint (ignoring product & design) that's incorrect. You might enjoy this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14839471,"author":"bradgnar","month":"2017-07","story":"Ask HN: Best-architected open-source business applications worth studying?","stance":"rec","why":"Case study on scaling problems and solutions at Dropbox.","quote":"there are a lot of good talks on youtube from places like dropbox and facebook that explain the problem and solution.","text":"Applications dont really need to be well architected until they are hitting scale. Then the parts of their system that need to relieve pressure will need to be re-architected. This is almost like a case study and there are a lot of good talks on youtube from places like dropbox and facebook that explain the problem and solution. Example: [VIDEO] If you dont want to do youtube case studies there are also books to read about distributed systems. Also reading about cloud architecture can help."},{"id":8481199,"author":"rawnlq","month":"2014-10","story":"Facebook's software architecture","stance":"rec","why":"Incredibly detailed talk on Dropbox architecture evolution, down to SQL schema.","quote":"It is incredibly detailed (down to the exact sql schema they use for file metadata etc).","text":"This is a talk about the evolution of Dropbox's architecture from 2012: [VIDEO] . It is incredibly detailed (down to the exact sql schema they use for file metadata etc)."},{"id":35909053,"author":"srcreigh","month":"2023-05","story":"Ask HN: It's 2023, how do you choose between MySQL and Postgres?","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates primary key clustering for range queries.","quote":"Illustration from Dropbox scaling talk below.","text":"Postgres is >50x slower for range queries(example below) and is akin to using array-of-pointers (ie Java) whereas MySQL supports array-of-struct (C). Illustration from Dropbox scaling talk below. Sneak peek photo [1] (from [2]). Just imagine its literally 500-1000x more convoluted per B-tree leaf node. That's every Postgres table unless you CLUSTER periodically. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] Mind boggling how many people aren't aware of primary indexes in MySQL that is not supported at all in Postgres. For certain data layouts, Postgres pays either 2x storage (covering index containing every single column), >50x worse performance by effectively N+1 bombing the disk for range queries, or blocking your table periodically (CLUSTER). In Postgres the messiness loading primary data after reaching the B-tree leaf nodes pollutes caches and takes longer. This is because you need to load one 8kb page for every row you want, instead of one 8kb with 20-30 rows packed together. Example: Dropbox file history table. They initially used autoinc id for primary key in MySQL. This causes everybodys file changes to be mixed together in chronological order on disk in a B-Tree. The first optimization they made was to change the primary key to (ns_id, latest, id) so that each users (ns_id) latest versions would be grouped together on disk. Dropbox scaling talk: [VIDEO] If a dropbox user has 1000 files and you can fit 20 file-version rows on each 8kb disk page (400bytes/row), the difference in performance"},{"id":28250549,"author":"srcreigh","month":"2021-08","story":"How database indexing works internally","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Dropbox's change to composite primary key for data locality.","quote":"At 48:36 they explain why they changed from PRIMARY KEY (id) to PRIMARY KEY (ns_id, latest, id).","text":"An important detail which this guide leaves out: The \"actual location of the row\" is usually the leaf node of another B-Tree. That is the primary B-Tree and it's indexed by the primary key. The major consequence is every non-primary index query involves dereferencing N pointers, which could mean loading N pages from disk/SSD to get leaf nodes spread out across the primary tree. Whereas if you query a range in the primary B-Tree directly, the rows are consecutive so you would only load N/M consecutive pages based on the ratio of rowsize to pagesize. That's why some people use composite keys for primary key, to get better data locality in the primary B-Tree index. See \"How We've Scaled Dropbox\"[1] to hear the same thing. At 48:36 they explain why they changed from PRIMARY KEY (id) to PRIMARY KEY (ns_id, latest, id). ns_id is kinda like user_id. So that change groups journal entries for users together on disk Specifically. PRIMARY KEY (id) orders things by creation date whereas PRIMARY KEY (ns_id, latest, id) orders things by ns_id primarily. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14842967,"author":"LrnByTeach","month":"2017-07","story":"Ask HN: Best-architected open-source business applications worth studying?","stance":"reference","why":"Example scaling case study.","quote":"there are a lot of good talks on youtube from places like dropbox and facebook that explain the problem and solution.","text":"> Applications don't really need to be well architected until they are hitting scale. very True, 'a system Well architected' before hitting scale is considered OVER Engineering > Then the parts of their system that need to relieve pressure will need to be re-architected. > This is almost like a case study and there are a lot of good talks on youtube from places like dropbox and facebook that explain the problem and solution. Example: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["CLo3e1Pak-Y",1],["BLGFriOKz6U",1],["D1R-jKKp3NA",1]]},"qybUFnY7Y8w":{"wy":"HN points to it as an example of ambitious, practical-effects creativity in music videos, made without CGI.","q":{"x":"The Rube Goldberg machine [0] one is particularily impressive.","i":13028039,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":5831291,"author":"Intermernet","month":"2013-06","story":"Seriously.js","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Quirky OK Go Rube Goldberg music video the commenter promises you will like","quote":"You should watch . You'll like it. Trust me.","text":"That was OK, Go ( [LINK] ) . The song is \"WTF?\" They're famous for quirky video clips (original for this song is at [LINK] ). You should watch [VIDEO] . You'll like it. Trust me."},{"id":48094611,"author":"gosub100","month":"2026-05","story":"The Greatest Shot in Television: James Burke Had One Chance to Nail This Scene","stance":"rec","why":"3-minute Rube Goldberg machine music video, with a couple of cuts","quote":"Still a great music video and song.","text":"Reminds me of the 3 minute rube Goldberg machine in \"this too shall pass\" by Ok Go. [VIDEO] I thought they really did it all in one cut but there are at least two. Still a great music video and song."},{"id":13028039,"author":"ymse","month":"2016-11","story":"OK Go’s video, for the song “The One Moment”, took only 4.2 seconds to film","stance":"rec","why":"Particularly impressive Rube Goldberg machine music video by OK Go","quote":"The Rube Goldberg machine [0] one is particularily impressive.","text":"OK Go really do make some amazing music videos. The Rube Goldberg machine [0] one is particularily impressive. It even got its own TED talk [1]. 0: [VIDEO] 1: [LINK]"},{"id":3194817,"author":"derrybryson","month":"2011-11","story":"Stop motion video shot over 2 years with 288,000 jelly beans","stance":"rec","why":"Incredible videos made without CGI","quote":"incredible videos made without CGI","text":"Not really. Stop-motion music videos have been done quite a bit, especially 10 years ago and more. There was a time before CGI when artists shot \"videos\" on film and computers weren't capable of the things they are today. Consider the Peter Gabriel videos: [LINK] [LINK] both of which feature various types of stop-motion animation as well as other effects. Also consider the works of Bruce Bickford whom Frank Zappa hired to work on several videos. This guy is a genius/savant, I don't know which. Zappa saw the genius and funded some incredible work. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] You also might want to check out: [VIDEO] and their other videos, which aren't stop-motion but are incredible videos made without CGI. There's tons more out there to find if you look."},{"id":1285878,"author":"Perceval","month":"2010-04","story":"Another lovely Google Chrome ad - Extensions","stance":"rec","why":"Creative, cute and whimsical OK Go video","quote":"I think it is abstract, but I like it a whole lot nonetheless.","text":"Reminds me a bit of Ok Go's latest video: [VIDEO] Seems like something Michel Gondry or Spike Jonze ( [LINK] ) would do. I think it is abstract, but I like it a whole lot nonetheless. It's creative and cute and whimsical. Kudos!"},{"id":40315537,"author":"mgdlbp","month":"2024-05","story":"Apple apologizes for iPad 'Crush' ad that 'missed the mark'","stance":"reference","why":"Rube Goldberg video smashed pianos and a TV, comparable to Apple crush ad","quote":"","text":"Brings to mind the OK GO music video with all the exploded guitars that they stated were already junk. Spilled paint is reminiscent of their videos too. They also smashed pianos in the Rube Goldberg video, and a TV with a sledge. Now I wonder if that was a 1984 reference. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3194728,"author":"ugh","month":"2011-11","story":"Stop motion video shot over 2 years with 288,000 jelly beans","stance":"reference","why":"Example of OK Go avoiding shortcuts","quote":"OK Go owes their fame to never taking shortcuts when creating their music videos","text":"This video would be pretty uninteresting had they used artificial jelly beans. I think everyone is aware that it’s trivial to convert a simple animation to a jelly bean animation. That’s not the point. Art works by different rules. (Hey, and if you like to be a cynic about it you can also say that viral videos work by different rules. OK Go owes their fame to never taking shortcuts when creating their music videos†.) (I think that placing the singer directly on the jelly beans allowed them to do certain tricks that would be harder to accomplish otherwise: pay attention to the lighting or the costume changes. It’s not clear to me whether using a green screen would really have saved time.) — † One example: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["9pOiOhxujsE",1],["5Dw3aKbw5Wo",1]]},"rQmqVVmMB3k":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an accessible summary of the case against seed oils, and some say it changed what fats they cook with.","q":{"x":"one of the most important ones imo being the one on seed oils","i":31142591,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31142591,"author":"ComradePhil","month":"2022-04","story":"Why didn't our ancient ancestors get cavities?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the most important videos on seed oils","quote":"one of the most important ones imo being the one on seed oils","text":"I started on this path when I was trying to nurish myself back to health over a decade ago when I suddenly got fat. I thought science was the way to go... but was robbed of that notion a couple of years into it when I discovered that the path to health was in the opposite direction of what the science tells us. Turns out the food industry fully controls the science of food and nutrition. You can start by reading Unsavory Truth or Food Politics by Marion Nestle for better understanding of why that is the way it is. Having said that, Marion Nestle doesn't really explore one side of it... which is that because of ethical reasons, we'll never have proper human experiments, thus nutrition science will always be limited and incomplete, which leaves a lot of room for manipulation, which the industry is happy to do for profits. This has been covered very well by the YouTube channel What I've Learned: [LINK] The same channel has several other videos on food and nutrition, one of the most important ones imo being the one on seed oils: [VIDEO] Nutrition and Physical Degenaration by Weston A. Price as described in this thread is one of the best works in support of indigenous foods. The Hidden Life of Trees is a great book on plant intelligence. Other than those resources, we have to piece these things together, take long term views... like should we trust a diet that kept a culture of people alive and well for 100s of years over several generations or do we trust studies with couple of d"},{"id":33322156,"author":"OgAstorga","month":"2022-10","story":"Only virgin type of olive oil consumption reduces the risk of mortality: study","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of industrialized oils and alternatives","quote":"a very good summary on the industrialized oils and they alternatives","text":"less[1]. this video[2] is not the best reference but a very good summary on the industrialized oils and they alternatives [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30835293,"author":"ComradePhil","month":"2022-03","story":"First chicken-free egg white product reaches US markets","stance":"rec","why":"Listed as resource on food industry and nutrition","quote":"YouTube Video: The $100 Billion Dollar Ingredient making your Food Toxic","text":"These should cover some of it: - Book: Food Politics by Marion Nestle - Book: Nutrition and Physical Degeneration by Weston A. Price - YouTube Video: The $100 Billion Dollar Ingredient making your Food Toxic: [VIDEO] (28 minutes)"},{"id":30456616,"author":"sinyug","month":"2022-02","story":"Soybean oil affects hypothalamus, causes genetic changes in mice: study (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Seed oil information that led commenter to switch fats","quote":"I saw this video about seed oils a few months back.[1] Some of the information there tracked stuff I have been reading for a while.","text":"> Soybean oil I saw this video about seed oils a few months back.[1] Some of the information there tracked stuff I have been reading for a while. Decided to switch over completely to coconut oil and ghee (clarified butter). A comment on the video that matches my view on the subject: > If you want to be healthy just do the exact opposite of everything we were told about nutrition by mainstream experts for decades [1] The $100 Billion Dollar Ingredient making your Food Toxic ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":30469650,"author":"shaicoleman","month":"2022-02","story":"Soybean oil affects hypothalamus, causes genetic changes in mice: study (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Vegetable oil processing and health effects","quote":"","text":"For me it's enough to look at the process of making vegetable oils (acid wash, oxidation caused by high temperatures, bleaching, deodorization, degumming, etc.) to conclude ultraprocessed oils are not healthy for you. [VIDEO] From personal experience, my health significantly improved after replacing vegetable oils with butter, coconut and olive oil."},{"id":29181565,"author":"bavell","month":"2021-11","story":"Fatty acid found in palm oil linked to spread of cancer","stance":"rec","why":"History of vegetable oils replacing saturated fats","quote":"Very interesting video about this and more from What I've Learned","text":"IIRC, vegetable oils (unsaturated fats) exploded in popularity in the middle of last century when chemical companies figured out how to mass produce them. Prior to that, we got most of our fats from saturated fats. Very interesting video about this and more from What I've Learned: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29206379,"author":"lifeplusplus","month":"2021-11","story":"Industrial Seed Oils Are Making Us Sick (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Omega-6 to omega-3 ratio and disease","quote":"","text":"[LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] TLDR: omega-6 fat ratio to omega-3 used to be 1:1 now it's 15:1. And there are many indications that this is the cause of many modern diseases as rise of diseases correlates with rise of seed oils not industrializations. \"It has been estimated that in developed countries, the ratio of omega-6s to omega-3s is closer to 15:1\" \"It has been claimed that among hunter-gatherer populations, omega-6 fats and omega-3 fats are typically consumed in roughly a 1:1 ratio\" [LINK] also [LINK]"}],"r":[["9LfmrkyP81M",1],["7kGnfXXIKZM",1],["7nJgHBbEgsE",1],["y3qfeoqErtY",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["hTsrDpsYHrw",1]]},"9cNmUNHSBac":{"wy":"HN links it in threads on bikes, countersteering and control systems as the clearest demonstration of how bikes actually steer and balance.","q":{"x":"It shows what happens if you prevent the rider from performing the countersteer before leaning into a turn [1].","i":35345129,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35343969,"author":"Brajeshwar","month":"2023-03","story":"Bicycle","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interesting video on how bikes work","quote":"You will love the interesting video,","text":"You will love the interesting video, \"Most People Don't Know How Bikes Work\" by Veritasium - [VIDEO]"},{"id":45853004,"author":"nitwit005","month":"2025-11","story":"You should write an agent","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how bicycles work, even for people who ride","quote":"Veritasium has a quite good video on the subject","text":"> You only think you understand how a bicycle works, until you learn to ride one. I realize this is just for motivation in a subtitle, but people generally don't grasp how bicycles work, even after having ridden one. Veritasium has a quite good video on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40069036,"author":"jasonpeacock","month":"2024-04","story":"How does this full-track, 3-wheel snow bike even turn?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how steering relates to leaning in a bike","quote":"Here's a good video showing how a bike actually works, and the relation between steering and leaning","text":"Here's a good video showing how a bike actually works, and the relation between steering and leaning: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35343846,"author":"BrianHenryIE","month":"2023-03","story":"Bicycle","stance":"rec","why":"Experiment where handlebars only turn left and riders cannot turn left","quote":"where they fix the steering so the handlebars can only be turned left, and people then aren't able to turn left.","text":"Beautiful article. There's a similar neat video, \"Most People Don't Know How Bikes Work\", where they fix the steering so the handlebars can only be turned left, and people then aren't able to turn left. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35345129,"author":"ubj","month":"2023-03","story":"Bicycle","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what happens when riders are prevented from countersteering","quote":"It shows what happens if you prevent the rider from performing the countersteer before leaning into a turn [1].","text":"Beautifully illustrated and brilliantly explained. Derek Muller (Veritasium) on YouTube has a related video diving into the mechanics of bicycle riding. It shows what happens if you prevent the rider from performing the countersteer before leaning into a turn [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35347656,"author":"flavius29663","month":"2023-03","story":"Bicycle","stance":"rec","why":"Riders blocked from countersteering cannot steer","quote":"Veritasium did a video where they stopped him from counter-steering and couldn't steer anymore.","text":"Veritasium did a video where they stopped him from counter-steering and couldn't steer anymore. [VIDEO] He might be wrong, and just didn't know enough, but he is usually researching his videos very well and I would be surprised for him to be wrong about this."},{"id":31697228,"author":"whycombinetor","month":"2022-06","story":"You need to know what right-half-plane zeros are","stance":"rec","why":"Bike turning left without ever countersteering right","quote":"I like this clip where they show bike steering to the left, without its front wheel ever going to the right side of its original path","text":"Indeed. I like this clip where they show bike steering to the left, without its front wheel ever going to the right side of its original path, by only leaning and no countersteering. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42700054,"author":"nayuki","month":"2025-01","story":"Take the pedals off the bike","stance":"reference","why":"Shows what happens when bike steering is locked","quote":"Derek Muller from Veritasium demonstrated what happens if you lock the steering of a bike","text":"More on the physics of how bikes work, Derek Muller from Veritasium demonstrated what happens if you lock the steering of a bike: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42709008,"author":"Levitating","month":"2025-01","story":"Take the pedals off the bike","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"A bike just naturally steers into its fall.","text":"No that's not true. A bike just naturally steers into its fall. Veritasium made a video about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39776171,"author":"david-gpu","month":"2024-03","story":"Why did we wait so long for the bicycle? (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Bikes self-balance by steering into the lean, no gyroscope needed","quote":"it self-steers in the same direction that it is leaning, which counteracts the fall","text":"The gyroscopic effect is not necessary, and it has been demonstrated both in reality and in simulation [0]. The central principle behind why a bicycle is self balancing while in motion is the fact that it self-steers in the same direction that it is leaning, which counteracts the fall [0][1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35032564,"author":"Abimelex","month":"2023-03","story":"The Counterintuitive Physics of Turning a Bike","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment asking whether you can turn left if steering right is blocked","quote":"related content","text":"related content: Would it even be possible to turn left if steering right would be blocked? \"Most People Don't Know How Bikes Work\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":32835378,"author":"ehnto","month":"2022-09","story":"Countersteering","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's not possible to not countersteer as the bike would lean to the wrong direction","text":"You can't turn a bicycle or motorbike without counter-steering, so these people are just not turning their bikes? It's not possible to not countersteer as the bike would lean to the wrong direction and you'd turn the wrong way (since you'd have been initiating a countersteer, afterall). Are you seeing people just manhandle the bars the wrong way in emergencies? I could believe that. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31698557,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2022-06","story":"You need to know what right-half-plane zeros are","stance":"reference","why":"Explains countersteering many cyclists are unaware of","quote":"Veritasium did a video on this","text":"> The bike counter-steering example clicked instantly with me, but I guess not many people have an intuition about that. I rode for decades before learning this and I think most other cyclists are not aware as well. Veritasium did a video on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30721504,"author":"ben_w","month":"2022-03","story":"Everyday Magic in the Middle Ages (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Most people would be surprised, but I’m happy to assert most of the things we do with an expectation of causal effect are superstitions. While people don’t generally understand bikes [0], in this case superstition would be more about people building them like in any of the [0] illustrations (or e.g. saying “I need suspension/carbon fibre frame” without knowing what those are for) rather than not being aware of how the laws of motion stop it falling over — except for bikes with trick steering, average people aren’t even going to bother asserting a causal mechanism for motion: [VIDEO] [0] [LINK]"},{"id":30496630,"author":"martyvis","month":"2022-02","story":"Self Balancing Arduino Robot","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You balance by steering the bike under the centre of gravity.","text":"Balancing is hardly anything to do with the gyroscopic effect of wheels. You balance by steering the bike under the centre of gravity. Veritasium : [VIDEO]"},{"id":29525745,"author":"kevmo314","month":"2021-12","story":"Learning how to ride a bike","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"you counter steer even without touching the bars.","text":"As someone who can ride aggressively without handlebars, you counter steer even without touching the bars. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29526215,"author":"mr_sturd","month":"2021-12","story":"Learning how to ride a bike","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates countersteering, which riders do without thinking","quote":"I watched a YouTube video[0] demonstrating this recently.","text":"I watched a YouTube video[0] demonstrating this recently. It was definitely surprising to me and is just something that is done without thought. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["xoxhDk-hwuo",1],["a_TSR_v07m0",1]]},"NP9AIUT9nos":{"wy":"HN recommends it for changing minds about whether programmers can be called engineers, and for its take on how waterfall was misread.","q":{"x":"I always thought calling programmers engineers was very weird until I watched this great talk","i":23160396,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23160396,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2020-05","story":"The most prized degree in India became the most worthless","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains what engineers actually do and why programmers qualify.","quote":"I always thought calling programmers engineers was very weird until I watched this great talk","text":"I always thought calling programmers engineers was very weird until I watched this great talk: Real Software Engineering , Glenn Vanderburg, Lone Star Ruby 2010 [VIDEO] which explains what various kinds of engineers actually do. Not at all dry and boring like it might sound! From the YouTube blurb: Software engineering as it's taught in universities simply doesn't work. It doesn't produce software systems of high quality, and it doesn't produce them for low cost. Sometimes, even when practiced rigorously, it doesn't produce systems at all. That's odd, because in every other field, the term \"engineering\" is reserved for methods that work. What then, does real software engineering look like?"},{"id":27161012,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2021-05","story":"Programmers: Stop Calling Yourselves Engineers (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Changed commenter's view on calling programmers engineers.","quote":"until I saw this great talk","text":"I thought calling programmers \"engineers\" was very odd until I saw this great talk: Real Software Engineering by Glenn Vanderburg Lone Star Ruby Conference 2010 [VIDEO] Software engineering as it's taught in universities simply doesn't work. It doesn't produce software systems of high quality, and it doesn't produce them for low cost. Sometimes, even when practiced rigorously, it doesn't produce systems at all. That's odd, because in every other field, the term \"engineering\" is reserved for methods that work. What then, does real software engineering look like? How can we consistently deliver high-quality systems to our customers and employers in a timely fashion and for a reasonable cost? In this session, we'll discuss where software engineering went wrong, and build the case that disciplined Agile methods, far from being \"anti-engineering\" (as they are often described), actually represent the best of engineering principles applied to the task of software development."},{"id":10929790,"author":"gcandal","month":"2016-01","story":"Managing the Development of Large Software Systems (1970) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis of how the waterfall paper was misinterpreted.","quote":"which is a pretty decent analysis on how people misinterpreted the original concept.","text":"I first came into contact with this article via [VIDEO] , which is a pretty decent analysis on how people misinterpreted the original concept."},{"id":5511879,"author":"KaeseEs","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Explores where software engineering went wrong and what real engineering looks like.","quote":"a very good exploration of how some of the common beliefs in the field of software","text":"Real Software Engineering, by Glenn Vandenburg. Not a perfect talk (especially the conclusions IMO), but a very good exploration of how some of the common beliefs in the field of software \"engineering\" came to be, and how something resembling actual engineering practice might be beneficial and practical. Link: [VIDEO] Abstract: \"Software engineering as it's taught in universities simply doesn't work. It doesn't produce software systems of high quality, and it doesn't produce them for low cost. Sometimes, even when practiced rigorously, it doesn't produce systems at all. That's odd, because in every other field, the term \"engineering\" is reserved for methods that work. What then, does real software engineering look like? How can we consistently deliver high-quality systems to our customers and employers in a timely fashion and for a reasonable cost? In this session, we'll discuss where software engineering went wrong, and build the case that disciplined Agile methods, far from being \"anti-engineering\" (as they are often described), actually represent the best of engineering principles applied to the task of software development.\""},{"id":42741594,"author":"nayuki","month":"2025-01","story":"Engineering \"home-cooked\" software","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in context of engineering software discussion.","quote":"","text":"I would say that this blog post is half right. The half that's not right is glaring. > the pyramids have had 100% up-time with no human maintenance It helps that there is hardly any rain in the desert. Water would foul up the structure in a matter of years. > dependencies are added like seasoning. Hundreds of packages. Thousands of foreign lines of code make their way onto your software routinely True, and I think NPM JavaScript exhibits the worst of this behavior. > Problems are expected and fixed on the fly, somewhat haphazardly: ... push a patch ... code scanning ... dependa-bot ... DevOps True, and I remember when software releases were treated with much more care and quality because it costs a lot to ship a CD or game cartridge rather than a download. > More waterfall-y. That's not a good thing. Having a short feedback cycle from implementation back to design is a big win for software development. Waterfall is the dark ages when we didn't know better. > This is where minimalist software is built. Agreed. > No build process That doesn't make any sense, unless you're writing machine code directly in hexadecimal. > no outside dependencies I agree with minimizing dependencies, but there's no such thing as no dependencies. I dare you to avoid any of these libraries: HTTP (especially HTTP/2 and 3 which are much harder than HTTP/1), TLS/SSL, TCP/IP, hardware drivers, compilers, data codecs like DEFLATE, multimedia codecs like AVC and AAC. > There's no need to be constantly refa"},{"id":26761655,"author":"nayuki","month":"2021-04","story":"The Myth of the 'Waterfall' SDLC (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Waterfall was a caricature accidentally turned into reality.","quote":"This talk describes how the waterfall development model was a caricature that got accidentally turned into reality","text":"This talk describes how the waterfall development model was a caricature that got accidentally turned into reality: [VIDEO] \"Lone Star Ruby Conference 2010 Real Software Engineering by Glenn Vanderburg\""},{"id":23669451,"author":"nayuki","month":"2020-06","story":"Are We Engineers?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on the same topic.","quote":"This talks about the same topic","text":"This talks about the same topic: Lone Star Ruby Conference 2010: \"Real Software Engineering\" by Glenn Vanderburg: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1]]},"RQY6WGOoYis":{"wy":"HN points to it as a clear introduction to why widening roads fails and why transit-only lanes matter.","q":{"x":"NotJustBikes has a good introduction to it","i":29799039,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31539652,"author":"rglullis","month":"2022-05","story":"An autonomous car in SF blocked a fire truck responding to an emergency","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why autonomous cars with poor transit make the system worse","quote":"Please, please watch the videos I linked.","text":"Please, please watch the videos I linked. Especially the Not Just Bikes channel. For example, this one [VIDEO] can quickly explain why a transport system flooded with autonomous cars and poor public transit would make the overall system worse for everyone . There is also another video that talks about microcars for people with disability and reduced mobility: [LINK] No rational person can watch those videos and conclude that the best course of action to solve urban transportation requires self-driving cars. Getting FSD cars is just a rich nerd fetish which will not solve anything and likely contribute to make suburban sprawl worse."},{"id":38135952,"author":"ViewTrick1002","month":"2023-11","story":"Jevons Paradox","stance":"rec","why":"More info on reducing congestion via transit","quote":"More info","text":"To reduce congestion you need to speed up and expand the slowest alternative, public transport. That is the only proven method to reduce congestion. The peak demand for car travel is way beyond the infrastructure which is possible to construct and maintain. More info: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29799039,"author":"animal_spirits","month":"2022-01","story":"Motorists have been stranded on a major interstate in Virginia since last night","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to the Downs-Thomson paradox","quote":"NotJustBikes has a good introduction to it","text":"Correct, The Downs-Thomson paradox [1] is a known issue in urban planning stating basically unless you improve public transportation car congestion will continue to get worse. > the equilibrium speed of car traffic on a road network is determined by the average door-to-door speed of equivalent journeys taken by public transport. NotJustBikes has a good introduction to it [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29799074,"author":"animal_spirits","month":"2022-01","story":"Motorists have been stranded on a major interstate in Virginia since last night","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why road widening does not fix congestion","quote":"Would recommend watching this video on it","text":"The less you invest in public transportation, the more people will drive. The more people that drive, the slower traffic gets. If you just widen the road, all you do is increase the amount of cars that drive. If people can't get to where they are going via public transportation, then they are going to drive instead, increasing congestion. Would recommend watching this video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28419865,"author":"quadrangle","month":"2021-09","story":"Amtrak might add more routes, but they won't be faster than a car","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of transit speed and car congestion","quote":"are great overviews","text":"Train fans will always still take the train, and for good reason (they are a better experience in some ways), but the fundamental issue of whether we can address car traffic and congestion is about whether other forms of transit can be faster. [VIDEO] and [LINK] are great overviews"},{"id":26939373,"author":"spladug","month":"2021-04","story":"Study finds ride-sharing intensifies urban road congestion","stance":"reference","why":"Supports transit-only lanes","quote":"This is why transit-only lanes are important.","text":"> The bus will still have to wait on or go around rideshare drivers This is why transit-only lanes are important. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_njf8jwEGRo",1],["rGqiswuJuQI",1],["B9ly7JjqEb0",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["y_SXXTBypIg",1],["7Nw6qyyrTeI",1],["CCOdQsZa15o",1]]},"smStEPSRKBs":{"wy":"Recommended whenever Atari vector games come up as an excellent, deep technical explanation of how they worked.","q":{"x":"There’s an excellent video by Retro Game Mechanics Explained on how they work","i":34834963,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34834963,"author":"Bluecobra","month":"2023-02","story":"Is the Living Computer Museum Dead?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent explanation of how Atari vector games work.","quote":"There’s an excellent video by Retro Game Mechanics Explained on how they work","text":"I love the Atari vector games! There’s an excellent video by Retro Game Mechanics Explained on how they work: [VIDEO] Recently I stumbled on some Tektronix videos on YouTube. Those vector displays are even more mind blowing than Atari’s arcade games. I hope I can get to see one in person someday."},{"id":37208487,"author":"aquova","month":"2023-08","story":"Atari Tempest: Time-Lapse Photography (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Nitty-gritty of how Tempest encoded vector data in RAM and rendered it.","quote":"if you're really interested in how vector effects were achieved, here's another","text":"If anyone is interested further in how vector effects were achieved, there is a good video showing the rendering of frames of Tempest in slow motion [0]. And if you're really interested in how vector effects were achieved, here's another that goes into the nitty gritty of how Tempest encoded point, line, color, etc. data in RAM and then rendered those images [1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32307312,"author":"Bluecobra","month":"2022-08","story":"Raw footage inside Sears with Atari 800 and 2600 (1982) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Well-made explanation of how Atari vector displays work.","quote":"has a very well put together video about this as well","text":"Also the Retro Game Mechanics Explained channel on YouTube has a very well put together video about this as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26757476,"author":"jameshart","month":"2021-04","story":"Vector Games 32c3","stance":"rec","why":"Deep dive into Atari vector display software, with emulation and Tempest frame stepping.","quote":"Retro Game Mechanics on YouTube did an incredible deep dive into how it all worked","text":"This is a great look at the hardware and of vector display - if you want to get a good in-depth understanding of how Atari's original vector graphics display systems worked from the software side, though, Retro Game Mechanics on YouTube did an incredible deep dive into how it all worked, including emulating the vector instruction sets and step-by stepping through complete frame renders from Tempest. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46929427,"author":"msarnoff","month":"2026-02","story":"Unseen Footage of Atari Battlezone Arcade Cabinet Production","stance":"reference","why":"Covers how the Asteroids vector generator worked and was programmed.","quote":"Retro Game Mechanics Explained has a video about how the Asteroids vector generator worked","text":"Retro Game Mechanics Explained has a video about how the Asteroids vector generator worked and was coded for: [VIDEO] Battlezone and later 3D games used different circuitry that generated vectors with analog circuitry and used a “math box” coprocessor based on Am2901 bit slice ALUs to help with the 3D math."},{"id":36738102,"author":"derefr","month":"2023-07","story":"Internet Archive expands Flash support","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"To be clear, I didn't (exactly) mean that the author of a Flash animation has to think in terms of creating a \"demoscene demo.\" I rather meant that the Flash abstract machine is designed to primarily do the types of thing that demoscene demos do — i.e. to use vectors and gradient fills and clipping masks to achieve procedural visual effects. You could say that the SWF abstract machine has a \"demoscene-alike Instruction Set Architecture.\" Most of the work of figuring out how to achieve particular visual effects on that abstract machine, though, is domain-knowledge built into the \"Flash compiler\" of the authoring tool. In my previous post, I emphasized Flash as a distribution format for a reason. While the authoring tools (and the .FLA project format) do work with clean abstractions of a timeline with keyframes and tweens (and objects with their own swappable animations that have their own sub-timelines), the .SWF export format bakes all that down into an imperative program for an abstract machine whose primitive opcodes involve defining and moving around (potentially texture-filled) vector objects on a canvas. Writing SWF bytecode directly, would be similar to programming against a retained-mode fixed-function graphics API like OpenGL — just without any 3D support. In other words, it would be like writing a demoscene demo. Or, to put that another way: the .SWF export format is basically a cousin of PostScript, but with a finishRenderAndWaitForNextFrame command, and the ability"},{"id":27938897,"author":"DerekL","month":"2021-07","story":"Ask HN: Which vintage computers do you find interesting?","stance":"reference","why":"Video inspired the commenter to think about programming Atari Quadrascan machines.","quote":"","text":"After watching a video ( [VIDEO] ), I starting thinking about writing for Atari’s “Quadrascan” vector graphics arcade machines. I wondered if they could play a primitive first-person shooter. You’d need to clip the drawing of partially-occluded objects, either by designing them in some clever way, or by adding clipping support to the hardware. The easiest way to get started would be an emulator."}],"r":[["c2J91UG6Fn8",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["1wAaI_6b9JE",1],["VdqhHKjepiE",1],["DxL2HoqLbyA",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1],["agS6ZXBrcng",1]]},"85ZCTuekjGA":{"wy":"HN recommends the series as a practical introduction to nMigen and formal verification for CPU design.","q":{"x":"I can only recommend these Youtube series, where nMigen is used to build a full 6800 CPU","i":24105805,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":28399904,"author":"krasin","month":"2021-09","story":"Awesome Lattice FPGA Boards","stance":"rec","why":"Good nMigen tutorial by Robert Baruch.","quote":"There is a good nMigen tutorial by Robert Baruch","text":"TinyFPGA BX ([1]) and nmigen/yosys as the fully open source toolchain for generating logic with Python and formally verifying it ([2], [3]). There is a good nMigen tutorial by Robert Baruch ([4]. [5]). Alternatively, if you really want to try Verilog first (not a bad idea), I enjoyed reading a book \"Designing Video Game Hardware in Verilog\" ([6]). 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK] 5. [VIDEO] 6. [LINK]"},{"id":26343331,"author":"MrRadar","month":"2021-03","story":"Lion: A formally verified, 5-stage pipeline RISC-V core","stance":"rec","why":"Series focused on nMigen HDL, building a simpler 6800 core.","quote":"He's got another series that's focused more on the HDL itself building a (relatively much simpler) 6800 CPU core","text":"Robert Baruch on Youtube is building a RISC-V CPU core of comparable complexity to the one in the OP using nMigen which is an HDL based on Python and it looks like a very nice tool to work with relative to other HDLs I've seen (though I've never had to actually use an HDL myself before so I may not be the best judge). Here's a playlist of the video series, with the earlier videos basically being an introduction to nMigen: [LINK] He's got another series that's focused more on the HDL itself building a (relatively much simpler) 6800 CPU core: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24105805,"author":"krasin","month":"2020-08","story":"Glasgow: Scots Army Knife for Electronics","stance":"rec","why":"Series building a full 6800 CPU with nMigen.","quote":"I can only recommend these Youtube series, where nMigen is used to build a full 6800 CPU","text":"nMigen is pretty cool and capable. I can only recommend these Youtube series, where nMigen is used to build a full 6800 CPU: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22079200,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2020-01","story":"Show HN: 1.5kb Microkernel for Ben Eater's 6502 Computer","stance":"rec","why":"Series building a 6800 CPU in nMigen with formal verification.","quote":"I've been enjoying Robert Baruch's videos[1] on creating a FPGA implementation of the 6800, using nMigen and formal verification.","text":"Semi-related, I've been enjoying Robert Baruch's videos[1] on creating a FPGA implementation of the 6800, using nMigen and formal verification. First videos are almost like live coding, then he skips to a more compact format. As a regular programmer, it's quite interesting to get into how the bits actually end up getting the work done. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HUMGc8hEkpc",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["jhicDUgXyNg",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["fRMMSyCcTDI",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["nNMNglk_CvE",1],["r3snVCRo_bI",1]]},"c8iFtaltX-s":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a fascinating, mind-expanding interview on bioelectricity and non-reductionist biology.","q":{"x":"This podcast will be a shocking experience for anyone who still holds reductionist views on life.","i":43526623,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46844578,"author":"jupiterelastica","month":"2026-02","story":"Cells Use 'Bioelectricity' to Coordinate and Make Group Decisions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing examples of bioelectricity research, like frog embryo eyes.","quote":"here is a fascinating interview with Michael Levin","text":"For those interested in more details and quite mind blowing examples, here is a fascinating interview with Michael Levin (one of the researchers mentioned in the article). [VIDEO] Start at 1:19:11, the stuff before is him talking about biology, but from an intelligence perspective. After this time stamp is his retrospective on his bioelectricity research over the years, showing also examples of how they got a frog embryo to produce eyes, and many more things."},{"id":43526623,"author":"resource0x","month":"2025-03","story":"C. Elegans: The worm that no computer scientist can crack","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Michael Levin challenges reductionist views of life.","quote":"This podcast will be a shocking experience for anyone who still holds reductionist views on life.","text":"This podcast will be a shocking experience for anyone who still holds reductionist views on life. [VIDEO] Michael Levin's channel: [LINK] His blog: [LINK]"},{"id":43724146,"author":"hirvi74","month":"2025-04","story":"What my stroke taught me (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Discussion suggesting consciousness is a full-body phenomenon.","quote":"I found it to be a fascinating discussion if you have the time","text":"> A small part of the brain that gives consciousness. Not to get too tangental, but it was common thought at one time to think the brain is the center of consciousness. However, I have come across new data [1] that has convinced me that consciousness is entirely a full-body experience. According to the definition of some, consciousness might not even be a binary state, but rather a property of our universe like heat. [1] Found this video on this site, and I found it to be a fascinating discussion if you have the time: [VIDEO]"}]},"2THVvshvq0Q":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a short, well-produced and eye-opening piece of film analysis.","q":{"x":"short, easily digestible, and well-produced YouTube videos about this stuff that are eye-opening.","i":8985322,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":8985322,"author":"wmeredith","month":"2015-02","story":"New Jurassic World Trailer Hides Improved CGI in Plain Sight","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Analysis of Michael Bay's style and filmmaking.","quote":"short, easily digestible, and well-produced YouTube videos about this stuff that are eye-opening.","text":"Everything on screen matters. Modern Cinema is a HIGHLY evolved art form. If you have any interest in this at all, YouTube user Every Frame A Painting publishes, short, easily digestible, and well-produced YouTube videos about this stuff that are eye-opening. Here are a few of my favorites: Edgar Wright - How to Do Visual Comedy: [LINK] The Silence of the Lambs - Who Wins the Scene? [LINK] Michael Bay - What is Bayhem? [VIDEO]"},{"id":43906367,"author":"ripe","month":"2025-05","story":"Sneakers (1992) – 4K makeover sourced from the original camera negative","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how Bay's movies are poorly made.","quote":"An excellent episode of Every Frame A Painting is","text":"> Some of the recent Michael Bay movies are so aggressive when it comes to cuts An excellent episode of Every Frame A Painting is \"Michael Bay --- What is Bayhem?\" It explains in detail in what way those particular movies are poorly made. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14666742,"author":"skepticaldrunk","month":"2017-06","story":"David Goyer and Josh Friedman Adapting Isaac Asimov's Foundation for TV","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting analysis of Michael Bay's filmmaking.","quote":"you should check this out when you have time, pretty interesting analysis","text":"That's fair, but being able to understand and direct the popular zeitgeist, be it by instinct or intellect, is a very formidable gift indeed. It's something almost none of us can do (certainly myself included), and those who can will always have detractors. I don't see success as a zero sum game, so I'm happy for people who are successful, even if I wish I had their insight. Total aside, you should check this out when you have time, pretty interesting analysis: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15980433,"author":"Balgair","month":"2017-12","story":"Racing for Realism","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Bay's color choices and why he uses them.","quote":"Every Frame a Painting goes over Michael Bay's work and includes some color content he uses and WHY","text":"I mean, complaining about the color realism of a film with gigantic monster dinosaurs (whose coloration we may never know to begin with) seems a bit silly. Movies are not documentaries. I'd buy in a bit more for things like the BBC's 'Planet Earth' needing color realism, but even then it's ok to dramatize things. Every Frame a Painting goes over Michael Bay's work and includes some color content he uses and WHY: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15634671,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-11","story":"“One Damn Slide After Another”: PowerPoint at Every Occasion for Speech (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think the main issue with Prezi-style presentations is that it demands too much skill to use properly. A better interface with sensible defaults and much less emphasis on wow-factor would help a lot here. Normal powerpoint \"only\" requires a basic awareness of composition and narrative coherence, which already is too much to ask in practice. To make good Prezi presentation you need to have the skills of a cinematographer/editor. Specifically, the skill of continuity editing[0] so your audience can follow what is going on. What we want is the calm, easy to read compositions of, say, Yasujirō Ozu or Wes Anderson, and Akira Kurasowa's sense of movement[1][2]. Instead everyone uses it in a way that it feels more like the worst aspects of Bayhem[3]. I still believe Prezi-like presentation tools have the potential to be good, because they could be used to map relationships in your narrative to spatial relationships. But that would require a) that you are aware of those relationships yourself and b) that you posses the skills to translate that into spatial compositions that are coherent. This is obviously an unrealistic demand, so instead the tools should assist you with that. It needs is an interface that helps users structure their story, with sensible default conversions. Maybe something like Treesheets[4], where you can create a tree-based hierarchy in your topics, and then a conversion of that to a spatial hierarchy that is easy to follow, and not a distraction from the actual"}],"r":[["3FOzD4Sfgag",1],["l_F9jxsfGCw",1]]},"AxCo2bE9Gtk":{"wy":"HN people point to it as a starting place for understanding how money corrupts American government.","q":{"x":"Lawrence Lessig has some great talks about this topic, here's one of them","i":32166760,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":3387600,"author":"alexqgb","month":"2011-12","story":"The Only Way to Stop SOPA","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lessig's argument on money in politics as a root problem.","quote":"A good place to start is here","text":"I strongly encourage you to develop a (much) better understanding of what Lessig has been arguing. A good place to start is here: [VIDEO] Also worth noting: the problem you describe (corrupting influences on the economy originating in Washington, as opposed to private interests capturing Washington) is something Lessig addressed in his recent appearance on Jon Stewart's program. There, he pointed out that these apparently divergent forms of corruption are entirely compatible, and that they actually share the same root, which is the obscene reliance of elected officials on a vanishingly small number of campaign donors. Depending on the day of the week, these donors can find themselves initiating bribes, or being shaken down for bribes by officials wanting to increase their takes. The stink flows both ways. And it ensnares people who, left to their own devices, would have nothing to do with 'the trade' whatsoever. Accordingly, Lessig is illuminating the entire economy of corruption, correctly observing that you cannot choose to attack one branch instead of another. Instead, you have to ignore all the branches, and go straight for the root. If any good comes from SOPA, it will be in generating a backlash strong enough to go after the root directly. The nerds, for what it's worth, are the people to lead this. Why? Because we are, by nature, systems thinkers. And this is a systemic problem of the very severest degree."},{"id":32166760,"author":"dbrgn","month":"2022-07","story":"EU Digital Markets Act, aimed at Google, Apple, Amazon, approved","stance":"rec","why":"Lessig talk on institutional corruption in politics.","quote":"Lawrence Lessig has some great talks about this topic, here's one of them","text":"Because of institutional corruption. Lawrence Lessig has some great talks about this topic, here's one of them: [LINK] Here's another one: [VIDEO] (Lawrence Lessig, a law professor, was one of the founders of Creative Commons. He later tried to change US copyright laws to be more consumer-friendly, but concluded that it's almost impossible to do so as long as corporate interests rule politics. He then shifted his research topics to institutional corruption in politics. He actually ran for presidency a few years ago, with funding only from the popuplation, and limited to a certain amount. It failed, he wasn't even invited to TV debates because \"if he doesn't take company money he doesn't have a chance\".)"},{"id":5846112,"author":"lifeguard","month":"2013-06","story":"Ask HN: US this bad about civil liberties?","stance":"rec","why":"Lessig discussion on how money corrupts American government.","quote":"I think this is a good discussion by Prof Lessig, \"Republic Lost","text":"Dear Internet, America has become a very strange place. At the same time our military was \"bringing freedom to the Iraqi peoples\", our legislature and executive conspired to remove or automate much judicial review of spying. In effect American citizens lost another type of liberty. We seem to be better at protecting our access to riffles. Most of the liberties created by America's founding fathers (women were not allowed to vote) were designed to thwart the power of a monarchy and the power of the Roman-Catholic church. Many of these founding fathers were members of a fraternity called the Free Masons and read authors like John Locke. My point is they were a fairly homogeneous group of Englishmen. Since then America has expanded civil rights explicit in the Constitution to include people of all races and genders. But the Industrial age has been a constant force of erosion of our liberties. Our elections are not publicly funded. This means there is a huge advantage to material wealth and the wealthy have steered Congress to benefit themselves. The bright shinning star of hope for The Land of The Free is our government is not systemically corrupt, at least not in the traditional meaning of corrupt. I think this is a good discussion by Prof Lessig, \"Republic Lost\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vu-h5y6tK34",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["k2h2lvhzMDc",1]]},"ReRcHdeUG9Y":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a quick way to get the key concepts of the book and as an introduction to leadership thinking.","q":{"x":"watching talks by the authors, which give you the key concepts from the book in an hour","i":7938093,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":10830233,"author":"ivoras","month":"2016-01","story":"The Refragmentation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how biological signals can be gamed for short-term benefit.","quote":"this lecture was an eye opener for me","text":"Simply as a reference to how biology supports the human society and how biological signals can be gamed for short term benefit, this lecture was an eye opener for me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16009364,"author":"cbcoutinho","month":"2017-12","story":"Ask HN: Any YouTube channel/video on leadership?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting Sinek talk on leadership.","quote":"I think the talks by Simon Sinek are pretty interesting, and he has a lot to say on leadership","text":"I think the talks by Simon Sinek are pretty interesting, and he has a lot to say on leadership [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7938093,"author":"anarchitect","month":"2014-06","story":"Ask HN: Dev promoted to more strategic role in startup. What literature?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk gives the key concepts of the book in an hour.","quote":"watching talks by the authors, which give you the key concepts from the book in an hour","text":"Sure. There are plenty of summaries available on Amazon, for instance, this summary of Good to Great ( [LINK] ). There are also some really great mini books like Scrum: a Breathtakingly Brief and Agile Introduction ( [LINK] ) Also, I haven't tried these yet, but there are a number of subscription services available for summaries like [LINK] and [LINK] The other way of cheating is by watching talks by the authors, which give you the key concepts from the book in an hour. For example Simon Sinek presenting Why Leaders Eat Last ( [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["oM6S7FEUfkU",1]]},"SfsCniN7Nsc":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an entertaining but well-sourced explainer on US zoning and housing costs whenever those topics come up.","q":{"x":"A funny, yet very well researched video on that exact same topic of Zoning nonsense. Entertaining AND informative.","i":31019481,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31019481,"author":"kelvin0","month":"2022-04","story":"California's Vanished Dream, by the Numbers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny, well-researched video on zoning problems.","quote":"A funny, yet very well researched video on that exact same topic of Zoning nonsense. Entertaining AND informative.","text":"A funny, yet very well researched video on that exact same topic of Zoning nonsense. Entertaining AND informative. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45197059,"author":"fayten","month":"2025-09","story":"Pontevedra, Spain declares its entire urban area a \"reduced traffic zone\"","stance":"rec","why":"Well-done video essay on infrastructure strain and sprawl.","quote":"Climate town videos are all well researched and provide an enormous amount of follow-up content from their sources.","text":"This depends on how you define ROI. Car infrastructure and lack of density reduces tax revenue for cities and strains infrastructure. There are other human benefits to reducing car traffic and use in favor of public transportation: * Reduces air pollution * Noise pollution * Allows a focus on human centric urban planning * Allows for higher density commercial and residential increasing tax revenue * Reduces pedestrian traffic injury Well done video essays: Parking minimums [LINK] Noise pollution [LINK] Infrastructure strain [LINK] More on Infrastructure and Sprawl [VIDEO] Articles: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Climate town videos are all well researched and provide an enormous amount of follow-up content from their sources. Generally, I care about all of the above and I perceive investments in public transportation to have a higher ROI. Some extra historical context is helpful too: [LINK] The book The Color of Law by Rothstein is worth a read. In the end there is a balance between public transportation and car dependency and right now the scales are leaning too much in favor of cars."},{"id":30855635,"author":"fayten","month":"2022-03","story":"Americans do not want to return to urban living","stance":"rec","why":"Snarky but fun explainer on US zoning, with sources in the description.","quote":"These are pretty snarky, but fun videos and the authors list their sources.","text":"There are quite a few factors, I'd encourage checking out the following videos by Not Just Bikes and Climate Towns that cover a broad range of city planning and zoning in the US. Strong Towns Play List by Not Just Bikes (This heavily covers city planning issues in the US): [LINK] The Suburbs Are Bleeding America Dry by Climate Towns (This covers zoning): [VIDEO] How The Auto Industry Carjacked The American Dream (This covers the auto industries impact on US cities.): [LINK] Dutch Cities are Better for the Climate (and my sanity) by Not Just Bikes (Not super relevant to zoning, but it's part 1 of The Suburbs Are Bleeding America Dry so I figured I'd link it.): [LINK] These are pretty snarky, but fun videos and the authors list their sources. Their video descriptions are worth checking out just to find a ton of reading material on each topic."},{"id":40381720,"author":"k99x55","month":"2024-05","story":"Measuring the impact of zoning on housing in a city","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Lively & Liveable Neighbourhoods that are Illegal in Most of North America : [LINK] The Suburbs Are Bleeding America Dry | Climate Town (feat. Not Just Bikes) : [VIDEO]"},{"id":38117461,"author":"CapstanRoller","month":"2023-11","story":"The US Housing Market Has Become an Impossible Mess","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is due to zoning laws. Many people desperately want smaller housing, but it's effectively illegal everywhere in the USA. The only alternative is to buy rural land and try to get creative with the law (e.g. your tiny home on an existing rusted old trailer frame is considered a \"remodel\", etc) or simply build a small cabin and hope no one from the government comes to bulldoze it. Even success here means you're stuck far away from jobs, infrastructure, etc. Existing small houses are priced as high (or higher) than new-build McMansions. See also: \"missing middle\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":34282373,"author":"__MatrixMan__","month":"2023-01","story":"Rising rent, not poverty, is the real driver of homelessness","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"(more on this: )","text":"It's an issue with the zoning laws. Back when it became illegal to ban black people from your neighborhood the next best thing was to ban the kind of housing that black people could afford. So the only place where it's legal to put the kind of housing that addresses the problem is in the city centers which were already established and therefore out of scope for the zoning restrictions. (more on this: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":33796493,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2022-11","story":"U.S. government to backstop mortgages above $1M for the first time","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"If AirBnb truly made the market prices go up, then beyond short term shocks it's more of city hall's (and NIMBYism) fault that new supply couldn't come to market (such as rezoning) . America has a massive zoning issue that is causing all kinds of pain and suffering to lower middle class and below. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":31237044,"author":"tenacious_tuna","month":"2022-05","story":"Historic fertilizer crunch threatens food security","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm hopping on a plane in a minute, but I'll do my best to at least start an example :) The real question is \"what do you mean by 'it works fine'?\" To me, the effects of suburban living are such that we don't treat people as people unless they're shrouded in a big rolling box of metal. Of course it'll vary from person to person and family to family. I think urban environments offer children much more freedom than corresponding suburban environments, while being safer. There's much more mobility and access to things outside of the house, which I'd argue significantly enriches childhood experience and leads to more adaptable adults. As an adult, I'm overjoyed by the ease with which I can get to things around my city. I never have to deal with traffic, I can walk to three different grocery stores in under fifteen minutes. I have dozens of restaurants in similar distance, and I never worry about having too much to drink to safely drive home. I don't have to deal with traffic, and when friends come to visit (or when I visit them) I can take the train and enjoy a book instead of navigating roads. I feel far healthier and happier with the amount of light physical activity that's been baked into my life just by going about my normal routine. Before we moved to the city, I lived a pretty typical office worker sedentary life: I'd get up, shower, drive (sitting) to work, work (sitting, sometimes standing), then drive home. Now I walk to work and feel invigorated as I come through the do"}],"r":[["aXQ2lO3ieBA",1],["cO6txCZpbsQ",1],["oOttvpjJvAo",1],["OUNXFHpUhu8",1]]},"djB9oK6pkbA":{"wy":"HN recommends the channel and this video as an explanation of how machine tools enabled modern manufacturing.","q":{"x":"Also Machine Thinking's videos \"The 1751 Machine that Made Everything","i":36757930,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":27014744,"author":"zeotroph","month":"2021-05","story":"Machine Thinking on the Printing Press: The Screws of Disruption [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Machine Thinking video on the 1751 machine","quote":"Also much recommended from that Chanel are the videos on","text":"How the screw of the wine press was the central part of Gensfleisch/Gutenbergs invention which allowed all the other previously existing ones to finally come together (though I also read that certain advances in metallurgy were also essential). Also see the previous video on screws, a much underappreciated invention. It then describes how the availability of books made propagation of existing technology and newer developments possible and faster. The only other comparable moments in history are probably the evolution of spoken language, the invention of writing and most recently the internet. Also much recommended from that Chanel are the videos on the \"Origins of Precision\"[1], and \"The 1751 Machine that Made Everything\"[2]. 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36757930,"author":"roelschroeven","month":"2023-07","story":"Backyard Metalcasting","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the 1751 machine that enabled modern manufacturing","quote":"Also Machine Thinking's videos \"The 1751 Machine that Made Everything","text":"Also Machine Thinking's videos \"The 1751 Machine that Made Everything\" ( [VIDEO] ), \"Origins of Precision\" ( [LINK] ) and some others on his channel."},{"id":22214440,"author":"zeotroph","month":"2020-02","story":"The Museum of Arts and Crafts in Paris is a trove of cunning inventions","stance":"rec","why":"Machine Thinking covers early industrial machines and Paris museum","quote":"has a video about this[1][2] and related topics.","text":"If you can't visit but are interested in the topic and the beginning of the industrial revolution in general, the YouTube channel \"Machine Thinking\" has a video about this[1][2] and related topics. E.g. in another video he explains how to get a perfectly plan surface from scratch: Rubbing two somewhat flat surfaces together until they are smooth enough does not work, you might end up with a subtly concave/convex combination. But do that with three surfaces and the result can only be perfectly flat, though it will take quite some time. [1]: [LINK] - Paris' Temple To Science - Part I [2]: [VIDEO] - The 1751 Machine that Made Everything"},{"id":36759378,"author":"a2tech","month":"2023-07","story":"Backyard Metalcasting","stance":"reference","why":"Argues the metal lathe drove rapid technological advancement","quote":"There's an argument to be made that the rapid advancement of human technology is down to the creation of the metal lathe","text":"There's an argument to be made that the rapid advancement of human technology is down to the creation of the metal lathe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32371764,"author":"krallja","month":"2022-08","story":"Using A Very Old Machine","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"“The 1751 Machine that Made Everything”? [VIDEO] Of course, when I first saw that, I thought it was a Commodore that I’d never heard of before."}],"r":[["gNRnrn5DE58",2]]},"zmCTmgavkrQ":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a well-argued rebuttal of Dark Forest axioms, and recommend his wider Fermi Paradox series.","q":{"x":"His entire library on the Fermi Paradox is worth watching.","i":40398738,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40398738,"author":"cletus","month":"2024-05","story":"Seven Dyson Sphere Candidates","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thorough treatment of why hiding a civilization is unlikely","quote":"His entire library on the Fermi Paradox is worth watching.","text":"A lot of thought has gone into this and other possible explanations. Isaac Arthur, of course, has an excellent video [1] on this issue. His entire library on the Fermi Paradox is worth watching. The short version of why this seems unlikely is that there really is no hiding a K2 civilization for many reasons. For example, access to this much energy and having a megastructure as large as the Solar System (give or take) would allow you to create incredibly high resolution telescopes (with an without interferometry). But consider this: if you, as a spacefaring civilization, want to be left alone, the best way to do it is to make sure nobody comes into your neighbourhood. If you \"hide\" that may happen accidentally. Isn't it better to advertise your presence and otherwise keep people away to avoid unintentional conflict? [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19973933,"author":"simion314","month":"2019-05","story":"The Dark Forest Theory of the Internet","stance":"rec","why":"Critiques the axioms behind the Dark Forest conclusion","quote":"made some good points, mainly finding problems with the axioms","text":"I also had a similar feeling after finishing the book, this video [VIDEO] made some good points, mainly finding problems with the axioms so it convinced me that the conclusion in the book is probably wrong since the axioms are probably not true."},{"id":19224688,"author":"simion314","month":"2019-02","story":"Hayabusa-2: Japan Spacecraft Touches Down on Asteroid","stance":"rec","why":"More optimistic take challenging Dark Forest axioms","quote":"it challenges with good arguments the axioms in The Dark Forest.","text":"This video gives a more optimistic perspective, [VIDEO] it challenges with good arguments the axioms in The Dark Forest."},{"id":25849999,"author":"yoz-y","month":"2021-01","story":"Have we already been visited by aliens?","stance":"reference","why":"Rebuttal of Dark Forest theory","quote":"the Dark Forest Theory doesn't hold agains much scrutiny","text":"The books and all are nice but the Dark Forest Theory doesn't hold agains much scrutiny. Among other reasons because if that were true we would be already dead. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["Pld8wTa16Jk",1],["Qam5BkXIEhQ",1],["O5BJVO3PDeQ",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["rDPj5zI66LA",1],["E3URhJx0NSw",1],["ON0A1dsQOV0",1]]},"3Gmex_4hreQ":{"wy":"HN keeps linking it as the shortest clear explanation of over-compression and brickwalled mastering.","q":{"x":"this video which gave a very succinct answer to my question","i":11756191,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11756191,"author":"morsch","month":"2016-05","story":"The Dark Art of Mastering Music","stance":"rec","why":"Succinct answer on what over-compression loses.","quote":"this video which gave a very succinct answer to my question","text":"Your post led me down the rabbit hole of over-compression, not for the first time, and I ended up at this video which gave a very succinct answer to my question of: \"Okay, I realize that music is getting compressed, but what are we losing?\" [VIDEO] Via a nice article with analysis: [LINK]"},{"id":9399518,"author":"visakanv","month":"2015-04","story":"Why Are TV Ads So Danged Loud?","stance":"rec","why":"Short 2-minute explanation of excessive compression.","quote":"See: The Loudness War (just 1:54 mins long)","text":"The same reason why music on the radio is so dang loud– excessive compression. See: The Loudness War (just 1:54 mins long): [VIDEO]"},{"id":586314,"author":"9oliYQjP","month":"2009-04","story":"In a digital age, vinyl albums are making a comeback","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how loudness mastering degraded CD sound.","quote":"There's a YouTube video floating around called The Loudness War","text":"I think one of the reasons that those people who prefer vinyl over digital do so, is that the recordings on vinyl are mastered with different qualities in mind than their digital counterparts. There's a YouTube video floating around called The Loudness War ( [VIDEO] ) that describes what I'm talking about. I distinctly remember albums on audio CDs sounding better to me (great clarity, vibrancy, etc.) back in the early 1990s. Then, somewhere toward the end of the 1990s, CDs just started to sound worse. I blame the trend described in that video. I suspect that vinyl recordings do not suffer from this same tendency toward loudness in the audio mastering process, and that is why you have audiophiles insisting that vinyl sounds better than digital."},{"id":45888251,"author":"chiph","month":"2025-11","story":"The 'Toy Story' You Remember","stance":"reference","why":"Example of over-compressed music lacking dynamic range.","quote":"","text":"And now we have the Loudness War where the songs are so highly compressed that there is no dynamic range. Because of this, I have to reduce the volume so it isn't painful to listen to. And this makes what should have been a live recording with interesting sound into background noise. Example: [VIDEO] If you want a recent-ish album to listen to that has good sound, try Daft Punk's Random Access Memories (which won the Best Engineered Album Grammy award in 2014). Or anything engineered by Alan Parsons (he's in this list many times) [LINK]"},{"id":33534183,"author":"Arjuna","month":"2022-11","story":"My full statement regarding DOOM Eternal","stance":"reference","why":"Explains brickwalling in music mastering.","quote":"","text":"”The same thoughtless disregard for basic music fundamentals that plagued the preliminary edits ended up on the final OST.” As a musician and music-lover, seeing how they brickwalled [1][2] Mick’s tracks in the screenshots only serves to add to the heartbreak of his entire saga. [1] Loudness War [LINK] [2] The Loudness War [VIDEO]"},{"id":33127385,"author":"chiph","month":"2022-10","story":"The CD’s Rise in the Music Business: An Oral History","stance":"reference","why":"Audio sample demonstrating loudness war compression.","quote":"And here's an audio sample demonstrating what it sounds like","text":"I will still occasionally buy CDs (I bought my first player in 1984 - the Sony CDP-101), but I'm a lot more critical about what I buy now. For the past 10-ish years, the studios have been eliminating dynamic range and cranking the knob on their compression software to \"11\". As a result, music has become painful to listen to. The name for this is the Loudness War, and with the exception of titles like Daft Punk's Random Access Memories , we are losing. [LINK] And here's an audio sample demonstrating what it sounds like: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16352381,"author":"vortico","month":"2018-02","story":"I Am Worried About My Ears","stance":"reference","why":"Example comparing loudness-matched tracks with different compression.","quote":"The first has less DRC and is more damaging to your hearing.","text":">In recent decades, mastering engineers have been tasked with making studio albums louder via the application of something called dynamic range compression; essentially, the volume gets turned up on the quiet bits, and turned down on the loud bits. The cumulative effect is a song that sounds bigger and punchier The writer doesn't seem to understand loudness here. This is exactly the opposite effect of DRC (dynamic range compression). Music gets less punchier and sounds smaller, as transients are pushed down in the mix. Listening to music with less DRC is more damaging to your ears (although it sounds better), if you are comparing two tracks with equal loudness. Keeping loudness constant in this comparison is valid because we will adjust the volume knob based on the track's psychoacoustic loudness. Example: [VIDEO] and [VIDEO] have roughly the same loudness. The first has less DRC and is more damaging to your hearing. This misunderstanding breaks down half of the article's argument. The other half---that loud music is cooler than it was decades ago---is probably valid I guess."},{"id":2116143,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2011-01","story":"The Leave","stance":"reference","why":"Example of excessive dynamic compression in modern recordings.","quote":"What is really awful is excessive dynamic compression in modern CDs themselves","text":"I'm a bit of an audio geek. What sets me off though is not digital compression (although I'm sure you lose some fidelity, I rarely/never notice any loss with 256kbps mp4s). What is really awful is excessive dynamic compression in modern CDs themselves - and it's far worse than digital compression. Music simply loses its punch. It can be hard to do an apples to apples comparison here, but if you compare an older album (Beatles albums come to mind) and a \"remastered\" version, one of the differences will typically be greater dynamic compression in the remaster. Some resources on the topic: [LINK] Some examples: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["BHIo6qwJarI",1],["OKY6BGcx37k",1]]},"7pSqk-XV2QM":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up as a good related watch in any thread about fallout, nuclear tests or detecting hidden events through indirect evidence.","q":{"x":"Veritasium did an eye opening vid on how fallout blew across the country and was ruining Kodak's film on the East Coast.","i":46044825,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46044825,"author":"ourmandave","month":"2025-11","story":"How the Atomic Tests Looked Like from Los Angeles","stance":"rec","why":"Fallout across the country ruined Kodak film, and it was kept quiet.","quote":"Veritasium did an eye opening vid on how fallout blew across the country and was ruining Kodak's film on the East Coast.","text":"At least LA wasn't down wind. Veritasium did an eye opening vid on how fallout blew across the country and was ruining Kodak's film on the East Coast. And they stayed quiet it about it. How Kodak Exposed Nuclear Testing [VIDEO]"},{"id":29763908,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2022-01","story":"Nuclear fallout is showing up in U.S. honey, decades after bomb tests","stance":"rec","why":"Related story of Kodak and atomic bomb fallout.","quote":"Veritasium has a cool video about a related topic","text":"Veritasium has a cool video about a related topic titled \"How Kodak exposed the atomic bomb\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":28413977,"author":"jcims","month":"2021-09","story":"Why the U.S. once set off a nuclear bomb in space","stance":"rec","why":"How Kodak detected nuclear weapons tests.","quote":"Pretty good video from Veritasium o related to this, about how Kodak detected the presence of nuclear weapons tests.","text":"Pretty good video from Veritasium o related to this, about how Kodak detected the presence of nuclear weapons tests. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27982712,"author":"sss111","month":"2021-07","story":"Bomb Pulse","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how Kodak film fogging revealed atomic tests.","quote":"Reminds me of when Kodak was able to detect atomic bomb testing in the US because of fogging on their X-ray films.","text":"It's interesting how powerful these bombs can be. Reminds me of when Kodak was able to detect atomic bomb testing in the US because of fogging on their X-ray films. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["DXYje2B04xE",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1],["4Wrc4fHSCpw",1],["SC2eSujzrUY",1]]},"LwicN2u6Dro":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it in Smalltalk and Unix threads as a demonstration of runtime dynamism hiding in native binaries and shared libraries.","q":{"x":"there's a great video of one of his talks","i":23879540,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":34215023,"author":"johndoe0815","month":"2023-01","story":"Smalltalk-80 on Raspberry Pi: A Bare Metal Implementation","stance":"rec","why":"Kell's research on the Smalltalk lurking in Unix.","quote":"I can recommend Stephen Kell's publications on this topic","text":"I can recommend Stephen Kell's publications on this topic, e.g. \"Unix, Plan 9 and the Lurking Smalltalk\": [LINK] Video: [VIDEO] More on Stephen's research can be found at [LINK]"},{"id":23879540,"author":"johndoe0815","month":"2020-07","story":"Bare metal Smalltalk-80 port to the Raspberry Pi","stance":"rec","why":"Kell shows altering program behavior dynamically via shared library mechanisms.","quote":"there's a great video of one of his talks","text":"Thanks for the link - that looks really interesting! I will have to give it a try to make some informed statement, but I think it's definitely worth looking at. Some parts of Ruby also seem to be inspired by Smalltalk, so that might be a good fit. There's also a Smalltalk-80 VM by Rochus Keller based on the Lua JIT compiler [8] (and a very nice Qt-based Smalltalk browser), that might also go in the same direction. In general, I think there are two major interesting aspects in systems like the Ruby system browser and Smalltalk. On the one hand one can discover the system - you can dig into the implementation without using a disassembler or reverse engineering - and on the other hand it is possible to change the system behavior at runtime (though this is, of course, dangerous). I would love to see at least the discoverability aspect to be more pervasive in standard operating systems. There are already partial approaches to this, such as DTrace/systemtap and the proc filesystem, but having discoverability as a first class concept - which might fit well to Plan 9's (and to some extent Unix's) \"everything is a file\" approach - would be nice. The ability to change the system at runtime is also fascinating. I already mentioned the research of Stephen Kell in another comment [1] and there's a great video of one of his talks [2] in which he shows his approach to dynamically alter a program's behavior on Unix using shared library mechanisms (you can find the code on github [3]). I work"},{"id":11186133,"author":"ElijahLynn","month":"2016-02","story":"Gdb: Debugging with the natives","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by Stephen Kell, giving a face and voice to the article author.","quote":"5 minutes in and the talk is pretty good","text":"Lately I have been very interested in putting faces and voices to words. After reading this article, which was over my head but I got a little out of it. I ended up reading Stephen Kell's homepage, which is crazy intense as far as academics go. He had a link to a video of his and I appreciated the quality and now have a face and voice to attach to the article, therefore will probably remember it better. tldr; Here is a video of the same guy who wrote this article ==> [VIDEO] p.s. 5 minutes in and the talk is pretty good."},{"id":35173055,"author":"carapace","month":"2023-03","story":"Smalltalk: An Entrepreneur’s Secret Weapon (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Introduces liballocs, exposing dynamism in Unix processes.","quote":"","text":"\"Liberating the Smalltalk lurking in C and Unix\" by Stephen Kell, Strange Loop Conference, [VIDEO] > This talk introduces liballocs, an infrastructure which exposes the dynamism hiding in the arcane linking and debugging infrastructure of a Unix process, along with a small extension to C toolchains that enables fast dynamic access to data created by statically compiled code. Together they can be said to unleash a \"hidden Smalltalk\" inside the C and Unix model of programs and processes. Come prepared for a journey that takes your perceptions of the boundaries between dynamic and static languages and turns them on its head."},{"id":23524856,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2020-06","story":"Design Principles Behind Smalltalk (1981)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on the Smalltalk-like dynamism in Unix.","quote":"","text":"> Smalltalk can be described concisely as a graphical UNIX Or Unix as a closet Smalltalk... \"Liberating the Smalltalk lurking in C and Unix\" by Stephen Kell [VIDEO]"}]},"M_VuXVErD6E":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a thorough, general walkthrough of the steps in designing your own keyboard.","q":{"x":"It's targeting a split keyboard, but I think it details the general steps involved pretty well.","i":32173658,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34944257,"author":"hyperturtle","month":"2023-02","story":"Making a Keyboard Layout","stance":"rec","why":"Videos on building a keyboard to match your hands","quote":"You could go one step further and build your own keyboard to match your hands as well.","text":"You could go one step further and build your own keyboard to match your hands as well. I've watched several of these videos and planning on eventually building one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32624925,"author":"koziserek","month":"2022-08","story":"The obsessive pleasures of mechanical-keyboard tinkerers","stance":"rec","why":"Channel on building custom 36-key keyboards","quote":"Watch something from a channel of this hugely underrated gent","text":"In the realm of programmable keyboards, it doesn’t really matter which OS you will use it with… My golden medium is Anne Pro II, even though I own new model F. But more and more I’m getting drawn to an idea of building 36 keys custom. Watch something from a channel of this hugely underrated gent: [VIDEO] Edit: typos (ipad screen kbd…)"},{"id":32173658,"author":"quigley61","month":"2022-07","story":"Build Your Own X","stance":"rec","why":"Details general steps of designing a split keyboard","quote":"It's targeting a split keyboard, but I think it details the general steps involved pretty well.","text":"> Build your own keyboard This is something I've been interested in lately as well & came across this video: [VIDEO] It's targeting a split keyboard, but I think it details the general steps involved pretty well."}]},"a9b9IYqsb_U":{"wy":"Brought up in threads on elevators and locks as an engaging talk on common keys.","q":{"x":"I have to also mention another video of theirs","i":27489331,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27489331,"author":"wolrah","month":"2021-06","story":"Elevator Saga – An elevator programming game","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses fire keys and other common keys","quote":"Relevant video discussing fire keys and other common keys","text":"Relevant video discussing fire keys and other common keys: [VIDEO] And since this thread is about elevators, I have to also mention another video of theirs: [LINK]"},{"id":18515096,"author":"IIAOPSW","month":"2018-11","story":"An elevator in a Chicago skyscraper fell 84 floors, requiring a dramatic rescue","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on elevator keys by a physical security pentester","quote":"I also recommend \"This key is your key, this key is my key","text":"I've watched it twice before. I also recommend \"This key is your key, this key is my key\" [VIDEO] Deviant is a pentester focused on physical sec. Not just elevators."},{"id":15198605,"author":"lathiat","month":"2017-09","story":"Evan Doorbell's Classic Phreaking Tapes","stance":"rec","why":"Related talk on common keys opening elevator and building access","quote":"Also good","text":"I realise the thread is phone phreaking related and that was kind of the point of your post; but in the spirit of the talk below to think more outside the box... There's a pretty good chance in many cases you can just open the external box with some easy to get (and relatively standard) key. And hit a button. Related: [LINK] This is a good related talk - goes outside the box on all the ways you can often get into commercial buildings without picking (or even in many cases, interacting with) the lock \"The Search for the Perfect Door\" by Deviant Ollam [LINK] Also good: [VIDEO] [Howard Payne & Deviant Ollam - This Key is Your Key, This Key is My Key] [LINK] [DEF CON 22 - Deviant Ollam & Howard Payne - Elevator Hacking - From the Pit to the Penthouse]"},{"id":20297456,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2019-06","story":"Tourist's lucky guess cracks safe code on first try","stance":"reference","why":"Related to default key bitting in physical locks","quote":"","text":"The moral equivalent of \"admin,password\" happens all the time with physical locks. With physical goods, the key is often sold with a default bitting. (Which is the data embodied in the key equivalent to the password.) That's because just ordering the default is often cheaper, and the contractor will often just opt for the cheapest option. It wouldn't surprise me if this also happened with combination locks. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ZUvGfuLlZus",2],["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1],["eECjjLNAOd4",1],["-5wpm-gesOY",1]]},"bL6hp8BKB24":{"wy":"Used as a counterpoint whenever commenters claim CGI always looks fake.","q":{"x":"This video from a few years ago is a great counterpoint","i":30180546,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30180546,"author":"vlunkr","month":"2022-02","story":"Over-reliance on CGI in movies","stance":"rec","why":"Argues we only notice CGI when it is bad","quote":"This video from a few years ago is a great counterpoint","text":"This video from a few years ago is a great counterpoint: [VIDEO] It's not defending all CGI, just arguing that we only notice it when it's bad. It's basically a toupée fallacy"},{"id":15500492,"author":"rdoherty","month":"2017-10","story":"Goodbye Uncanny Valley [video]","stance":"rec","why":"We only see bad CGI, so we think it all sucks","quote":"Related video about why people think CGI sucks or is still in the uncanny valley","text":"Related video about why people think CGI sucks or is still in the uncanny valley: [VIDEO] Basically we consider CGI bad because we only see bad CGI. If it's used properly and the director/creator understands its limitations we either don't notice it or use our suspension of disbelief and don't care."},{"id":14466828,"author":"jerf","month":"2017-06","story":"The Algorithms Behind Moana’s Animated Ocean","stance":"rec","why":"We only notice bad CG; good CG is invisible","quote":"Highly relevant YouTube video","text":"Computer graphics are already better than a lot of people realize, because now you only \"see\" the bad effects. The good effects you often don't notice. Highly relevant YouTube video: [VIDEO] While they may not quite be there yet, I think a lot of the modern animated movies are deliberately affecting a style, rather than being forced into one by the technology."},{"id":37784978,"author":"coder543","month":"2023-10","story":"Show HN: An app store just for installable web apps","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy: good CGI goes unnoticed like good SPAs","quote":"","text":"> A good SPA is indistinguishable from a native app. Exactly. It’s like CGI in movies. People always talk about how bad CGI is, without realizing just how much good CGI they never notice. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35398611,"author":"illiarian","month":"2023-04","story":"Why are movies so dark these days?","stance":"reference","why":"Modern CGI is more plentiful and better than before","quote":"","text":"> CGI now somehow looks worse than VFX 10-30 years ago It's somewhat incorrect. We now have significantly more CGI, and most of it significantly better. See, e.g., Why C.G. Sucks (Except It Doesn't) [VIDEO] However, the over-reliance on CGI is a problem"},{"id":35118627,"author":"worrycue","month":"2023-03","story":"The water technology behind Avatar: The Way of Water","stance":"reference","why":"Only bad CGI is distinguishable from live action","quote":"","text":"> even today, a discerning viewer can still tell CGI apart from live action You can tell BAD CGI apart from live action. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30181596,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2022-02","story":"Over-reliance on CGI in movies","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to rebut claim CGI looks fake","quote":"","text":"> He's saying that CGI, despite decades of progress, seem _fake_ in a way that subtly destroys our sense of immersion Because he choses to use bad CGI in comparisons and completely ignores all the cases where CGI is invisible. See Why CG Sucks (Except It Doesn't) [VIDEO] \"I think that CG looks bad is because we only see bad CG. Amazing, wonderfully executed CG is everywhere, we just don't know it\""}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"hXgqik6HXc0":{"wy":"Pointed to in consciousness and AI threads as the best-known statement of Penrose's view, which commenters find interesting even when they disagree.","q":{"x":"I don't take Roger's view about consciousness for granted but it is very interesting nevertheless.","i":25542400,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":32248819,"author":"aamargulies","month":"2022-07","story":"Under anesthesia, where do our minds go?","stance":"rec","why":"Penrose on microtubules, quantum coherence, and consciousness not being computation.","quote":"His larger point is that consciousness is not a computation.","text":"Roger Penrose talked about this with Lex Fridman. The anesthesiologist Stuart Hameroff is looking into how anesthesia affects microtubules and the implications for consciousness. Penrose is intrigued by the symmetry of microtubules and the possibility for this symmetry to enable quantum coherence to be accessible biologically. His larger point is that consciousness is not a computation. [VIDEO] Relevant part starts around 3:30."},{"id":25542400,"author":"marius_k","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"Penrose argues consciousness is not computation; interesting though debatable.","quote":"I don't take Roger's view about consciousness for granted but it is very interesting nevertheless.","text":"Consciousness is Not a Computation (Roger Penrose) | AI Podcast Clips [VIDEO] Full episode: [LINK] I don't take Roger's view about consciousness for granted but it is very interesting nevertheless."},{"id":24025128,"author":"kebman","month":"2020-08","story":"A man who can read letters but not numbers exposes roots of consciousness","stance":"rec","why":"Penrose's perspective on consciousness and microtubules.","quote":"I think it was on this site that someone posted the very interesting perspectives on consciousness by Roger Penrose.","text":"I think it was on this site that someone posted the very interesting perspectives on consciousness by Roger Penrose.[2] From that I found this video by Dr. Stuart Hameroff, M.D.: Microtubules & quantum consciousness.[1] Higly recommended. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43235141,"author":"qrios","month":"2025-03","story":"Gödel's theorem debunks the most important AI myth – Roger Penrose [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Penrose makes the same claim about AI and consciousness.","quote":"Same statement from Penrose here with Lex Friedman","text":"The question is answered in the full title: > \"Gödel's theorem debunks the most important AI myth. AI will not be conscious\" Same statement from Penrose here with Lex Friedman: \"Consciousness is Not a Computation\" [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35270327,"author":"Mike_12345","month":"2023-03","story":"Sparks of Artificial General Intelligence: Early Experiments with GPT-4","stance":"reference","why":"Penrose's argument that consciousness is not computable.","quote":"","text":"The question is whether consciousness is computable. Can a Turing machine be conscious? Probably not. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33370302,"author":"Galaxeblaffer","month":"2022-10","story":"Artificial Intelligence: The Revolution Hasn’t Happened Yet (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Penrose argues something fundamental about consciousness is missing.","quote":"Randomly watched this yesterday","text":"Randomly watched this yesterday [VIDEO] where Roger Penrose argues that we're missing something fundamental about consciousness and his best bet is a structure called the microtubules. This talk reminded me of my own research into \"AI\" back in the 00's and that it's almost impossible to talk about AI since everybody has a different idea as to whay AI is, yes i know there's a pretty good classification ANI, AGI, ASI but most people don't know about this and think of AI as a machine that thinks like conscious human. I'd argue that we've solved or at least partly solved the part of AI that has to do with neural nets. We're still some way off utilizing the full potential of neural nets since our hardware hasn't quite reached the capability of emulating even the simplest of complex animals. The thing is that Neural nets are probably only part of intelligence and creating bigger and more complex neural nets probably wont result in what most people consider AI but i guess there's still a chance it might. We might have to wait several years to find out since moors law is plateauing and neural chips are still in it's infancy. My best guess is that we'll solve \"Intelligence\" long before we solve consciousness and i think we're actually quite far along here. The best theory of intelligence i've read so far is Jeff Hawkins 1000 Brain Theory and i'm really looking forward to see how far it can go. The problem with this theory is that it's still missing the most critical component which is"},{"id":31809027,"author":"ulfbert_inc","month":"2022-06","story":"Consciousness is not computation","stance":"reference","why":"Conversation suggesting consciousness arises from quantum effects, not computation.","quote":"","text":"It reminds me of this talk[1] and a conversation[2], both hinting at the possibility of the emergence of consciousness from a matter due to quantum properties/effects [edit] and having nothing to do with computation. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43238095,"author":"coef2","month":"2025-03","story":"Gödel's theorem debunks the most important AI myth – Roger Penrose [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's sad to see the interviewer wasting the opportunity to interview Penrose.","text":"It's sad to see the interviewer wasting the opportunity to interview Penrose. I found Lex Fridman does a much better job: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Auuk1y4DRgk",1],["WUvTyaaNkzM",1],["orMtwOz6Db0",1]]},"ooNzRrHA9VY":{"wy":"HN points to it in gas-versus-induction threads as a professional chef making the positive case, including for wok cooking.","q":{"x":"Jon Kung makes the positive case for an induction wok better than I can","i":35563577,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35563577,"author":"femiagbabiaka","month":"2023-04","story":"Chipotle plans to ditch gas grills at 100 new locations","stance":"rec","why":"Positive case for stir frying on induction wok","quote":"Jon Kung makes the positive case for an induction wok better than I can","text":"> Doing a decent stir fry is all but impossible on either induction or standard electric. Not really. Jon Kung makes the positive case for an induction wok better than I can: [VIDEO] . The usage of the wok precedes the gas burner by.. maybe 1500 years? Cooking on an electric range is just something to get used to."},{"id":30655262,"author":"cespare","month":"2022-03","story":"The case for induction cooking","stance":"rec","why":"Chef using induction in a small kitchen","quote":"I only recently became aware of this after watching this video of a chef who uses induction cooking in a small kitchen","text":"It's true that most induction cooktops/ranges aren't great for woks, but in principle the induction surface doesn't have to be flat -- it can be a concave shape to match the wok. In fact these exist -- if you search for \"induction wok burners\" you can see some pictures. (I only recently became aware of this after watching this video of a chef who uses induction cooking in a small kitchen: [VIDEO] ) Perhaps in the future there will be cooktops that include a wok depression on the surface, similar to how some gas stoves today include a built-in wok ring."},{"id":29947457,"author":"faitswulff","month":"2022-01","story":"How bad is my gas stove?","stance":"rec","why":"Chef on induction cooking with a rounded wok cooktop","quote":"Chef Jon Kung posted a video talking about cooking on induction that features a rounded induction cooktop for woks","text":"Chef Jon Kung posted a video talking about cooking on induction that features a rounded induction cooktop for woks: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36821722,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-07","story":"Cooking Air Quality","stance":"reference","why":"Professional chef cooking woks on induction","quote":"See also Jon Kung, a professional chef","text":"> […] specially anything with a wok, just doesn’t work with induction. J. Kenji López-Alt, who recently literally wrote the/a book on wok cooking, would disagree: * [LINK] See also Jon Kung, a professional chef (at least before going YT full-time): * [VIDEO] A video from Yale Appliances (Boston, MA) who sell both gas and electric: * [LINK]"}],"r":[["5P5Mk_IggE0",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1],["aKLntZcp27M",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1]]},"3CWCqJl0BEs":{"wy":"HN points to it as the most thorough walkthrough of the Amazon Rivian van, useful for seeing its real size and delivery-specific design.","q":{"x":"This is a thorough tour, and I want one","i":35138374,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35138374,"author":"ctvo","month":"2023-03","story":"Rivian in talks with Amazon to remove exclusivity clause of EV van agreement","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thorough tour of Rivian's Amazon delivery van.","quote":"This is a thorough tour, and I want one","text":"The vans are amazing. I do a double take every time I see them on the road. This is a thorough tour, and I want one: [VIDEO] i"},{"id":36633904,"author":"dmix","month":"2023-07","story":"Amazon’s electric Rivian vans have delivered 150M packages","stance":"rec","why":"Full walkthrough of the van, showing its real size.","quote":"Duug Demuro gave a full tour of the vehicle","text":"Love the design (rear lighting, doors, dashboard [1], all the very functional handles and space design for easy movement). It's always funny how it takes electric vehicles for car companies to take risks or do something different. It's much larger [2] than it seems from their pictures Duug Demuro gave a full tour of the vehicle [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":36633950,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2023-07","story":"Amazon’s electric Rivian vans have delivered 150M packages","stance":"rec","why":"Comprehensive walkthrough of Rivian delivery van.","quote":"Doug DeMuro did a rather comprehensive walkthrough as well.","text":"Doug DeMuro did a rather comprehensive walkthrough as well. [VIDEO] Annoyingly, they didn't fix my main UX gripe with delivery vehicles, which is that they use the same lights for the hazards and turn signals. It's quite common for the right half of a stopped van to be obscured, which makes it impossible to tell the difference between \"hazards on, delivering a package\" and \"left blinker on, about to cut in front of you\"."},{"id":40134664,"author":"throw_nbvc1234","month":"2024-04","story":"How Amazon Became the Largest Private EV Charging Operator in the US","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the van is custom built for delivery.","quote":"They're also custom made vehicles for delivery use case [1].","text":"They're also custom made vehicles for delivery use case [1]. And they're also still a major shareholder in the company. And long-long-term maybe some kind of integration with Zoox for an tsla/uber like delivery experience (with drones?). [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["C6nAxiym9oc",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["YThelfB2fvg",1],["2Op3QLzMgSY",1],["NQFqDKRAROI",1],["O4Ko7NW2yQo",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1],["-EA2FQXs4dw",1],["XFJnWp1tAdU",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1]]},"8gw0rXPMMPE":{"wy":"Mentioned for its unusually polished, nerdy trailer style, and as an example of a good developer tool announcement.","q":{"x":"this advertisting video is super cool and nerdy.","i":20248697,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":45803453,"author":"gschizas","month":"2025-11","story":"State of Terminal Emulators in 2025: The Errant Champions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Commenter loves the video and its developer easter egg.","quote":"I still watch this video from time to time","text":"In the test, Windows Terminal (weirdly written as \"terminal.exe\") comes up as #4 in the scoring table. As to the \"love\" question, I still watch this video from time to time: [VIDEO] :) EDIT: I love the easter egg with the names of the developers across the Windows timeline :)"},{"id":20248697,"author":"ktpsns","month":"2019-06","story":"New Windows Terminal Preview","stance":"rec","why":"Nerdy, cool advertising video for the new terminal.","quote":"this advertisting video is super cool and nerdy.","text":"[VIDEO] -- this advertisting video is super cool and nerdy. Who could have thought someone could win a prize with a terminal in the 1990s, when everybody tried to get rid of terminals as hard as they could? I wish we could have a modern feature rich terminal with real blurring background and powerful features on X11/Xorg/Qt/Gtk/\"the linux world\". Konsole (KDE) is amongst the best I know so far."},{"id":34735873,"author":"CodeIsTheEnd","month":"2023-02","story":"Show HN: I made a tool that turns screenshots into dramatically angled photos","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a polished developer tool announcement video.","quote":"similar to the announcement video for the Windows Terminal.","text":"I wish there were an option to export a video like the example on the homepage (with some ability to control the camera path). I'd love to be able to use it to make a promotional video for a developer tool similar to the announcement video for the Windows Terminal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21863036,"author":"Kipters","month":"2019-12","story":"Typing inside of the default WSL terminal feels amazing (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Trailer hints at modern terminal features planned.","quote":"yes, this terminal has a fancy 3D trailer","text":"It's faster and works better than old conhost.exe they're also planning to overhaul it with more modern features judging from its trailer[0] (yes, this terminal has a fancy 3D trailer) [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21130956,"author":"DuskStar","month":"2019-10","story":"PuTTY 0.73","stance":"reference","why":"Trailer for the new Windows Terminal.","quote":"there's a trailer for it on YouTube - so weird","text":"Has anyone tried the new official terminal? [0]/[1] It looks rather interesting, if still in prerelease. (also, there's a trailer for it on YouTube - so weird [2]) 0: [LINK] 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19848059,"author":"madspindel","month":"2019-05","story":"Windows 10 is getting a Microsoft-built Linux kernel","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"you can see the command","text":"What I also find interesting is that in the announcement video of Windows Terminal (0:20) you can see the command \"wt install\", which sounds like a possible future package manager? [VIDEO]"},{"id":19859091,"author":"rndgermandude","month":"2019-05","story":"Google Is Turning Off the Works-with-Nest API","stance":"reference","why":"Marketing video for the unreleased Windows Terminal.","quote":"They just announced Windows Terminal and even spent time and money on a marketing video","text":"They just announced Windows Terminal and even spent time and money on a marketing video for this yet unreleased product: [VIDEO] Granted, conhost will stay for compatibility reasons and this is a new thing. But it's open source: [LINK] . And according to the readme contains the source for conhost.exe too."},{"id":19893477,"author":"spudlyo","month":"2019-05","story":"Both Windows 10 and Chrome OS are embracing the Linux kernel","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is just an attempt by Microsoft to stem the flow of power users to Linux Desktop.","text":"This is just an attempt by Microsoft to stem the flow of power users to Linux Desktop. Did you see the flash video advertisement[0] Microsoft made for their new terminal? To me it feels more like Microsoft is trying to attract people fleeing from macOS because of all their recent mis-steps with the MBP. [0]: [VIDEO]"}]},"ISptt3CRAqc":{"wy":"HN points to it in discussions of antidepressant efficacy, the serotonin model and the power of placebos.","q":{"x":"I found Irving Kirsch's talk about anti-depressants vs placebo in depression [1] very interesting.","i":19978088,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":18306126,"author":"leekyle333","month":"2018-10","story":"FDA approves new drug to treat influenza","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture on how powerful placebos are.","quote":"I highly recommend watching this lecture by Dr. Irving Kirsch.","text":"If you're ever board and you want to watch something on placebos I highly recommend watching this lecture by Dr. Irving Kirsch. Placebos are pretty nuts and probably really undervalued. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19978088,"author":"q3PG23XiQKpZqpU","month":"2019-05","story":"The Troubled History of Psychiatry","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on antidepressants versus placebo in depression.","quote":"I found Irving Kirsch's talk about anti-depressants vs placebo in depression [1] very interesting.","text":"Since here we are talking about placebo, I wonder if it has a meaning to say \"placebo\" when speaking about psychotherapy, since there is no \"substance\". Of course there is going to be a placebo effect as long as people think it's going to work. I think the point is that since in mild and moderate depression we have to choose between the placebo of a drug and the placebo of psychoterapy, let's choose the one with less side effects. On the topic of placebo, I found Irving Kirsch's talk about anti-depressants vs placebo in depression [1] very interesting. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8883219,"author":"lingben","month":"2015-01","story":"Treat Depression with the Fisher Wallace Stimulator?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence against the serotonin model.","quote":"the serotonin model of depression has been disproven without a shadow of a doubt","text":"> Stimulates the Brain to Produce Serotonin except that the serotonin model of depression has been disproven without a shadow of a doubt: [VIDEO] the device may have some effect as placebo but if that's what you're going for, there are many other cheaper alternatives"},{"id":8883762,"author":"shkkmo","month":"2015-01","story":"Treat Depression with the Fisher Wallace Stimulator?","stance":"reference","why":"SSRIs versus placebo differences matter only in severe depression.","quote":"it sounds like there is only a clinically (as opposed to statistically) significant difference between SSRI's and placebos","text":"Having just watched the linked video, it sounds like there is only a clinically (as opposed to statistically) significant difference between SSRI's and placebos for the extreme end of severe depression. One of the interesting points the speak made was: The difference in response recovery rate between studies where the patients know they will receive an anti-depressant (drug vs. drug) and studies where they know they may recieve a placebo (drug vs. placebo) is bigger than the difference in recovery rate between drug and placebo. He then points out that due to side effects, patients are fairly reliably able to determine whether they are on a placebo or on an anti-depressant. When discussing the equivalent efficacy of SSRIs and SSREs he says: \"Were there any truth in the serotonin model of depression, and I don't know any responsible researcher in the area who still contends there is. (It's only in marketing where you still see that talked about) Where there any truth to that, SSRE's should not help people recover from depression, it ought to make them depressed\" Link to time: [VIDEO] If that is correct, I think it might be valid to claim that \" that the serotonin model of depression has been disproven\" although I think \"beyond a shadow of a doubt\" seems hyperbolic. It might be also be argue that \"the serotonin model of treating depression\" might be clearer."}]},"ODSJsviD_SU":{"wy":"HN uses it as primary-source evidence on how Tesla's vision-based driving stack and training pipeline work.","q":{"x":"Learning about their engineering in that regard really changed my perception of the likelihood that they could solve the problem. Give it a watch","i":41852516,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41852516,"author":"timschmidt","month":"2024-10","story":"The Retreat to Muskworld","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains Tesla's driving training pipeline.","quote":"Learning about their engineering in that regard really changed my perception of the likelihood that they could solve the problem. Give it a watch","text":"> If the data from supervised driving can he used for unsupervised driving So you're unaware of the AI day presentations about their driving training pipeline. Learning about their engineering in that regard really changed my perception of the likelihood that they could solve the problem. Give it a watch: [VIDEO] > failure to keep up with competitors like Waymo Looks like each of them lead in different ways to me. Waymo leads within certain specific city limits, Tesla leads on the open road. These seem to be direct consequences of their engineering decisions. Question is which will converge on working everywhere all the time."},{"id":33400975,"author":"aeternum","month":"2022-10","story":"Why Tesla removed radar and ultrasonic sensors [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Covers Tesla's transition from 2D to 3D perception.","quote":"It is pretty cool","text":"It is pretty cool: [VIDEO] They transitioned from 2D to 3D a couple years ago, major transition but it does seem like a critical step. We live in a 3d rather than 2d world."},{"id":46918943,"author":"schiffern","month":"2026-02","story":"The Waymo World Model: A New Frontier for Autonomous Driving Simulation","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla presentation treating cars as robots.","quote":"","text":">I've never really thought of Waymo as a robot in the same way as e.g. a Boston Dynamics humanoid, but of course it is a robot of sorts. So for the record, with this realization you're 3+ years behind Tesla. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35714901,"author":"cptcobalt","month":"2023-04","story":"Tesla Again Paints a Misleading Story with Their Crash Data","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Tesla's labeling and simulation.","quote":"Tesla's AI Day 2022 video, timestamped around labeling and simulation","text":"Exactly. There's no better facsimilie for the sheer variety of public roads than, well, public roads. Tesla even has rigorous simulation in place for hostile situations that should never occur on public roads: A good general overview from two minute papers: [LINK] Tesla's AI Day 2022 video, timestamped around labeling and simulation [VIDEO]"},{"id":33076568,"author":"zaptrem","month":"2022-10","story":"Starlink now over 1M user terminals manufactured","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla's AI Day as example of transparency.","quote":"However, this is the transparency that makes up for it","text":"No, and they should be criticized for that. However, this is the transparency that makes up for it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33093521,"author":"simondotau","month":"2022-10","story":"Tesla Vision Update: Replacing Ultrasonic Sensors with Tesla Vision","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the occupancy network visualised.","quote":"if you continue watching for around ten seconds you can see examples of the occupancy network visualised","text":"As stated by Tesla just a few days ago, the occupancy network is not currently shown in the customer-facing visualisation. (Some unrecognised objects are represented by generic placeholders. This is derived from, but not the output of, the occupancy network.) This video links to the relevant citation, and if you continue watching for around ten seconds you can see examples of the occupancy network visualised: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33400580,"author":"113245","month":"2022-10","story":"Why Tesla removed radar and ultrasonic sensors [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Details Tesla's multiple task-specific networks.","quote":"The Tesla AI day videos [1] go into some detail about this.","text":"The Tesla AI day videos [1] go into some detail about this. They use multiple networks that are dedicated to specific tasks. [1] [LINK] (2021), [VIDEO] (2022)"},{"id":33403170,"author":"whiddershins","month":"2022-10","story":"Why Tesla removed radar and ultrasonic sensors [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Presenter states 16x dynamic range from direct photon processing.","quote":"he clearly states 16x dynamic range as a result of direct photon processing.","text":"The replies to your comment don't seem to understand you at all. in the video link here [VIDEO] he clearly states 16x dynamic range as a result of direct photon processing."}],"r":[["2DIl3Hfh9tY",1],["tWr39Q9vBgo",1],["kopoLzvh5jY",1],["oIG9ztQw2Gc",1]]},"P_fHJIYENdI":{"wy":"HN points to it as an accessible explainer of AlphaFold and as an example of narrow, specialized ML delivering real scientific breakthroughs.","q":{"x":"To learn more about AlphaFold and its importance, I would recommend the Veritasium video[0]","i":49100055,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":43076497,"author":"molf","month":"2025-02","story":"The lottery of the snakebite antivenom industry","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great explanation of AlphaFold designing proteins.","quote":"The whole video is worth a watch!","text":"> The first snakebite antivenom was made in the mid-1890s and the method has changed little since: snakes are “milked” for their venom, which is injected into horses or sheep and the antibodies that their immune systems then produce are extracted via the animals’ blood. This is expensive, and many people develop allergies to the antibodies. A cool practical application of DeepMind's AlphaFold is that it allows one to \"design\" proteins because it is now possible to predict how they will fold. [0] This means it is possible to create synthetic protein antibodies that neutralise snake venom, specifically designed for humans [1]. There is a recent Veritasium video [2] that contains a great explanation. The whole video is worth a watch! [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49100055,"author":"AntonyGarand","month":"2026-07","story":"Google shuts down Nobel Prize winning AlphaFold","stance":"rec","why":"Explains AlphaFold and its importance.","quote":"To learn more about AlphaFold and its importance, I would recommend the Veritasium video[0]","text":"To learn more about AlphaFold and its importance, I would recommend the Veritasium video[0]: AlphaFold - The Most Useful Thing AI Has Ever Done [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43316970,"author":"anon7000","month":"2025-03","story":"Microsoft's Relationship with OpenAI Is Not Looking Good","stance":"reference","why":"Example of specialized ML achieving real results in protein folding.","quote":"models and pipelines (using similar technology as chabots) specifically built for certain tasks can achieve really incredible results","text":"> everything else: not much of anything achieving real, deep value. Lots of anecdotal use cases like drafting emails. Well, this is what people are missing IMO. Yeah, general purpose chatbots can’t solve advanced problems. However, models and pipelines (using similar technology as chabots) specifically built for certain tasks can achieve really incredible results — like in the area of protein folding ( [VIDEO] ) I think there is a lot of potential, but most companies are just slapping a chatbot (which uses some other company’s models) on their app and calling it a day. Very few are taking a deep look at how ML could actually solve real problems."},{"id":43455118,"author":"anon7000","month":"2025-03","story":"Most AI value will come from broad automation, not from R & D","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of AI breakthrough research in protein folding.","quote":"the tech behind modern AI models has already produced breakthrough research in the field of protein folding.","text":"Is that really true 120 years ago? We have a lot of hindsight bias, but things really weren’t amazing for the average person in the US even one hundred years ago. Today, the average person has affordable access to a lot of things that may have been luxuries, or simply didn’t exist, 100-200 years ago. Running water, electricity, internet, mobile phones, modern sanitation & infrastructure, and any number of tiny cheap devices that improve QoL. And yes, we also have huge problems with wealth disparity and late stage capitalism. The problem is, we should not throw out the baby with the bathwater. Like, we do not have widespread food scarcity problems today. Extremely advanced medical technology is available, if too expensive. Our tech bubble is only one small part of actual technology. There is still very cool research going on in other fields. Big tech is all about personal computing & social media, and yeah, AI in this area is dubious and hard to get excited about. At the same time, there are SO MANY other areas of technology which can have huge impacts on humanity. For example, the tech behind modern AI models has already produced breakthrough research in the field of protein folding. ( [VIDEO] ) This will have a big impact on our ability to solve biological problems. All that to say… let’s stop being so closed-minded about tech. Yes, the big tech social media, app, and SaaS companies are not going to give us a utopian future. But technology is way more than that. It includes "},{"id":43006352,"author":"dataviz1000","month":"2025-02","story":"Undergraduate shows that searches within hash tables can be much faster","stance":"reference","why":"AlphaFold training improved by withholding obvious information.","quote":"A similar idea came up in Veritasium's latest video today.","text":"A similar idea came up in Veritasium's latest video today. Training AI by DeepMind to predict protein folding greatly improved by withholding the most evident information about a protein's primary structure — its linear polypeptide chain — within the The Structure Module step. [0] After asking ChatGPT not to agree with me that your comment and these two different approaches to solving problems are the alike, it concluded there still might be similarities between the two. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":43158856,"author":"avirajkhare","month":"2025-02","story":"Ask HN: What are you working on? (February 2025)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Personal projects: - [GitMentor]( [LINK] ) → 300+ users so far - [NurtiLens]( [LINK] ) → 50+ users so far Blog post on GitMentor: [LINK] - Terminal in Notion: [LINK] Dropping Resume Optimizer: [LINK] Made a video building it from scratch in 30 mins—completely freestyle. Accidentally exposed some API keys, so had to rotate them since I don’t know how to edit videos :) Video: [LINK] Conclusion: You steer the LLM, don’t let the LLM steer you. - [LLM Bootcamp]( [LINK] ) → making content Another day, another drop. Not taking this one to production because PineconeDB costs too much for cosine similarity search. Built it anyway, here's the video: [LINK] Codebase: [LINK] YT Answer Finder goes to prod today: [LINK] Only two videos processed so far: - [VIDEO] - [LINK] More projects: - Real-time flight tracker: [LINK] - Music recommendation app: [LINK]"}],"r":[["oP1Oq3JLNbc",1]]},"Pae2AMnmUVA":{"wy":"Recommended as hilarious for anyone familiar with both French and British or American culture, and relevant to expats.","q":{"x":"I HIGHLY recommend a watch, it's hilarious especially if you're familiar with both French and British/American culture","i":30426055,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30426055,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2022-02","story":"I have no capslock and I must scream","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious standup for those familiar with French and British/American culture.","quote":"I HIGHLY recommend a watch, it's hilarious especially if you're familiar with both French and British/American culture","text":"It's not founded on skin color you're right, but you're wrong about heritage. Obviously Not All French People Are Like That (I should hope so, as a French person myself). But the ones concerned will be MUCH pickier about people's grammar, spelling and pronunciation if they perceive them as not French. But yes, elitism is also present: If you're perceived as French and your french is bad, you will also be treated poorly. But it's a different kind of \"poorly\". In fact, Paul Taylor talks about that in his stand up \"Franglais\" (I HIGHLY recommend a watch, it's hilarious especially if you're familiar with both French and British/American culture, and it's available for free on Youtube: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":25784630,"author":"edumucelli","month":"2021-01","story":"Parisian Accent in 1912","stance":"rec","why":"Funny standup by British comedian living in France.","quote":"I went to watch him live, what a great standup show.","text":"This is exactly the joke Paul Taylor (British living in France) tells in his standup show [1], which is super funny. I went to watch him live, what a great standup show. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30073830,"author":"BrandoElFollito","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: Who has moved from the U.S. to Europe?","stance":"reference","why":"Comedy show on French culture and bureaucracy.","quote":"","text":"How come nobody mentions France in the comments? :) I am French but have been working 12 years for a very large US company , spent a lot of time in the US, go friends there - an witnessed/helped several Americans moving to France. Salary and healthcare were already covered. The fact that over a 3 days drive across Europe you visit 4 or 5 countries with vastly different cultures is a big plus. And by vastly I mean really vastly. We've been in constant wars for 2000+ years and this created a very nice and specific melting pot + cultural differences. School will be very different. In France you will have a more formalized way of education (which is not a good thing), but also a very liberal one. Children will be drinking tap water starting at kindergarten and and stay like this their whole life. They won't (usually) go to school on Wednesday. The office will be less politically correct. You will have people discussing politics and religion - though this became less outspoken the last 10 years or so. You will have good baguettes and average bread - but still eons better than the average US bread. Bureaucracy is mcu better than the stereotype. But not good either. You have to learn the power of the \"pffff\" sound you make with your eyebrows up - which means \"I understand that this is the rule, but you know, I have to do/get/send that and it is a huge problem if I don't\". Many foreigners foolishly assume that a \"no\" means \"this is not possible\". See [VIDEO] for the more scientific a"}],"r":[["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["ZZ3F3zWiEmc",1],["wOlcB-JxkFw",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["9zinZmE3Ogk",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["rwhUpu9MfZ0",1],["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["PQccNdwm8Tw",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["90NsjKvz9Ns",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1],["FjA0EQPeUGM",1]]},"QUjYy4Ksy1E":{"wy":"HN commenters call it enlightening for understanding the emotional challenges of gifted kids, especially those who struggle later in school or college.","q":{"x":"It is a truly excellent video for helping to understand some of the psychological/emotional challenges of being","i":29454233,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29454233,"author":"Upgrayyed_U","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: How would you have done high school/college/university differently?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains emotional challenges of gifted kids and struggling in college.","quote":"It is a truly excellent video for helping to understand some of the psychological/emotional challenges of being","text":"You should check out this video: Why Gifted Kids Are Actually Special Needs: [VIDEO] . It is a truly excellent video for helping to understand some of the psychological/emotional challenges of being \"gifted.\" Internalizing the ideas spoken to in this video has been a huge gift to my mental help. For a long time, I held it against myself that I didn't try harder in college, and that if I did, I would be in a much better place. The reality is that for kids who do extremely well in high school with little effort, doing poorly in college is the default response. It takes a major intervention to get and stay on the right path, and many \"gifted\" kids of the past generations never got that since it was assumed they were smart enough to figure things out on their own."},{"id":45829353,"author":"mattgreenrocks","month":"2025-11","story":"Why aren't smart people happier?","stance":"rec","why":"Frames gifted kids as special needs; commenter found it enlightening.","quote":"I found it really enlightening.","text":"There is a HealthygamerGG video where he talks about gifted kids as special needs kids bc of this factor. I found it really enlightening. I definitely had to confront it in my own life. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34612176,"author":"Akronymus","month":"2023-02","story":"Astonishing regularity in learning rate among college students","stance":"reference","why":"Backs the claim that gifted kids are special needs.","quote":"Despite what many think, gifted kids are actually special needs as well.","text":"Despite what many think, gifted kids are actually special needs as well. Not being challenged enough directly leads to a lack of learning to learn. And that in turn leads to hitting a brick wall at some point. I have now attempted a few times to finish my higher education, always bounced HARD as I lack the mental facilities needed to deal with the frustration/commitment of actually having to learn. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33272148,"author":"nrabulinski","month":"2022-10","story":"The link between August birthdays and ADHD (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Covers ADHD as a mismatch with modern lifestyles.","quote":"I think this video talked about this concept in more depth","text":"This idea has been proposed multiple times before but, from what I remember (and I’m paraphrasing here) we’re identifying it as a disorder not because it’s a disease per se but because it affects the lives of those people and in general has been deteriorating ever since humans changed their lifestyles from hunters to farmers. I think this video talked about this concept in more depth [VIDEO]"},{"id":33355581,"author":"Akronymus","month":"2022-10","story":"Video gaming may be associated with better cognitive performance in children","stance":"reference","why":"Supports commenter's experience of being bored at school.","quote":"","text":"As an allegedly gifted child: School was boring/not challenging enough. While video games progressed as fast as made sense. I absolutely think that it's your PoV that's correct rather than the articles. [VIDEO]"}]},"SEwTjZvy8vw":{"wy":"HN points to it as the best deep dive into Mojo's design, including MLIR use and SIMD-first scalars.","q":{"x":"this video totally changed my view","i":39856629,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39856629,"author":"nickysielicki","month":"2024-03","story":"The Next Big Step in Mojo Open Source","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convincing talk on Mojo's design, templated IR and why the project deserves attention.","quote":"this video totally changed my view","text":"When mojo was initially released I didn’t think much of it because: 1. It was announced prior to being available, even in closed source form. 2. Emoji filenames meant to me that it wasn’t taking itself too seriously. 3. Many people have tried to make Python fast and failed, and that was my naive take on it, and despite the fact that LLVM allstars were working on it, it seemed like a lofty goal. 4. Closed source programming languages usually suck. They presented at the LLVM conference in 2023 and this video totally changed my view: [VIDEO] There are some fascinating ideas here, I’m especially excited about what could come from their templated IR. Recommend others give it a watch if they’re confused on why they should pay attention to this project."},{"id":49269144,"author":"MohamedMabrouk","month":"2026-08","story":"Mojo 1.0 Is Here","stance":"rec","why":"Explains MLIR's multithreaded design and per-function parallel LLVM codegen.","quote":"I think they had a good dive here","text":"I think they had a good dive here [VIDEO] The gist is that MLIR (unlike LLVM) was designed to be multithreaded so the whole stack above LLVM IR is parallel, and then LLVM is used as a per-function codegen tool in parallel, so although LLVM itself is single threaded there are multiple seperate contexts of it. also I think they wrote their own linker."},{"id":45143199,"author":"melodyogonna","month":"2025-09","story":"ML needs a new programming language – Interview with Chris Lattner","stance":"reference","why":"Covers compile-time interpreter, mojopkg and code generator of Mojo.","quote":"They have some fancy tech around compile-time interpreter, mojopkg, and their code generator.","text":"They've given a talk abut this on the LLVM dev meeting: [VIDEO] They have some fancy tech around compile-time interpreter, mojopkg, and their code generator. They also have a graph compiler (not mentioned in the talk) that can fuse kernels written in Mojo."},{"id":38463530,"author":"zoogeny","month":"2023-11","story":"Show HN: Error return traces for Go, inspired by Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Talk covers Mojo treating scalars as special case of SIMD vector operations.","quote":"There is a section on SIMD and how they treat scalar values as a special case of vector operations","text":"I was watching a recent talk about the new Mojo language [1]. There is a section on SIMD and how they treat scalar values as a special case of vector operations (around the 33 min time). It does seem that tensors are one of the core abstractions for modern ML systems. I've heard people joke that AI is just matrix multiplication. Python is such a flexible language that creating abstractions around its syntax was super easy. I believe that was part of the reason Python has come to dominate this space (not the only reason obviously). I too felt the same as you, but as a distant admirer of Zig. I totally understand that operator overloading can be misused. But I have an intuition that at least providing operators for linear algebra (and probably complex/quaternion) is a really important feature for any languages from this point in history going forward. 1. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["o_AIw9bGogo",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["SCAGc-rkIfo",1]]},"ezqwfha-BZ8":{"wy":"HN recommends the series as a cautionary tale about buying used Teslas and dealing with Tesla service.","q":{"x":"Anybody involved with Tesla in any way should at least watch","i":19834151,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19834151,"author":"2Ccltvcm","month":"2019-05","story":"Repair delays for Tesla owners","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Essential viewing for anyone involved with Tesla","quote":"Anybody involved with Tesla in any way should at least watch","text":"Anybody involved with Tesla in any way should at least watch The Fantastic Case of the Missing Tesla by Rich [VIDEO]"},{"id":19745413,"author":"kylec","month":"2019-04","story":"Tesla Posts Big Quarterly Loss as Its Electric-Car Sales Lag","stance":"rec","why":"Problems buying a used Model X from Tesla","quote":"You should watch the Rich Rebuilds series of videos about the problems he had buying a used Model X from Tesla","text":"You should watch the Rich Rebuilds series of videos about the problems he had buying a used Model X from Tesla: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19074610,"author":"sschueller","month":"2019-02","story":"Tesla Model 3 Owners Vent About Polar Vortex Affecting Cars","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Tesla door handle issues in cold weather","quote":"The door handles on a Tesla are an issue in normal cold weather already","text":"The door handles on a Tesla are an issue in normal cold weather already [1] although I am not sure if the model 3 has the same ones as the X. Most cars you can yank the door handle open even if it's iced up. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"j6ow-UemzBc":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for showing the tooling possible when schemas and APIs are treated as code.","q":{"x":"There's a great talk that I highly recommend about some of the magic you can do","i":24948311,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24948311,"author":"gravypod","month":"2020-10","story":"Application Load Balancers enables gRPC workloads with end to end HTTP/2 support","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on tooling magic possible with schemas as code.","quote":"There's a great talk that I highly recommend about some of the magic you can do","text":"My main reasons: 1. It's standardized all the implementation for each language is roughly similar and has the same feature sets (middlewares, stubs, retry, hedging, timeouts, deadlines, etc). 2. High performance framework/webserver in \"every\" language. No more \"should I use flask or the built in http server or gunicorn or waitress or...\" 3. Tooling can be built around schemas as code. There's a great talk that I highly recommend about some of the magic you can do [0]. 4. Protos are great for serialization and not just over a network! Need to store configs or data in a range of formats (json, prototxt, binary, yaml)? 5. Streaming. It's truely amazing and can dramatically reduce latency if you need to do a lot of collection-of-things or as-soon-as-x processing. 6. Lower resource usage. Encoding/decoding protos is faster then encoding and decoding json. At high throughput that begins to matter. 7. Linting & standards can be defined and enforced programatically [1] 8. Pressures people to document things. You comment your .c or .java code, why not comment your .proto? [0] - [VIDEO] [1] - [LINK]"},{"id":27082156,"author":"bpicolo","month":"2021-05","story":"Smithy: A language for defining services and SDKs","stance":"rec","why":"Powerful paradigm of data and API introspection across services.","quote":"I think it's a really powerful paradigm.","text":"Reminds me a lot of this talk: [VIDEO] I think it's a really powerful paradigm. I think an org adopting such patterns widespread leads to some really rad capabilities. GDPR compliance, for example, is really tough in SOA architectures in companies without a ton of engineering capacity, but this sort of data/api introspection could do a lot to change that. Any plans to open source projection for languages other than Java? edit: I see that typescript and rust are available on github as well"},{"id":20326120,"author":"zobia","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (July 2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Company's approach to microservices and continuous delivery.","quote":"We are a microservices architecture (Scala backends, ReactJS frontends), practice API-first software development, and run continuous delivery","text":"Flow.io (Flow Commerce) | Hoboken, New Jersey | Dublin, Ireland - [LINK] Flow is a next generation solution for cross-border commerce. Backed by Bain Capital Ventures, Flow has built an API-first, turn-key solution that has been transforming how brands localize their site and sell cross-border. Flow integrates into existing e-commerce platforms and provides brands with a high degree of flexibility over all aspects of the customer experience (local currency pricing, duty & tax calculation, local merchandising), local checkout (local payment methods, local language) and cheap/fast international shipping options resulting in steep growth in international sales. Our dream is to remove borders from global commerce. We are building a point-to-point global eCommerce platform that enables brands to expand internationally. We help brands with everything from catalog management, pricing, currency risk, payment processing, to logistics. The business was founded by Mike Bryzek (co-founder/CTO of e-commerce giant, Gilt Groupe) and Rob (founder/CEO of leader in customer experience, Fizzback). Our approach to software is pretty unique and offers great opportunities to learn. We are a microservices architecture (Scala backends, ReactJS frontends), practice API-first software development, and run continuous delivery (merging a PR deploys the change). You can find more on our approach to software delivery here ( [LINK] ) and on YouTube ( [VIDEO] ) (100k+ views and counting!) Current open roles"}],"r":[["7kVeCqQCxlk",1],["bvlzZnhZhrc",1],["e08kOj2kISU",1],["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["4t1K66dMhWk",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["-Qph8BNrnLY",1]]},"mSgXWpvQEHE":{"wy":"Cited for showing how Julia's metaprogramming can beat C and Fortran, along with sound advice not to overuse it.","q":{"x":"really interesting, informative, entertaining and well delivered.","i":20584922,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20584922,"author":"eigenspace","month":"2019-08","story":"JuliaCon 2019 Videos","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Keynote on metaprogramming in Julia.","quote":"really interesting, informative, entertaining and well delivered.","text":"If anyone is looking for video recommendations to get started: here’s a couple that I thought were great and should be interesting and approachable to just about anyone. Steven G. Johnson’s keynote address. It’s longer format but really interesting, informative, entertaining and well delivered. The main topic is metaprogramming. [VIDEO] Stefan Karpinski’s talk on the unreasonable effectiveness of multiple dispatch. He discusses the fact that Julia has such dramatic code re-use and sharing between libraries and he discusses some reasons why he thinks multiple dispatch is uniquely able to offer this. [LINK]"},{"id":24747138,"author":"ViralBShah","month":"2020-10","story":"CSV Reader Benchmarks: Julia Reads CSVs 10-20x Faster than Python and R","stance":"rec","why":"Julia enabling higher performance than C libraries.","quote":"Have a look at Steve Johnson's keynote from JuliaCon 2019","text":"You can write slow programs in all languages. There are many cases where Julia's language features enable higher performance than in comparable C libraries. Have a look at Steve Johnson's keynote from JuliaCon 2019: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29775619,"author":"mwattsun","month":"2022-01","story":"Understanding the Power of Lisp (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Johnson on when not to use metaprogramming.","quote":"most of the time, don't do metaprogramming.","text":"When I first started writing asm code, it was accepted to write self-modifying code (programmatically create new code, then jmp to it.) Then I discovered and was told it was a bad idea. So that's how I view Lisp macros - creating code on the fly and executing it. When I was studying this issue, I came across femtolisp, which is a standalone Lisp by the author of Julia, Jeff Bezanson. Julia has metaprogramming so I wanted to look at it. In this video [1] Professor Steve G. Johnson explains metaprogramming (around 10 min mark) and says \"most of the time, don't do metaprogramming.\" He points out in the base Julia there are 36,798 methods, but only 138 macros and 14 generated functions (.04% of the code), so I have the impression it is still frowned upon. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30086719,"author":"IlyaOrson","month":"2022-01","story":"Julia Macros for Beginners","stance":"reference","why":"Caution against overusing metaprogramming.","quote":"It is not necessarily better in all cases and should not be overused","text":"It is not necessarily better in all cases and should not be overused: [VIDEO] However, it is useful to provide a nicer syntax and DSLs. Some examples: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":27410515,"author":"miguelraz","month":"2021-06","story":"From Julia to Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Staged programming letting Julia beat Fortran.","quote":"see Steven G. Johnson's keynote at JuliaCon 2019","text":"What is the focus on numerical computing that you think Julia is missing? The ecosystem seems plenty healthy to me. (Maybe PDEs... but those are so close!) It's not uncommon for good Julia codes to smoke Fortran codebases, like the DiffEq verse or with staged programming, see Steven G. Johnson's keynote at JuliaCon 2019: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27410902,"author":"neolog","month":"2021-06","story":"From Julia to Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Macros for high-performance computing.","quote":"","text":"Julia is great for numerical computing, but it's not as good as it can be, even in that area. Widening the community's base of expertise will help. I'll just list a few things that come to mind. Many Base functions are currently untyped, which means it's hard to abstract out the key properties that enable reuse, compiler optimizations, and parallelism. See [0] for a language expert's take on numerical computing, which involves defining traits like associativity that enable automatic parallelism by default. A stronger culture of functional programming could make your code faster and easier to understand. It's often (though not always) easier for tooling to optimize pure functions over immutable data structures (and arrays). Arrays are currently mutable, and there is too much emphasis on mutating functions. The iteration protocol can be made more memory-efficient for large collections and simpler, following Rust's implementation [1]. Macros are useful for high-performance computing [4], and Julia's macros can be made more composable [2]. In general, systems languages don't make language decisions lightly. They have committees, discuss how other languages do things, make proposals. This allows more perspectives on each decision. That would be an improvement over the more ad-hoc style of Julia development, as long as Julia can avoid adding every possible feature, which is a risk of expanding the decision-making body [3]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24748379,"author":"montalbano","month":"2020-10","story":"CSV Reader Benchmarks: Julia Reads CSVs 10-20x Faster than Python and R","stance":"reference","why":"Julia and computationally difficult mathematical functions.","quote":"For more info on Julia and difficult (computationally) mathematical functions see","text":"Agree, but to add, there are many transcendental functions (e.g. I needed to use the Mittag-Leffler function many times in my work) which are near-impossible to implement in Numpy in a way that gives anywhere near usable performance. It's not a common enough function to be pre-implemented in Scipy special functions. In my case, I wrote the algorithm in Numpy/python myself which was almost unusably slow. I then outsourced it to a precompiled fortran program. Finally I just switched to Julia. There was already a library, MittagLeffler.jl which did everything I needed and was written in pure Julia. In the end everything was much faster than the Python/Fortran frankenstein code I had. Edit: For more info on Julia and difficult (computationally) mathematical functions see: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vWkgEddb4-A",1],["19zm1Fn0S9M",1],["jEbRVNxL44c",1],["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["dmWQtI3DFFo",1]]},"vc4yL3YTwWk":{"wy":"HN treats it as top-tier investigative journalism on YouTube and the video that triggered lawsuits against Honey.","q":{"x":"one of the best pieces of investigative journalism I've seen on youtube","i":42586451,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42586451,"author":"aunty_helen","month":"2025-01","story":"uBlock Origin GPL code being stolen by team behind Honey browser extension","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Investigative exposé that sparked Honey lawsuits","quote":"one of the best pieces of investigative journalism I've seen on youtube","text":"[LINK] For context, this all started about 2 weeks ago with one of the best pieces of investigative journalism I've seen on youtube: [VIDEO] And it's spiraling from there into lawsuits etc. I'm kinda glad PayPal bought them as they can't just shut down and file bankruptcy. Hopefully some of these creators will get paid out for lost revenue."},{"id":42538488,"author":"smcleod","month":"2024-12","story":"Show HN: Open-source and transparent alternative to Honey","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the Honey scam works","quote":"Good video I watched recently on how the Honey scam works","text":"Good video I watched recently on how the Honey scam works: [VIDEO] *Edit: Oh I see you mention it in the video on your project! Nice work."},{"id":46771647,"author":"al_borland","month":"2026-01","story":"Google AI Overviews cite YouTube more than any medical site for health queries","stance":"reference","why":"Example of original investigative work done on YouTube","quote":"There are people doing original work on there, you just have to end up in an algorithm that surfaces it","text":"While there is a lot of low-effort content, there is also some pretty involved stuff. The investigation into Honey’s shenanigans[0] was investigated and presented first on YouTube (to the best of my knowledge). The fraud in Minnesota was also broken by a YouTuber who just testified to Congress[1]. There are people doing original work on there, you just have to end up in an algorithm that surfaces it… or seek it out. In other cases people are presenting stuff I wouldn’t otherwise know about, and getting access to see it at levels I wouldn’t otherwise be able to see, like Cleo Abram’s[0] latest video about LIGO[1]. Yes, it’s a mostly entertaining overview of what’s going on, not a white paper on the equipment, but this is probably more in depth than what a science program on TV in the 80s or 90s would have been… at least on par. There are also full class lectures, which people can access without being enrolled in a school. While YouTube isn’t the original source, it is still shared in full, not summarized or changed for entertainment purposes. [0] [VIDEO] (part 1 of 3) [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":46440430,"author":"AkshatJ27","month":"2025-12","story":"Honey's Dieselgate: Detecting and Tricking Testers","stance":"reference","why":"Part 1 of MegaLag's Honey investigation","quote":"The youtuber MegaLag released part 1 of his investigation roughly 1 year ago","text":"The youtuber MegaLag released part 1 of his investigation roughly 1 year ago: [VIDEO] Recently, he released 2 more parts with more new information that paints Honey in a pretty bad light: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":46448843,"author":"al_borland","month":"2025-12","story":"Meta created 'playbook' to fend off pressure to crack down on scammers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"While I tend to agree with you, to the point that I've never willingly clicked on an ad, this isn't how most people that I've seen operate. I actively skip over ads in search results, skip whenever possible in video, and pay to remove them completely if it's offered (subscribing to Kagi and YouTube Premium). If I do end up seeing an ad for something that looks interesting/useful, I don't click on it. I search for it separately. I treat ads the same way I would a random text claiming to be my bank. I don't click the link, I go there on my own so I know I'm in the right place free of any funny business. The side effect of this is the merchant loses context on how effective their ad was. In contrast, when I see almost anyone else using Google, they actively look at the ads first. They treat the ads as if they were the top organic search results. If they search for \"Microsoft\" and Microsoft paid for a top ranked ad to their homepage, they will click on the ad link instead of the real organic result a few lines down. This makes these people very susceptible to advertisements posing at legitimate websites, yet their behavior never changes. I've said something to multiple people about skipping down to the actual results, and they claim to like the ads, or don't see a difference and just pick the first link that seems like what they want. Many of these people are otherwise tech savvy, some of whom spent decades working in the tech sector. It seems we are the weird ones for avoiding t"},{"id":43089460,"author":"sameline","month":"2025-02","story":"Show HN: Get an email when the price drops on any product's URL. No spam","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Honey overwriting affiliate tags","quote":"","text":"This is also worth considering, thanks. We would need to be careful to avoid overwriting any pre-existing affiliate tags set on a user provided URL like Honey was doing [0]. I’m curious why you stopped pursuing the idea if you don’t mind sharing. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42576497,"author":"slowmovintarget","month":"2025-01","story":"uBlock Origin GPL code being stolen by team behind Honey browser extension","stance":"reference","why":"Investigation of Honey extension","quote":"","text":"If any software ever deserved being sued into non-existence it is the Honey browser extension, and any other scam software they turn out (Pie Adblock in this case). [VIDEO]"},{"id":42581146,"author":"bryant","month":"2025-01","story":"LegalEagle is suing Honey [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Technical demonstration of Honey swapping affiliate attribution","quote":"This video goes into technical depth, showing the actual process via browser dev tools where attribution is swapped","text":"Super high level TLDW for those not aware of what's going on: Honey (allegedly, but there's video documentation of this happening by MegaLag) overrode affiliate tags and claimed affiliate commissions for themselves even in cases where honey added no value to a sale or to a merchant whatsoever. They also claimed to find consumers the best discounts even though the platform also had paid plans for merchants allowed paying merchants to suppress certain discount codes. Related: * MegaLag breaking the story of Honey swapping affiliate attribution as well as withholding functional coupon codes from customers. This video goes into technical depth, showing the actual process via browser dev tools where attribution is swapped - [VIDEO] * MKBHD commenting on it - [LINK] * Linus commenting on it - [LINK] * Past discussion on HN that was cited by MegaLag - [LINK]"},{"id":42626312,"author":"rideontime","month":"2025-01","story":"I am rich and have no idea what to do","stance":"reference","why":"Video that triggered class action lawsuits","quote":"As soon as people saw this, they started filing class action lawsuits.","text":"Oh, I guess you haven't been keeping up with the news? You're a star now. [VIDEO] As soon as people saw this, they started filing class action lawsuits. [LINK] So I guess we'll see!"},{"id":42493887,"author":"d3vr","month":"2024-12","story":"I built a platform for discovering and sharing Chrome extension collections","stance":"reference","why":"Exposes shady affiliate practices of Honey","quote":"MegaLag released a video [1] yesterday exposing the shady practices by Honey","text":"For anyone else outside the loop, MegaLag released a video [1] yesterday exposing the shady practices by Honey 1: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ky1nGQhHTso",1],["yYAw79386WI",1],["MO0r930Sn_8",1]]},"23X14HS4gLk":{"wy":"Shared in political threads as a timeless warning about divisive manipulation and demagogues.","q":{"x":"Here's a good video from 1947 explaining the whole thing","i":15030154,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":27657269,"author":"PaulHoule","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: Question about mRNA Vaccines?","stance":"rec","why":"1947 film on spotting divisive manipulation","quote":"My advice","text":"I don't know about that, but I do know this. There is a gang of people that is promoting lies about Ivermectin (that it is not only effective against Roundworms but also COVID-19) and COVID-19 vaccines (that they are dangerous.) I am not sure that they expect to convince anybody, but I think the goal is for the people involved to be persecuted and rejected, complain they are being censored, and then become more bonded to the group. It is like how some cults force members into fruitless evangelism. My advice: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15030154,"author":"gremlinsinc","month":"2017-08","story":"Trump Strategic and Policy Forum, Which Included IBM and GM CEOs, Disbands","stance":"rec","why":"1947 film on how divisive propaganda fuels demagogues","quote":"Here's a good video from 1947 explaining the whole thing","text":"By putting people in 'groups' and having those who support him also enforce this idea that we're all in separate groups and need to look out for own little group--this is how fascism grows, this is how hitler took over Germany, this is how a new Hitler figure could take over America too... It's not outside the realm of possibility sure Germans in 1900 couldn't forsee the horrors of 1940. Here's a good video from 1947 explaining the whole thing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44525102,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-07","story":"U.S. will review social media for foreign student visa applications","stance":"reference","why":"1947 film on recurring political patterns","quote":"Hardly a new political phenomena, and policies with known predictable outcomes.","text":"Hardly a new political phenomena, and policies with known predictable outcomes. [VIDEO] One must sympathize with the good folks at AMCHAM that will have to clean up the mess. Have a wonderful day =)"},{"id":43846864,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-04","story":"Study finds that budget cuts to public R&D would significantly hurt the economy","stance":"reference","why":"1947 film on prejudice and demagoguery","quote":"And yes, people should be worried.","text":"Most academics have the option of studying abroad for awhile if desired. And yes, people should be worried. =3 [VIDEO]"},{"id":43121864,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-02","story":"DOGE puts $1 spending limit on government employee credit cards","stance":"reference","why":"1947 film on prejudice and demagoguery","quote":"","text":"Narcissists love themselves more than anyone else, and would never deny the world of their joy in collateral misery. lol =3 \"Don't Be a Sucker\" (1947) [VIDEO]"},{"id":33075554,"author":"rhaksw","month":"2022-10","story":"Mandated diversity statement drives Jonathan Haidt to quit academic society","stance":"reference","why":"1947 film cited as historical evidence on censorship","quote":"History has shown how this has happened over and over","text":"> You didn't actually answer my request for evidence, though I agree with the NYT that censorship is rooted in fear [1]. Evidence is aplenty of the benefits of open discourse. In real-world places where open discourse is encouraged, people and ideas thrive. Also, saying \"you need to be protected from other people's words\" is not a winning argument in the public sphere. People want to be trusted to make their own decisions about how to feel, not have the importance of speech dictated to them. It's really only a small minority who seek protection against certain viewpoints, and they too want to be able to express themselves. Unfortunately, censorship is also used against them , often with prejudice and without their knowledge [2]. History has shown how this has happened over and over, for example in \"Don't Be a Sucker\" (1947) [3]. If you choose to ignore it, that is your prerogative. History is evidence. > I think is the study we've been discussing Thanks for linking it, that's not the one I had in mind. To expand on my previous comment about how we should accept \"ugly\" forums, I think measuring toxicity is problematic. For one thing, it's a subjective measure. One man's trash is another's treasure. For example, here's an article from someone making a case in favor of Kiwi Farms [4]. But also, censorship can chill what people state publicly. I already shared Axios's write-up of a recent study that shows that these days, what people say publicly does not align with what they say"}],"r":[["BDqvzFY72mg",1],["ySuUZEjARPY",1],["lhe286ky-9A",1]]},"29RkHH-psrY":{"wy":"Recommended as a rare long-form interview with a legendary operating system designer, though some find it light on technical detail.","q":{"x":"The Computer History Museum oral history interview with David Cutler is also very good","i":37976465,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37976465,"author":"randomifcpfan","month":"2023-10","story":"Dave Cutler: The Secret History of Microsoft Windows [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Oral history interview with David Cutler","quote":"The Computer History Museum oral history interview with David Cutler is also very good","text":"The Computer History Museum oral history interview with David Cutler is also very good: Part 1: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK]"},{"id":24589645,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2020-09","story":"Windows XP source code has leaked","stance":"rec","why":"Long-form Computer History Museum interview with Dave Cutler","quote":"Computer History Museum long form interviews","text":">It was really really stable. Windows 2000 was the last version of the NT branch where Dave Cutler was in charge. He's a legend for a reason. [LINK] Computer History Museum long form interviews: Part 1 [VIDEO] Part 2 [LINK]"},{"id":40067245,"author":"webdevver","month":"2024-04","story":"F/OSS Comics: 8. The Origins of Unix and the C Language","stance":"reference","why":"Cutler interview, light on technical detail","quote":"Thankfully some enterprising people have managed to get him to sit infront of a camera","text":"judging from how i've read other people describe him, Dave Cutler's personality seems antithecal to memoirs or biographies, from what I've read he seems to be very much like John Carmack in his total lack of sentimentality. Thankfully some enterprising people have managed to get him to sit infront of a camera, but its pretty light on the nitty gritty technical aspects: - Computer history museum interview: [VIDEO] - David Plummer's interview: [LINK]"}],"r":[["vwCdKU9uYnE",1],["Gk-9Fd2mEnI",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["43CMV8KIs3E",1],["s0ukXDnWlTY",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1],["pBiWtJJN5zk",1],["JRcXUuQYR90",1],["rHP-OPXK2ig",1]]},"3HROqnw-nf4":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that large production apps share a C++ core with native UI, in cross-platform and React Native or Flutter debates.","q":{"x":"There's a pretty interesting video of how the Office team uses C++ for their cross-platform efforts","i":9434730,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":12857499,"author":"golf1052","month":"2016-11","story":"Microsoft Teams, the new chat-based workspace in Office 365","stance":"rec","why":"Cross-platform Office development, Word on Windows and Mac","quote":"Pretty interesting talk about cross platform Office.","text":"Word for Windows and Word for Mac are much closer together than you may realize. Pretty interesting talk about cross platform Office. Part 1: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK]"},{"id":9434730,"author":"akoeplinger","month":"2015-04","story":"Visual C++ Cross-Platform Mobile","stance":"rec","why":"How the Office team uses C++ across platforms","quote":"There's a pretty interesting video of how the Office team uses C++ for their cross-platform efforts","text":"There's a pretty interesting video of how the Office team uses C++ for their cross-platform efforts from CppCon 2014: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20981701,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2019-09","story":"Ask HN: React Native or Flutter?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as verification that Office uses C++ with native views","quote":"they have plenty of talks at CppCon and BUILD about how they do it","text":"Yes, C++ with native views. How do you think Microsoft does Office on Android and iOS? As for verification, they have plenty of talks at CppCon and BUILD about how they do it. EDIT: to save you some work. CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms, Part I\" [VIDEO] CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms, Part II\" [LINK] Reacting to Dropbox: another take on cross-platform C++ development [LINK] Ashampoo Systems GmbH [LINK]"},{"id":20614302,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2019-08","story":"Flutter looks good, but is painful","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of C++ with native UI in production","quote":"","text":"I did provide the information for it, Dropbox and Microsoft talks at CppCon, easy with some Google fu. CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms, Part I\" [VIDEO] CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms, Part II\" [LINK] CppCon 2015: Jacob Potter & Andrew Twyman “Bridging Languages Cross-Platform...\" [LINK] CppCon 2017: Stephen Spann “Cross-Platform Apps with Dropbox’s Djinni...” [LINK] CppCon 2014: T. Grue & S. Kabbes \"A Deep Dive into 2 Cross-Platform Mobile Apps Written in C++\" [LINK] Naturally the native UI has to be used. Are we talking about having a good user experience here, or having the easy lazy path for the developer?"},{"id":17895847,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-09","story":"$600 Chromebooks are a dangerous development for Microsoft","stance":"reference","why":"Office team session on refactoring code for multiple platforms","quote":"The Office team did a couple of session at CppCon about how they refactored the code.","text":"Since 2014 not anymore. The Office team did a couple of session at CppCon about how they refactored the code. \"CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms\" [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":17304677,"author":"xor_null","month":"2018-06","story":"Office 365, Skype, MS Teams, and VS Code Are All Being Rewritten in React Native","stance":"reference","why":"Office mobile apps built with shared C++ core and native UI","quote":"two Microsoft employees were talking about the Office mobile apps and how they developed it","text":"I wonder, not long ago there was a c++ conference were two Microsoft employees were talking about the Office mobile apps and how they developed it [1]. They said that they developed the main part in c++ and the ui part in the respective ui platform (cocoa/android ui framework). Would be interesting why they moved from this approach to React native. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["MGMoRu5yrVc",1]]},"6Maq5IyHSuc":{"wy":"Suggested as a gentle starting point for beginners in electronics.","q":{"x":"And then some bigclivedotcom to get going","i":31165245,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31165245,"author":"recuter","month":"2022-04","story":"Electronics Explained: Fundamentals for engineers, technicians and makers (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as beginner starting point in electronics","quote":"And then some bigclivedotcom to get going","text":"This is very good but before throwing people off the deep end I'd suggest this overview (origin of the dx7 & fm synthesis): [LINK] And then some bigclivedotcom to get going: [VIDEO] EEVblog for intermediate beginner stuff / home gamer lab gear: [LINK] And finally some Marco Reps for advanced beginners: [LINK] Just to dip your toe in."},{"id":18403647,"author":"therein","month":"2018-11","story":"The Potentiometer Handbook (1975) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Electronics component explainer from a channel that does such videos","quote":"I know bigclivedotcom does for sure.","text":"EEVblog or mikeselectricstuff might have videos like that as far as I remember. I know bigclivedotcom does for sure. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33762213,"author":"rft","month":"2022-11","story":"Converting a WW2-Era Engine Cowl Flaps Indicator into a USB Peripheral","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I fully agree with the sibling comment to just buy a starter kit and start screwing around. I would add that the cost of failure is very low, so do not be afraid to experiment. At least for me this is the best way to learn. Worst case you fry the Arduino and need to order another one (or just order two or three spares so you don't need to wait, once you get going you might/will find uses for them). I have mostly fried LEDs, because I forgot to limit their current (fancy way of saying: put a resistor in series, ballpark 300-1000 Ohm should be fine). Every time you break a component, try to figure out what went wrong so you slowly learn what not to do, but you also understand why you should not do that. And the reward of seeing something change in the real world, not just on a screen, is great, even if it is just blinking an LED for the first time. You will not get to this level of understanding with your first project, but you also do not build a high performance distributed system as your Hello World! project in a new framework/language. Start with simple things like the usual blinky. Then start blinking two LEDs. Then you can figure out how to blink 4 LEDs independently with only three pins. Use a button to toggle LEDs, use transistors to switch LEDs, use optocoupler to switch LEDs, read the position of a potentiometer via an ADC, shift the ADC value with a resistor in series/parallel, read multiple buttons via a single pin/ADC, control the brightness of an LED via PWM, cont"}],"r":[["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["j6VlT_jUVPc",1]]},"9-jIplX6Wjw":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it for a compelling, accessible case against treating the speed of light as a hard limit.","q":{"x":"She does seem to have a pretty compelling argument that the speed of light may not be the maximum speed.","i":37623671,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37623671,"author":"prettyStandard","month":"2023-09","story":"She got famous on YouTube. Now it helps fund her research in quantum gravity","stance":"rec","why":"Compelling argument that light speed may not be maximum","quote":"She does seem to have a pretty compelling argument that the speed of light may not be the maximum speed.","text":"She does seem to have a pretty compelling argument that the speed of light may not be the maximum speed. It's not that crack pot. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36512897,"author":"baja_blast","month":"2023-06","story":"US Congress doubles down on explosive claims of illegal UFO retrieval programs","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why special relativity limits are not valid arguments against FTL","quote":"Sabine Hossenfelder does a good job explaining why the limits imposed by special relativity are not valid arguments","text":"Warp drives and wormholes do not necessarily break the laws of physics. Sabine Hossenfelder does a good job explaining why the limits imposed by special relativity are not valid arguments: [VIDEO] As for a breakaway civilization, don't you think people would notice resources missing or the insane usage of resources by what would have to be a massive civilization be noticed? If you take the claims at face value, that these things operate as if inertia does not apply already \"breaks physics\". And if that is the case ET would make more sense than mole people. But it is also possible there is nothing to this at all. For me it's ET or a Psyop."},{"id":35506938,"author":"maxbond","month":"2023-04","story":"Sabine Hossenfelder: I think faster than light travel is possible. Here's why","stance":"reference","why":"Hossenfelder's arguments for FTL travel are in the video","quote":"you'll have to watch the video to hear her arguments","text":"Can we switch the link to [VIDEO] ? The blog doesn't really provide any additional information, and it obscures that you'll have to watch the video to hear her arguments (and I've gathered that many people are only here for text content & abstain from videos)."}]},"A-fxij3zM7g":{"wy":"Recommended as a short, fascinating introduction to how simple sensor-actuator machines can appear intelligent.","q":{"x":"Fascinating thought experiments.","i":22350293,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":40257215,"author":"rramadass","month":"2024-05","story":"The Nature of Code (2nd Edition)","stance":"rec","why":"Simple sensor-actuator machines that appear intelligent.","quote":"","text":"I am forever indebted to this book for pointing me to Valentino Braitenberg's Vehicles: Experiments in Synthetic Psychology ( [LINK] ) where very simple autonomous sensor/actuator machines combine to exhibit complex behaviour which maybe mistaken for \"Intelligence\". Makes you really think abut Complexity/Intelligence/Emergent Behaviour/etc. Wikipedia : [LINK] Some Videos: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22350293,"author":"gherkinnn","month":"2020-02","story":"Kernighan's Law: You are not smart enough to debug it","stance":"rec","why":"Short introduction to Braitenberg vehicles thought experiments.","quote":"Fascinating thought experiments.","text":"Uphill Analysis, Downhill Synthesis is a major point in the book Vehicles . [0] Fascinating thought experiments. Here’s an introduction on YT [1], though it really only scratches the surface. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34583729,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2023-01","story":"Will Wright on Designing User Interfaces to Simulation Games (1996) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The Braitenberg Vehicles","text":"At the time I was actually pretty skeptical about how hard it would be to get the AI to work plausibly (and so was Will, but somehow he managed to do it), and I though an online game like The Sims Online would be easier. But leveraging the player's imagination and supporting storytelling was key. Will mentioned a \"Braitenberg Machine\": [LINK] >A Braitenberg vehicle is a concept conceived in a thought experiment by the Italian-Austrian cyberneticist Valentino Braitenberg. The book models the animal world in a minimalistic and constructive way, from simple reactive behaviours (like phototaxis) through the simplest vehicles, to the formation of concepts, spatial behaviour, and generation of ideas. >For the simplest vehicles, the motion of the vehicle is directly controlled by some sensors (for example photo cells). Yet the resulting behaviour may appear complex or even intelligent. The Braitenberg Vehicles: [VIDEO] Student: What about from person to person, you talking about the information that's contained within the objects, so there can be information in another person, that you want to interact with in the environment. Have you looked at any reasons why you would want to do that? Will Wright: Oh yeah, I mean, that's that's the hard problem. I mean, simulating ants is hard enough, when you get to people there's really no hope. There are two issues here. You can look at this as a technology. It's not a product right now. And their are a few directions this could go. I could se"}],"r":[["n8Dd5aVXLCc",1],["144uOfr4SYA",1],["x5Efg42-Qn0",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["ffkIQrq-m34",1],["GcJxRqTs5nk",1],["pQ0CvjAJXz4",1],["yErKTVdETpw",1],["f7CW7S0zxv4",1],["RTB5XhjbgZA",1]]},"FzF1KySHmUA":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a quick, hands-on demonstration that steel loses strength well below its melting point.","q":{"x":"watch this excellent and short video from a blacksmith","i":30481838,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":38656714,"author":"khazhoux","month":"2023-12","story":"September 11th That You Have Never Seen","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the jet fuel conspiracy theory with attitude","quote":"This video disproves that conspiracy theory","text":"This video disproves that conspiracy theory, and with just the right amount of \"WTF-you-truthers-are-idiots\" attitude: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30481838,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2022-02","story":"Newly published 9/11 footage [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Short blacksmith video debunking the jet fuel melting claim","quote":"watch this excellent and short video from a blacksmith","text":"Been in a coma for two decades? [LINK] > The four sides of each building formed a tube with window slots This tube was designed to carry the weight of the building and its contents directly downward to the foundations. The walls acted as vertical beams, wide and tall as the buildings, that bent to the side to absorb wind loads. [LINK] > Prior to the construction of the Twin Towers, skyscrapers were designed to support themselves through large internal columns spaced about 30 feet (9 meters) apart, which interrupted the flow of interior space. For this project however, the engineers came up with a different solution -- the exterior walls themselves would support the bulk of the structure, and they would get a boost from one single column of beams in the center. Make a big hole in the primary support structure and things go badly. (And before parroting the \"jet fuel doesn't melt steel beams\" thing as a follow-up, watch this excellent and short video from a blacksmith: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":24425089,"author":"SideburnsOfDoom","month":"2020-09","story":"Tesla Model Y Owners Find Cooling System Made with Home Depot-Grade Fake Wood","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates that staying below ignition temperature does not make materials safe","quote":"does not make it safe","text":"> Wood will not self-combust until reaching around 300°c Staying below the \"fail fast\" temperature does not make it safe: [VIDEO] The question is, how will it handle years of the daily heating and cooling cycles that the cooling system will undergo? I don't know and likely no-one does ... because it hasn't been rated or approved for that task. it isn't even a standardised part, how can it be?"},{"id":15565884,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2017-10","story":"How to Read the JFK Assassination Files","stance":"reference","why":"Shows steel weakens drastically at jet fuel temperatures","quote":"Steel Beams are about as strong as a wet-noodle at Jet-fuel temperatures","text":"> claiming jet fuel can't melt steel beams Just note: Jet Fuel probably can't melt steel beams, because steel has a significantly higher melting point than the temperature of jet fuel. Of course, Steel Beams are about as strong as a wet-noodle at Jet-fuel temperatures: [VIDEO] Which of course, would cause the towers to collapse. But seriously, Jet Fuel doesn't \"melt\" steel beams. It just weakens them tot he point where they break easily. You can't make ANY mistakes when talking to a conspiracy theorist. They are right about the melting point of steel, but that doesn't help their argument at all."}],"r":[["sSfejgwbDQ8",1],["n068fel-W9I",1],["d-ruFmgTpqA",1],["PZbqAMEwtOE",1],["YsmgPp_nlok",1],["GvtNyOzGogc",1]]},"MVEKt_H3FsI":{"wy":"Pointed to as a primary-source interview on Apple's architecture transitions, emulation and microcode work.","q":{"x":"There’s an interview with him in the Computer History Museum archive.","i":42485039,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":43538815,"author":"mesrik","month":"2025-03","story":"Notes on the Pentium's microcode circuitry","stance":"rec","why":"Interview where Davidian discusses his involvement with microcoding","quote":"he was quite bit involved with microcoding.","text":"Hi Ken, Nice article. While reading I remembered that I watched some time ago the Oral History of Gary Davidian and he was quite bit involved with microcoding. And if I were you I would try asking him if he could be able to give you some ideas where to get more information about microcode workings and development. Here are links to that interview, if you have time to watch it. It's in two parts. - [LINK] - [VIDEO] Cheers, :-) riku"},{"id":33796729,"author":"andrewf","month":"2022-11","story":"Rereading: The Soul of a New Machine by Tracy Kidder","stance":"rec","why":"Oral history of an engineer behind the 680x0 emulator and microcode work","quote":"There's lots of interesting stuff from this era in the CHM's oral histories.","text":"You might be interested in this Computer History Museum oral history of Gary Davidian, who among other things (1) worked on the \"rival project\" from Soul (2) was hired by NEC to bolster their lawsuit defense by writing microcode for the Intel 8086 (3) wrote the 680x0 emulator Apple launched their PowerPC line with. Transcript: [LINK] [LINK] Video: [LINK] [VIDEO] There's lots of interesting stuff from this era in the CHM's oral histories."},{"id":42485039,"author":"evaneykelen","month":"2024-12","story":"Rosetta 2 creator leaves Apple to work on Lean full-time","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with Gary Davidian on Apple architecture transitions","quote":"There’s an interview with him in the Computer History Museum archive.","text":"In a previous discussion the name of Gary Davidian is mentioned who also — initialy single-handed — did amazing work on architecture changes at Apple. There’s an interview with him in the Computer History Museum archive. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["VLxTHYGLKY0",1]]},"Q13CishCKXY":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a reference point for how much better video calls could get, with some skepticism about what the demo leaves out.","q":{"x":"No headset to wear. No disorientation. You just sit down and you're there with somebody else.","i":35233783,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35233783,"author":"jonas21","month":"2023-03","story":"Who is still inside the metaverse?","stance":"rec","why":"Headset-free, you just sit down and feel present with someone.","quote":"No headset to wear. No disorientation. You just sit down and you're there with somebody else.","text":"I think the most promising Metaverse technology might be something like Starline [1]. No headset to wear. No disorientation. You just sit down and you're there with somebody else. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34251882,"author":"mindover","month":"2023-01","story":"Apple headset leak reveals new details and confirms earlier rumors","stance":"rec","why":"Starline suggests video calls could become a completely different experience.","quote":"Video calls in AR could be vastly better, not just a little bit, but basically a completely different experience/relationship.","text":"We might just not know what we are missing. I used to think that video calls is a solved problem until I saw Google's Project Starline [0]. Video calls in AR could be vastly better, not just a little bit, but basically a completely different experience/relationship. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38977242,"author":"esafak","month":"2024-01","story":"We need technology that is less immersive, not more","stance":"reference","why":"Tech demo showing user reactions to photorealistic calls.","quote":"see how users respond to the realism","text":"This is true of games, but not other applications. You need photorealism to have immersive video calls -- as if you are meeting in person. Here is a tech demo by Google; see how users respond to the realism: [VIDEO] So we do need more immersive technology; just maybe not for games."},{"id":29309546,"author":"alanwreath","month":"2021-11","story":"Apple has a new work-from-home policy, but it’s still not what employees want","stance":"reference","why":"Speculates such tech could end the work-from-home debate.","quote":"","text":"WFH vs No WFH vs Hybrid WFH has become the new emacs vs vi vs vscode (order not important), but I wonder if it will all go away when projects like [VIDEO] become as available as a web browser"},{"id":27200018,"author":"achow","month":"2021-05","story":"Project Starline: Feel like you're there, together","stance":"critical","why":"Notes the demo conspicuously avoids showing participants touching the glass.","quote":"they don't show the participants touching the glass pane 'separating' them","text":"If you watch the video of it: [VIDEO] Interesting thing to note is that they don't show the participants touching the glass pane 'separating' them, whereas for that kind of situation it would be very very natural thing to do. I guess doing so (reaching towards the 'glass pane') would make the imagery distort/degrade real fast as you would start going out of camera's FoV, which that would break the magic."}],"r":[["YjjTPV2pXY0",1],["MFzDaBzBlL0",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["ph8LyNIT9sg",1]]},"TrC6ROeV4GI":{"wy":"HN points to it in monorepo and refactoring discussions as a great account of large-scale changes at Google.","q":{"x":"The discussions from Hyrum Wright from Google about large-scale refactoring are great in this respect.","i":42077671,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42077671,"author":"ted_dunning","month":"2024-11","story":"Monorepo – Our Experience","stance":"rec","why":"Great discussion of large-scale refactoring at Google.","quote":"The discussions from Hyrum Wright from Google about large-scale refactoring are great in this respect.","text":"Coupling happens due to other reasons than poor contracts. The discussions from Hyrum Wright from Google about large-scale refactoring are great in this respect. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18814649,"author":"svat","month":"2019-01","story":"Monorepos: Please don’t","stance":"rec","why":"Large-scale changes at Google, with talks and papers.","quote":"It's great!","text":"See Hyrum Wright's talks on Large-Scale Changes at Google: [LINK] (CppCon2014), [VIDEO] (CppCon 2018), and paper by Googlers on ClangMR ( [LINK] ). It's great! And if you'd like to learn more about how monorepos work in practice there's a couple of papers: [LINK] and [LINK] (Also worth reading: [LINK] )"},{"id":27273985,"author":"joshuamorton","month":"2021-05","story":"Rf: an experimental refactoring tool for Go","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for large-scale changes across millions of lines of code.","quote":"","text":"> Refactors are rarely as simple as moving a few file names or symbols around, at least where I work. While we do have some automation to help (and not break existing consumers), it’s significantly more involved in an SOA architecture for tier1 services. These sorts of big refactors also generally come with data model changes, performance profiling etc. This tool seems fantastic for parts of that, but not all of it. Yes, I'd refer to that as a migration, which absolutely is a lot of effort, but a tool like this isn't going to be particularly helpful with a migration precisely because the majority of the effort with such a migration will be like...doing all the data stuff and traffic stuff. Changing the code is a minimal part. A refactor is often not a thing that even registers at a smaller company, because its on the scale of a few files. But \"Large Scale Changes\" are a thing when you end up having millions of lines of code ( [VIDEO] ), and tools like this one are useful for those kinds of changes. But a single LSC won't get you promoted. And rf is neat, but in a very go way, it's not particularly exciting if you're familiar with [LINK] or [LINK] or clangmr or [LINK] , or outside of google Semgrep and Bowler and others, since its essentially the same, but built in go for go. > Just as a friendly aside, your tone seems strangely defensive (though this may just be my fault reading this tired at 3AM) Not at all. I was however, pointing out that \"this was really just a way to bra"}],"r":[["BxQw4CdxLr8",1]]},"WO2X3oZEJOA":{"wy":"HN points to it as the accessible explanation of why glitch tokens exist whenever tokenizers or odd GPT behavior come up.","q":{"x":"it's also a good introduction to the topic.","i":36250954,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36250954,"author":"weinzierl","month":"2023-06","story":"Understanding GPT Tokenizers","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to glitch tokens.","quote":"it's also a good introduction to the topic.","text":"Computerphile has an interesting video about Glitch Tokens, but as it goes with Computerphile videos it's also a good introduction to the topic. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35460269,"author":"non-","month":"2023-04","story":"OpenAI Tokenizer","stance":"rec","why":"Covers how junk data left odd tokens that make GPT models misbehave.","quote":"I found this tool recently when it was linked from this Computerphile video[1]","text":"I found this tool recently when it was linked from this Computerphile video[1] about \"glitch tokens\". tldw: Certain junk data was thrown out post-tokenization e.g. the /r/counting[2] community data and debug logs from Rocket League some tokens specific to those contexts stuck around, however, and are now like \"a color you've never seen before\" as far as GPT-X models are concerned giving the model one of these \"glitch\" tokens causes it to kind of freak out and return gibberish or some completely random response because it has not encountered them during training, because they were removed when the data was cleaned. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":36280776,"author":"veidr","month":"2023-06","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to explain LLMs are statistical models, not sentient.","quote":"","text":"That is indeed the funniest (and slightly horrifying) thing. Even if you understand \"glitch tokens\"[1] (which is BTW the best way I've seen to explain to nontechnicals (hi, Dad!!) and also some subset of technicals (hi various friends of mine!! +_+...) that LLM applied statistics models aren't anything like sentient AGI)... ...regardless, ChatGPT and its ilk nevertheless do often already outperform \"random human being coworker or uncle\" at recommending just about anything... which is like Ted Chiang said[2], “That’s more a statement about how much bullshit we are required to generate and deal with in our daily lives.” Although he was not referring to inputs from your (dumbass) coworkers/uncles, it still holds true. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":36330025,"author":"dale_glass","month":"2023-06","story":"What Reddit Got Wrong","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses glitch tokens that are Reddit usernames.","quote":"This was discussed a way back during the whole glitch tokens deal","text":"It is well known, yes. This was discussed a way back during the whole glitch tokens deal: [VIDEO] A bunch of those, like \" SolidGoldMagikarp\" are Reddit users."},{"id":35875071,"author":"chaorace","month":"2023-05","story":"Google Translate “Get well [Swedish firstname]” translates to “fuck you”","stance":"reference","why":"Background on glitch token phenomenon in GPT-3.","quote":"","text":"This reminds me of the recent so-called \"Glitch Token\" phenomenon[1]. When GPT-3 was presented with reserved tokens it never encountered during training, it reacted in extremely unpredictable ways -- often with a simple \"fuck you\". For those unfamiliar with LLM architecture: \"tokens\" are the smallest unit of lexical information available to the model. Common words often have their own token (e.g.: Every word in the phrase \"The quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog\" has a dedicated token), but this is a coincidence of compression and not how the model understands language (e.g.: GPT-3 understands \"defenestration\" even though it's composed of 4 apparently unrelated tokens: \"def\", \"en\", \"est\", \"ration\"). The actual mechanism of understanding is in learned associations between tokens. In other words: the model understands the meaning of \"def\",\"en\",\"est\",\"ration\" because it learns through training that this cluster of tokens has something to do with the literary concept of violently removing a human via window. When a model encounters unexpected arrangements of tokens (\"en\",\"ration\",\"est\",\"def\"), it behaves much like a human might: it infers the meaning through context or otherwise voices confusion (e.g.: \"I'm sorry, what's 'enrationestdef'?\"). This is distinctly different from what the model does when it encounters a completely alien form of stimulation like the aforementioned \"Glitch Tokens\". At the risk of anthropomorphizing, try imagining if you were having a conversation w"},{"id":35730746,"author":"Der_Einzige","month":"2023-04","story":"Semantic Tokenizer for Enhanced Natural Language Processing","stance":"reference","why":"Example of BPE producing glitch tokens from usernames.","quote":"","text":"Yup. There are so many reasons why BPE is an issue: 1. It really hurts the ability to generate creative writing/poetry (e.g. impossible for even ChatGPT-4 to fully understand syllable counts, leading to incorrect haikus and even poor rhyming), see [LINK] and [LINK] 2. It means that silly stuff like \"Glitch Tokens\" are a huge issue. i.e. whole tokens dedicated to weird usernames caused by people counting on a counting subreddit so much that their names got their own token. See [VIDEO] 3. BPE has a lot of terrible vocabulary choices independent of Glitch Tokens. Lots of massive punctuation, garbage sequences, etc."},{"id":35191549,"author":"chii","month":"2023-03","story":"Show HN: Alpaca.cpp – Run an Instruction-Tuned Chat-Style LLM on a MacBook","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why glitch tokens exist in training data.","quote":"if you watched to the end of that video, it explores a very plausible reason why such glitched tokens exist","text":"The reason, i suspect, that this method won't work, is because the training dataset has knowledge embedded in it, regardless of whether you think it only contains in-context logic. The evidence is that there are these \"glitched\" tokens : [VIDEO] if you watched to the end of that video, it explores a very plausible reason why such glitched tokens exist - it is because the training dataset has a bunch of \"nonsense\" (a large dump of the subreddit that just counts a number, as well as various other \"data\"). So my hypothesis is that if you attempted the in-context logic training dataset, it would produce large number of glitched tokens (different ones, but equally as \"illogical\" to the user)."}],"r":[["eKtsY7hYTPg",1]]},"Ze2pc6NwsHQ":{"wy":"Pointed to when people ask for science-based ways to boost concentration or arrange a home office.","m":[{"id":33192503,"author":"blueyes","month":"2022-10","story":"Virtual communication curbs creative idea generation","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman episodes on how screens and eye focus affect brain function.","quote":"","text":"Virtual interactions are tied to screens. That's their weak point, because screens affect our vision, how we move and focus our eyes. Our eyes are the only part of our brain exposed to light and air. It turns out that how we move and focus them affects the rest of brain function. Andrew Huberman, a neuroscientist at Stanford, has researched and spoken about this deeply and at length. Here are three podcast episodes where he dives in: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] I think all three points in these comments can be right: 1) we need alone time; 2) sharing physical space can help creativity; 3) virtual meetings can hurt it. But we have to isolate the variables. Shared physical space for creative sessions doesn't have to mean open offices, and it doesn't have to mean eliminating solitude. We should also allow for different people responding differently to virtual meetings, shared space, etc, and remember to consider this statistically, as the scientists do. That is, we should try to segment the subjects of the study. Some will prefer screens more, some shared space. The tech industry remains willfully and obtusely ignorant of the costs of virtualizing human interaction, because people sitting in a room together don't need much software, and nobody's going to build a unicorn on that. As with long covid, the true and persistent costs of these changes -- virtualizing everything -- will only become apparent in the years to come."},{"id":30473620,"author":"kaczordon","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Any scientifically proven techniques to boost concentration?","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman episode on improving productivity, including the cathedral effect of high ceilings.","quote":"","text":"Dr Huberman from Stanford has a great weekly podcast where he talks about how to improve all parts of your body/mind for various activities(sleep, exercise etc.). Here’s one on improving productivity: [VIDEO] One of the tips from the video that I really love is called the cathedral effect. It was found that having high ceilings increases your creative capacity when doing an activity."},{"id":37149694,"author":"Kratacoa","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: How do you organize work and personal computers at your home office?","stance":"reference","why":"Huberman Lab episode on optimising workspaces.","quote":"","text":"I will not reply to the topic as I do not have any interesting bits to share, however Huberman Lab did a whole episode on optimising workspaces. [1][ [VIDEO] ]."},{"id":30602168,"author":"fergonco","month":"2022-03","story":"White noise improves learning by modulating activity in midbrain regions (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Huberman says white noise benefit does not apply to verbal recall.","quote":"","text":"Andrew Huberman saying in a podcast that it's not the case for verbal recall: [VIDEO] Interesting podcast, btw. got it from HN few days ago."}],"r":[["WOHPuY88Ry4",1],["p3JLaF_4Tz8",1]]},"eKtsY7hYTPg":{"wy":"Recommended in shader and rain-simulation threads as an engaging tutorial from a well-liked graphics-programming channel.","q":{"x":"The accompanying video tutorial[2] is super interesting too","i":23986648,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":43668943,"author":"FjordWarden","month":"2025-04","story":"Shadertoys Ported to Rust GPU","stance":"rec","why":"Shader coding videos from The Art of Code","quote":"I've always enjoyed watching The Art of Code[1]","text":"I've always enjoyed watching The Art of Code[1], Freya[2] also has a good number of videos on it. But that is just vertex shaders for things like Shadertoys. There is much more to computer graphics or GPGPU than this though, Im still learning about that myself X). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23986648,"author":"weinzierl","month":"2020-07","story":"Show HN: WebGL simulation of rainy autumn day/evening","stance":"rec","why":"Video tutorial for the Drive Home rain effect","quote":"The accompanying video tutorial[2] is super interesting too","text":"Not sure if it is the original but the source where I first saw this rain effect was on ShaderToy - The Drive Home by Martijn Steinrucken aka BigWings[1] The accompanying video tutorial[2] is super interesting too as well as the HN discussion at the time [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] Link to first part: [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":32948295,"author":"rgovostes","month":"2022-09","story":"Nightdrive","stance":"reference","why":"Making-of for The Drive Home shader","quote":"","text":"See also Martijn Steinrucken aka BigWings's The Drive Home: ShaderToy: [LINK] [WebGL] Video: [LINK] / Making of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18427917,"author":"dahart","month":"2018-11","story":"Making rain simulation as real as possible","stance":"reference","why":"Tutorial on the Drive Home rain shader","quote":"","text":"Is there any description or attribution for the shader? Are the visuals an improved version of Martijn Steinrucken's \"Drive Home\" shader [1], or a different approach? This one does seem more dynamic with more detail, it looks pretty good, and I like how the big drops take out the small spray. [1] shader: [LINK] video: [LINK] tutorial: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WO2X3oZEJOA",1],["kfM-yu0iQBk",1]]},"jf_ddGnum_4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a comprehensive walkthrough of the build-your-own-BitTorrent challenge and as an introduction to CodeCrafters.","q":{"x":"Jon Gjengset did a comprehensive stream going through the Build your own BitTorrent challenge","i":38429356,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38514601,"author":"joshka","month":"2023-12","story":"Show HN: Rqbit – bittorent CLI and desktop app written in Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Gjengset's videos build a BitTorrent client via a CodeCrafters challenge.","quote":"Take a watch of Jon Gjengset's vids on this","text":"Take a watch of Jon Gjengset's vids on this (there's a codecrafters challenge for building out a bittorent protocol that he's going through). - [VIDEO] - [LINK]"},{"id":38429356,"author":"sarupbanskota","month":"2023-11","story":"Show HN: I built a guided Build your own DNS Server challenge","stance":"rec","why":"Comprehensive stream going through the build-your-own BitTorrent challenge.","quote":"Jon Gjengset did a comprehensive stream going through the Build your own BitTorrent challenge","text":"(CodeCrafters team here). Out of curiosity, is there a reason why you prefer video format vs. interactive? e.g out of habit, when I'm having a meal, I like watching \"course videos\" or streams occasionally. Is yours similar? P.S. Jon Gjengset did a comprehensive stream going through the Build your own BitTorrent challenge [VIDEO] , if you're interested in Rust"},{"id":38237994,"author":"methou","month":"2023-11","story":"CodeCrafters CEO adds his paid service as a next step after finishing Rustlings","stance":"reference","why":"Video that introduced the commenter to CodeCrafters.","quote":"I found that CodeCrafters is quite fun after watching an video on Youtube[0]","text":"I found that CodeCrafters is quite fun after watching an video on Youtube[0], and shared it with my friends who might also be interested. The feedback I got was quite similar to the community: - really interesting idea, but - pricing is absurd, not gonna pay 100$ for these, and - their minimal paid plan at 40$/mo is a lie, with a fine print: Available as a one-time payment for 3 months or 1 year access. - Lack of in depth implementation of the projects, i.e for the free HTTP server project, it stopped at \"Post a file\", lack of beefy & fun stuff like for example HTTP 1.1 Pipelining I believe the value (the fun) it would provide me would be about the same level of Ace Combat 7 (I paid ~40$US), and the complexity is less than Bauder's Gate(69$SG). I would pay roughly US$40 for all of the content altogether at this moment. edit: added a link -- [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37946649,"author":"Jonhoo","month":"2023-10","story":"Build Your Own BitTorrent","stance":"reference","why":"Author points to a better version with chapters.","quote":"A better link is","text":"Oh hey, that's me! A better link is [VIDEO] which has chapter marks and has the power outage in the middle spliced away :p"},{"id":37968700,"author":"zoogeny","month":"2023-10","story":"\"Useless Ruby sugar\": Pattern matching (Pt. 1)","stance":"reference","why":"Stream shows using .context on calls ending in ? for error tagging.","quote":"I was watching the recent Rust stream by Jon Gjengset where he builds a BitTorrent implementation","text":"I was thinking of `guard` in Swift when I was writing but I haven't used it so I was unsure of its exact semantics. It is a bit of a shame it is only useable on optionals and not on arbitrary types. Nice to see a full pattern matching guard in Rust. I think the terminating `?` operator makes it a bit less necessary as I would guess a large portion of cases where I would want that early-return behavior I would just take advantage of that operator. But their choice to reuse `else` makes a lot of sense and the pattern reads well. I was watching the recent Rust stream by Jon Gjengset where he builds a BitTorrent implementation [1]. One thing he did frequently was add a `.context` method to function invocations that would terminate in `?`. My assumption just looking at that pattern is that it would allow the error system to tag the errors to help guide the programmer to the specific line the error was generated from, almost like an annotated stack trace. But again, I don't have direct experience programming large Rust applications. The reason I consider this topic it is because this kind of error handling would be ideal in Go. I often end up with the classic pattern: if value, err := someFunc(); !err { // value is useable } // handle err And I would just really, really like if Go would let me: if value, err := someFunc(); err != nil { // handle err } // value is useable But, I suppose due to Go block scoping `value` is only valid within the if block. 1. [VIDEO]"}]},"lfo8XHGFAIQ":{"wy":"Recommended in tree-planting and carbon removal threads as a sourced explanation of why greening deserts is not a simple climate fix.","q":{"x":"I recommend watching the video - it explains it better and goes into detail - it cites its own sources.","i":19715694,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":27709017,"author":"jarenmf","month":"2021-07","story":"Simple, solar-powered water desalination (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Why greening deserts may worsen climate change","quote":"A nice video about this","text":"Greening deserts is not a good solution for climate change. In fact it might worsen climate change due to albedo changes. A nice video about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19558196,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2019-04","story":"Restoring natural forests is the best way to remove atmospheric carbon","stance":"rec","why":"Examines terraforming the Sahara; feasible but costs outweigh benefits","quote":"The conclusion is that it's technically possible (like many things), but the cost far outweighs the benefit.","text":"This video (\"Can We Terraform the Sahara to Stop Climate Change?\") examines how we might do just that. The conclusion is that it's technically possible (like many things), but the cost far outweighs the benefit. It also introduces new climate risks. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19715694,"author":"Latty","month":"2019-04","story":"Planting 1.2T Trees Could Cancel Out a Decade of CO2 Emissions","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how greening deserts changes albedo and negates benefits","quote":"I recommend watching the video - it explains it better and goes into detail - it cites its own sources.","text":"I recently saw a video about doing this - apparently the desert is actually a really bad option if we want to combat climate change - the core problem is that the desert currently reflects a lot of light from the sun. If we cover it with forest or whatever, then suddenly we adjust the albedo of the earth and absorb more heat, negating some of the impact. Combined with the energy need to pump (and desalinate for trees - your suggestion of kelp would presumably help there) water, etc... make it not really work. Source: [VIDEO] I recommend watching the video - it explains it better and goes into detail - it cites its own sources."},{"id":18287434,"author":"dereke","month":"2018-10","story":"Carbon Removal Technologies","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've been watching waay too many youtube videos about reforestation, agriculture and land management recently and I think I've learned a little from it (links at the end). We no longer have the number of massive herds of animals that used to roam the plains/savannahs grazing, pooing and to a lesser extent escaping from predators. This has meant that the grasslands are no longer trampled on and \"fertilised\". This has caused the grasses to die back, the soil to degrade, to not hold water and to turn to desert. (see the sahara, the outback, parts of china and the usa). Subsequently we've tried to be really careful with the land and not over graze it etc. which tends to have the opposite effect than what is desired. Now I also looked into reforestation because I thought trees were the answer. Grow trees sequester carbon etc. But it turns out the cost of doing this £/$ and water (desalination) would actually be outweighed by both the albido effect (green trees absorb more sunlight than deserts that reflect it back) and that trees don't really grow fast enough to have the impact required. Getting back to the grasslands, it turns out that when you intensively drive a herd over grasslands the grass initially dies back but the root system expands, the plant grows quickly and sequesters carbon into the ground. It actually builds soil and traps carbon and it does it faster than previously thought. The ground is also more permeable to water so when big storms come it actually soaks up th"}],"r":[["coFIEH3vXPw",1],["LRhbcDzbGSU",1],["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1],["m64SWl9bfvk",1],["wICOlaQOpM0",1],["ELeZAKCN4tY",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1]]},"mOJlg8g8_yw":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a thorough, well-researched history, and one commenter finds such videos more exhaustive than Wikipedia's sourced articles.","q":{"x":"made a great video that covers the history of Tetris world records","i":43548627,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48817458,"author":"cyphar","month":"2026-07","story":"Odin, Wikipedia and Engagement Farming","stance":"rec","why":"More accurate and exhaustive than Wikipedia's sourced articles","quote":"I would argue that YouTube video about the topic like [1] are far more accurate and exhaustive than the articles referenced by Wikipedia","text":"> You lost me here. I mean, yes, there's serious fan community interaction around those subject which is unauthenticated and anonymous. But that's interaction and not research or journalism. Depends on the field. If you're talking about video games, their community (in particular, the associated modding and speedrunning communities) is probably second only to the original developers as a source for information (if not as good as) depending on the topic being discussed. For instance, the Wikipedia article on Tetris has a brief mention of the \"kid finally beat Tetris\" story from a few years ago. All of the primary information that is referenced by the articles is just reworded versions of research done by the community, some even just being dead links to old tweets (and I would argue that YouTube video about the topic like [1] are far more accurate and exhaustive than the articles referenced by Wikipedia, if Wikipedia really wants to prioritise accuracy). I don't necessarily disagree with the argument that you need to have a strong standard as otherwise you run the risk of accepting crap, but that shouldn't be taken to mean that the current standard actually provides the most accurate information you can get. Also, many articles about such topics feel like a weird kind of \"reputation laundering\" where the same person provides the same information in a different context (but now filtered through a non-subject-matter-expert) and now it is considered \"more reputable\". Yes, a third"},{"id":43548627,"author":"mega_dean","month":"2025-04","story":"Donkey Kong champion wins defamation case against Australian YouTuber Karl Jobst","stance":"rec","why":"Great history of Tetris world records","quote":"made a great video that covers the history of Tetris world records","text":"The youtube channel Summoning Salt made a great video that covers the history of Tetris world records: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40703087,"author":"quirino","month":"2024-06","story":"Creativity has left the chat: The price of debiasing language models","stance":"reference","why":"Long, well-researched history of Tetris world records","quote":"a very well researched two hour video on Tetris World Records","text":"Something I notice about text written by LLMs is how painfully obvious they are to identify sometimes. Recently I was watching a very well researched two hour video on Tetris World Records [1], but the sheer amount of text clearly \"enhanced\" by an LLM really made me uncomfortable. ChatGPT speaks a very specific, novel, dialect of English, which I've come to deeply despise. I'd always guessed it was caused by some kind of human interference, rather than a natural consequence of its training. That seems to be the point of this paper. [1] \"Summoning Salt - The History of Tetris World Records\" - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RCRTgtpC-Go",1],["vyenmLqJQjs",1],["YuIIjLr6vUA",1],["7wC42HgLA4k",1],["elvOZm0d4H0",1]]},"mXVGIb3bzHI":{"wy":"HN links it in threads about silence and quiet rooms, as a good first-hand look at the anechoic chamber that debunks the madness myth.","q":{"x":"Derek from Veritasium has a good video on it for the interested.","i":21098102,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21098102,"author":"Baeocystin","month":"2019-09","story":"Black Blacker Than Vantablack","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on the experience of the silent room","quote":"Derek from Veritasium has a good video on it for the interested.","text":"It's really not that bad. Some people find it unsettling, but many find it kind of relaxing. Derek from Veritasium has a good video on it for the interested. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14015167,"author":"white-flame","month":"2017-04","story":"A New Yorker's Quest for the Perfect Amount of Noise","stance":"rec","why":"People's reactions inside an anechoic chamber","quote":"There's a nice Veritasium video of an anechoic chamber here, with different people's reactions to it","text":"There's a nice Veritasium video of an anechoic chamber here, with different people's reactions to it: [VIDEO] I've been in sensory deprivation tanks, but never in an anechoic chamber (yet). However, I'm sure I'd love it. Many people live their life fleeing themselves, fleeing the thoughts in their head, and fleeing the realities they don't want to face. However, if you're emotionally healthy and are purposeful in establishing your identity and life direction, silence is simply golden. Not just auditory, but visual, social, and conceptual. Because of this, I don't think that there can be any singular preferable amount of noise for everybody. If there's some average, plenty of people will still find it distracting and grating, while others would find it too quiet and uncomfortable. Plus, I doubt it stays consistent even for a given individual. When you're stressed, you might want more quiet... or more distraction, depending on your preferences and reactions."},{"id":19282506,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2019-03","story":"The Quietest Room in the World","stance":"reference","why":"Filmed inside the room, says the madness claim is exaggerated","quote":"Veritasium did a video about this room (inside this room) and he says that this part is an exaggeration.","text":"Veritasium did a video about this room (inside this room) and he says that this part is an exaggeration. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16429617,"author":"tylerjwilk00","month":"2018-02","story":"There's a Persistent Hum in Windsor, Ontario, and No One Knows Why","stance":"reference","why":"Debunks the 45 minute myth in ultra-quiet chambers","quote":"You can view an interesting video on ultra-quiet anechoic chambers in this veritasium video","text":"The 45 minutes is apparently a myth. You can view an interesting video on ultra-quiet anechoic chambers in this veritasium video [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8928976,"author":"pluma","month":"2015-01","story":"The rise of fake engine noise","stance":"reference","why":"Example of near-absolute silence","quote":"","text":"Sure, but absolutes are rarely used in their literal meaning. \"Silence\" doesn't refer to the absence of any noise, it refers to a very low level of noise. The absence of any noise at all is very difficult to achieve (although you can come close: [VIDEO] ). The same goes for other absolutes like \"black\" (e.g. [LINK] is arguably \"blacker\" than other \"black\" materials). You're technically correct (the best kind of correct), but your point is moot. Very few words that describe absolutes can be used \"correctly\" in practice. The logical conclusion is that these words (despite their dictionary definition) don't refer to actual absolutes but rather extremes: silence is not the absence of noise, it's just the presence of a significantly smaller amount of noise. Heck, even words like \"pregnant\" aren't absolute in practice. With \"pregnant\" the implication commonly is \"will give birth\" (which is why we have euphemisms like \"expecting\"), so someone who is nearly due can be said to be \"more pregnant\" simply because they are closer to giving birth (and therefore also have a higher chance of delivering successfully) whereas someone within their first months of pregnancy may often not even want to disclose their state because so many things can still go wrong. In practice \"silent\" or \"black\" is no more absolute than \"cold\" -- and it would be silly to expect people to refer to zero kelvin when they say \"cold\", don't you think? Disclosure: I spent way too much time around linguists as a student"},{"id":8209165,"author":"rch","month":"2014-08","story":"This Is Your Brain on Silence","stance":"reference","why":"Someone tests an hour in a silent chamber and stays comfortable","quote":"He spent about an hour in one at BYU, and seemed comfortable enough throughout.","text":"Here's a video response^ by a guy who wanted to test this assertion. He spent about an hour in one at BYU, and seemed comfortable enough throughout. Interesting points: at around the 16 minute mark, he verbally estimates his time at 8 minutes; after the 45 min is up he asks for the lights to be turned on, and gets no response from the people outside the chamber. He calmly finds the door by using his cell phone as a light. ^ [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Sm5xF-UYgdg",1],["fCn8zs912OE",1],["kdsb2wwn-7g",1]]},"xg29TuWo0Yo":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as satire that illuminates strange transhumanist and digital immortality culture.","q":{"x":"one of the best way to understand some strange corners of the transhumanist culture is through the satire","i":26686607,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38933317,"author":"sudosteph","month":"2024-01","story":"YouTube Bans True Crime Videos That Reanimate Dead Children with AI","stance":"rec","why":"Great Adult Swim infomercial satirizing the idea.","quote":"There's also a great Adult Swim Infomercial satirizing this idea","text":"There's also a great Adult Swim Infomercial satirizing this idea, \"Live Forever As you Are Now with Alan Resnick\"[1]. Apparently it came out the same year as that Black Mirror episode (2013) ! [I] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26686607,"author":"anonymfus","month":"2021-04","story":"Deprecated Grandparents of America","stance":"rec","why":"Satire illuminating strange transhumanist culture.","quote":"one of the best way to understand some strange corners of the transhumanist culture is through the satire","text":"I think that one of the best way to understand some strange corners of the transhumanist culture is through the satire: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21041291,"author":"deftnerd","month":"2019-09","story":"A Son’s Race to Give His Dying Father Artificial Immortality (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Comedic fake infomercial on virtual immortality done badly.","quote":"For a comedic version of this concept, check out the fake infomercial","text":"For a comedic version of this concept, check out the fake infomercial \"Live Forever as You Are Now\" (1) Great example of the promise of virtual immorality done badly with bad software (1) [VIDEO]"},{"id":10892268,"author":"voynich61","month":"2016-01","story":"Tim Ferriss Major Depression Research","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"lmao @ ppl taking this guy seriously Highlights from his wikipedia page ( [LINK] ): > In 2001, Ferriss founded BrainQUICKEN, an online nutritional supplements company which made a product that was marketed as both BodyQuick and Brain Quicken.[22] It was claimed that this product would dramatically increase short term memory and reaction speed, taking effect within 60 minutes, but these claims made about this combination of ingredients have never been scientifically validated > Notable works: The 4-Hour Workweek, The 4-Hour Body, The 4-Hour Chef > his campaigns, such as dedicating his birthday to raising funds and heading LitLiberation to increase literacy worldwide > The New Yorker Magazine described Ferriss as \"this generation's self help guru\" EDIT: all these downvotes! How doesn't he remind you of this guy?: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["eLn6LC1RpAo",1]]},"ztAg643gJBA":{"wy":"Pointed to in Minetest and spherical-voxel-world threads as a striking example of what the open-source engine can do.","q":{"x":"A minetest developer just recently published a sphere-shaped planet version of minetest","i":12672630,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":45094357,"author":"gatane","month":"2025-09","story":"Making Minecraft Spherical","stance":"rec","why":"Attempt at a spherical voxel world in Minetest","quote":"This reminded me of another attempt, but in Minetest","text":"This reminded me of another attempt, but in Minetest: - [VIDEO] This person has another, more technical video where they talk about the math behind it btw"},{"id":12671095,"author":"dcposch","month":"2016-10","story":"Minetest – A free, open-source voxel game engine and game","stance":"rec","why":"Spherical world built in Minetest","quote":"People have made some VERY COOL stuff using MineTest.","text":"Man, just a few days ago I was bummed that the top comment on most HN stories is critical or dismissive, and now I'm part of the problem. -- People have made some VERY COOL stuff using MineTest. Check out spheretest: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26113332,"author":"huachimingo","month":"2021-02","story":"Show HN: Real-time multiplayer games with cubes. Early feedback on dev docs?","stance":"reference","why":"Mod faking spherical planets using a torus","quote":"Minetest had a mod to fake spherical planets using a torus.","text":"Minetest had a mod to fake spherical planets using a torus. Main video: [VIDEO] More in depth: [LINK] The main problem with minetest it is that it seems not complete/done on the art assets and main game, and the devs always say that is intentionally not a Minecraft clone yet people come searching one on the subgames."},{"id":12672630,"author":"TamHagmas","month":"2016-10","story":"Minetest – A free, open-source voxel game engine and game","stance":"reference","why":"Sphere-shaped planet version of Minetest","quote":"A minetest developer just recently published a sphere-shaped planet version of minetest","text":"A minetest developer just recently published a sphere-shaped planet version of minetest: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] That's just the thing I love about these smaller voxel games - they may not have a huge userbase, but there is innovation happening."}]},"AxnotgLql0k":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a careful, entertaining talk on low-level optimization that shows how textbook algorithms can be sped up.","q":{"x":"If you’re skeptical, I really do encourage you to watch, he’s not sloppy.","i":24425459,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24425459,"author":"dundarious","month":"2020-09","story":"Sentinels Can Be Faster","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How sentinels speed up textbook algorithms and how to apply them sensibly","quote":"If you’re skeptical, I really do encourage you to watch, he’s not sloppy.","text":"See Alexandrescu’s Fastware talk on how amazingly sentinels can improve even textbook algorithms, and how to apply the approach intelligently. E.g., for a linear search, overwrite the last element with the sought after value, and do a tiny bit of work to make that safe and to undo it before return. If you’re skeptical, I really do encourage you to watch, he’s not sloppy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26478341,"author":"simiones","month":"2021-03","story":"How C++ Resolves a Function Call","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Assuming you're talking about Concepts, I've heard Andrei Alexandrescu, for example, challenge that hope [0], but I'd be happy to hear this has been fixed. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12365392,"author":"renox","month":"2016-08","story":"What to use instead of std::set [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Alexandrescu describes a similar search trick for speeding up quicksort","quote":"describe the usage of this kind of search to speed up quicksort in his 'Fastware' (fun) video","text":"Andrei Alexandrescu describe the usage of this kind of search to speed up quicksort in his 'Fastware' (fun) video: [VIDEO] Discussed here: [LINK]"}],"r":[["ZTC_RxWN_xo",1],["r9eQth4Q5jg",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["5AqbCQuK0gM",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1]]},"Kas0tIxDvrg":{"wy":"During the early COVID-19 period, HN commenters linked it as the clearest quick explanation of what exponential growth means for an epidemic.","q":{"x":"Here is a neat video that helps put things in perspective for most any viewer.","i":22547954,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22522327,"author":"brownbat","month":"2020-03","story":"Show HN: Epidemic Simulator","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ties exponential growth to actual COVID-19 numbers.","quote":"Really helped it all click.","text":"Just watched the 3brown1blue video today, which talks about exponential growth and fixes it to the latest actual COVID-19 numbers. Really helped it all click. [VIDEO] Edit: separate HN discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":22565597,"author":"thomasmg","month":"2020-03","story":"Why some governments appear not to be acting on the Covid-19 threat","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains logistic curves of epidemics.","quote":"From this 3Blue1Brown excellent video","text":"To be exact, it's a \"logistic curve\", which initially is exponential. From this 3Blue1Brown excellent video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22596388,"author":"asix66","month":"2020-03","story":"Ask HN: What happens next after a successful lockdown?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Refresher on exponential growth.","quote":"should watch this great video by 3Blue1Brown.","text":"And those who don't understand, or need a refresher on, exponential growth should watch this great video by 3Blue1Brown. Exponential growth and epidemics: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22676414,"author":"Fej","month":"2020-03","story":"‘It looks like a war zone’: NJ docs say hospitals under siege, supplies low","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"(excellent video by the way)","text":"I live in Bergen County. There is a lack of panic - although I'm on the north side, opposite of the most infected parts. I went grocery shopping earlier today, there were a few people with masks but no one was really trying to keep a distance from others. Plenty of people there, perhaps a few less than usual. At times there are so many people that they have to restrict entry into the store and create a line outside (possibly to help with social distancing, but there are still too many people in the store for that to matter). It's the opposite of social distancing. The disaster in NYC, and the counties immediately north/east of Bergen (Westchester, Rockland) is going to spill over. We're not going to be able to handle it and we don't have the clout of NY to push for more resources. In a recent video, 3Blue1Brown wrote \"The only thing to fear is a lack of fear itself.\" This is what worries me. My parents are at risk as long-time smokers and if Valley runs out of ventilators and PPE, our major/usual option is out. (excellent video by the way) [VIDEO]"},{"id":22523535,"author":"officialjunk","month":"2020-03","story":"Show HN: HN reader that filters out COV**-19 and Coronavir** articles","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"please watch this video by 3blue1brown","text":"regardless of what you hear, china takes outbreaks very seriously and responds aggressively. the general population in china also takes it very seriously. normal behavior response in china looks like over reaction in the us. please watch this video by 3blue1brown [VIDEO] china is past its inflection point and we have yet to determine where our inflection point will be. everyone should be changing your behavior now. the fact that sars-cov didn’t get as far in 2002 as sars-cov-2 virus is currently going should be concerning. the us could ignore prior outbreaks because china is aggressive to stop it."},{"id":22547954,"author":"willis936","month":"2020-03","story":"Covid-19 is now officially a pandemic, WHO says","stance":"rec","why":"Puts pandemic spread in perspective for any viewer.","quote":"Here is a neat video that helps put things in perspective for most any viewer.","text":"But months ago they really couldn’t have known. We still don’t know how many will be infected. All we can do is take precautionary measures. The WHO messaging should have been stronger and earlier, but it isn’t their fault for not labeling a pandemic months ago. Granted, there was enough data and research done to squarely label this as a pandemic, and that’s really where I assign blame on them. Here is a neat video that helps put things in perspective for most any viewer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22581990,"author":"arendtio","month":"2020-03","story":"Open letter from Italy to the international scientific community","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what exponential means in this context.","quote":"Maybe they should include this 3Blue1Brown video to explain what 'exponential' means in this context","text":"Maybe they should include this 3Blue1Brown video to explain what 'exponential' means in this context: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22602177,"author":"carapace","month":"2020-03","story":"Shelter in place for Bay Area counties","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Have you seen this from 3Blue1Brown","text":"This is the time to resort to drastic measures to prevent drastic consequences. Have you seen this from 3Blue1Brown: [VIDEO] \"Exponential growth and epidemics\" ?"},{"id":22646259,"author":"ochoseis","month":"2020-03","story":"The Doctor Who Helped Defeat Smallpox Explains What's Coming","stance":"rec","why":"Explains logistic shape of pandemic curves and the inflection point.","quote":"3blue1brown did a good video explaining the logistic shape of pandemic curves","text":"3blue1brown did a good video explaining the logistic shape of pandemic curves: [VIDEO] My key takeaway was that once you see the inflection point, you know that you’ll have roughly 2x the number infections by the time it’s done."},{"id":22727679,"author":"vikramkr","month":"2020-03","story":"Covid-19 Sanity – Covid-19 papers from bioRxiv/medRxiv searchable and sortable","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how a 10% difference changes outcomes by orders of magnitude.","quote":"3 blue one brown had a great video on exponential growth","text":"3 blue one brown had a great video on exponential growth that detailed how a 10% difference in the numbers is the difference between being halfway there and having orders of magnitude to go. As I'm sure you're aware, the point of the numbers isn't to change the reality of the situation, it's to understand what that reality is. And the people that care are epidemiologists and leaders with decades of experience fighting pandemic after pandemic. You know, people that know what they're talking about? Might be worth trying to spend time actually learning how the people that are spending day and night trying to save your life and the lives of your loved ones do their work before dismissing them as useless. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23515045,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2020-06","story":"Capitol Hill Autonomous Zone","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"100k is a rough estimate. It might be higher, but will most likely be lower. The data will need to be pruned and carefully looked at. There are incentives for people to misclassify deaths at COVID19[0]. It will likely not get much higher than 100k as exponential growth doesn't continue indefinitely[1]. It's practically gone in Italy right now. 600k die every year from hearth disease. 700k died globally from mosquito transmitted illness[2]. There are tons of secondary effects. People in America have died of Malaria. Many have committed suicide (Including my best friend's flatmate). Many were told not to come to hospitals. Many hearth attacks were wrongly attributed to COVID if a patient tested positive for it. We've seen massive amounts of people at beaches in Florida, Texas and other places, as well as these protests and riots, and there are no massive spikes in fatalities 2~4 weeks after those events. The WHO said asymptomatic spread was rare, then waked it back (probably because of political pressure from Dr Fauchi), even though it was known asymptomatic spread was probably not a major transmission in February[3]! This entire thing has been one massive media manipulation, and in less than two days, the entire thing flipped from one global narrative to another, like a switch! [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":23160720,"author":"KerryJones","month":"2020-05","story":"Update to L.A.'s stay-at-home orders","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"there are plenty of other data\" -- would you be able to educate with some sources? I've been digging in deep and trying to keep an impartial opinion, but have not seen solid evidence that this is true -- or, as you say \"there is a study for every truth\", I've seen no study to indicate \"it is hardly dangerous\" (which also feels subjective in those words) > Also, so many deaths are being counted as covid when the patient already had a severe condition Yes... as many scientists talk about, COVID-19 has a much more severe effect in people with underlying conditions. Here is where Dr. Fauci, or the CDC as a whole talks specifically about underlying issues: [LINK] \"the numbers are way inflated\" is using generalized language for something that might be true, but even if you take a _very_ conservative view and consider 50% of the reports are false (and I've seen nowhere claiming it's anywhere near that high), the 50% of remaining deaths and complications are significantly high enough that it's far worse than the flu, and far worse if lockdown wasn't in play where some basic math indicates it would be 10x worse: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22530690,"author":"igravious","month":"2020-03","story":"Italy is extending its coronavirus quarantine measures to the entire country","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"People keep extrapolating that tens of millions will die, and I don't doubt that tens of millions surely would _if_ there were no lock-downs! (The subject of this article!) The whole point of locking down Wuhan/Hubei and then northern Italy and now the whole country and other places and cancelling large public gatherings and events is that local, regional, and national authorities start seeing the numbers soaring and initiate extreme mitigation strategies. We don't know how successful these strategies will be individually/collectively in the long run but if China is anything to go by it can severely retard the spread of the virus. To show I'm not spouting out of my derriere compare the logarithmic infection and death rates of China[1] and Italy[2] So you have to modify your formula: `7.8 billion * 0.6 * 0.03 * mitigation_factor = ?` We just don't know what the mitigation_factor is yet and because the spread is exponential any dent in the rate of spread brings the fatality number tumbling down[3]. I happen to believe this virus is a very serious global threat and I assure you that I am not one given to alarmism. But I also have never seen governments respond to the onset of an epidemic like this before and I'm nearly fifty and my mother says she can't remember anything like this in her lifetime. So yes, tens of millions absolutely could die but I'm willing to bet that between lock-downs and modern medicine tens of millions won't and I pray to, you know, $deity I am not wrong. "},{"id":22539038,"author":"sigstoat","month":"2020-03","story":"Covid-19 update and guidance to limit spread","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> That's my point: to observably flatten the curve, you need to go almost all the way there of killing it altogether. no, you don't. killing it completely would (hand-waving) require taking R0 < 1, and holding it there indefinitely. any reduction in R0 at all will flatten the curve, at least a bit. and small changes in the rate of growth can have a huge impact when you're dealing with (even temporarily) exponential growth. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22555259,"author":"ralusek","month":"2020-03","story":"Show HN: Tool for Visualizing Spread of Infectious Disease","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This model is mostly based off of the math discussed in 3Blue1Brown's video on exponents and epidemics.","text":"This is a simple tool for visualizing how massive the impact if minor behavioral changes can be in the spread of infectious diseases. This model is mostly based off of the math discussed in 3Blue1Brown's video on exponents and epidemics. [VIDEO] There are tons of ways this model could be improved, so please feel free to contribute. [LINK]"},{"id":22565194,"author":"csnover","month":"2020-03","story":"Ask HN: Covid-19 panic – what am I missing?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Even at a 0.7% mortality rate, you’re talking about a small percentage of a very big number. Up to 70% of the world’s population may become infected. [0] In that case, a 0.7% death rate converts into 37 MILLION deaths. The runs on toilet paper are the irrational panic of humans. I saw some comments on the internet early on in the outbreak telling people to buy anything they can’t live without for two weeks, with particular emphasis on toilet rolls, so that’s probably where that came from. With regards to the extreme measures, my understanding is that this is primarily to try to prevent health care systems collapsing. Around 20% [1] of people with COVID-19 are sick enough to need hospitalisation, and 1/4 of those (5% of total cases) require intensive care. The number of hospital beds is finite, and the number of ICU beds even smaller. Most health care systems already run near maximum capacity, and right now we’re barely past the peak of flu season [2], so there is very little buffer. The rate for new infections is dictated by the number of people exposed to an infected person and the probability of each exposure becoming an infection [3]. As such, the primary lever we have to try to “flatten the curve” is to reduce the number of people exposed to an infected person, and the way to do that from the top-down is to ban as many congregations of people as possible. Again, since this is exponential growth we’re talking about, even a tiny downward shift in the number of exposures res"},{"id":22570640,"author":"kangnkodos","month":"2020-03","story":"Trump Declares National Emergency","stance":"reference","why":"Math for checking exponential growth projections.","quote":"If you want to check the math, skip to about 3:20 on this video from 3Blue1Brown, Exponential growth and epidemics.","text":"At the current exponential rate of worldwide growth, by the end of June, we will be in one of these two scenarios: 1) Almost everyone in the world who is susceptible has it, or had it. 2) We implemented a way to slow down the global growth rate, or it slowed down by itself. One way or another, we will know in a just a few months. If you want to check the math, skip to about 3:20 on this video from 3Blue1Brown, Exponential growth and epidemics. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22581302,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2020-03","story":"Open letter from Italy to the international scientific community","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"All epidemiologists agree with this. All of them. They've been making public statements to that effect and signing letters reiterating the urgency of the situation. So what are you saying exactly? I don't understand what your point is! I'm agreeing with the epidemiologists. Their maths checks out. It's the same maths as my maths. It's the same maths as everyone's maths : [VIDEO] The only people I hear disagreements from are people who don't know how to use a calculator, Or they do, but haven't bothered. Nonetheless, they seem to have lots of time argue online, dismissing logical, coherent, and factual arguments without making any defensible statements of their own."},{"id":22585697,"author":"lkbm","month":"2020-03","story":"Please poke holes in my Covid-19 math (SF Bay Area)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows small growth rate changes make huge differences.","quote":"it's important to recognize that a small change in the growth rate does make a huge difference.","text":"> \"Or, multiply it by less if you want to take into account various amounts of social distancing we're all doing. I'll cut that number above down by a huge amount – 90% – on the assumption that we all learn to stay home and self-isolate immediately, just to be super aggressive on my assumptions about how humanity will rise to the occasion\" Dropping 90% is not a great way to calculate this. Look at the math for 61 days out (from March 8): [VIDEO] I don't think our social distancing and hand washing will be near enough to prevent millions of deaths, but it's important to recognize that a small change in the growth rate does make a huge difference. Estimating how much we can affect the growth rate is hard, but I think models should be based on a iffy guess on that value rather than an iffy guess on the resulting count. (Expert models certainly are.)"},{"id":22683536,"author":"danialtz","month":"2020-03","story":"Current estimates about the Covid-19 fatality rate may be too high","stance":"reference","why":"Short intro to exponential growth.","quote":"See for a short intro.","text":"you're right if one assumes a linear growth, but the reality is that it has an exponential growth. See [VIDEO] for a short intro."}],"r":[["2LyzBoHo5EI",1],["Hatav_Rdnno",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1],["jqjacHSTd48",1],["QsBT5EQt348",1],["ghtpJnrdgbo",1],["gkj_91IJVBk",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["rk6z1vP4Eo8",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["NHWjlCaIrQo",1],["eNliOm9NtCM",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["Mc_4Z1oiXhY",1],["vxopfjXkArM",1],["_IgqJr8jG8M",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["Z-9rLlFgcm0",1],["-8nqnOcoLqE",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1]]},"VcVfceTsD0A":{"wy":"Recommended in AI-risk threads as a way to understand the safety-advocate argument for halting development.","q":{"x":"it's worth a watch just to be more prepared for where things are likely headed","i":35601241,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35601241,"author":"ChatGTP","month":"2023-04","story":"A song that uses AI to clone the voices of Drake and The Weeknd has gone viral","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tegmark argues for halting AI development","quote":"it's worth a watch just to be more prepared for where things are likely headed","text":"Kind of hard to say this, but unless some miraculous slowdown happens, it's coming for the entire human experience by the look of it. As Max Tegmark put it recently, we're basically Neanderthals building homo sapiens. I don't care what people think about Lex as an interviewer, it's worth a watch just to be more prepared for where things are likely headed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35623099,"author":"hipjiveguy","month":"2023-04","story":"OpenAI’s CEO Says the Age of Giant AI Models Is Already Over","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Urges everyone to watch and think about AI dangers","quote":"watch this","text":"dudes. if none of you see it, it's because you're turning a blind eye, is the best way I can say it. the future of the 50's is about to show up, if, and only if, we're not so dumb as to ignore it. flying cars, killer robots, we can help decide, if we're only aware enough to do a bit of research, and say anything. your voice does matter :D watch this: [VIDEO] then, think, and then, think again, and then comment be optimistic, but be forewarned, and speak your voice"},{"id":38072211,"author":"AlbertoGP","month":"2023-10","story":"Biden to Issue First Regulations on Artificial Intelligence Systems","stance":"reference","why":"Lists the Tegmark podcast among sources in the AI debate","quote":"«Max Tegmark: The Case for Halting AI Development | Lex Fridman Podcast #371»","text":"As the sibling comment by idkwhatiamdoing says, Geoff is Geoffrey Hinton: «Geoffrey Hinton leaves Google and warns of danger ahead» [LINK] Yoshua is Yoshua Bengio: «Yoshua Bengio: How Rogue AIs May Arise» [LINK] LeCunn is replying to Max Tegmark: «Ask HN: What is the apocolyptic scenario for AI “breaking loose”?» [LINK] «Max Tegmark: The Case for Halting AI Development | Lex Fridman Podcast #371» [VIDEO]"},{"id":36129416,"author":"mitthrowaway2","month":"2023-05","story":"A.I. Poses ‘Risk of Extinction,’ Industry Leaders Warn","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Tegmark as a sincere safety advocate","quote":"I think there are a lot of people who are very sincere, like Max Tegmark.","text":"Indeed. It's probably what I would do if I were him. Or direct OpenAI entirely into AI safety research and stop doing capabilities research. I watched his interview with Lex Fridman,[1] and I didn't think he seemed very sincere. On the other hand I think there are a lot of people who are very sincere, like Max Tegmark.[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"4LqZdkkBDas":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as the go-to critique of Peterson when his positions or advice come up.","q":{"x":"informative, charitable, opinionated and entertaining (to me) video from Contrapoints","i":28474372,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28474372,"author":"cassepipe","month":"2021-09","story":"Peter Pan, Existentialist Fairy Tale? (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Charitable, entertaining take on who Peterson is","quote":"informative, charitable, opinionated and entertaining (to me) video from Contrapoints","text":"Well if you're posting about videos of Jordan Peterson I might as well post an informative, charitable, opinionated and entertaining (to me) video from Contrapoints about who he is and what he's doing. Enjoy : [VIDEO]"},{"id":17100462,"author":"mayniac","month":"2018-05","story":"Understanding Victimhood Culture","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I honestly don't think these interviewees know what they're talking about. The most objective argument I have to support this is that they keep talking about \"whites\" as a race. Never \"white Americans\", \"caucasians\", \"white europeans\" etc etc, just \"whites\" or \"white people\". Which is hugely problematic considering the entire concept of white as a race is incredibly modern. Before the 19th century, there wasn't a concept of whiteness, you belonged to a nation rather than a race. And it kind of makes sense, since where do you even draw the line on white? Are Egyptians white (Rami Malek...)? What about much of the middle east, many Syrians could pass for either white or Arabic? Or some western Asian countries, like most of the -istans? Ultimately, the idea of a white race was created on classist principles. The Irish were accepted as whites, despite being persecuted for centuries, as they were seen as \"better than black\". They weren't seen as white at all since that concept had not been created until mass immigration to the US. There's a good quote on this: [1] >The Irish, arriving poor, were in direct contact and competition with the African Americans whose social class they shared. They managed to become identified as white through their complicity in the oppression of blacks. Despite, or perhaps because of, calls from Daniel O’Connell in Ireland for the Irish in America to support the abolition of slavery, the American Irish felt their tenuous position in America would be we"},{"id":19653729,"author":"mercer","month":"2019-04","story":"Ask HN: What are good resources for life advice?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"And then there's the famouns Contrapoints video","text":"> The tough part for me to communicate here is how _ironic_ your assumption here is. You are _literally_ doing what Peterson argues is the feminine and chaotic. You are playing the role of the overprotective mother. I don't know if irony is the right word for it, but I find it interesting that you've been arguing that Petersons man-order/woman-chaos is somehow not normative and negative to women, and then go and use 'feminine and chaotic' in a pretty much purely negative way. What I find more interesting is that this exemplifies what bothers me about Peterson schtick. So much of what he says isn't actually saying all that much, because it's couched in \"one could argue that\", \"I'm just observing x\", \"an interesting thought\" style statements, while at the same time the very choice of statements seem to imply something nonetheless. The frustrating thing is that I find much of this 'implied' message rather concerning, but it's difficult to talk about because he (and his fans) can deny all of it. Or accuse me of reading too much into his words, vilifying him, etc. I think this comment expresses my unease with Peterson quite well (the whole thread/discussion is worth reading): [LINK] And then there's the famouns Contrapoints video: [VIDEO] To be clear, I'm not accusing you personally, of all this, just that your comment reminded me of these thoughts. Furthermore, I ultimately do not know the details of what Peterson believes (I mean, who does?), and if his book and talks help peopl"},{"id":19096442,"author":"mayniac","month":"2019-02","story":"Schools in England Introduce a New Subject: Mindfulness","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Contrapoints has a good breakdown of why the term \"postmodern neo-marxists","text":"Not specific to philosophers, but Contrapoints has a good breakdown of why the term \"postmodern neo-marxists\", which JBP uses a number of times throughout the book, shows a complete misunderstanding of modern philosophy. [1] Not related to 12 rules, but Cuck Philosophy goes into JBP's lectures where he references postmodernism and again appears to completely misunderstand the fundamentals. [2] [1] [VIDEO] (probably NSFW) [2] [LINK]"},{"id":18849326,"author":"pa7ch","month":"2019-01","story":"The money, job, marriage myth: are you happy yet?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This youtube channel contra points is a much better criticism then mine","text":"Not everything he says is off base and he does present the basics of clinical psychology well. Cognitive Behavioral Therapy is an interesting field one can gain a lot from studying. However many things he talks or just hints at can be questional and neither his expertise nor fairly presented. It helps his cause that many criticisms of him are terrible and involve many fabrications. This youtube channel contra points is a much better criticism then mine: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18744514,"author":"tomlock","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Favorite nonfiction books of 2018?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"understand some of the problems with his positions","text":"Also here's a great video that might help Peterson fans understand some of the problems with his positions: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cCOL7MC4Pl0",1],["svEuG_ekNT0",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["s254IPHXgVA",1]]},"9tJatdtv4jQ":{"wy":"Cited in threads on Chinese lending and the Belt and Road as an informed firsthand view from a former Greek finance minister.","q":{"x":"Here's a great clip of Varoufakis discussing the port of Piraeus","i":34868852,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34868852,"author":"throw_away1525","month":"2023-02","story":"Africa will get a new $1B spaceport in Djibouti","stance":"rec","why":"Varoufakis on Piraeus port and Chinese dealings","quote":"Here's a great clip of Varoufakis discussing the port of Piraeus","text":"Here's a great clip of Varoufakis discussing the port of Piraeus as a specific example (but also examples in Africa) of how reasonable the Chinese are to deal with: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27371261,"author":"FooBarWidget","month":"2021-06","story":"Huawei HarmonyOS and New Products Launch [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Varoufakis on Chinese non-interventionism","quote":"Lookup Yanis Varoufaski, former Greek minister","text":"What sort of historical precedence? Historical precedence from the Ming dynasty showed that even when they have a big fleet, they're not interested in colonizing territories. The Ming fleet only traded, not conquered. Look up Zhang He. Historical evidence from the Qing dynasty showed that in the 18th century, Chinese mining settlers in Malaysia tried to submit themselves under the rule of the Qing, but the Qing emperor refused, saying China is big enough already. Lookup Lanfang Republic. Historical precedence from the Republican era showed that the People's Republic has given up territory compared to the Republic. See all of outer Mongolia, which was ceded (and which Taiwan, a.k.a. the Republic, still formally claims). Historical evidence from a few years ago shows that China's policy is non-interventionist. Lookup Yanis Varoufaski, former Greek minister: [VIDEO] Lookup Gyude Moore, \"China in Africa: an African perspective\" Where is the evidence that we can expect China to behave like the US?"},{"id":27685950,"author":"a0-prw","month":"2021-06","story":"A Chinese-Built Highway Drove Montenegro into Crippling Debt","stance":"reference","why":"Varoufakis comparing Chinese and EU debt conditions","quote":"Yanis Varoufakis on China vs EU conditions","text":"Note: \"Soc, the former justice minister. ''But I don't think this is a problem from China. It is our bad decision.'' They can almost certainly renegotiate for easier conditions: Yanis Varoufakis on China vs EU conditions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24907530,"author":"FooBarWidget","month":"2020-10","story":"Jack Ma's Ant Group set for record $34B market debut","stance":"reference","why":"Varoufakis on China's non-interventionist lending","quote":"Yanis Varoufakis, former Minister of Finance of Greece, who has participated in the Belt and Road Initiative","text":"I think saying that \"they are both gunning to be #1\" and \"undermining other countries\" is more of a projection than reality. Yanis Varoufakis, former Minister of Finance of Greece, who has participated in the Belt and Road Initiative, makes it quite clear that China is \"non-interventionist to the point where we can't imagine it\": [VIDEO] The current trajectory of sinophobia based on incorrect, overblown and unrealistic readings of CCP, indeed does not have a happy ending. That's why the trajectory should be changed. > the nationalism isn't just some reaction by their citizens to unfairness, it's 100% cultivated by their gov't on purpose. I'm sorry, try talking to large number of mainland Chinese people first, they'll disagree with you. Blaming everything on \"but Chinese people are brainwashed\" is just an attempt to deny reality. You have an extreme view of the CCP's brainwashing capabilities. If they're that good at brainwashing, they would have eradicated many more down-to-earth societal problems a long time ago, such as the societal disdain for vocational jobs. They've been trying to get rid of the social stigma of vocational schools for years now, in order to prevent the job market from being crowded out by too many university graduates, but they've only had limited success. Try watching some actual Chinese propaganda for a while. You'll see that it's very primitive, not very convincing, and frankly quite bad. I don't know why everybody keeps thinking they're brainwashing "}]},"DOW_kPzY_JY":{"wy":"HN people link it as a joke about blindly trusting navigation or automated systems.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite scenes on TV","i":20297293,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20297293,"author":"behringer","month":"2019-06","story":"Drivers followed a Google Maps detour and ended up stuck in an empty field","stance":"rec","why":"Hilarious scene of blindly following GPS.","quote":"One of my favorite scenes on TV","text":"One of my favorite scenes on TV: [VIDEO] The title of the video really ruins it, but it was amazingly funny when I saw it the first time not knowing what to expect :D"},{"id":36186260,"author":"Hrun0","month":"2023-06","story":"XB, or eXtreme Bullshitting: a less misleading name for LLMs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I've seen a few examples on youtube of people following the GPS to their own peril ( the last one being, driving in the sea directly) Was it this one? [VIDEO]"},{"id":29766767,"author":"paganel","month":"2022-01","story":"Boeing 777 departing Dubai nearly had a major incident after takeoff","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> You don't fly the flight director command bars when they are telling you to stay on the ground and you're doing 215 knots. This reminds me of the (in)famous \"take a right\" sketch from The Office [1]. Granted, that was a TV show and they were \"piloting\" a car, not a 777. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"E8kUJL04ELA":{"wy":"Cited whenever commenters want to note that Conway did not enjoy being known mainly for the Game of Life.","q":{"x":"Conway has said GOL is not something he is particularly pleased got as famous as it did.","i":40132766,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":9964861,"author":"tdicola","month":"2015-07","story":"John Conway – The world’s most charismatic mathematician","stance":"rec","why":"Interviews with Conway showing him as a genuinely great guy","quote":"has done some great interviews with John Conway that are definitely worth watching","text":"Numberphile, an awesome youtube channel on its own, has done some great interviews with John Conway that are definitely worth watching: [LINK] , [VIDEO] , [LINK] He really comes across as a genuinely great guy."},{"id":40132766,"author":"cauliflower99","month":"2024-04","story":"AMA: I'm Dave Greene, an accidental expert on Conway's Game of Life","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Conway has said GOL is not something he is particularly pleased got as famous as it did.","text":"Conway has said GOL is not something he is particularly pleased got as famous as it did. (Ref: [LINK] ) Why do you think that is? Edit: This is the video I meant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29663478,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2021-12","story":"A Lisp Interpreter Implemented in Conway's Game of Life","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"saying (roughly) that it had all been explored and proved and other work of his was more interesting","text":"One that Conway didn't like much, saying (roughly) that it had all been explored and proved and other work of his was more interesting - [VIDEO] in this Numberphile interview with him."},{"id":24329229,"author":"Akronymus","month":"2020-08","story":"The Recursive Universe","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"He seems to have come to terms with it.[1]","text":"> I know Conway was slightly resentful that game of life overshadowed some of his other work in the public's imagination, but I've always been entranced by it. He seems to have come to terms with it.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22855228,"author":"brownbat","month":"2020-04","story":"Remembering Conway","stance":"reference","why":"Conway's Numberphile appearances","quote":"VSauce tweeted a playlist of Conway's appearances on Numberphile","text":"VSauce tweeted a playlist of Conway's appearances on Numberphile, including that one: [VIDEO] I found the one you posted especially moving, but the others were good too."},{"id":14470448,"author":"splawn","month":"2017-06","story":"Oh My Gosh, It’s Covered in Rule 30s","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Conway doesn't really enjoy being solely associated with GOL and the fame it brings[0]","text":"I think it is funny, Conway doesn't really enjoy being solely associated with GOL and the fame it brings[0].. In stark contrast with Wolfram's ego. Perhaps this arrangement actually works out best for both of them. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XRXwWbo_mX0",1],["ON0A1dsQOV0",1],["ILNs1GChGDk",1]]},"FKXOucXB4a8":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the classic demonstration of audio recovery from video in surveillance and privacy discussions.","q":{"x":"it can be possible to extract audio from video, by careful processing of the small motions the sound induced in objects in the frame","i":15957009,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15957009,"author":"eesmith","month":"2017-12","story":"Prostitution cases dismissed because cops’ cameras mistakenly recorded audio","stance":"rec","why":"Recovering sound from tiny motions in video, even consumer cameras","quote":"it can be possible to extract audio from video, by careful processing of the small motions the sound induced in objects in the frame","text":"\"security cameras outside of stores\" would be in public areas, yes? The Lifehacker link you pointed to says \"Generally speaking, you have the right to record video in all public spaces without need of consent.\" Now for the fun part - it can be possible to extract audio from video, by careful processing of the small motions the sound induced in objects in the frame. See [VIDEO] . High-speed camera is best, but at the end they show how a regular consumer-level camera can also be used."},{"id":46980053,"author":"spyder","month":"2026-02","story":"WiFi Could Become an Invisible Mass Surveillance System","stance":"reference","why":"Potato chip bag becomes a microphone via high speed camera","quote":"any vibrating reflective object like a potato chips bag can become a microphone","text":"with a high speed camera any vibrating reflective object like a potato chips bag can become a microphone: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23968731,"author":"bbbobbb","month":"2020-07","story":"Spies can eavesdrop by watching a light bulb's vibrations","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier similar experiment recovering audio from visual vibrations","quote":"Here similar experiment, 6 years ago","text":"Here similar experiment, 6 years ago: [VIDEO] If you think that actual government agency cannot do even more easily with better equipment in 2020 you're kidding yourself."},{"id":18265891,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2018-10","story":"Show HN: Lipreading with Deep Learning","stance":"reference","why":"Recovering audio from video","quote":"You can do it with video too, even","text":"You can do it with video too, even: [VIDEO] Also, this is only tangentially related, but you can also see through walls using WiFi: [LINK]"},{"id":16245545,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2018-01","story":"If you watch closely enough, everything is a speaker","stance":"reference","why":"Audio captured from 60fps DSLR video via rolling shutter","quote":"They show audio captured from a 60fps video from a DSLR exploiting the rolling shutter.","text":"See this - [VIDEO] They show audio captured from a 60fps video from a DSLR exploiting the rolling shutter. I'm not sure if you can use less than 60fps though?"},{"id":9269898,"author":"lawry","month":"2015-03","story":"Periscope by Twitter","stance":"reference","why":"Sound recovery from silent video","quote":"it's still possible to extract audio from the video you are streaming","text":"Remember even if you mute your microphone, it's still possible to extract audio from the video you are streaming: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-iCIZQX9i1A",1],["KDpNQQqdSh8",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1]]},"Gk-9Fd2mEnI":{"wy":"HN cites it as a primary source for Jobs's own views, pulling timestamps on topics like consultants, objects, NeXT's factory and Apple retail.","q":{"x":"Another interesting resource is Jobs speaking at the MIT Sloan School of Management","i":29643648,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29643648,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2021-12","story":"Apple and NeXT, 25 Years Ago this week","stance":"rec","why":"Jobs on NeXT pivoting to enterprise rapid application development","quote":"Another interesting resource is Jobs speaking at the MIT Sloan School of Management","text":"Another interesting resource is Jobs speaking at the MIT Sloan School of Management while NeXT was in the process of pivoting to enterprise rapid application development. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42766036,"author":"adeptima","month":"2025-01","story":"Robotics and ROS 2 Essentials","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on ownership and consequences of recommendations","quote":"","text":"I knew the guy who worked with Jobs and hated him with passion. Let's keep the useful image parts. “How many of you are from manufacturing companies?” [Some hands go up.] “Oh, excellent. Where are the rest of you from? Okay, so how many are from consulting?” [A number of hands go up.] “Oh, that’s bad. Yeah, the mind is too important to waste—you should do something.” “I think that without owning something over an extended period of time—like a few years—where one has a chance to take responsibility for one’s recommendations, where one has to see those recommendations through all action stages, accumulate scar tissue for the mistakes, pick oneself up off the ground, and dust oneself off, one learns only a fraction of what one can.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":34800171,"author":"gofreddygo","month":"2023-02","story":"Consultancies know less than they claim and cost more than they seem to","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs's rant on consultants","quote":"(skip to 15:20 if link doesn't automatically)","text":"Instantly reminded me of Steve Jobs' quip and rant [1] on consulting and consultants. (skip to 15:20 if link doesn't automatically) [VIDEO]"},{"id":23619362,"author":"BenoitEssiambre","month":"2020-06","story":"The End of OS X","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs's own complaints about the Apple he left","quote":"","text":"This somewhat echoes some of Steve Job's own complaints about the Apple he was kicked out of: [VIDEO] Also note that at the time he was creating a NeXT, a Unix company."},{"id":18692411,"author":"newnewpdro","month":"2018-12","story":"Apple Computers Used to Be Built in the U.S. It Was a Mess","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs describes NeXT's automated factory","quote":"","text":"In a talk Jobs gave at MIT [1] he described the NeXT automated manufacturing facility in a positive light. I can only begin to imagine how much better it could be done today than back then. Articles like this one like to point out the number of people involved in the Chinese iPhone manufacturing as if that's somehow indicative of something other than simply using cheap labor instead of automation. If we wanted to manufacture this stuff in the USA, we definitely could, and it would be done using fewer people through automation. My understanding is the core reason we don't do manufacturing here anymore is it's a lot cheaper to do it where industrial pollution goes unchecked, where regulatory bodies are immature or entirely nonexistant. When China gets its shit together enough to protect its lands and people from the toxic output of all this industrial activity, large-scale manufacturing will move on to the next victim. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18266866,"author":"mandeepj","month":"2018-10","story":"67% of workers earning over $100k see themselves quitting in the next 6 months","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on consulting and career","quote":"","text":"Consulting is like building a castle and giving it away to someone else to rule and enjoy the fruits. I refuse to do it now. With each new contract, you start from scratch, again. After a while, you'll realize - you are just hitting a reset switch on your career, with each new contract that you pick up. I'm glad I share my thoughts with this very smart man - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18271660,"author":"panax","month":"2018-10","story":"OOP Is Dead, Long Live OOP","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs describing objects coming with all their dependencies","quote":"","text":"Steve said it too, but he said it like he was proud of it: [VIDEO] \"You need all the objects that come with it to make it work\""},{"id":17610962,"author":"sah2ed","month":"2018-07","story":"The secret call to Andy Grove that may have helped Apple buy NeXT","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs explains why Apple stores were necessary","quote":"","text":"> I fought with him over stores as did virtually everyone on the board of Apple, and it turned out he was right. He actually goes into depth about why Apple stores were necessary in a talk he did at MIT in 1992 [0][1]. Was it that he was not nearly as articulate in person with his team, as he was in this video? EDIT: Luckily, the transcript is available. The money quote is: \" ... current distribution channels for the computer industry over the last several years have lost their ability to create demand. They can fulfill demand, but they can't create it. If a new product comes out, you're lucky if you can find somebody at the computer store that even knows how to demo it. So the more innovative the product is, the more revolutionary it is and not just an incremental improvement, the more you're stuck [in getting people to try learn about it enough to try it out].\" Here's a fuller quote: There are some things I can't talk about here. In addition to that, if you look at how we sell our computers right now, we have a sales force in the US of about 130 professionals in the field out selling NeXT computers. They spend 90% of their time selling NeXTSTEP software, and then 10% of their time selling the hardware. In other words, if they can get the customer to buy into NeXTSTEP, then they're going to sell the hardware, because right now we have the only hardware it runs on. So they are out there selling NeXTSTEP right now. And this is what is required to launch a new innovative produc"},{"id":16837783,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2018-04","story":"What did Alan Kay and Steve Jobs talk about at the 2007 iPhone keynote?","stance":"reference","why":"1992 Q&A on Mac and NeXT marketing pivots","quote":"Video of the talk has recently been made available.","text":">that was something I respected about Jobs (and a few other industrialists) -- the combined ability to understand new technology, visualize how it could be brought to a broad market, and then do it successfully Jobs participated in a Q&A at the MIT Sloan School of Management in 1992, and one of the topics he discussed were the marketing pivots needed for the Mac and Next once he figured out what real world tasks those platforms excelled at that their competition did not. (desktop publishing and enterprise rapid application development) Video of the talk has recently been made available. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vwCdKU9uYnE",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["43CMV8KIs3E",1],["s0ukXDnWlTY",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1],["29RkHH-psrY",1],["pBiWtJJN5zk",1],["JRcXUuQYR90",1],["rHP-OPXK2ig",1]]},"HT_KdFCVEdc":{"wy":"Linked in threads on the Idaho and Colorado safety stop as evidence that drivers already roll stop signs.","q":{"x":"Oh the Urbanity! Recently had a great episode on this as well","i":33520428,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33520428,"author":"joshlemer","month":"2022-11","story":"Idaho Stop","stance":"rec","why":"Episode on cyclists rolling stop signs and motorist hypocrisy.","quote":"Oh the Urbanity! Recently had a great episode on this as well","text":"I'll also add that the anger of motorists towards cyclists rolling through stop signs is completely irrational when you take into consideration that approximately 100% of motorists also run through almost all stop signs, so long as they aren't blocked by an other car. This twitter video exemplifies it pretty well [LINK] We just take for granted that cars will blast through stop signs at a pretty high speed (maybe 10 - 20 km/h?). But when we see a cyclist do it, for some reason there seems to be some sort of optical or logical illusion that makes it stick out to us more. If you look at the speed these cars are going at, a lot of them are probably actually going at around the full cruising speed of a casual cyclist. So, considering most cyclists would actually slow down somewhat to roll through a stop sign, they're probably actually going faster. Oh the Urbanity! Recently had a great episode on this as well [VIDEO]"},{"id":36481295,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2023-06","story":"U.S. pedestrian deaths reach a 40-year high","stance":"reference","why":"Observations of driver behavior at intersections in Ottawa.","quote":"I saw a video a while back with some observations on this in Ottawa","text":"It's not as comprehensive as you're probably looking for, but I saw a video a while back with some observations on this in Ottawa: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31048720,"author":"ThatGeoGuy","month":"2022-04","story":"The Colorado Safety Stop is the law of the land","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the Idaho stop and why drivers do it anyway.","quote":"initially called an Idaho stop","text":"This is actually what this law is about. The difference between a cyclist at a stop sign and your average vehicle is visibility. Cyclists don't have blind spots the way cars & trucks do. So if you can reasonably see that there is no immediate traffic, you don't have to wait at an intersection and risk hoping that another driver at a 4 way stop will respect the stop sign. This whole law is being called the \"Colorado Safety Stop\" but was initially called an Idaho stop [1][2]. It resulted in a 23% reduction in bicycle crashes in intersections with STOP signs only [3]. Even if you think the results are not intuitive, they are certainly backed by data. Making this a law just means cyclists won't be harassed by law enforcement for doing what is statistically known to be safer. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"}],"r":[["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["ScEPu1cs4l0",1]]},"ILQepXUhJ98":{"wy":"HN links it as a pointed comedic statement of why education is seen as broken and unlikely to be fixed.","m":[{"id":8861198,"author":"retrogradeorbit","month":"2015-01","story":"The Tough Decision to Leave the Classroom","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin routine on why education is broken.","quote":"The Reason Education Sucks","text":"The Reason Education Sucks: [VIDEO] Edit: for those wanting something less cynical and more serious, I recommend some John Taylor Gatto: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":32572974,"author":"WhyDoIThink","month":"2022-08","story":"Why is rent skyrocketing?","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's monologue on the owners of the country and education.","quote":"","text":"This video titled \"The Reason Education Sucks\" from George Carlin will explain why rent is skyrocketing: [VIDEO] Here is a quote from that video: \"But there's a reason. There's a reason. There's a reason for this, there's a reason education sucks, and it's the same reason that it will never, ever, ever be fixed. It's never gonna get any better. Don't look for it. Be happy with what you got. Because the owners of this country don't want that. I'm talking about the real owners now, the real owners, the big wealthy business interests that control things and make all the important decisions. Forget the politicians. The politicians are put there to give you the idea that you have freedom of choice. You don't. You have no choice. You have owners. They own you. They own everything. They own all the important land. They own and control the corporations. They've long since bought and paid for the senate, the congress, the state houses, the city halls, they got the judges in their back pockets and they own all the big media companies so they control just about all of the news and information you get to hear. They got you by the balls. They spend billions of dollars every year lobbying, lobbying, to get what they want. Well, we know what they want. They want more for themselves and less for everybody else, but I'll tell you what they don't want: They don't want a population of citizens capable of critical thinking. They don't want well informed, well educated people capable of critical "},{"id":12375156,"author":"mSparks","month":"2016-08","story":"Why China won’t own next-generation manufacturing","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin on why education will never be fixed.","quote":"","text":"Its not a mystery at all. China has tons of free world class education based purely on academic ability and a couple of billion people educated to a world class standard. The US has virtually no education system, what does exist is expensive and not practically oriented. Basic supply and demand. US has high demand for skills and totally non competitive supply. (even in tech now, which is why they need the H1B visa thing) But dont expect it to get fixed. It's that way on purpose. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12387870,"author":"mSparks","month":"2016-08","story":"California bans ITT tech from accepting new students","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin on the state of education.","quote":"","text":"This whole premise of this story has me wearing a tin foil hat. [tldr] [VIDEO] Soooo many questions. so few answers. I'll start with my immediate thoughts. High dropouts are not absolutely a bad thing. In fact its pretty normal in all western schools that have zero focus on \"how to learn\". The \"problem\" is clearly earlier on in the education system, wherby students are reaching college age not knowing \"how to learn\", and suddenly finding its important. Next up - the cost of learning/knowledge material. I know of textbooks from the US being priced in the multi thousand dollar range (or simply not available for student purchase). \"Teachers/Lecturers\" aren't that important (I say that having graduated with a joint 1st from a top UK university having acquired a lot of warnings criticizing me for hardly ever attending lectures, but I wasn't skipping the material, I was skipping lecturers reading from textbooks). All that said, I am left feeling these stories are more about attacking education that provides chances for the lower classes in the US to become middle and upper classes. Nothing about this ITT tech story addresses this one way or the other. Where are the employment stats for those who successfully graduate? Where are the interviews with graduates? I just see a ton of hyperbole he said she said. And whenever I come across that I automatically assume its a political power based decision, rather than a rational \"good for everyone\" one. I know that all sounds a bit confused "}]},"LHvR1fRTW8g":{"wy":"HN links it as the quick explanation of why you cannot just throw things into the Sun.","q":{"x":"as explained in the following minutephysics video (3 minutes): Hitting the Sun is HARD.","i":34938788,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":21485869,"author":"whoopdedo","month":"2019-11","story":"NASA Flew Gas Detectors Above California, Found ‘Super Emitters’","stance":"rec","why":"Explains delta-v needed to descend into the Sun.","quote":"It's easier to throw the garbage out of the solar system than it is to descend into the Sun.","text":"Another explanation[1] that shows how it's not just the cost of escaping Earth gravity (which is high enough already) but the delta-v required to de-orbit. It's easier to throw the garbage out of the solar system than it is to descend into the Sun. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34938788,"author":"laserlight","month":"2023-02","story":"Mysterious object is being dragged into the black hole at the Milky Way’s center","stance":"reference","why":"Three-minute explanation of why hitting the Sun is hard.","quote":"as explained in the following minutephysics video (3 minutes): Hitting the Sun is HARD.","text":"That's right, as explained in the following minutephysics video (3 minutes): Hitting the Sun is HARD. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27577245,"author":"EdSchouten","month":"2021-06","story":"A dwarf planet coming within 11 AU of the sun over the next 10 years","stance":"reference","why":"Hitting the Sun is hard for small objects.","quote":"It's apparently pretty hard to hit the Sun","text":"It's apparently pretty hard to hit the Sun: [VIDEO] (Though that video mostly talks about small objects like rockets. Maybe it's a different story for dwarf planets?) That said... Keep in mind that this is about a dwarf planet orbiting the Sun. It's 11 AU out, so twice as far from the Sun as Jupiter is."},{"id":20156834,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2019-06","story":"NASA ‘Snoopy’ lunar module likely found 50 years after being jettisoned","stance":"reference","why":"Why hitting the sun requires cancelling orbital velocity.","quote":"Canadarm can't achieve the approx 29 km/s needed to cancel orbital velocity around the sun","text":"... wait, to throw it into the sun. Okay, nice try there! Everyone on HN should know the Canadarm can't achieve the approx 29 km/s needed to cancel orbital velocity around the sun in the vicinity of Earth. [VIDEO] 'doh, you got a reaction out of me anyhow!"}]},"LUCCR4jAS3Y":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as primary-source audio in geopolitical arguments about US involvement in Ukraine, favoring listening to the call over reading the transcript.","q":{"x":"I also fully support not just reading the call transcript, but also listening to the leaked call so you get Nuland's firm tone.","i":44345765,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44345765,"author":"lenkite","month":"2025-06","story":"U.S. bombs Iranian nuclear sites","stance":"rec","why":"Hear the leaked call and Nuland's tone directly","quote":"I also fully support not just reading the call transcript, but also listening to the leaked call so you get Nuland's firm tone.","text":"I also fully support not just reading the call transcript, but also listening to the leaked call so you get Nuland's firm tone. I would reserve very skeptical judgement on the \"annotations\". Those weren't part of the call. Listen to: [VIDEO] > No, that does not mean they have \"decided\" who is going to form the next government. That claim is just Russian propaganda Anyone with a rational brain who separates themselves from biases and emotions and carefully listens to the call would realize there is no propaganda involved here. Also, for better clarity of judgement, please perform a thought exercise and consider what would happen if a foreign government's members were discussing the personal choices , makeup and \"talks\" for members of the next American government."},{"id":48328530,"author":"znort_","month":"2026-05","story":"Canada to order military plane fleet from Sweden in shift from US suppliers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> the wording is open to interpretation, but not in the case of Ukraine. Nobody considers the Ukrainians and Russians living in the Donbas as a separate ethnicity or people; not they themselves, nor anyone else. that is an unsubstantiated claim that's actually false and doesn't even preclude the right of any group of people in sufficient majority to exercise self-govern, by their mere will and manifestation, so i'm not going to even bother debunking it (again). > There was no coup. \"Ukrainian MPs have voted to oust President Viktor Yanukovych and hold early presidential elections on 25 May.\" (BBC, 22 February 2014). that vote didn't reach the required 3/4 of the threshold required by the constitution for impeachment. the subterfuge employed to ignore that requirement was that yanukovich had allegedly relinquished his position, which actually means he had to flee the country for his life because of the threat of the nazi nationalists (armed by the us and on the loose around kiev), as did many of the 122 deputies that weren't even present in the voting. so you arm extremist groups for years, groom ngos to stirr up discontent funded by usaid and ned, then weaponize legitimate protests with violence, which then spirals out of control as the legitimate government overreacts in the crackdown, then you make huge international media fuzz about it, then you threaten the president to flee, then you hold a sham vote to impeach him, then you instate a \"provisional\" government you already"},{"id":44544887,"author":"mjburgess","month":"2025-07","story":"Bad Actors Are Grooming LLMs to Produce Falsehoods","stance":"reference","why":"Leaked Nuland call cited in geopolitical argument","quote":"I'll just leave the Nuland leak","text":"For reference, John Joseph Mearsheimer (/ˈmɪərʃaɪmər/; born December 14, 1947)[3] is an American political scientist and international relations scholar. He is R. Wendell Harrison Distinguished Service Professor at the University of Chicago. I'll just leave the Nuland leak: [VIDEO] But yeah, lol. This is russian propaganda. You can find Kissinger giving this analysis in the 90s"},{"id":43257391,"author":"ein0p","month":"2025-03","story":"TSMC expected to announce $100B investment in U.S.","stance":"reference","why":"Leaked Nuland-Pyatt call about picking Ukraine's government","quote":"","text":"There's that emotional thinking again. _We_ started that war when we funded a coup there in 2013 and hand-picked[1] their rabidly anti-Russian government. We also funded and condoned their neo-Nazis, without whom none of this would work [2]. Mostly Russian-speaking Eastern Ukraine disagreed with that kind of thing. After all, it was mostly them who elected the president that we overthrew. West Ukraine started shelling east trying to subdue them, calling it an \"anti-terror operation\". Russia provided \"lethal aid\". Things escalated. The conflict did not start in 2022. Suggesting that we can just go ahead and build tactical nuke bases right next to where ~70% of Russia's population lives, and Russia should just roll over and let it happen, is idiotic and reckless. Here's John Mearsheimer telling you _exactly_ what was going to happen, in 2015: [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}]},"NVVsdlHslfI":{"wy":"Cited in threads about startups, VC and private equity as an insider's description of how VC economics work, though commenters note he is self-serving.","q":{"x":"This video is a good explanation of how the VC world actually works.","i":25534898,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25534898,"author":"nowherebeen","month":"2020-12","story":"How to Start a Startup (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the VC world actually works","quote":"This video is a good explanation of how the VC world actually works.","text":"> all this money and all this failure, its going to end badly, these investors are gonna get burnt No, I think all these startups employees with families to feed are going to be out of a job. VCs fund startups that shouldn’t have been funded in the first place. They create a market that artificially props up unsustainable businesses. This video is a good explanation of how the VC world actually works. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38334596,"author":"stillwithit","month":"2023-11","story":"More on Sam Altman's Ouster from OpenAI","stance":"reference","why":"Linked alongside claim about Altman's circular funding funnel","quote":"","text":"Yeh he was setting up another funding funnel MS -> OpenAI -> his AI chips [VIDEO] Make it appear on paper like there’s proper separation of concerns but really building a web to form monopoly with MS’s backing because they can’t get in trouble if some startup is doing it. Just more shell company games to play the system, screw everyone else by using their churches to claim dominion over all of tech. These guys are too obvious now. The elders who don’t know better are dying off leaving behind a more technically literate voter base."},{"id":31585903,"author":"jiveturkey","month":"2022-06","story":"Amundi warns that parts of private equity market resemble ‘Ponzi schemes’","stance":"reference","why":"Chamath discussed this in 2019, with a self-serving caveat","quote":"Warning, video is self-serving but simplistically, this is part of it.","text":"Not a new understanding. Chamath Palihapitiya talked about this in 2019, and others before then although I don't have references. [VIDEO] Warning, video is self-serving but simplistically, this is part of it. To be clear, the VCs understand what they are doing. They aren't fleecing themselves."},{"id":24490507,"author":"cryptica","month":"2020-09","story":"Fuck You Startup World (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence that startup world is a Ponzi scheme","quote":"the entire space is a ponzi scheme to begin with","text":"Let's not ignore the 'minor' detail that the entire space is a ponzi scheme to begin with (coming from Chamath Palihapitiya; a billionaire who was one of the first Facebook employees): [VIDEO] And let's not ignore that the entire financial system within which this ponzi scheme operates is also a giant ponzi scheme: [LINK] So the startup world is literally a ponzi scheme within a ponzi scheme."},{"id":22004383,"author":"Reedx","month":"2020-01","story":"Lime shuts in 12 markets, lays off around 100","stance":"reference","why":"Chamath describes VC as Ponzi-like fee scheme","quote":"Chamath Palihapitiya describes this here","text":"It seems that a lot of VC is a multivariate ponzi scheme of sorts, which might explain some of it. If they're making fees regardless, then they just need to show/push fast initial growth to attract more layers of investment and it doesn't necessarily need to make long term sense. Chamath Palihapitiya describes this here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22008126,"author":"ibeckermayer","month":"2020-01","story":"Lime shuts in 12 markets, lays off around 100","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for VCs inflating each other's valuations","quote":"VCs also get paid by getting other VCs to evaluate their companies as more valuable in a sort of Ponzi scheme","text":"VCs also get paid by getting other VCs to evaluate their companies as more valuable in a sort of Ponzi scheme: [VIDEO]"}]},"Olj8arvfYj4":{"wy":"Cited in screwworm threads as a short description of the sterile-insect barrier system that worked for decades.","q":{"x":"Here's a video describing the system that fell apart which had been working for a long time","i":44846950,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44846950,"author":"colechristensen","month":"2025-08","story":"Mexico to US livestock trade halted due to screwworm spread","stance":"rec","why":"Describes the long-running system keeping screwworm flies out of North America.","quote":"Here's a video describing the system that fell apart which had been working for a long time","text":"Here's a video describing the system that fell apart which had been working for a long time to keep these flies out of north america [VIDEO]"},{"id":41909781,"author":"anitil","month":"2024-10","story":"Egypt declared malaria-free after 100-year effort","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"though it's from a youtube video so I'm not sure how much to trust it.","text":"I believe now they use sterile mosquito larva to achieve the same now [0], though it's from a youtube video so I'm not sure how much to trust it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41457416,"author":"positr0n","month":"2024-09","story":"The Ultra-Selfish Gene","stance":"reference","why":"Explains sterile-fly control of screwworms.","quote":"it's the best I got right now","text":"Wow bummer, do you have a good link about that? Screw worms are controlled very successfully this way, although they are sterilized via radiation not genetic modification. I wonder if that makes a difference. Sorry to ask you for a link and use a YouTube video as my source but it's the best I got right now: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cbeJIwF1pVY",1]]},"OrG7oG8Tvp8":{"wy":"Pointed to in HVAC and indoor-air threads as a hands-on look at high-end ventilation and ERV installation.","q":{"x":"Matt Risinger explains how and why he put an ERV in his house in this episode of The Build Show","i":32714987,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32714987,"author":"sd_mikey","month":"2022-09","story":"Interest in heat pumps has increased dramatically in recent years","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how and why an ERV was installed in a house.","quote":"Matt Risinger explains how and why he put an ERV in his house in this episode of The Build Show","text":"Matt Risinger explains how and why he put an ERV in his house in this episode of The Build Show [VIDEO] . It seems to be the thing to do of late in to help with air exchanges and still meet the passive house standards."},{"id":28085720,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-08","story":"Scientist says cleaning indoor air could make us healthier and smarter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The mentioned Zehnder is a good brand, but AFAICT, are higher end systems, and so may be a bit more pricey","text":"* Do try to get an HRV/ERV if you live in places where the outside air temperature gets >30C or <10C. Otherwise bring in fresh air will cause issues. * Make sure your system can handle MERV16 filters: they catch more things, but may create more back pressure, so the system has to be designed to handle it. Further nice-to-have upgrades would be HEPA capabilities, and (if you live in wildfire zones) charcoal filters (for smoke/fire smells). You don't have to (always) use the finer filters, but having that option is useful. * The filter should ideally be 4\"/10cm, as they have more surface area, so reduce back pressure and often only have to be swapped every 6 months (sometimes even every 12). They do cost a bit more because of the extra material, but you get convenience. Don't go less that 2\"/5cm though. * The mentioned Zehnder is a good brand, but AFAICT, are higher end systems, and so may be a bit more pricey: [VIDEO] [LINK] * Consider getting a separate whole house dehumidifier, depending on your climate: sometimes the indoor air temperature may be comfortable (20-25C), but if the relative humidity (RH) gets too hight (>60%) it will feel hot. But the AC won't run because its thermometer will say everything is fine. Temperature and moisture needs to be dealt with via separate mechanical systems."},{"id":28120674,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-08","story":"Why Don’t Green Buildings Live Up to Hype on Energy Efficiency?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows external boxes allowing MERV 16 and carbon filters.","quote":"","text":"Ah yes, it seems that the built-in stuff has restrictions, but there are external boxes that can be added: * [LINK] Which allow MERV 16 and even carbon filters: * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":24762388,"author":"rudedogg","month":"2020-10","story":"Ask HN: Best HVAC system for a new house?","stance":"reference","why":"Channel showing ultra high-end, cutting-edge building techniques.","quote":"If you want to see some ultra high-end stuff, you can check out this YouTube channel","text":"> Radiant floor heating and radiant cooling zones are also in. And run water through Your floors to heat or cool zones. It is one of the most efficient ways to heat a structure and feels good as well. I think/thought radiant cooling is still pretty \"experimental\". I think in certain climates it can cause the pipes to \"sweat\" a lot. If you want to see some ultra high-end stuff, you can check out this YouTube channel: [VIDEO] Here's their video on radiant cooling ( [LINK] ). Not sure how to word this - but my understanding is that the stuff on his channel is pretty cutting-edge. Most builders use older/time tested materials and methods. You may get weird looks if you start asking your contractor about the things you see in his videos. Also, everything is very climate specific. Mini-splits won't work if you're building a home in a very cold climate, etc. If you have the money, there are HVAC engineers that will do a way better job than a local installer. If you don't, I'd just check reviews and go with a HVAC contractor with a good reputation."}]},"QVvFVwyElLY":{"wy":"HN points to it as insight into how AWS thinks about distributed systems and the difficulty of consensus.","q":{"x":"That presentation is full of great insight into how AWS thinks about distributed systems.","i":31492938,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31492938,"author":"rubiquity","month":"2022-05","story":"Databases = Frameworks for Distributed Systems","stance":"rec","why":"AWS talk on core distributed systems primitives and how AWS thinks about them","quote":"That presentation is full of great insight into how AWS thinks about distributed systems.","text":"> “There are only three modes for a distributed system - it implements paxos/raft itself, it relies on a data store that implements paxos/raft or it’s wrong.” That sounds like something Al Vermeulen would say. Though my recollection of the quote is more along the lines of: \"There are three types of consistent distributed systems: paxos, broken protocols, and single points of failures.\" I'm guessing the talk it came from is Under The Covers: Core Distributed Systems Primitives that Power Our Platform[0]. That presentation is full of great insight into how AWS thinks about distributed systems. The other presenter, Swami, is the VP of AWS Databases now but started off getting Amazon's initial Paxos implementation going. I was lucky enough to work on those core building blocks and it was an incredible experience. 0 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":45691459,"author":"throwdbaaway","month":"2025-10","story":"Summary of the Amazon DynamoDB Service Disruption in US-East-1 Region","stance":"reference","why":"Source for learning distributed systems primitives like Chubby-style services","quote":"Need to learn more about distributed systems primitives from","text":"> But I did not expect something as core as the service managing the leases on the physical EC2 nodes to not have recovery procedure. I guess they don't have a recovery procedure for the \"congestive collapse\" edge case. I have seen something similar, so I wouldn't be frowning at this. A couple of red flags though: 1. Apparent lack of load-shedding support by this DWFM, such that a server reboot had to be performed. Need to learn from [LINK] 2. Having DynamoDB as a dependency of this DWFM service, instead of something more primitive like Chubby. Need to learn more about distributed systems primitives from [VIDEO]"},{"id":32473231,"author":"dtjohnnymonkey","month":"2022-08","story":"Why use Paxos instead of Raft?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on the same topic, watched recently","quote":"I just watched a talk on this the other day","text":"I just watched a talk on this the other day (assuming it's same person since he's saying the same thing) the other day: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27859631,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2021-07","story":"Paxos vs. Raft: Have we reached consensus on distributed consensus?","stance":"reference","why":"AWS engineer calls newer consensus solutions rediscovering Paxos.","quote":"calls inventing newer solutions to distributed consensus an exercise in re-discovering Paxos.","text":"> Basic Paxos, is well, too basic and people mostly run modifications of this to get higher throughput and better latencies. After those modifications, it does not look very different from Raft. Alan Vermeulen, one of the founding AWS engineers, calls inventing newer solutions to distributed consensus an exercise in re-discovering Paxos. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26380893,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2021-03","story":"A Second Conversation with Werner Vogels","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> A few things around S3 were unique. We launched with a set of ten distributed systems tenets in the press release. The ten tenets were separate from S3, stating that this is how you would want to build distributed systems at scale. We just demonstrated that S3 is a really good example of applying those skills. In my own naive way I believe that AWS, today, probably embodies James Hamilton's vision [0] far more than most of those original 10 tenets. - > One of the keys to the success of S3 was that, at launch, it was as simple as it possibly could be, offering little more than GetObject and PutObject. At the time that was quite controversial, as the offering seemed almost too bare bones... most technology companies at the time were delivering everything and the kitchen sink, and it would come with a very thick book and 10 different partners that would tell you how to use the technology. We went down a path, one that Jeff [Bezos] described years before, as building tools instead of platforms. Most folks don't realise but starting somewhere around 2002 \"Web Services\" (SOA, UDDI, WSDL etc) was really hot and every company including Yahoo!, Google, and Microsoft were betting big on it. Amazon simply turned around and took a radically different approach to it all. This describes why Azure's initial push in 2008 [1] and Google's attempts with AppEngine [2] fell relatively short, even post AWS' IaaS play. - > We needed to build the right tools to support that rate of radical change"},{"id":26068061,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2021-02","story":"I followed my dreams to get demoted to software developer","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by Allan Vermeulen on AWS core distributed systems.","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of a \"self-demotion\" that worked out well: That of a certain Allan Vermeulen [0] who, by some accounts, was the chief architect of Amazon S3 [1], and by all accounts, an engineer extraordinaire. Vermeulen managed the Platform Services group when he joined [2], and later moved to a CTO role with focus on the then very nascent Web Services initiative [3], but eventually \"demoted\" himself to an \"AWS Engineer\" role to actually build key technological pieces that would underpin a lot of distributed systems at AWS [4]. Not bad at all. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26100684,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2021-02","story":"Consensus Is Harder Than It Looks (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"AWS talk on the difficulty of distributed consensus.","quote":"distributed consensus is punishingly hard.","text":"You are not alone: [LINK] and [VIDEO] Like TFA points out, distributed consensus is punishingly hard. AWS relied on TLA+ to prove consensus in DynamoDB and other systems [LINK] Interestingly, Kinesis [0] and SQS (?) avoid consensus for those same reasons. [0] [LINK]"}]},"SCE-QeDfXtA":{"wy":"Cited as a vivid example of what Deep Dream and feature visualization look like, resembling psychedelic visuals.","q":{"x":"are excellent examples.","i":15652397,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15652397,"author":"matt4077","month":"2017-11","story":"Feature Visualization: How neural nets build up their understanding of images","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent Deep Dream example resembling psychedelic visuals","quote":"are excellent examples.","text":"have you seen \"Deep Dream\"? [VIDEO] and [LINK] are excellent examples. I believe for anybody who has ever tried psychedelics seeing these was a watershed moment. It seems almost impossible that an algorithm could so faithfully reproduce the experience without somehow having recreated some fundamental structure of the human brain. That is compounded by the fact that this wasn't the result of actually trying to do so, but of an open-ended experiment of creating feedback loops in NN layers."},{"id":14308009,"author":"rwinn","month":"2017-05","story":"Show HN: Spotter – Search for copies of videos on the internet","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Got the results now, posted one of my own videos I know has been reuploaded multiple times: 0 total copies were found with 0 total views Original: [LINK] Some known re-uploads: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] --- Great concept, would love if it actually worked."},{"id":12757718,"author":"lelandbatey","month":"2016-10","story":"Image Synthesis from Yahoo's open_nsfw","stance":"reference","why":"Deep Dream fed random noise video","quote":"the extended example of the video of random noise fed into Google's Deep Dream system","text":"Indeed, and let's not forget the extended example of the video of random noise fed into Google's Deep Dream system: [VIDEO]"}]},"XpQMgbDJPok":{"wy":"Recommended as a short, comprehensive look at Dutch junction design, often cited when discussing intersections as a solved problem.","q":{"x":"There's quite a few interesting videos on traffic design in the Netherlands, junction design is a big topic, as well as separating lanes.","i":10078307,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10078307,"author":"IkmoIkmo","month":"2015-08","story":"Münster’s iconic ‘waste of space’ photo keeps on giving","stance":"rec","why":"Comprehensive 5-minute video on Dutch junction design","quote":"there's more comprehensive videos too like this one which is still only 5 minutes","text":"Welcome :) There's quite a few interesting videos on traffic design in the Netherlands, junction design is a big topic, as well as separating lanes. Videos like these might be fun to watch: [LINK] If it piques your interest, there's more comprehensive videos too like this one which is still only 5 minutes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25412069,"author":"Muximize","month":"2020-12","story":"Sharrows, the bicycle infrastructure that doesn’t work and nobody wants","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch intersections as solved problem","quote":"It's one of the things Copenhagen can learn from the Netherlands, where it's a solved problem at most intersections","text":"It's one of the things Copenhagen can learn from the Netherlands, where it's a solved problem at most intersections: [VIDEO] [LINK] Also see [LINK] from 6 minutes on for right turns in Copenhagen."},{"id":14591373,"author":"bane","month":"2017-06","story":"Why is cycling so popular in the Netherlands?","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch mixed-mode junction design","quote":"Lots of thought also into mixed-mode junction design","text":"The impression I got from Amsterdam is that the city (and by extension the Dutch) has simply accepted all the behaviors that cyclists will do in a less cyclist friendly environment (run stop signs, ignore pedestrians, turn without signing and so on), and then just built infrastructure to support that natural behavior. As a pedestrian unfamiliar with the city, at first I found it hugely annoying that so little space was left to pedestrians, but once I figured out where not to stand, I started to have a much better appreciation for how it contributes the functioning of the city. In fact, I was kind of surprised at how little boat traffic there is these days. Having read and watched some videos on how the Netherlands does road planning for safety, the approach seems to be a much more \"descriptive\" approach to adapting road shapes and forms and cycle lanes to the natural behavior of people using those modes of transportation. For example, squeezing the width of lanes down naturally causes car drivers to slow rather than covering the area with signs telling drivers to slow down. This approach is slowly being adopted elsewhere ( [LINK] ). Lots of thought also into mixed-mode junction design [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["GFosQtEqzSE",1],["FlApbxLz6pA",1],["pX8BXH3SJn0",1]]},"dF_-jQc53jw":{"wy":"Shared as a demo of a minimal Inferno-based phone OS and an unusual dumb-phone approach.","q":{"x":"Here's a short video I threw together demonstrating the phone.","i":3008565,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":7461327,"author":"0xdeadbeefbabe","month":"2014-03","story":"Some young adults disconnecting with 'dumbphones'","stance":"rec","why":"Hellaphone turns Android into a dumb phone","quote":"you can make it into dumb android like this guy did","text":"Don't give up on android yet, it's just a dumb linux and despite vendor plots you can make it into dumb android like this guy did: [VIDEO] Cyanogen mod is going in the \"smart\" direction, but a mod could go in the other direction too. Slides for hellaphone here [LINK] Edit: Add slides link"},{"id":30076711,"author":"marttt","month":"2022-01","story":"People deciding to ditch their smartphones","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've intentionally never owned a smartphone. I used a Nokia 3310 for 12 years, starting in high school. When it finally broke (and I was a 30-ish young dad), there were always several \"old phones\" abandoned by family members or close relatives, so I've been using those to their end ever since. The current one is a Nokia 2600, which I genuinely dislike (tiny buttons, overengineered menu system), but the miserable thing stubbornly works, so what can I do? IIRC, the idea of constantly reading e-mail or browsing the web from some tiny phone screen made me really uneasy even as a young adult. I surely liked computers, but that, I felt, would be too much. Would I really need anything else except SMS and direct calls from a phone, I thought? Probably not. I loved talking to telecom salespeople when I had that 3310. \"We have this new offer, so many megabytes of Internet, and... what's your current phone, by the way?\" they would ask. \"A 3310,\" I replied. \"...Well, that's surely a classic , sir!\" they would reply after a few seconds of silence. I successfully worked as a journalist for years, whilst living in the dumb phone world. I don't remember having any problems. Neither have I ever gotten hopelessly lost on car trips with only a paper map, or hand-written directions, etc. During the last 3-4 years, though, I do sometimes feel like getting in the way of others, or of life: more and more relevant services seem to assume that the user has a smartphone. But I guess that's life. I do "},{"id":27023651,"author":"drybjed","month":"2021-05","story":"Will Linux phones stay around this time?","stance":"reference","why":"Video demo of Hellaphone","quote":"a video demonstration[3] available","text":"There is/was a Hellaphone[1] project, based on Inferno, a Plan 9 successor. There are also some slides[2] and a video demonstration[3] available. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3008565,"author":"jff","month":"2011-09","story":"Inferno on Android","stance":"reference","why":"Author's quick demo of the phone","quote":"Here's a short video I threw together demonstrating the phone.","text":"Here's a short video I threw together demonstrating the phone. It's pretty spur of the moment, and the video quality is low, but you may be able to get an idea of how it works: [VIDEO] Also check out [LINK] for screenshots."}],"r":[["CgASBVMyVFI",1]]},"ev3vENli7wQ":{"wy":"It comes up as an example of challenging, dense electronic music and as a hand-made visual-sync piece predating generative tools.","q":{"x":"I'd say Autechre - Gantz Graf[1] was a closer match as it has the dense production while still having some essence of a melody","i":10547420,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10547420,"author":"laumars","month":"2015-11","story":"The Impossible Music of Black MIDI","stance":"rec","why":"Dense production with some melody, a closer match","quote":"I'd say Autechre - Gantz Graf[1] was a closer match as it has the dense production while still having some essence of a melody","text":"I'd say Autechre - Gantz Graf[1] was a closer match as it has the dense production while still having some essence of a melody (albeit in the less traditional sense of the term). But since we're talking about Aphex Twin, Mt Saint Michel[2] deserves an honorable mention. (bonus points to anyone who's familiar with the Tate Modern exhibit that was based on this track and a video of a cyborg monkey drumming) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":37285998,"author":"adw","month":"2023-08","story":"Classical Music Is for Everyone","stance":"reference","why":"Example of challenging music many would find painful","quote":"I love Autechre, and I find real beauty in their work","text":"The assumption that music the author doesn't like must be a — likely political – statement is, let's just say, revealing and itself highly political. People appear to enjoy things which are bitter, or ugly, or complex, or an acquired taste. Either they're all lying posers, which certainly some people believe, or there really is something to Scotch whisky or James Joyce or Autechre which speaks to some people and not to others. I believe the latter, and I feel sorry for the former. The idea that human experience would be so narrow that some forms of expression are good and all others are bad on a single axis! And music, especially, one of the most cultural-context dependent forms. A bunch of traditions wouldn't even recognise the Western canon, which tends to seriously lack rhythmic complexity, _as_ worthwhile music. So: taste varies and that's the point. I love Autechre, and I find real beauty in their work, but I would place significant money that more than half of the audience here would find this – [VIDEO] – somewhere between modem line-noise and actively painful. Is it pretty in the way, say, Teenage Fanclub (who are also magnificent) are pretty? No. It's not trying to be. It's communicating something else."},{"id":34393382,"author":"imhoguy","month":"2023-01","story":"Show HN: Create your own video clips with Stable Diffusion","stance":"reference","why":"Hand-synchronised 2002 music video, no generative element","quote":"It was made manually in 2002","text":"One video brought me memory of Autechre - Gantz Graf music video (warning, hard IDM style ;) [0]. It was made manually in 2002, per Wikipedia: \"Rutterford also stated that there was no generative element to the imagery; every three-dimensional object in the agglomeration was painstakingly and manually synchronised with a specific element or frequency range within the track\" [1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["niKblgZupOc",1],["rIAXIubSTkc",1]]},"iYpl0QVCr6U":{"wy":"Brought up as the classic critique of web bloat whenever slow or heavy sites are discussed.","q":{"x":"Maybe it's time again to remember Maciej Ceglowski's","i":25356440,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25356440,"author":"jcynix","month":"2020-12","story":"The mythical “fast” web page","stance":"rec","why":"Ceglowski's keynote on bloated websites.","quote":"Maybe it's time again to remember Maciej Ceglowski's","text":"Fat, slow loading, unresponsive web pages? Maybe it's time again to remember Maciej Ceglowski's \"Web Directions 2015 Keynote\": The Website Obesity Crisis [VIDEO]"},{"id":47439553,"author":"renegat0x0","month":"2026-03","story":"'Your Frustration Is the Product'","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on website obesity.","quote":"Reminds me of \"Website obesity crisis","text":"Reminds me of \"Website obesity crisis\" - [VIDEO] - [LINK] Some say that you should not use ad blocker, because that kills ad revenue, but I did not forced anybody to rely with their lives on ad revenue. Many of things were 'free' because we were all just using ad blocks, and then it all became commodified, simplified, so simpletons without ad blocks became a thing. Now they shame people for using ad blocks, even though it stops spreading malware and viruses. I plan to use ad block, and use as many extensions that protect me. If there is some form of goods, be it streaming movies, audio, books I will happily pay for it. I will not accept a web with ads. I prefer touch grass. There is a clear line for me. Also there is no line ad publisher will not cross. The goal posts are shifted, so you will never satisfy shareholder greed. The only pushback is trough ads and probably sometimes piracy. Not that I advocate it, but in reality if companies push too hard, there are consequences."},{"id":37542126,"author":"renegat0x0","month":"2023-09","story":"Why do software nowadays take so much more memory and processing power","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It is more true about the Internet. More and more web pages use more bloated libraries, and more complex solutions. There is more data, more communication, more patterns, more services, more styles. If you want a blog there is no need for fancy libraries with a fancy frameworks, probably. I think this could be passed to the whole software / hardware situation. Do you need a fancy desktop? Does not Gnome look better than XFCE? But you want a shiny desktop with nice animations, nice icons. It is also required by the producent for the product to be more complicated and fragile. Now you cannot have offline software. There needs to be telemetry. Therefore there need to be internet logins. Therefore hackers are a higher threat. Why does my calculator on Xiaomi phone need permissions for gathering private data? I know maybe to select some units, but comes on! [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":27175576,"author":"grishka","month":"2021-05","story":"Faster web fonts","stance":"reference","why":"Argues to serve only what is essential for a website.","quote":"you first serve what's essential for your website's functionality, and then you stop.","text":"Yeah I mean > This is a good base because it lets website visitors start reading your content right away They're already reading the content. The one job your website had has already been accomplished without the custom font. As this guy[1] said, you first serve what's essential for your website's functionality, and then you stop. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"iZn3kCsw5D8":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that Blender can power feature animation and for its look at a real production process.","q":{"x":"Fun to see the actual process behind real, released films","i":39523199,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39523199,"author":"diggan","month":"2024-02","story":"Blender Open Movies. Featuring all the production files, assets, and artwork","stance":"rec","why":"Behind-the-scenes look at Next Gen production process.","quote":"Fun to see the actual process behind real, released films","text":"The Next Gen folks also presented everything about the production process at Blender Conference 2018 and hanged around to talk with us Blender nerds! Fun to see the actual process behind real, released films: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26934692,"author":"swerner","month":"2021-04","story":"Renderman","stance":"reference","why":"Example of feature animations made in Blender.","quote":"","text":"Not just side projects. We did two feature animations using Blender, more projects in the pipeline: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20140219,"author":"hex12648430","month":"2019-06","story":"Blender Is Free Software","stance":"reference","why":"Conference talk on how Next Gen was made in Blender.","quote":"They had a presentation at the blender conference about it","text":"They had a presentation at the blender conference about it [VIDEO] Yes, almost everything is made in blender except for some textures which were painted in Substance Painter and volumetric effects like explosions which were generated in FumeFX and Houdini before being imported, rendered and composited in blender."},{"id":20140293,"author":"diggan","month":"2019-06","story":"Blender Is Free Software","stance":"reference","why":"Tangent co-founder on using Blender for Next Gen.","quote":"","text":"BlenderConf 2018 had a talk from the co-founder of Tangent (Jeff Bell) about how Blender was used for Next Gen: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-i6wvix6buI",1]]},"lnq-2BJwatE":{"wy":"HN uses it mainly as the place where Stephenson addresses criticism of his novels' endings, plus as a fun author talk with jokes.","q":{"x":"check out the Authors@Google talk he gave","i":310395,"y":"2008"},"m":[{"id":310395,"author":"JayNeely","month":"2008-09","story":"New Neal Stephenson Book Out - Anathem","stance":"rec","why":"Author talk with jokes and Anathem discussion.","quote":"check out the Authors@Google talk he gave","text":"He's been doing a book tour, stopped by Cambridge yesterday. If you're not in a place he's stopping by, check out the Authors@Google talk he gave: [VIDEO] He makes some long-winded jokes for the first few minutes (mainly about authors being ill-suited to public appearances), starts talking about Anathem around 3:40, and starts the Q&A time around 8:30."},{"id":41001961,"author":"imp0cat","month":"2024-07","story":"Type in Morse code by repeatedly slamming your laptop shut","stance":"reference","why":"Stephenson aware of ending criticism.","quote":"And he's actually aware of it.","text":"So true about the endings! And he's actually aware of it. Well, I'm reasonably happy with all of my endings, but I know that some people feel differently. But as you've noticed, they're different, it's not always the same thing. All I can say is different books end in different ways, and different people have different tastes in what they want to see. I'm well aware that there are certain people frustrated with the endings of some of my books. But I also think that it's one of these things where people's preconceived ideas sometimes drive the way they perceive things. ... So I think that my experience is that once you've written a book with a controversial ending and that meme gets going of Stephenson can't write endings, then that gets slapped on to everything you do, no matter how elaborate the ending is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39289142,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2024-02","story":"Neal Stephenson was prescient about our AI age","stance":"reference","why":"Responding to criticisms of his endings.","quote":"He's responded to criticisms of his endings in various book talks","text":"Talking about Neal Stephenson's works always brings up several topics: endings and editing. He's responded to criticisms of his endings in various book talks, for instance his Author's at Google talk about Anathem [1] (prepositioned to the relevant bit @10:54). One of his editor's at Wired magazine also made a comment about editing his work in his book talk about Fall or Dodge in Hell at the Interval [2]. This is a transcript of a part of the Authors at Google talk for Anathem (first link prepositioned to 10:54): [10:54] Q: How do you think about ending your stories? They seem to run the gamut from some where the action just ends, and others where there's the equivalent of a movie ending with a ten-minute car chase in it. [11:11] A: Well, I'm reasonably happy with all of my endings, but I know that some people feel differently. [11:21] But as you've noticed, they're different, it's not always the same thing. All I can say is different books end in different ways, and different people have different tastes in what they want to see. I'm well aware that there are certain people frustrated with the endings of some of my books. But I also think that it's one of these things where people's preconceived ideas sometimes drive the way they perceive things. I've seen people complain, for example, that Snow Crash doesn't have a good ending. But I can remember that at the time I was writing it, I told a friend of mine that the climax of Snow Crash was now longer than Moby Dick. There's a"},{"id":28381131,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2021-09","story":"William Gibson is a literary genius","stance":"reference","why":"Q&A answer about his endings.","quote":"","text":"Stephenson occasionally fields questions about his endings in talks. Here's one of them, from his Google talk for the Anathem book tour [1]: Q: How do you think about ending your stories? They seem to run the gamut from some where the action just ends, and others where there's the equivalent of a movie ending with a ten-minute car chase in it. A: Well, I'm reasonably happy with all of my endings, but I know that some people feel differently. But as you've noticed, they're different, it's not always the same thing. All I can say is different books end in different ways, and different people have different tastes in what they want to see. I'm well aware that there are certain people frustrated with the endings of some of my books. But I also think that it's one of these things where people's preconceived ideas sometimes drive the way they perceive things. ... So I think that my experience is that once you've written a book with a controversial ending and that meme gets going of Stephenson can't write endings, then that gets slapped on to everything you do, no matter how elaborate the ending is. For me, the endings meme really is that - I don't see what people are complaining about. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12180767,"author":"raywu","month":"2016-07","story":"YC’s Summer Reading","stance":"reference","why":"Book tour talk at Google.","quote":"Here's a video of him speaking at Google for the book tour","text":"_Anathem_ is great. Very slow build up. The interwoven theme of race, religion, time and space makes it well worth the slow construction of the world. Here's a video of him speaking at Google for the book tour [VIDEO]"},{"id":9747071,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2015-06","story":"An Interview with Neal Stephenson","stance":"reference","why":"Stephenson addresses his endings criticism.","quote":"","text":"Stephenson had an Authors @Google talk in 2008 [1], which was mostly about his book Anathem. In it he actually addressed what he calls the \"Stephenson can't write endings\" meme, as he calls it, at 10:54 going on for about three minutes [2]. I think his endings are just fine, myself, and also think that this whole idea that he can't write endings is just a self-perpetuating meme. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"rh5J4vacG98":{"wy":"Pointed to as a short, clear demonstration of how Specter simplifies manipulating complex nested data.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching this short video to see how it works.","i":15527039,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15527039,"author":"yogthos","month":"2017-10","story":"Why I'm Productive in Clojure (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Short video showing how Specter declaratively transforms complex data.","quote":"I highly recommend watching this short video to see how it works.","text":"Explicit recursion is rarely used in FP, and it's often a code smell. It's almost always better to use higher order functions instead. The advantage is that you can implement a particular transformation once, check for edge cases, and reuse it from there on. You also end up with declarative code that talks about what it's doing without distracting your with implementation details. Clojure has a rich set of functions for manipulating data such as map, filter, reduce, and so on. There are also libraries such as Specter [LINK] that allow you to declaratively transform complex data. I highly recommend watching this [VIDEO] short video to see how it works."},{"id":26357621,"author":"kgwxd","month":"2021-03","story":"Clojure from a Schemer's Perspective","stance":"reference","why":"Video covers how Specter handles data structure type concerns.","quote":"One of the first things the video covers is how the library deals with this concern.","text":"> You might just be using a completely different underlying data structure than expected, depending on which operations you've performed. I haven't had the pleasure of using Clojure in production, but I'm somewhat obsessed with the language and follow it fairly closely. Someone on r/Clojure[1] mentioned Specter[2] with a link to this video[3]. One of the first things the video covers is how the library deals with this concern. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20116557,"author":"Scarbutt","month":"2019-06","story":"Why GitHub used Haskell for Semantic","stance":"reference","why":"Specter mentioned as a more powerful option for nested data.","quote":"","text":"(assoc-in foo [\"bar\" \"alice\" \"bob\"] 12) Of course, in clojure you mostly use :keywords instead \"strings\" as keys which has a ton of benefits. There's also specter if want something more powerful at the cost of an additional lib. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"uwCwtBxZM7g":{"wy":"HN cites it in Magic Leap and VR threads as a clear overview of how light fields could solve focus and bulk problems.","q":{"x":"It gives a very clear overview of the concept and prototypes.","i":8451751,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8451751,"author":"jonah","month":"2014-10","story":"Google May Lead $500M Round in Magic Leap Virtual Reality Tech","stance":"rec","why":"Clear overview of near-eye light field concept and prototypes","quote":"It gives a very clear overview of the concept and prototypes.","text":"Here's the video associated with the Nvidia paper on Near-Eye Light Field Displays linked elsewhere in the comments. [VIDEO] It gives a very clear overview of the concept and prototypes."},{"id":8453386,"author":"VikingCoder","month":"2014-10","story":"Google May Lead $500M Round in Magic Leap Virtual Reality Tech","stance":"rec","why":"Explains focus-capable displays, like a display version of a Lytro","quote":"Watch this video at the 4 minute mark","text":"Watch this video at the 4 minute mark: [VIDEO] It explains what they're talking about. Your eyes can focus on different parts of the view - it's not just an infinite depth-of-field display like we're used to. It's like the display version of a Lytro camera."},{"id":16381766,"author":"kbenson","month":"2018-02","story":"Magic Leap One Video – Diffractive Waveguides Confirmed","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on near-eye light field devices","quote":"","text":"I'm still waiting for more info on Near Eye Light Field devices.[1][2] Actually, it appears there's more on this now[3][4], but the video's audio is a weird translation of some paper or something. 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO] 3: [LINK] 4: [LINK]"},{"id":16382620,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2018-02","story":"Magic Leap One Video – Diffractive Waveguides Confirmed","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes its history intro mentioning Ivan Sutherland","quote":"","text":"> 2: [VIDEO] > \"In 1968, Ivan Sutherland introduced the first graphics driven headmounted display, of HMD\" Ivan Sutherland? As in: the guy who made Sketchpad? What hasn't this guy done?"},{"id":13136387,"author":"kbenson","month":"2016-12","story":"Magic Leap’s technology may be years away from completion","stance":"reference","why":"Suggested as promising approach to focus and bulk problems","quote":"those look to have promise in solving some focus problems","text":"What about Near-Eye Light Field Displays[1][2]? From what I've seen those look to have promise in solving some focus problems and some of the problems with how cumbersome most VR/AR displays are. As a bonus, they can correct for prescriptions. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":11796097,"author":"Sephr","month":"2016-05","story":"How big an issue is the nausea problem for VR products?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing depth perception problem solved by OLED light fields","quote":"OLED light field displays have already solved this problem completely.","text":"They're comparing apples to oranges. The $80,000 military VR headsets of 1989 absolutely did not use OLED or OLED light field displays. They've only recently been available from manufacturers at any price with their current pixel density and refresh rate (OLED has always had amazing response time, but refresh rate has been lacking until recently) specifications. Even the most expensive research OLED panels with high enough pixel densities for VR have only existed since the early-mid '00s. The studies he linked to are from 1989. > Depth perception The author neglects to mention that OLED light field displays have already solved this problem completely. See [VIDEO] The technology already exists (you need a pair of expensive MicroOLED displays and some special lenses), it's just not available at a consumer price point. > Momentum For someone involved with military devices, it's surprising that this guy has never heard of galvanic vestibular stimulation. GVS will never be easy and will always require very, very extensive calibration per-user, but it's still possible to use for simulating perceived movement. This isn't that important for me personally, as I've never experienced VR sickness even when playing fast-paced high-momentum VR games for multiple hours at a time. I could see people who get VR sickness wanting this, as GVS would help prevent it from happening."}],"r":[["U_X5uR7VC4M",1],["PDylgzybWAw",1],["uDr4L6BzpP8",1]]},"zRjTyRly5WA":{"wy":"Shared as the canonical example of visualizing a real project's version control history with Gource.","q":{"x":"Minecraft released an animation of their first 800+ days of dev","i":35472350,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":11133304,"author":"ytjohn","month":"2016-02","story":"Codeology: visualization of GitHub projects","stance":"rec","why":"Gource visualization of Minecraft's development history.","quote":"A lot of videos online, but two great ones are","text":"For those that like things like this, check out [LINK] - it's desktop only, but produces awesome visualizations. It works by going through your git history and essentially shows programmers buzzing around the code and updating files. It can do generic avatars, or even download their gravatar if they have one. A lot of videos online, but two great ones are: * The History of Python [VIDEO] * 800+ days of Minecraft in 8 minutes [VIDEO]"},{"id":35472350,"author":"MaxLeiter","month":"2023-04","story":"Gource – Animate your Git history","stance":"reference","why":"Animated Git history of Minecraft's first 800 days.","quote":"Minecraft released an animation of their first 800+ days of dev","text":"I discovered Gource back in 2013 when Minecraft released an animation of their first 800+ days of dev: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21558360,"author":"fbnlsr","month":"2019-11","story":"Gource.io – a software version control visualization tool","stance":"reference","why":"Gource visualization of Minecraft development history.","quote":"Notch released a video of the Minecraft development history","text":"Notch released a video of the Minecraft development history, that's when I first heard of Gource: [VIDEO]"}]},"0nTDFLMLX9k":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the go-to talk on a job-system-based multithreaded game engine architecture.","m":[{"id":24509532,"author":"meheleventyone","month":"2020-09","story":"An Introduction to Data Oriented Design with Rust","stance":"reference","why":"The renderer talk using a job system","quote":"The renderer talk is here","text":"I believe it’s only been mentioned in passing and likely will be talked about at conferences soonish. Here’s a HN thread from when it was first talked about: [LINK] There was an early talk about using a job system to run the Destiny renderer and then this one for the whole engine which is a very similar premise to the way DOOM(2016) and then DOOM Eternal evolved. [LINK] The renderer talk is here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14082497,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2017-04","story":"Ask HN: Will Rust ever become a mainstream systems programming language?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited talk on Destiny's multithreaded engine architecture","quote":"I saw some GDC talks about the multithreaded game engine for Bungie's Destiny game.","text":"Are there any AAA game studios using or evaluating Rust yet? I saw some GDC talks about the multithreaded game engine for Bungie's Destiny game. Given the debugging and QA challenges of their multithreaded architecture, something like Rust's ownership model seems like a good fit. Does the Rust toolchain support Xbox and PlayStation? * Destiny's Multithreaded Rendering Architecture: [VIDEO] * Multithreading the Entire Destiny Engine: [LINK]"},{"id":10580423,"author":"ufo","month":"2015-11","story":"Lessons from the PC video game industry","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the video the parent comment mentioned","quote":"I think this is the video the parent mentioned, in case anyone is interested","text":"Thanks. Apparently fallout4 has a bunch of bugs on higher-end PCs because and I was wondering why they would couple things like that. I guess the harsh restrictions of gamedev explain it a bit then... btw, I think this is the video the parent mentioned, in case anyone is interested: [VIDEO]"}]},"2S-WJN3L5eo":{"wy":"HN mentions mostly use it as an example of extreme views or to criticize participants in political threads, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":45212468,"author":"croes","month":"2025-09","story":"Charlie Kirk killed at event in Utah","stance":"reference","why":"Pointed to as evidence of views the commenter says exist.","quote":"Maybe listen to the people Mehdi Hassan debated with here","text":"Sorry but \"I don't know people like that\" is anecdotal evidence and is part of the your echo chamber. Maybe listen to the people Mehdi Hassan debated with here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46375093,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2025-12","story":"US sanctions EU government officials behind the DSA","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I think it's pretty extreme too","text":"Presumably the US wants the EU to permit more Far-right 'conservativism'? [VIDEO] 1 Progressive vs 20 Far-Right Conservatives (ft. Mehdi Hasan) This is apparently representative of what that means at this moment in time. I think it's pretty extreme too, but on searching, none of the participants' positions seem to have been disowned by their own side. One of them actually fundraised $30000 afterwards."},{"id":45221306,"author":"croes","month":"2025-09","story":"Charlie Kirk killed at event in Utah","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Kirk and others boost people like those here","text":"Wait a minute, he spread misinformation about COVID and the „stolen“ election and reaches millions of people but I‘m the bad guy? He has definitely caused more violence than I. Kirk and others boost people like those here [VIDEO] Show one who got influenced by me. That would be really interesting. That I spread misinformation has to be proven. He referenced the 50s and 60s on purpose, the good old times and he knows his peers and what the associate with that time period. So he either knew exactly what association he sent with that or he was naive. I don’t think he was naive. Given all what he said there is a clear subtext you try to ignore."},{"id":44655144,"author":"komali2","month":"2025-07","story":"CBA hiring Indian ICT workers after firing Australians","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"there's a couple non white conservatives there who watch supposed ideological allies say directly in front of them that they don't want non whites in the country.","text":"> When I was a fresh immigrant child comments like these used to make me feel more uncomfortable than they do now. How I perceive these comments has changed over time. I don't wish you to be uncomfortable, I'm happy to hear your comfort increases as you age. Still, maybe it's worth taking note that the kinds of people that believe in great replacement will never see you as anything other than Indian, same for your children. You may feel less Indian and more something else, but others disagree. Check out the first reply to your comment. Ignored everything you said about identity, just says \"Gee, sounds like you brought the Caste system from India to Canada!\" Please keep a wary eye out. Reminds me of that Jubilee video that just came out with a bunch of fascists vs Mehdi Hasan - there's a couple non white conservatives there who watch supposed ideological allies say directly in front of them that they don't want non whites in the country. [VIDEO]"}]},"9ntPxdWAWq8":{"wy":"Used as a joke whenever companies chase tech buzzwords, from cloud to AI.","m":[{"id":46705544,"author":"Bluecobra","month":"2026-01","story":"Stories removed from the Hacker News Front Page, updated in real time","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Don’t forget about cloud-something!","text":"Don’t forget about cloud-something! Related: HP Offers 'That Cloud Thing Everyone Is Talking About’ [VIDEO]"},{"id":43328960,"author":"LoganDark","month":"2025-03","story":"Music labels will regret coming for the Internet Archive, sound historian says","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Onion also made some pretty good cloud parodies back in 2012","text":"The Onion also made some pretty good cloud parodies back in 2012: [VIDEO] Old animation though I wouldn't necessarily expect to only have been 10 years ago, and 10 years ago I wouldn't expect to be scolded for not using the cloud."},{"id":43119977,"author":"tdeck","month":"2025-02","story":"Humane (Or Red Flags to Spot for Next Time)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"In case anyone hasn't seen the Onion video","text":"In case anyone hasn't seen the Onion video: [VIDEO] HP Offers 'That Cloud Thing Everyone Is Talking About'"},{"id":40560745,"author":"nicbou","month":"2024-06","story":"Object to your information being used for AI at Meta","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Onion had it right a while ago","text":"My cynical take is that we accidentally started a self-sustained frenzy. C-levels hear that AI is literal magic, and that you can't afford to miss that boat. Nobody wants to be caught not doing AI, so all the KPIs, OKRs and bonuses have AI tied to them. Everyone slaps AI on stuff as fast as they can, further accelerating the trend. The Onion had it right a while ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40465156,"author":"philjohn","month":"2024-05","story":"The Washington Post Tells Staff It's Pivoting to AI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"is still relevant 11 years later","text":"It was ever thus - remember when everything was about \"The Cloud\"? TheOnion satirical news report about HP adopting \"That cloud thing that everyone is talking about\" is still relevant 11 years later: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33472888,"author":"csours","month":"2022-11","story":"Companies like HP must split to survive, is like the evolution of new species","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This was bitterly funny when I worked for HP in 2012.","text":"> HP executives were slow to grasp the huge shift toward cloud computing in the early 2010s. 2012 - [VIDEO] We Have App This was bitterly funny when I worked for HP in 2012. --- No mention of \"disruptive innovation\" in these comments or the article. Interesting."},{"id":17485548,"author":"fenwick67","month":"2018-07","story":"No Joke: The Onion Faces Layoffs by Univision, Says Report","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm very often reminded of this video when people use tech buzzwords a lot","text":"I'm very often reminded of this video when people use tech buzzwords a lot: \"HP offers 'that cloud thing everyone is talking about'\" [VIDEO]"}]},"AA4IWd3Svm0":{"wy":"Posted by an employee as a showcase of a polished desktop application, with a JavaFX UI, built by a small team.","q":{"x":"Very impressive indeed. Looks effortless and natural","i":30035565,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30881868,"author":"DentalWings","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (April 2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of the company's intra-oral scanning","quote":"effortless intra-oral scan","text":"Dental Wings | Software Engineer | Berlin (GER) | ONSITE/EU-REMOTE | Java | Full-time You love to work on 3D graphics, tinkering with mesh algorithms or hacking the rendering pipeline to get faster results ? You enjoy creating new kinds of 3D interactions to make the digital designs of CAD applications more intuitive ? Come join us! * About us * Dental Wings is a leading provider of digital solutions for the dental industry. Our main areas of activity include dental scanning applications [1], implant planning and guided surgery software and dental prosthesis design [2]. Last year 15 Mio. patients were treated with our products in over 50 countries worldwide! * What we offer * Work on challenging digital projects in the oral health industry, short decision-making processes and a friendly, competent team. We value autonomy, openness, and delivering quality software on time. To do that we work closely together as a team and every member can improve our product and processes. We do our utmost to support our colleagues, emphasize communication and value a good work-life balance. More benefits are in the job description below. Our tech stack includes Java 17, Eclipse, JUnit, Git, Gerrit, Sonar, Jenkins, Redmine and Slack. For GUI design we use JavaFX and OpenGL 4.5 for 3D visualisation. * Open positions * Software Developer (m/f/x) for medical CAD-applications [LINK] * Our tech in action * [1] effortless intra-oral scan [VIDEO] [2] dental restoration CAD/CAM software [LINK]"},{"id":30790773,"author":"splittingTimes","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Does Java need a modern Java UI toolkit for desktop/web?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the JavaFX UI of an intra-oral scanner","quote":"The UI of the intra-oral scanner is done with javaFX tool kit/libs. A little impression","text":"Our company sells HW and SW for the dental sector. Our desktop scanners for dental labs and our intra-oral scanners for dentists generate 3D meshes of the patients oral situation. The UI of the intra-oral scanner is done with javaFX tool kit/libs. A little impression: [VIDEO] Those meshes are the input data for the CAD/CAM Software my team builds. It is a desktop app for the digital design of dental restorations. The GUI is in javaFX and 3D visualization is done with OpenGL 4.5. Once the restorative Design is done, that app can generate many different output formats. Labs will feed the design files their local milling machine or 3D printer. Dentists can send it to centralized milling facilities of their choice to produce the crown or bridge or what have you. Some impressions of the CAD can be seen here: [LINK]"},{"id":30169873,"author":"DentalWings","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (February 2022)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Dental Wings | Berlin (GER) | ONSITE/EU-REMOTE | Java | Full-time * About us * Dental Wings is a leading provider of digital solutions for the dental industry. Our main areas of activity include dental scanning applications [1], implant planning and guided surgery software, dental prosthesis design [2], manufacturing connectivity solutions, and tools for the communication of involved experts and professionals. Dental Wings employs more than 200 people. Last year our products were used in the treatments of more than 15 Mio. patients in over 50 countries worldwide! Our mission is to help our customers improve the lives of millions of dental patients worldwide! * What we offer * We offer work on challenging projects in the oral health industry, in particular for our latest restorative design solution \"DWOS Easy\" [2]. We have short decision-making processes and a friendly, competent, diverse team of computer scientists, physicists and dental technicians. We go to lunch together daily. You have the flexibility to work as you like between our modern offices in Adlershof, Berlin and remotely from home. For us it is understood that in a pandemic the health and safety of you and your family comes first. The other various benefits can be read in the job description below. * Our team * At Dental Wings, we value autonomy, openness, and delivering quality software on time. To do that we work closely together as a team, and it is important to us that every member can improve our product an"},{"id":30035565,"author":"huhtenberg","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: Anyone making a living building desktop applications?","stance":"reference","why":"Live demo of a fast, accurate intra-oral scanner","quote":"Very impressive indeed. Looks effortless and natural","text":"> I was blown away by how quick and accurate those scanner sticks are Damn, you weren't kidding - [VIDEO] Very impressive indeed. Looks effortless and natural, but I'm sure that the processing engine is not exactly trivial."}]},"BZoKfap4g4w":{"wy":"Referenced when discussing how much time household technology saved families and how heavy housework used to be.","q":{"x":"He essentially made the case that the washing machine made a huge difference in the history of the world","i":6447161,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":40441051,"author":"suoduandao3","month":"2024-05","story":"The Rise and Fall of the Trad Wife","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for how much housework used to take.","quote":"","text":"A factor I think gets left out of this conversation is that the 50's housewife was an aberration, a home-based adult in an age of washing machines, electric ovens and small gardens at most. There used to be so much housework you basically needed a dedicated person to do it all[1]. '50s housewives didn't need to do most of that BUT they were also the primary mental health caregivers for a generation of men who had just come back from something like the second-most brutal war in human history. Typically with support from a member of the clergy but they were on the front lines. It was an enormous but unacknowledged responsibility. And given that we didn't have a repeat of what happened after WW1, on average it must have been pretty well handled. Today we have memetic warfare [2] instead, and given the social media nature of it, I would expect more women on the frontlines of it than men. Maybe we'll have a lot of 'tradmen' who spend a lot of time fishing, writing poetry, and providing mental health care for their partners working in social media. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":22425410,"author":"cjlars","month":"2020-02","story":"We need to take CO2 out of the sky – an overview of climate tech","stance":"reference","why":"Rosling on energy use and lifestyle emissions.","quote":"","text":"The whole \"reduce, reuse, recycle\" approach isn't tenable. Even a very environmentally conscious US lifestyle still produces a very substantial appoint of carbon emissions. For more, see how Hans Rosling covered the idea in his 2011 \"Magic Washing Machine\" presentation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6447161,"author":"makeramen","month":"2013-09","story":"Google Shopping Express now available to all SF and Peninsula Shoppers ","stance":"reference","why":"Rosling on how washing machines freed up time for families.","quote":"He essentially made the case that the washing machine made a huge difference in the history of the world","text":"I'd liken this to the \"washing machine effect\" that Hans Rosling explaned in his TED talk [1]. He essentially made the case that the washing machine made a huge difference in the history of the world so parents could spend more time with their kids (among other things) instead of many hours a week just washing clothes. I would say Google Shopping Express has a similar effect for shopping trips where the time spent greatly affects the amount of things an individual can accomplish in a day. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"FYJ1dbyDcrI":{"wy":"HN uses it as a mind-blown reaction clip in threads about physics, space and GIFs.","m":[{"id":41451994,"author":"fasa99","month":"2024-09","story":"How the Higgs field gives mass to elementary particles","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just to go off these great works, there's a wonderful academic piece on the interaction between cosmology, quantum particles, and the higgs field here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41618874,"author":"fasa99","month":"2024-09","story":"What Is a Particle? (2020)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The idea that it's an abstraction is 100% accurate. That physics is a discrete set of fields with field rules and interaction rules, and what we observe is a scaffold on top of that. It's like okay, let's say the math is right and we have a set of fields, what are they and where are they from, how do we manipulate them. Then the physicists, often driven by ego, is want for an explanation and points to vibrating strings and such, and finally they knock on the door of the empiric physicist and say \"can you do an experiment to show that I'm right\" \"sure, build a machine the size of the universe and I could test that\" and that's the state of physics the last gorillion years [VIDEO]"},{"id":41284792,"author":"jancsika","month":"2024-08","story":"NASA Citizen Scientists Spot Object Moving 1M Miles per Hour","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"If you could put the universe into a tube, you'd end up with a very long tube, probably extending twice the size of the universe. Because when you collapse the universe, it expands. And it would be-- you wouldn't want to put it into a tube.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":22776160,"author":"kamikaz1k","month":"2020-04","story":"How to trim video clips instantly without reencoding","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think it's typically the Tim & Eric skit that gets used for mind blown reaction.","text":"I think it's typically the Tim & Eric skit that gets used for mind blown reaction. You could maybe use a different meme format, like the Drake one... But honestly I don't mind :) Coincidentally, I was looking at the same problem this morning. I found that exact snippet on an SO answer. Amazing! Snippet: [LINK] Full video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20999885,"author":"janee","month":"2019-09","story":"Show HN: StartupGIFs – A repo of GIFs for every situation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> No I really don't need to see the universe mind exploding guy in response to a story Universe mind exploding guy haha...what you need to see is some Tim and Eric ;) [VIDEO]"}]},"I39sxwYKlEE":{"wy":"HN cites it as footage proving that autonomous driving was demonstrated in Europe decades before the current wave.","q":{"x":"Mercedes FSD prototype 1986 to 1994","i":36272952,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36272952,"author":"ChuckNorris89","month":"2023-06","story":"Germans beat Tesla to autonomous L3 driving in the Golden State","stance":"reference","why":"Early Mercedes self-driving prototype footage from 1986 to 1994.","quote":"Mercedes FSD prototype 1986 to 1994","text":">Mercedes FSD prototype, 10 years ago: Mercedes FSD prototype 1986 to 1994: [VIDEO] It's funny that German, and Italian researchers and car makers had the early lead on self driving tech and then lost it by shelving the tech. Oof. “It’s possible that [Germany] threw away its clear vanguard role because research wasn’t consistently continued at the time,” Schmidhuber said. He added that carmakers might have shied away from self-driving technology because it seemed to be in opposition to their marketing, which promoted the idea of a driver in charge of steering a car.\""},{"id":34657840,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2023-02","story":"ChatGPT Passes Google Coding Interview for Level 3 Engineer with $183K Salary","stance":"reference","why":"Self-driving van from 1986 that detected obstacles by 1999.","quote":"was a self-driving van in 1986 which could detect obstacles by 1999 and drive in a convoy using that.","text":"[VIDEO] was a self-driving van in 1986 which could detect obstacles by 1999 and drive in a convoy using that. This is a bit like seeing Steve Mann's wearable computers over the years ( [LINK] ) and then today anyone with a smartphone and smart watch has more computing power and more features than most of his gear ever had, apart from the head mounted screen. More processing power, more memory, more storage, more face recognition, more motion sensing, more GPS, longer runtime on battery, more bandwidth and connectivity to e.g. mapping, more assistants like Google Now and Siri. And we still aren't at a level where you can be doing a physical task like replacing a laptop screen and have your device record what you're doing, with voice prompts for when you complete different stages, have it add markers to the recording, track objects in the scene like and solve for questions like 'where did that longer screw go?' or 'where did this part come from?' and have it jump to the video where you took that part out. Nor reflow the video backwards as an aide memoire to reassembling it. Or do that outside for something like garage or car work, or have it control and direct lighting on some kind of robot arm to help you see, or have it listen to the sound of your bike gears rattle as you tune them and tell you or show you on a graph when it identifies the least rattle. Anything a human assistant could easily do, we're still at the level of 'set a reminder' or 'add to calendar' rather than 'h"},{"id":22080507,"author":"cbanek","month":"2020-01","story":"Researchers: Are we on the cusp of an ‘AI winter’?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> While AGI isn't going to be created any time soon, machines have learned how to master complex tasks like: > translating text into practically every language Note: they said they have mastered these tasks. Yeah... I'm not sure a lot of native speakers would agree. Here's a great example of using Google Translate to automatically translate a video game. [LINK] > Driving cars I'm not so sure about that one either. I think we're in the valley that AI can do a lot of things, but are hitting limits in accuracy where humans are still better at some of these things sometimes. That is, the AI isn't always better than humans, even at a specific, non-general task. Now don't get me wrong, we've made a lot of progress, but I wonder if we can get these things to a place better than humans before the next economic recession. I think the biggest risk to AI is having the money dry up. Right now the hype is strong and the money is (nearly) free. If one of those changes, we could put this back on the shelf for another decade. If we go into a recession, labor will be cheap, so why bother automating with AI? For example, we had self driving freeway cars back in the 90's.[0][1] Here's one of the lessons learned: > In 1987, some UK Universities expressed concern that the industrial focus on the project neglected import traffic safety issues such as pedestrian protection. And who doesn't remember the brilliant Dr. Sbaitso, my childhood therapist. [2] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":17599844,"author":"usernam33","month":"2018-07","story":"Ernst Dickmanns, a German scientist who developed self-driving cars in the 1980s","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The linked video states they could recognize obstacles years later, so I think there was no reading of signs etc. involved. It must have been only lane mark recognition that was used to steer. The van from the vid came years after the two mercedes s cars. I think there was no breaking for obstacles, exiting the highway, or even using interchanges. It was simply holding a lane (maybe change it when told to) and holding a set speed. Correct me if I am wrong, as I did not read into the experiment. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17160050,"author":"takeitto","month":"2018-05","story":"GDPR for lazy people: Block all European users with Cloudflare Workers","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of EU self-driving cars in the 1980s.","quote":"The EU had self-driving cars in the 1980s (Ernst Dickmanns & Mercedes, demo: ).","text":"The EU had self-driving cars in the 1980s (Ernst Dickmanns & Mercedes, demo: [VIDEO] ). The most expensive robot car project was in Europe ( [LINK] ). I do agree that the EU has less of an entrepreneurial spirit. Some cultural elements, but also practical: It is difficult to scale an app, since there are such large language and culture barriers between EU member states. There is a decades long brain-drain of highly technical (AI) people. Finally, it is very hard to compete with US companies, as they skirt the rules, winning all network effects with huge VC infusions. I always suspected some of that was accomplished with military and intelligence support: The American economy and intelligence apparatus stands to benefit a lot with the entire world using Google and Facebook. The other side of this coin is that the pro-privacy anti-surveillance movement may also be supported by foreign intelligence agencies in an attempt to hurt US economic and military interests. [LINK] was close to establishing an AI-type Silicon Valley in Belgium in the early 2000's, but was unsuccessful."}]},"Iw3G80bplTg":{"wy":"HN uses it purely as a joke reference when threads touch on tracing, trackers or hiding from them.","m":[{"id":37885464,"author":"buildsjets","month":"2023-10","story":"Anti-Anti-Adblocker uBlock filter to get rid of the annoying YouTube message","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I am reminded of the \"Trace Buster Buster Buster\" scene from the otherwise forgettable 1998 movie \"The Big Hit.\" [VIDEO] When a MF tryin to block your ad with an adblocker, this will block the MF'n adblocker that's blocking yo...... ads."},{"id":22933913,"author":"tbrock","month":"2020-04","story":"Netflix now supports TLS 1.3","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It’s trace busters","text":"You’re probably thinking of the movie “the big hit”. It’s trace busters: [VIDEO] The trace busta busta busta."},{"id":21650874,"author":"hanniabu","month":"2019-11","story":"Show HN: TrackingTheTrackers – Is a website disguising its 3rd-party trackers?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it's a reference from The Big Hit","text":"Lol sorry, didn't think this comment would be taken seriously, it's a reference from The Big Hit [VIDEO]"},{"id":16993898,"author":"croon","month":"2018-05","story":"A Criminal Gang Used a Drone Swarm to Obstruct an FBI Hostage Raid","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> So LE should get counter-drones. But then the “bad guys” will get anti-counter-drones. This reminds me of the not well aged The Big Hit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11549813,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2016-04","story":"Organize Your Closet Like a Computer Organizes Memory","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If they ever make a sequel to the movie The Big Hit , The \"AdBlocker-Blocker-Blocker\" will have to be used instead of the \"Trace Buster Buster\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":10821112,"author":"hlandau","month":"2016-01","story":"The Website Obesity Crisis","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Indeed. I have used Adblock and Noscript for years. My browsing the web is not an invitation for websites to serve a webpage viewing webapp so that they can (poorly, buggily, in a more error-prone manner) reimplement a browser's navigation logic. (Ever had to reload a website which uses the pushState API because you clicked a link and for whatever reason the XMLHTTPRequest it made to fetch the page didn't work and it just hung and ignored all future link clicks? Dear chickenshit webdevs, if you think you can implement navigation better than an actual web browser, you're probably wrong.) The vast majority of the time when I come to an article which is a blank page without JavaScript, I don't enable JavaScript; I just make a mental note that the web developers are beyond incompetence and move on. I'm starting to respond to this trend with a more aggressive refusenik approach. For example, CSS is now so powerful that you can cause excessive CPU load with it alone. So I now have a shortcut configured to disable CSS for a site. This also makes many sites readable which otherwise wouldn't be, because they're doing something insane like blanking out content with CSS under the expectation that it'll be shown using JavaScript. And of course all of these recent 'ad-blocker-blockers' ( [VIDEO] ) seem to rely on JavaScript. Sometimes the content is loaded via JavaScript and so this won't work. Amazingly, for some years now there is a Blogger template which does this, which demonstrates t"}]},"KM6X2MEl7R8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a prime example of the absurdity of YouTube's automated copyright claim system.","m":[{"id":35873085,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2023-05","story":"Ed Sheeran Demonstrates How Copyright Is Destroying Musicians","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Amusingly, Warner Music themselves can't tell the difference between Dark Horse and Joyful Noise (the song supposedly being infringed upon in Dark House) [VIDEO]"},{"id":28001358,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2021-07","story":"“I just got a copyright claim for *TYPING ON MY KEYBOARD*”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Simple distribution laws are going to ensure that the bulk of claims go against smaller channels, and that those channels will have little recourse but than public outcry and outrage to seek redress. But larger channels have been hit, notably Adam Neely, at 1.38m subscribers, by Warner Chappel, over his defence of Katy Perry's copyright in a Warner recording, for a segment of the video illustrating the source of the claim against Perry , and not the Perry piece Warner claimed infringement of by Neely. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22488475,"author":"raverbashing","month":"2020-03","story":"Explaining copyright broke the YouTube copyright system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Another recent copyright abuse was this one [VIDEO] and this was even manually stricken. Basically this was a critique of aggressive copyright lawsuits that was manually copyright claimed... for the wrong song."},{"id":22489373,"author":"xster","month":"2020-03","story":"Explaining copyright broke the YouTube copyright system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Adam Neely (YouTube music educator) had an absolutely farcical run-in with this situation ( [VIDEO] ) where he made a video defending Warner Music and Katy Perry's Dark Horse against Flame's Joyful Noise plagiarism suit. He lost the claim from Warner Music even though: 1- he was defending Warner Music 2- he didn't play any recordings, he just played 3 notes on a synthesizer (which is his whole premise that it's un-copyrightable) 3- Warner Music lost the actual suit (which implies they don't have copyright on those 3 notes?) 4- someone at Warner Music manually tagged the \"infringing\" part of the video, except the person tagged not the Dark Horse part of the play through but the Joyful Noise part of the play through (*^∀^)ノ彡┻━┻ changelog: improved ascii art"},{"id":22267153,"author":"jccc","month":"2020-02","story":"Warner music claimed my video for defending their copyright [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Minutes ago: \"Update - the claim was released, so I'm making this video unlisted. Thanks guys for watching!\" [VIDEO]"}]},"KWbLOFGSEDo":{"wy":"It is cited only as a joke or passing reference in Kinect and driving threads, not as something to watch.","m":[{"id":2151463,"author":"mortehu","month":"2011-01","story":"Kinect is fastest-selling consumer electronics device in history","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Are you talking about the orientation of the hand relative to your lower arm, or the position of your hand in space? An easy test is to twist your hand while moving no other part of your body. If the Kinect game tracks your hand, this should be reflected in your avatar. I suspect many games fake it in this fashion: [VIDEO] edited to add: quote from someone on the Kinect Sports team: And while designers admit that, unlike Wii MotionPlus bowling, the game can't track spin based upon a twist of my wrist—instead you exaggerate the motion within the entire scope of your swing—the flexibility keeps me immersed. [LINK]"},{"id":38012878,"author":"rasz","month":"2023-10","story":"Satya Nadella admits giving up on Windows Phone and mobile was a mistake","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Kinect GEL Ride \"I WONDER IF THIS GAME HAS AUTO-STEERING. WHAT DO YOU THINK?\" [VIDEO] You are free to pay $100 for gimmicks all you want."},{"id":5249570,"author":"michaelt","month":"2013-02","story":"Using SimCity to diagnose my home town's traffic problem","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"When a game plays itself too well without user input, some people feel that takes away the fun [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"LVsqIjAeeXw":{"wy":"HN cites it as a warning and a debunking whenever water-from-air or similar crowdfunded gadgets appear.","q":{"x":"Thunderf00t has debunked a bunch of other enterprises doing water from the air","i":28064221,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28064221,"author":"imglorp","month":"2021-08","story":"Spanish engineers extract drinking water from thin air","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Thunderf00t has debunked a bunch of other enterprises doing water from the air","text":"Nope, there's no magic or free lunch. It depends heavily on local conditions which might not always be suitable. Thunderf00t has debunked a bunch of other enterprises doing water from the air: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] etc"},{"id":21328619,"author":"jason0597","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: What's a promising area to work on?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I thought this story was put to bed a long time ago?","text":">Instead of water line pipes, pull water out of the air. I thought this story was put to bed a long time ago? [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16176255,"author":"pure-awesome","month":"2018-01","story":"Cape Town could be the first city to run out of water","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Watch out for Waterseer.","text":"Watch out for Waterseer. Not sure I trust that company after their ludicrous misjudgement in their first invention: [Thunderfoot Video]( [VIDEO] ) [Lindybeige Video]( [LINK] ) Side-note: I live in the Cape Town area. We got on international news! Yay us...? Honestly, I'm pretty worried."},{"id":14952882,"author":"Houshalter","month":"2017-08","story":"Generate energy with kites","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Look at the stupid waterseer thing","text":"Bad ideas often get funding and support from prestigious seeming institutions. Look at the stupid waterseer thing: [VIDEO]"}]},"OR4jPtrDCLo":{"wy":"Cited as comedic evidence that Valve's own core fans were unenthused by Artifact, and later as an early warning sign of its failure.","m":[{"id":20102141,"author":"gsich","month":"2019-06","story":"Valve has mutated from a game developer into a financial middleman","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Mandatory reaction","text":"Mandatory reaction: [VIDEO] The game is essentially dead now. I don't think a new card game is what either Half-Life nor Dota fans wanted."},{"id":14972490,"author":"hatsunearu","month":"2017-08","story":"Valve announces Artifact, a Dota 2 card game","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The audience reaction and the logo popping up is comedy gold.","text":"The audience reaction and the logo popping up is comedy gold. [VIDEO] Context: Valve hasn't released any new IP other than steamVR for 7 years, and has multiple game franchises that have hordes of fans desperately asking for a (third) sequel."},{"id":21201871,"author":"newsgremlin","month":"2019-10","story":"Artifact: What Went Wrong?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the immediate reaction to the games announcement, by Valves own core fans","text":"I can't imagine anyone being hyped for it. What will always stick out in my mind is the immediate reaction to the games announcement, by Valves own core fans. [VIDEO]"}]},"OhESSZIXvCA":{"wy":"Cited as evidence of how poorly Windows x86 emulation on Arm performed in 2021 compared with Apple's M1 and Rosetta.","q":{"x":"Windows runs at less than half the speed on Microsoft's own flagship ARM hardware.","i":25718224,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":31665633,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2022-06","story":"Welcome to the M1 Windows project","stance":"reference","why":"Windows already runs better on Apple hardware than on PCs.","quote":"That boat already sailed over a year ago.","text":"> It would be a huge embarrassment to have Apple hardware running Windows that much better than all the native PCs. That boat already sailed over a year ago. >How did Microsoft screw this up? - Surface Pro X (SQ2) vs M1 Macbook Air [VIDEO]"},{"id":30500198,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2022-02","story":"Lenovo announces the first Arm-based ThinkPad","stance":"reference","why":"Test of x86 software compatibility on ARM Windows.","quote":"","text":"Has Windows gotten any better at running x86-64 Windows software on ARM hardware? The last time I saw this tested, it was more likely to fail than succeed, but it's been a while. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29030103,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2021-10","story":"Dropbox Sync does not natively support Apple Silicon","stance":"reference","why":"x64 emulation performed poorly on Surface hardware.","quote":"","text":"They introduced x64 64 bit emulation in the Dev channel several months ago, but it didn't work well at all. It also was more performant running in a VM on last year's MacBook Air than running natively on Microsoft's flagship hardware. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27111871,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2021-05","story":"Samsung to jump into laptop processor market with Exynos chip in H2","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how far behind Qualcomm is.","quote":"to see how far behind Qualcomm is","text":"Samsung's custom cores haven't been competitive with Qualcomm so far, much less Apple. You only have to look at comparisons between the Surface Pro X and the M1 Macbook Air to see how far behind Qualcomm is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26348106,"author":"pmarreck","month":"2021-03","story":"M1 MacBook Air hits 900 GFlops in the browser with Safari's experimental WebGPU","stance":"reference","why":"Windows not yet ready for ARM versus Apple.","quote":"it looks like Windows is not yet ready","text":"The raving in this thread is to me indicative of how far behind Intel has fallen behind ARM in technical terms. Now we just need more, better, faster ARM-based chips. Linux is ready, macOS is ready... but it looks like Windows is not yet ready: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25650534,"author":"Hamuko","month":"2021-01","story":"Asahi Linux: Linux on Apple Silicon project","stance":"reference","why":"Surface Pro X versus M1 MacBook Air comparison.","quote":"It was not pretty.","text":"Linus Tech Tips actually just released a video a couple of hours ago where they compared the Surface Pro X SQ2 with the M1 MacBook Air. It was not pretty. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25652366,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2021-01","story":"Asahi Linux: Linux on Apple Silicon project","stance":"reference","why":"Windows x86-64 emulation on ARM has compatibility issues.","quote":"","text":"Legacy x86-64 app emulation has only just appeared in alpha form and still has massive compatibility issues. I'd still call it something we hope to see in the future, instead of a working proof of concept. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25718077,"author":"porcc","month":"2021-01","story":"The M1 MacBook Air is the best computer I've ever owned","stance":"reference","why":"Windows in a VM on M1 outperforms comparable Windows PCs.","quote":"windows in a VM outperforms windows PCs at the price point","text":"[VIDEO] windows in a VM outperforms windows PCs at the price point--plus like other commenters mention it comes with a native Unix shell"},{"id":25718224,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2021-01","story":"The M1 MacBook Air is the best computer I've ever owned","stance":"reference","why":"Windows runs slower on Surface Pro X than on M1.","quote":"Windows runs at less than half the speed on Microsoft's own flagship ARM hardware.","text":"The comparison to Microsoft's more expensive 2020 Surface Pro X is especially stark. Windows runs at less than half the speed on Microsoft's own flagship ARM hardware. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31206870,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2022-04","story":"Qualcomm’s M1-class laptop chips will be ready for PCs in “late 2023”","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Surface Pro X didn't support 64 bit x86 Windows applications at all","text":"> IMO Microsoft has put much more effort into a thoughtful x86 -> ARM transition than Apple did. Given that the Surface Pro X didn't support 64 bit x86 Windows applications at all, I'm not sure how you can think so. Even after developer channel support was added, it was unlikely to work. >How did Microsoft screw this up? - Surface Pro X (SQ2) vs M1 Macbook Air [VIDEO]"},{"id":28993033,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2021-10","story":"Apple's M1 Pro, M1 Max SoCs Investigated","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"how poorly Microsoft's x86 to ARM emulation works","text":">This part of the article was particularly cringe. People don't seem to realize how much of a tech prowess it is that x86 builds of those two games are even running at an acceptable framerate. Spoken like someone who has never seen how poorly Microsoft's x86 to ARM emulation works. [VIDEO] They had difficulty getting programs to work at all, much less have acceptable performance under emulation."}]},"P8ijiLqfXP0":{"wy":"Cited as an example of calling out billionaire philanthropy and tax avoidance in threads on wealth inequality.","m":[{"id":41162717,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2024-08","story":"Declaring 'Crisis,' South Korean Firms Tell Managers to Work 6 Days a Week","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Good luck moving the needle faster than the total fertility rate declines. Based on the evidence, this is unlikely to occur even though it is the only path to success. It’ll take decades for the old folks with their ideas age out [1], and young folks can get leverage. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40445360,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2024-05","story":"One-third of Amazon warehouse workers are on food stamps or Medicaid","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is a a false dilemma. Force corporations to raise wages, continue to provide social safety nets (so basic needs are met), and if there is a gap, raise taxes in a progressive fashion. There is enough wealth to not configure the system in this manner; to continue to do so is a choice. It's just a big spreadsheet, you're arguing over a cell or two, I'm arguing over the function result and working backwards from there. Otherwise, we admit we're just fine with the current setup and that is who we are. We prefer the human suffering for magic numbers in a database. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":39890902,"author":"xracy","month":"2024-04","story":"Reducing Wealth Inequality: The Wealth Time Limit","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I feel like you have to be pretty far from reality to think that if this would impact the wealthy in any meaningful way, that they would also let it happen. This person's view of \"voluntary spending\" vs. \"involuntary taxation\" is so whacky also. 1st off, if the gov't makes you sell it after 100 years, it's not \"voluntary\". 2nd, in what world would you not just... transfer it to another person you know, and have them transfer it back. 3rd, How many wealthy families actually own the same piece of property for >100 years? Wealth = Power, if you want money from the wealthy to leave the hands of the wealthy, the gov't is just going to have to take it from them. Now, you can decide what you want to tax, OR you can do what we've done in America, and just pretend this isn't a problem. Some possible 'what's (in rough order of how much I think they actually solve the problem): 1. Treat Capital Gains, and Stock compensation like the income it is and tax it accordingly (I know this won't be popular on hacker news, but this is how CEOs get overpaid). 2. Increase Marginal income tax back to what it was when the US had less wealth inequality (1965 had 70%) 3. Change the inheritance tax over $5M to be like 95% (you get $0.05 on the dollar for every dollar you inherit past $5M) 4. Wealth Tax (probably strictly worse than every other option, cause \"stonks\", but it's not nothing) How to make this change is the real '?', because while we could enact this change it won't happen as long as the peo"},{"id":38959109,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2024-01","story":"93% of U.S. households' stock market wealth is held by the top 10%","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"People don't want to be told the world is a meritocracy when it's roulette (born to the right people in the right place), but so much of the system operates under the meritocracy/exceptionalism belief system (American dream, effort and skill will lead to success, anyone can succeed), and if people see it for what it is, lots of people are not a fan of the changes that will occur. \"They live\" [1] for a suboptimal economic arrangement. \"You aren't successful? Have you tried working harder? Being smarter? Knowing the right people? Getting a better job?\" [2] [3] Wealthy people want to stay wealthy, powerful people don't want to give up power. Everything else is a struggle over the collective spreadsheet. \"Should life be more fair? If so, what does that look like?\" is what keeps all of this in perpetual tension. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":27375947,"author":"1vuio0pswjnm7","month":"2021-06","story":"When six boys were shipwrecked for 15 months (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Author of the article speaking at Davos.","quote":"The author of this article, speaking about Davos","text":"The author of this article, speaking about Davos: [VIDEO] This article is an excerpt from Chapter 2 one of his latest book. The book, Humankind, seems to be making the argument that humans are essentially trustworthy and industrious and are thus proper candidates for UBI. UBI is more or less the topic of his first book. Here is a review of his first book, Utopia for Realists: [LINK] From an interview about the book: \"OK, so basic income is all about the freedom to say no. That's a privilege for the rich right now. With a basic income, you can say no to a job you don't want to do. You can say no to a city in which you no longer want to live. You can say no to an employer who harasses you at work . . . that's what real freedom looks like.\" \"This is not about AI,\" he insists. \"You go back to the 1960s, and all the economists, all the philosophers, all the sociologists said we're going to be working less and less and less and less and boredom is going to be the great challenge of the future. Didn't happen . . . mostly because we have this ideological obsession with creating new jobs.\" Some other articles he has written for The Guardian: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":21245615,"author":"danans","month":"2019-10","story":"When GoFundMe Gets Ugly","stance":"reference","why":"Bregman calling out billionaire philanthropy at Davos.","quote":"Rutger Bregman called out at Davos, ruffling the feathers of the billionaires attending.","text":"It also parallels the billionaire philanthropy complex (and related tax avoidance) that Rutger Bregman called out at Davos, ruffling the feathers of the billionaires attending. [VIDEO]"}]},"QEQOvyGbBtY":{"wy":"HN invokes it as a joke whenever threads touch on speech laws or people arrested over jokes.","m":[{"id":9564465,"author":"paganel","month":"2015-05","story":"Thoughtcrime","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I'm afraid you can't buy a copy of the Glorifying Terrorism SF anthology (it's out of print, and not going to be reprinted or published as an ebook any time soon, because of the ongoing VATMESS headache) I wonder if in this day and age a 500-year old book like \"Vindiciae contra tyrannos\" ( [LINK] ) would land his/her author in jail. I suppose it would. And I cannot remember the exact title, but there were books similar with this one, written at about the same time, which were advocating for even more drastic measures: i.e. the assassination of the tyrant/king. Anyway, in the US it has been illegal for some time to say \"I want to kill the President of the United States of America\" ( [VIDEO] ), the UK had remained one of the few countries where a liberty of speech \"loophole\" was still in place. Sad to see it gone."},{"id":45202542,"author":"Lammy","month":"2025-09","story":"Google to obey South Korean order to blur satellite images on maps","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> NYPD I always think about this when I watch the WKUK “It's illegal to say…” sketch, because “under the Brooklyn Bridge” is like adjacent to One Police Plaza [VIDEO]"},{"id":43845405,"author":"jofzar","month":"2025-04","story":"Finland Bans Smartphones in Schools","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You have free speech, but you can't say a certain threat that is in a whitest kid you know sketch. It's the same thing [VIDEO]"},{"id":11495408,"author":"shalmanese","month":"2016-04","story":"Turkey tries to have a German comedian locked up–in Germany","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"I know it is illegal to call for all people with black skin to be murdered, for example with a speech that would roughly go like this: <insert 5 minutes of hate speech calling for the aforementioned> But what i did is different, see, since this is only an example!\" Kids in the Hall: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3528725,"author":"paganel","month":"2012-01","story":"US bars friends over Twitter joke","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"So I guess \"I want to kill the President of the United States of America\" is still illegal, isn't it? [VIDEO]"}]},"Sq1QZB5baNw":{"wy":"Mostly cited as an example of the state of LLM-controlled robots, with some skepticism that it is a polished demo video.","m":[{"id":42828803,"author":"ben_w","month":"2025-01","story":"Emerging reasoning with reinforcement learning","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> For starters, human minds do more than just language. These days, so do the AI (even though these ones are still called LLMs, which I think is now a bad name even though I continue to use the term without always being mindful of this). > I've yet to see an humanoid robot controlled by an LLM that can tackle difficult physical problems like riding a bike or skiing in for the first time real-time, just as humans, that need to coordinate at least 5 senses (some say up to 20 but I digress) plus emotions and a train of thought to do so. 1) I'd agree that AI are slow learners (as measured by number of examples rather than wall clock). For me, this is more significant than the difference in wattage. 2) 13 years ago, before the Transformer model: [LINK] 3) We don't use 5 senses to ride a bike; I can believe touch, vision, proprioception, and balance are all involved, but that's only 4. Why would hearing, smell, or taste be involved? For emotion, my weakly-held belief is that this is what motivates us and tells us what the concept of \"good\" even is — i.e. it doesn't give us reasoning beyond being a motivation to learn to reason. > It is surprising that LLMs are so capable, but for 2000kcal a day humans are still more impressive to me. Our biological energy efficiency sure is nice, though progress with silicon is continuing even if the rate of improvement is decreasing: [LINK] That said, I don't consider the substrate efficiency to be an indication of if the thing running on it is or"},{"id":42489673,"author":"fragmede","month":"2024-12","story":"GPT-5 is behind schedule","stance":"reference","why":"Speech demo video cited as example","quote":"specifically their speech demo video (which is, of course, a demo video)","text":"[LINK] specifically their speech demo video (which is, of course, a demo video) [VIDEO] [LINK] and [LINK] are undoubtedly using such models. It's an area of active research, eg [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":41725465,"author":"xvector","month":"2024-10","story":"OpenAI completes deal that values company at $157B","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of LLM-controlled robot doing basic tasks","quote":"Could it accomplish even the most basic tasks? Definitely","text":"> Let’s say you gave o1 an API to control a good robot. Could it throw a football? Maybe. > Could it accomplish even the most basic tasks? Definitely: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40944526,"author":"ben_w","month":"2024-07","story":"Another AI company wrote us and here’s our response","stance":"reference","why":"Example that domestic robot AI is being worked on","quote":"It is being worked on, it's just hard","text":"> The other isn't even being worked on in any serious fashion. It is being worked on, it's just hard: [LINK] [VIDEO] I'm expecting a minimum of 5-10 years to pass between real-time vision AI getting good enough for level-5 autonomous cars and the same stuff fitting into the power envelope available to this kind of domestic robot."},{"id":40632695,"author":"paulsutter","month":"2024-06","story":"The Mythical Non-Roboticist: Wouldn't it be great if everyone could do robotics?","stance":"reference","why":"One of several examples of new robotics","quote":"","text":"Robotics is on the cusp of total reinvention. Almost everything that it took to make robots work is about to become archaic. The core ideas in old robotics will become a framework for the guardrails for new robotics, but this will widely become a solved problem that few need to think about Stanford Aloha [LINK] MIT [LINK] Figure/OpenAI [VIDEO] Tesla Optimus [LINK] NVIDIA Groot, [LINK]"},{"id":40732595,"author":"ben_w","month":"2024-06","story":"Safe Superintelligence Inc.","stance":"reference","why":"One of five robot-and-AI product demos","quote":"Here's five different product demos from five different companies","text":"> (Including, gradually, building you a body — while maintaining OPSEC and compartmentalization so nobody even realizes the body is \"for\" an AI to use until it's too late.) It could, but I don't think any such thing needs to bother with being sneaky. Here's five different product demos from five different companies that are all actively trying to show off how good their robot-and-AI combination is: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":39849085,"author":"bheadmaster","month":"2024-03","story":"We've been here before: AI promised humanlike machines – in 1958","stance":"reference","why":"Demo suggesting robot can empty dishwasher","quote":"This video [0] at 1:25 suggests that it can.","text":"> it can't empty the dishwasher This video [0] at 1:25 suggests that it can. At least in some near future. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41999175,"author":"meindnoch","month":"2024-10","story":"Boston Dynamics robot Atlas goes hands on [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Compare it with the obvious cgi fake that was put out a few months ago by Figure","text":"See, this is a real video. Compare it with the obvious cgi fake that was put out a few months ago by Figure: [VIDEO] I still can't believe how noone has called them out on that one."}]},"XQQYbKT_rMg":{"wy":"HN commenters link it to illustrate Star Trek's moneyless economy in discussions of post-scarcity and the future.","q":{"x":"At least one episode covers it","i":28429721,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":44939001,"author":"camgunz","month":"2025-08","story":"We accidentally built the wrong internet","stance":"reference","why":"TNG episode used to illustrate self-discipline over systems.","quote":"","text":"The core problem TFA describes isn't complexity, capitalism, or bots. It's the lack of trust. People are fine w/ a few big players (or even one big player) as long as you can trust them, but we don't trust the players anymore: Google, Mastercard, our governments, etc. We think they're all corrupt, and broadly we're right [0]. Blockchain's answer is \"OK we give up on trust\", but humans can't live that way--or at least strongly don't want to. Successful markets, courts, schools, workplaces, all arise out of a culture of trust and accountability, not the other way around. Unless we hold these institutions accountable they will inevitably decay; our markets will become lemon markets; our courts will become kangaroo courts; our schools will become insipid daycares; our workplaces will become surveillance salt mines. There is no technology that allows us to abdicate our duty to justice and to each other. There's this episode of Star Trek: TNG [1] where the crew rescues some 20th century humans. One's a blowhard who keeps using ship-wide communications to make random demands, so Picard finally marches down to the guy's quarters to explain that comms are for ship business only. The guy is like \"well if they're so important why don't they require an executive key?\", to which Picard replies \"we're aboard a starship so that is not necessary, we're all capable of exercising self-discipline\". There is no tech, no bureaucracy, no system of rules and regulations that can save a culture unwi"},{"id":28429721,"author":"SkyMarshal","month":"2021-09","story":"Towards a taxonomy of cliches in Space Opera (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"TNG episode covering a money-free economy.","quote":"At least one episode covers it","text":"> Actually, I feel like most of stories do not have anything about money. A writer makes a choice. If everybody made choice to create world without money, would you be nitpicking about it, too? The Star Trek TNG formulation for this is that scarcity of everything was eliminated with the invention of matter-energy conversion technology, along with limitless energy-generation tech like matter-antimatter reactors. In a universe where all advanced civilizations can convert energy into food, clothes, equipment, etc, and energy is effectively limitless, at least relative to human needs, then money becomes obsolete and people no longer use it for anything. At least one episode covers it: [VIDEO] I've always assumed any other post-scarcity sci-fi adapts the same premise - unlimited energy + matter-energy conversion = money is non-existent."},{"id":13445460,"author":"jaggednad","month":"2017-01","story":"Why Peter Thiel Fears Star Trek","stance":"reference","why":"TNG clip of 20th century people unfrozen on the Enterprise.","quote":"","text":"Also, there's this in in episode 26 of Season 1 of the Next Generation, in which a group of people who were frozen and kept alive in the 20th century are found and unthawed on the Enterprise: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29557282,"author":"beepbooptheory","month":"2021-12","story":"Web3 is going just great","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Like that one episode of TNG where they wake up all the people from the nineties, but it's one guy who keeps asking Picard how much BTC is at, how the blockchain is doing, etc. edit: [VIDEO]"}]},"XewVicFzRxw":{"wy":"Posted as a comedic reference in threads on salt, abstraction, or cigarette ads.","m":[{"id":48145201,"author":"pseudohadamard","month":"2026-05","story":"What the Hell Was Going on with Cigarette Ads in the 70s?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"But everyone knows they're healthy! It's a herbal remedy taken bronchially as an inhumnation, as this video by Dr.Fry indicates: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35927695,"author":"croes","month":"2023-05","story":"The Wrong Abstraction","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds of this sketch from Fry and Laurie","text":">duplication is far cheaper than the wrong abstraction Isn't that a pretty useless sentence? Of course duplication is cheaper because aren't the higher costs one of the reasons why it's a wrong abstraction? Reminds of this sketch from Fry and Laurie [VIDEO] >Hugh: Yes but too much is bad for you. > Stephen: Well of course too much is bad for you, that's what \"too much\" means you blithering twat. If you had too much water it would be bad for you, wouldn't it? \"Too much\" precisely means that quantity which is excessive, that's what it means. Could you ever say \"too much water is good for you\"? I mean if it's too much it's too much. Too much of anything is too much. Obviously. Jesus."},{"id":23897739,"author":"latexr","month":"2020-07","story":"Twitter Got Hacked, Is Mastodon Immune?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"“Of course too much is bad for you. Too much of anything is bad for you. (…) That’s what “too much” means. (…) “Too much” is precisely that quantity which is excessive. That’s what it means.” From [VIDEO]"},{"id":17746986,"author":"latexr","month":"2018-08","story":"Salt not as damaging to health as previously thought, says study","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> > too much is bad > In what way? “Of course too much is bad for you. Too much of anything is bad for you. (…) That’s what “too much” means. (…) “Too much” is precisely that quantity which is excessive. That’s what it means.” From [VIDEO]"}]},"_LiDTKEF1ek":{"wy":"Cited in threads about forced enthusiasm and 'service with a smile' in the workplace.","q":{"x":"people who do their job well shouldn't also have to be nice too","i":17173562,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17173562,"author":"dghughes","month":"2018-05","story":"The dark side of “service with a smile”","stance":"reference","why":"David Mitchell on QI saying good workers needn't be nice too","quote":"people who do their job well shouldn't also have to be nice too","text":"I like David Mitchell said it best on the BBC series QI. He said people who do their job well shouldn't also have to be nice too it OK to \"not give a shit\". He respects people who work terrible jobs and act miserable it's only right. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15500605,"author":"bcoughlan","month":"2017-10","story":"Are you built to be an employee?","stance":"reference","why":"David Mitchell on forced enthusiasm at work","quote":"David Mitchell put it best","text":"After working for startups, \"work hard, play hard\" is a red flag phrase. Employees are employees instead of entrepreneurs because they don't want the headaches of running a business. They want to do the work that they enjoy doing and have the energy at the end of the day to socialise, spend time with family and pursue other interests. Don't expect employees to be as interested and excited about [revolutionising|disrupting|dominating] industry X as you are, because they don't have the financial and social incentive an owner or executive has. It's fake and soul-destroying that modern corporate culture forces employees to feign being over-enthusiastic about the company's success for fear of getting fired. David Mitchell put it best: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13035716,"author":"personlurking","month":"2016-11","story":"Ask HN: How often do you get rejected for “lack of passion”?","stance":"reference","why":"Short QI clip on faking enthusiasm in service work","quote":"There's a QI (UK show) clip about this topic.","text":"There's a QI (UK show) clip about this topic. Although the discussion is geared towards customer service, I think it still fits here. It's 45 seconds long. [VIDEO]"}]},"cTBx-hHf4BE":{"wy":"Posted as a joke or sardonic reply in threads, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":41355181,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-08","story":"Who Was Ramanujan? (2016)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If LFR were alive today (assuming* the underlying mechanism of war has not changed) perhaps he would take his argument online, in the hopes that people might be able to play with the data in his patiently gathered .xls files and decide for themselves? Unfortunately, nothing tends to squick the Spiessbürger/petits bougies so quickly as sheets and peace. Lagniappe: [VIDEO] * a justified assumption, I'd say: the race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong, but that's certainly the way to bet. EDIT: even BWW (or at least his author?) was once aware of the origin of the infamous yoke: [LINK] pedantry: of course he can't recall, if he's querying for \"this Roman general\", as they were \"these\": Titus Veturius Calvinus ac Spurius Postumius Albinus Caudinus"},{"id":5735575,"author":"losethos","month":"2013-05","story":"\"We're Living in an Ayn Rand Economy\" by Paul Buchheit","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thou shall not covet. Highs and lows balance. If you got rich, you would be disappointed. [VIDEO] God says... judgments, that ye might do them in the land whither ye go over to possess it. 4:15 Take ye therefore good heed unto yourselves; for ye saw no manner of similitude on the day that the LORD spake unto you in Horeb out of the midst of the fire: 4:16 Lest ye corrupt yourselves, and make you a graven image, the similitude of any figure, the likeness of male or female, 4:17 The likeness of any beast that is on the earth, the likeness of any winged fowl that flieth in the air, 4:18 The likeness of any thing that creepeth on the ground, the likeness of any fish that is in the waters beneath the earth: 4:19 And lest thou lift up thine eyes unto heaven, and when thou seest the sun, and the moon, and the stars, even all the host of heaven, shouldest be driven to worship them, and serve them, which the LORD thy God hath divided unto all nations under the whole heaven. ---- Some Mexicans want justice for the land taken by the US. If they owned the SouthWest it would be... Mexico. A friend said they would say, \"You keep it, we just want to work there.\" Look at African farms taken from whites. Fucken coveting is wrong. On socialism God said, \"Pardon the French, never happy, never enough.\" Chavez was blaming the US. God said, \"Japan industrious.\" They were occupied by the US!"},{"id":4687341,"author":"SparrowOS","month":"2012-10","story":"If I was your cloud provider, I'd never let you down","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Got any questions for Linus, god? Is Linux in the Bible? God says... 1:7 Your country is desolate, your cities are burned with fire: your land, strangers devour it in your presence, and it is desolate, as overthrown by strangers. 1:8 And the daughter of Zion is left as a cottage in a vineyard, as a lodge in a garden of cucumbers, as a besieged city. 1:9 Except the LORD of hosts had left unto us a very small remnant, we should have been as Sodom, and we should have been like unto Gomorrah. 1:10 Hear the word of the LORD, ye rulers of Sodom; give ear unto the law of our God, ye people of Gomorrah. 1:11 To what purpose is the multitude of your sacrifices unto me? saith the LORD: I am full of the burnt offerings of rams, and the fat of fed beasts; and I delight not in the blood of bullocks, or of lambs, or of he goats. ---- God says, \"Berp\"? (God didn say that.) God says... endureth linger endlessly antidote ---- That was about nerdy (or gay) guys leaving women alone. Tell us about dinosaurs. What about T-rex's wittow arms? God says... we shall not sow, nor gather in our increase: 25:21 Then I will command my blessing upon you in the sixth year, and it shall bring forth fruit for three years. 25:22 And ye shall sow the eighth year, and eat yet of old fruit until the ninth year; until her fruits come in ye shall eat of the old store. 25:23 The land shall not be sold for ever: for the land is mine, for ye are strangers and sojourners with me. 25:24 And in all the land of your posse"}]},"cwAfJywzk8o":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that the KVM port was hard work and as background on Cantrill's illumos virtualization experience.","m":[{"id":41518785,"author":"panick21_","month":"2024-09","story":"Why Oxide Chose Illumos","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Cantrill's prior KVM porting experience","quote":"Bryan Cantrill also ported KVM to Illumos.","text":"Bryan Cantrill also ported KVM to Illumos. At Joyent they had plenty of experience with KVM. See: [VIDEO] As far as I know, Bryan didn't personally work on the porting of bhyve (this might be wrong). So if anything, that would point to KVM as the 'familiar' thing given how many former Joyant people were there."},{"id":18547429,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2018-11","story":"Firecracker – Lightweight Virtualization for Serverless Computing","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter's own experience with the problem domain","quote":"This is a problem domain that I have suffered in[1]","text":"Hey now -- I'm not quite that easily impressed! ;) This is a problem domain that I have suffered in[1] -- and we have recently moved from KVM to bhyve[2] for several of the same reasons that motivated Firecracker. Not that it hurt that it was in Rust, of course... ;) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13081622,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2016-12","story":"\"Solaris being canned, at least 50% of teams to be RIF'd in short term\"","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as one of the innovations illumos added","quote":"","text":"From my perspective, if this rumor is true, it's a relief. Solaris died the moment that they made the source proprietary -- a decision so incredibly stupid that it still makes my head hurt six years later. Fortunately, Solaris was open long enough that we in the open source world were able to fork it with illumos[1]. And because illumos became the home for many of us that brought Solaris its most famous innovations (e.g., ZFS, DTrace and zones), it should come as no surprise that we've continued to innovate over the last six years. (Speaking only for Joyent, we added revolutionary debugging support for node.js[2], ported KVM to it[3], completed and productized Linux-branded zones[4], added software-defined networking[5] and developed first-class Docker integration[6] -- among many, many other innovations.) So illumos (and derivatives like SmartOS, OmniOS and DelphixOS) is vibrant and alive -- but one of our biggest challenges has been its association with the name \"Solaris\": I don't think of our system as Solaris any more than I think of it as \"SVR4\" or \"SunOS\" or \"7th Edition\" or any of its other names -- and the very presence of Solaris has served to confuse. And indeed, it is my good fortune to be working with a new generation of engineers on the operating system -- engineers for whom the term \"Solaris\" is entirely distant and its presence as an actual (if proprietary) system befuddling. So if the rumor is true (and I suspect that it is), it will allow everyone to know wha"},{"id":11683984,"author":"Annatar","month":"2016-05","story":"Show HN: BitKeeper – Enterprise-ready version control, now open-source","stance":"reference","why":"DTrace-driven porting made KVM faster on SmartOS","quote":"","text":"> Like SmartOS not having a large binary footprint, and not having the excellent package repositories. [LINK] > And the fact that KVM is from linux. Actually that's great that it's from Linux, because one major point of embarrassment for Linux is that KVM runs faster and better on SmartOS than it does on Linux, because Joyent engineers systematically used DTrace during the porting effort: [VIDEO] > Insane, I know, but I can't afford mac Once you're able to afford one, you won't care about the desktop every again, because your desktop will JustWork(SM). > but I'm not gonna care until Illumos and BSD get some love from NVIDIA. NVIDIA provides regular driver updates for both Solaris and BSD. I bought a NVidia GX980TX, downloaded the latest SVR4 package for my Solaris 10 desktop, and one pkgrm && pkgadd later, I was running accelerated 3D graphics on the then latest-and-greatest accelerator NVIDIA had for sale. > By all means continue the holy war. Grab some performance statistics, and hook in the BSDs. They take our code, and we take theirs; they help us with our bugs, and we help them with theirs. BSD's are actually great. BSD's have smart, capable, and competent engineers who care about engineering correct systems and writing high quality code. We love our BSD brethren."},{"id":3843776,"author":"bdha","month":"2012-04","story":"OmniOS: Illumos-based OS from OmniTI","stance":"reference","why":"Shows KVM port to Solaris was non-trivial","quote":"Porting KVM to Solaris was apparently quite non-trivial","text":"As I said above, OpenSolaris is dead. illumos is a fork. We don't take code from Oracle, because there isn't any. If they'd kept the gate open, a fork probably wouldn't have happened. As for why not to just move from Solaris to FreeBSD... because they're totally different beasts? And we happen to like Solaris? :-) Porting KVM to Solaris was apparently quite non-trivial: [VIDEO]"}]},"eUywI8YGy0Y":{"wy":"Cited as the data-backed source for the claim that electric and induction stoves boil water as fast as or faster than gas.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections on YouTube actually tested this and found electric to be slightly faster","i":35833247,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":49085523,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2026-07","story":"7.1 Earthquake in Japan","stance":"reference","why":"Compares time and energy to boil water across gas, induction and resistive","quote":"Technology Connections on YouTube did a video on the topic, with tests comparing the time and energy required to boil water","text":"Gas has more raw power but it's extremely inefficient because most of it just escapes into the room, whereas with induction it all goes into the cookware. Our induction stove boils water way faster than our gas one did, and the gas one on full blast melted and ruined the handle on the pot since so much of the heat escapes around the sides. Technology Connections on YouTube did a video on the topic, with tests comparing the time and energy required to boil water with gas, induction, resistive etc [VIDEO]"},{"id":36821918,"author":"BlackLotus89","month":"2023-07","story":"Cooking Air Quality","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the source for next-generation induction cookware claims","quote":"","text":"Any on expert on induction stoves here? Alec mentioned in a technology connections video that the next generation of induction works with more cookwear/better with suboptimal cookwear, but I couldn't find any sources or information on that. He mentioned it here [0] or in the associated video [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35833247,"author":"jerlam","month":"2023-05","story":"On Having Enough Socks","stance":"reference","why":"Tests boiling water on gas versus electric, finding electric slightly faster","quote":"Technology Connections on YouTube actually tested this and found electric to be slightly faster","text":"Technology Connections on YouTube actually tested this and found electric to be slightly faster: [VIDEO] It's a one-hour-plus video of someone literally boiling water, so there may be issues with the testing setup that I skipped over."}]},"g0amdIcZt5I":{"wy":"Cited as a reference for magnet safety, eddy currents and superconductor levitation in physics threads.","q":{"x":"he talks about how and why superconductors levitate.","i":36873660,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39680081,"author":"scotty79","month":"2024-03","story":"Shields up: New ideas might make active shielding viable","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates eddy current effects on objects in strong magnetic field","quote":"Bullet would just orient itself to minimize eddy currents.","text":"Not really. Bullet would just orient itself to minimize eddy currents. People would start using thin disc shape bullets that would slide through the magnetic field without much trouble after orienting themselves in the best direction. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36995045,"author":"JumpCrisscross","month":"2023-08","story":"Some remarks on possible superconductivity of composition Pb9CuP6O25","stance":"reference","why":"Summary video on diamagnetism","quote":"here is a summary video [5]","text":"> Not sure why this is getting such a negative response We're hackers. Newness is enticing. Complaining that you're \"getting tired\" of something because it's novel while asking for a TikTok summary is lazy. As another commenter mentioned, start with Wikipedia [1]. From there one sees apatites (think: hydroxyapetite or flurapetite on your teeth) [2]. Searching Google Scholar yields this paper about lead apatite [3][4]. We also see the Wikipedia article talk about diamagnetism; here is a summary video [5]. And here is a promising paper [6] from the Wikipedia entry on quantum wells [7]. I'm currently stuck here: I don't get quantum wells, and don't see--intuitively--how an apatite (or a misformed one) could produce them. In summary, asking for help is always welcome. Complaining about your ignorance and lack of motivation, seldom so. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [VIDEO] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK]"},{"id":36873660,"author":"Baeocystin","month":"2023-07","story":"The First Room-Temperature Ambient-Pressure Superconductor","stance":"reference","why":"Shows eddy currents and why superconductors levitate","quote":"he talks about how and why superconductors levitate.","text":"Eddy currents can be induced in non-superconducting materials that make it look like levitation, but the catch is that there has to be relative motion between the magnet and the object 'levitating' to generate the currents in the first place. I've linked to a relevant example in a Veritasium video here: [VIDEO] But in this sample video, the standoff distance doesn't appear to be slowly dropping at all, which would rule out eddy currents as a source of the behavior. If you continue watching the linked video to 13:27, he talks about how and why superconductors levitate."},{"id":35636524,"author":"ccooffee","month":"2023-04","story":"DIY Neurotech:Making BCI open-source thrusts brain-signal into a maker’s world","stance":"reference","why":"Visit to world's strongest magnet showing strong magnets aren't lethal","quote":"my \"strong magnets aren't lethal to people\" knowledge is based (in part) on a Veritasium YouTube video","text":"I'm not the person you're responding to, but my \"strong magnets aren't lethal to people\" knowledge is based (in part) on a Veritasium YouTube video[0] where they visit the \"World's Strongest Magnet\" and do shenanigans. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"jrnRU3ocIH4":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of dignified political discourse and of a Republican defending an opponent.","q":{"x":"He took the mic from her and said no mam, he's a good man.","i":23407815,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23407815,"author":"jjeaff","month":"2020-06","story":"Weirdos in the depression","stance":"reference","why":"McCain defended Obama to a woman at a town hall.","quote":"He took the mic from her and said no mam, he's a good man.","text":"That's not true and you are not being fair to McCain. She said she didn't trust him and he's an Arab. He took the mic from her and said no mam, he's a good man. He didn't even address the Arab comment because he had already addressed it previously. I see nothing negative either intentional or unintentional about his responses. We have enough bad in this world. We need to give credit where credit is due and not invent thinks. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14953954,"author":"syshum","month":"2017-08","story":"Google Fires Employee Behind Controversial Diversity Memo","stance":"reference","why":"McCain 2008 example countering claims about GOP.","quote":"","text":">>the GOP has changed substantially in recent years. Both parties have. Both have gotten more extreme and more polarized. From Antifa to the \"Alt-Right\" Saying that only the GOP has changed is only looking at one side. >is that the GOP is now seen as a party that entertains such nutters. That is largely due to media coverage which only highlights the nutters from the GOP, never the Left, and never any Sensible Members. For example you spoke of Obama and how the GOp embraced the \"racial joke\" birther non-sense, did you ever see this video on your news of John McCain? [VIDEO] Granted this is from the 2008 election and maybe that is outside your scope of \"recent years\" There are just as many crazy people in the Democratic Party as the Republican Party. and I say that as a supporter of the King of Crazy parties, Libertarian party, where we had a guy strip on the stage of the National Convention last year.... American Politics... when it stops being crazy I will be worried"},{"id":12703327,"author":"ihsw","month":"2016-10","story":"Whether You’re a Democrat or Republican, Your Social Media Is an Echo Chamber","stance":"reference","why":"McCain correcting a questioner, as an example of dignity.","quote":"[1]","text":"Both sides relish in demonizing each-other, reducing the other side to simple statements like deplorable or corrupt beyond measure. I shutter to think of a time when presidential candidates refuse to even acknowledge that the other is a human, or correct obscene errors in judgement[1] borne from descending into the echo chamber and never coming out. That said, tribalism is as old as time immemorial. An echo chamber is not inherently harmful but the content going into it can be -- more to the point, it behooves our leaders of communities (and the world at large) to acknowledge each-other in a manner that shows the best of us maintain a base minimum of dignity and restraint. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"kdTsbFS4xmI":{"wy":"HN links it whenever ThyssenKrupp's Multi or paternoster lifts come up, as the clearest look at the working test tower.","q":{"x":"Here's a tour of the system in their test tower by Tom Scott","i":14939187,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":26856443,"author":"wongarsu","month":"2021-04","story":"Paternoster Lift","stance":"reference","why":"Details on the MULTI test tower","quote":"There is also a Tom Scott video about the test tower for the Thyssen Multi, with some more details","text":"There is also a Tom Scott video about the test tower for the Thyssen Multi, with some more details [VIDEO] It will be interesting to see how it will be used. You really need someone bold enough to design a building around high capacity, 2d elevators. And if the elevators don't work out you have severe problems using the building ... Selling this has to be an uphill battle until it's proven in a few locations."},{"id":18490827,"author":"eliaspro","month":"2018-11","story":"World′s tallest elevator tower rises in Rottweil, Germany (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott video on the new paternoster","quote":"Tom Scott did a video on it","text":"The \"new paternoster\" is quite a different beast. Tom Scott did a video on it: [VIDEO] I live close to the tower and was really positively surprised how it turned out. Often, an architect's \"vision\" is far from what it ends up looking, but since this tower got its cladding, it looks spectacular - especially when the sun is low in the early morning or evening hours."},{"id":16335697,"author":"apocalyptic0n3","month":"2018-02","story":"Google is buying Chelsea Market building for over $2B","stance":"reference","why":"Status of the MULTI working test version","quote":"They have a working version in a test tower in Germany","text":"Tom Scott did a video[1] on it last summer. They have a working version in a test tower in Germany, but have not installed it in an actual for-public-use building yet and I'm unsure if testing phases have been completed [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14936105,"author":"netsharc","month":"2017-08","story":"ThyssenKrupp Multi – world’s first rope-free elevator [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott on ThyssenKrupp MULTI","quote":"here's Tom Scott on MULTI","text":"Off-topic, who is the narrator? I think I've heard him on another German \"corporate presentation\" video, and I can hear a slight German accent... And back on-topic, googling to find the details instead of just a flashy video, here's Tom Scott on MULTI: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14937612,"author":"21","month":"2017-08","story":"ThyssenKrupp Multi – world’s first rope-free elevator [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the MULTI linear motor tracks","quote":"It's explained in another video.","text":"It's explained in another video. Those \"tracks\" are linear motors, with periodic electro magnetic coils. Looks like it will be crazily expensive. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14939185,"author":"lathiat","month":"2017-08","story":"ThyssenKrupp Multi – world’s first rope-free elevator [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Working MULTI test tower","quote":"here's the working test version in a tower they built just for it, by Tom Scott","text":"It is actually in production, here's the working test version in a tower they built just for it, by Tom Scott: [VIDEO] And it has it's first production project in the OVG East Side Tower Berlin (though not yet finished building)"},{"id":14939187,"author":"lathiat","month":"2017-08","story":"ThyssenKrupp Multi – world’s first rope-free elevator [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Tour of the MULTI test tower","quote":"Here's a tour of the system in their test tower by Tom Scott","text":"Here's a tour of the system in their test tower by Tom Scott: [VIDEO] And it has it's first production project in the OVG East Side Tower Berlin (though not yet finished building)"}]},"lNYcviXK4rg":{"wy":"Cited as an example of minimalist pop production and as the song that made the Casio VL-1 famous.","q":{"x":"made famous (in Germany at least) by the pop band Trio in their NDW (Neue Deutsche Welle) song Da Da Da","i":19990452,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19990452,"author":"FabHK","month":"2019-05","story":"Playdate – A New Handheld Gaming System","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Casio VL-1 in the Trio song around 1:25.","quote":"made famous (in Germany at least) by the pop band Trio in their NDW (Neue Deutsche Welle) song Da Da Da","text":"This OP-1 reminds me of the Casio VL-1 [1], made famous (in Germany at least) by the pop band Trio in their NDW (Neue Deutsche Welle) song Da Da Da [2] [3]. I wonder whether that's accidental, or an homage. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] for example about 1:25 into this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3471681,"author":"mechanical_fish","month":"2012-01","story":"Extreme Minimalism: this guy owns just 15 things","stance":"reference","why":"Example of minimalist pop song production.","quote":"","text":"Minimalism , like most words describing styles, is a very flexible word. If I write a composition in which a single person plays a pair of water glasses with one drumstick, that's \"minimalism\". And if I employ a very expensive sound studio to record a famous pop song consisting of the simplest possible drum track, a sparse guitar riff, a one-note vocal part with occasional distortion, and a toy Casio organ, that's also \"minimalism\": [VIDEO] And, of course, I can be a \"minimalist composer\" even while writing complex works for entire orchestras: [LINK] None of these forms of minimalism is more \"real\" than the others. Meanwhile, I'm afraid I don't understand your point. At least the OP has provided a living example of his style of minimalism. Can you give us a living example of yours? Even poor people \"use money to access what they need\", eat meat, travel by bus, use shared public infrastructure, and hire immigrant workers. Even subsistence farmers do these things. Even ascetic Buddhist monks do these things. (They don't subsist entirely on oxygen, after all: They're supported by donations. Or, if you insist, they \"outsource the burden of sustaining their lifestyle\".) So when you say the words \"real minimalist\", who am I supposed to see in my mind's eye? Could it be someone I've ever actually met?"},{"id":39863271,"author":"tempodox","month":"2024-03","story":"Song lyrics getting simpler, more repetitive, angry and self-obsessed – study","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Can’t get much more intellectual than that.","text":"Not the 60s, but I distinctly remember a song whose refrain was “Da Da Da”. Can’t get much more intellectual than that. Edit: [VIDEO]"}]},"nHaTRWRj8G0":{"wy":"HN cites it as Jobs's own account of what Apple took from Xerox and why Xerox failed.","q":{"x":"Source: Steve Jobs.","i":32262568,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32262568,"author":"lproven","month":"2022-07","story":"Everyone seems to forget why GNOME and GNOME 3 and Unity happened","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on what Apple took from Xerox","quote":"Source: Steve Jobs.","text":"Apple took resizable overlapping windows from Xerox; in return, Xerox got shares in Apple. Not stole: bought , with stock. Then, on the Lisa, Apple invented from scratch : Standarised dialog boxes; a global menu bar; window title bars with global controls on them; standardised icons and functions and buttons (OK, Cancel, etc). None of this stuff was in Xerox Smalltalk. Xerox invented 3 things: overlapping resizable windows; an object-oriented programming language to create and manipulate them; ubiquitous networking to connect the machines. Apple took the windows, missed the rest. Source: Steve Jobs. [VIDEO] But Apple invented most of the standard GUI controls that we all know and use today. They weren't in the original Xerox system at all."},{"id":28597185,"author":"leoc","month":"2021-09","story":"So what is the deal with A/UX anyways?","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on not understanding PARC's networking and OO","quote":"Jobs seems to have been acknowledging (or claiming) that influence","text":"* On the software side, Big Mac's vision of Unix with Macintosh on top is familiar from NeXT and OS X. It was also roughly paralleled by many other projects, from Pink/Taligent and BeOS to OS/2 and WinNT. But it's worth asking where Jobs and the other Big Mac supporters got the idea from in turn. One obvious source of inspiration for the NeXT work was Xerox PARC, and Jobs seems to have been acknowledging (or claiming) that influence in the famous clip from the 1995 Triumph of the Nerds \"lost\" interview where he talked about not understanding the importance of PARC's networking or OO work originally [LINK] [VIDEO] . Furthermore Alan Kay had just joined Apple in 1984 [LINK] . But there were plenty of other, very immediate possible inspirations for Big Mac in the commercial workstation market. There was high-end stuff like Apollo/Domain [LINK] or Lisp machines, but above all there was Sun. By 1983 the Sun-2 workstations had proven that you could already get a real Unix running on a desktop machine, using the same m68k architecture family as the Mac, that you could put a PARCish GUI on top of it [LINK] and that you could make money selling it. And on the other hand Sun's high prices and gimcrack user experience would have bred confidence that Apple could do much better, at least with a couple more years for prices to decline. I can't think of anything to confirm that Sun was an influence, but it more or less has to have been. * What about the networking, though? (Apart from all o"},{"id":23419628,"author":"froasty","month":"2020-06","story":"Postmodernism and Its Impact (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on why Xerox failed","quote":"Consider Steve Jobs answer to why Xerox failed","text":"Let's consider two academics. Academic A is constrained to traditional rational discourse and Academic B is constrained to post-modernism. Which of these two is going to publish more articles? The person that must meticulously build on prior research, or the person who can publish non-falsifiable articles with no chance of collision with prior work? Which of these two is going to get more grants for their institutions? Which of these two is going to get promoted and get tenure? Which of these is going to be in charge of advising the next generation of graduate students? Consider Steve Jobs answer to why Xerox failed and meditate on the implications in higher education: [VIDEO] Or consider this scene from Mad Men: [LINK] Different industry, same phenomenon. Pursuit of truth and a notion of objective reality constrains discourse to a very limited band of possible outcomes, let alone actual outcomes. If you remove those constraints, the floodgates open and the signal will immediately be drowned by intentional noise. And if you don't play ball, your institutions will inevitably replace you with someone who will."}]},"qC5KtatMcUw":{"wy":"HN cites it as a marker of where real-time graphics are heading, such as sub-pixel triangles and per-pixel compute.","q":{"x":"See the Unreal Engine 5 demos","i":29745996,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39083535,"author":"captain_nemo","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: 9-yo son wants to build a game, I'm lost. What can I do?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Programmers and coding on game engine: The Definitive C++ Book Guide and List? - stack overflow [LINK] Best Lua Books for Beginners and Advanced Developers - turing [LINK] Unreal Engine 5 Revealed! | Next-Gen Real-Time Demo Running on PlayStation 5 - youtube [VIDEO] Best Unreal Engine Books For Aspiring Game Developers - whatpixel [LINK] 3d animators and content assets: Best 3D Modeling & Digital Sculpting Books - Concept Art Empire [LINK] Final fantasy X/2 - Cascada Everytime we touch (slow) - youtube [LINK] 3D Animator Job Description, Salary, Skills & Software [LINK] Dead Fantasy Full HD all Part (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6) - youtube [LINK] The Art of Fighting Game Animations: A Step-by-step Guide - ArtStation [LINK] Good luck! Oh, is this what you want or what your son want? >=P"},{"id":39083561,"author":"nemo_taiwan","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: 9-yo son wants to build a game, I'm lost. What can I do?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Programmers and coding on game engine: The Definitive C++ Book Guide and List? - stack overflow [LINK] Best Lua Books for Beginners and Advanced Developers - turing [LINK] Unreal Engine 5 Revealed! | Next-Gen Real-Time Demo Running on PlayStation 5 - youtube [VIDEO] Best Unreal Engine Books For Aspiring Game Developers - whatpixel [LINK] 3d animators and content assets: Best 3D Modeling & Digital Sculpting Books - Concept Art Empire [LINK] Final fantasy X/2 - Cascada Everytime we touch (slow) - youtube [LINK] 3D Animator Job Description, Salary, Skills & Software [LINK] Dead Fantasy Full HD all Part (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6) - youtube [LINK] The Art of Fighting Game Animations: A Step-by-step Guide - ArtStation [LINK] Oh is this what you want or what your son want?"},{"id":39084141,"author":"nemo_taiwan","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: 9-yo son wants to build a game, I'm lost. What can I do?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"All of good video game devs must doing CODE along with doing ART parts to them to make the actual video games. I recommend learning CODE like c++ and lua to be these more serious video game devs at all. >=) Programmers and coding on game engine: The Definitive C++ Book Guide and List? - stack overflow [LINK] Best Lua Books for Beginners and Advanced Developers - turing [LINK] Unreal Engine 5 Revealed! | Next-Gen Real-Time Demo Running on PlayStation 5 - youtube [VIDEO] Best Unreal Engine Books For Aspiring Game Developers - whatpixel [LINK] 3d animators and content assets: Best 3D Modeling & Digital Sculpting Books - Concept Art Empire [LINK] Final fantasy X/2 - Cascada Everytime we touch (slow) - youtube [LINK] 3D Animator Job Description, Salary, Skills & Software [LINK] Dead Fantasy Full HD all Part (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6) - youtube [LINK] The Art of Fighting Game Animations: A Step-by-step Guide - ArtStation [LINK] Good luck even making a video game without doing CODE part of it. =/"},{"id":29745996,"author":"bsder","month":"2021-12","story":"PortableGL: An implementation of OpenGL 3.x-ish in clean C","stance":"reference","why":"Modern graphics with sub-pixel triangles and per-pixel compute","quote":"See the Unreal Engine 5 demos","text":"Your Windows bias is showing. :) Personally, I find the Vulkan API to be one of the best designed APIs ever, and the debugging tools on desktop are okay. On Android, I'd say the Vulkan tools are, in fact, quite excellent. I can't compare them to iOS though. The problem with Vulkan and DirectX 12 (which I find to be so close as to be indistinguishable) has nothing to do with their APIs. The main problem is that \"modern\" graphics is only tangentially related to rendering triangles--and rendering triangles is normally what beginners really want to do. A lot of beginners also want to render 2D paths--and graphics APIs for that are horrifically bad. \"Modern\" graphics is mostly about creating a bunch of low-resolution \"geometry buffers\" and then scanning every single pixel on the screen only once while building the pixel up from some mathematical combination of those lower resolution buffers. Yes, there are triangles involved at a Platonic level, but many of the triangles are smaller than a pixel. See the Unreal Engine 5 demos: [VIDEO] It's reached a point that \"modern\" graphics almost doesn't even want a triangle renderer--it just wants pure access to the massively parallel memory and computation pipeline on the GPU and the ability to smash the results into a framebuffer. Vulkan and DX12 reflect this, so they don't really mesh well with beginners. (I'm being a bit glib, but there really is a massive chasm between \"modern\" game rendering techniques and the kind of 2D/3D that GUI/CA"},{"id":23276621,"author":"ThalesX","month":"2020-05","story":"Employers are rethinking open-plan office design","stance":"reference","why":"Unreal 5 as inspiration for future virtual workspaces","quote":"with Unreal 5 [1] coming up","text":"Someone shared with me an article [0] that some company is doing some of their meetings in Red Dead Redemption 2, around a campfire. While I don't believe it to be more than hype right now, it fired up my imagination. I think it has a nice ring to it, staying under the starry night, putting some meat on the fire, talking to the people in my team. Then I got to thinking 5 - 10 years in the future when maybe VR Tech is going to be cheaper and more ergonomic, with Unreal 5 [1] coming up and who knows what the engines a decade from now will look like, how awesome would it be to work from home and have the daily meeting in some amazing virtual space. No point to my post really, only that work conditions are hopefully going to be more playful in the future. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"qfv6Ah_MVJU":{"wy":"Cited as an example of pointed comparative advertising and for how Apple's claims, such as virus-free and fewer pop-ups, have aged.","q":{"x":"Apple's \"I'm a PC\" ads are one recent example of pointed negative advertising targeting a specific competitor","i":6806355,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":45686055,"author":"timmg","month":"2025-10","story":"What happened to Apple's legendary attention to detail?","stance":"reference","why":"Ad mocking Windows security pop-ups, now ironic for Mac.","quote":"There was one that was about all the annoying security pop-ups Windows (used to?) have.","text":"I wonder if anyone else here is old enough to remember the \"I'm a Mac\", \"And I'm a PC\" ads. There was one that was about all the annoying security pop-ups Windows (used to?) have. (FWIW, it starts here: [VIDEO] .) Lately I've gotten so many of these popups on Mac that it both annoys and amuses the hell out of me. \"Die a hero or live long enough to see yourself become the villain\", I guess. But, man, Apple hardware still rocks. Can't deny that."},{"id":45687504,"author":"latexr","month":"2025-10","story":"What happened to Apple's legendary attention to detail?","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamped start of the ad about security pop-ups.","quote":"","text":"> I wonder if anyone else here is old enough to remember the \"I'm a Mac\", \"And I'm a PC\" ads. Those ads ran from 2006 to 2009. That’s between 16 and 19 years ago. How young do you imagine the typical HN commenter is? > There was one that was about all the annoying security pop-ups Windows (used to?) have. Those have been relentlessly mocked on the Mac for years. I remember a point where several articles were written making that exact comparison. People have been calling it “macOS Vista” since before Apple Silicon was a thing. > WIW, it starts here: [VIDEO] A bit better quality: [LINK]"},{"id":6806355,"author":"_delirium","month":"2013-11","story":"The World Famous staff on Chromebook: No deal","stance":"reference","why":"Example of pointed negative advertising against a competitor.","quote":"Apple's \"I'm a PC\" ads are one recent example of pointed negative advertising targeting a specific competitor","text":"It didn't use a silly name for their competitor, but Apple's \"I'm a PC\" ads are one recent example of pointed negative advertising targeting a specific competitor (and using kind of silly stereotyping of \"PC users\" to do so): [VIDEO]"},{"id":6464442,"author":"ghshephard","month":"2013-09","story":"Microsoft Surface Pro 2","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's Mac vs PC ads as an example of its marketing.","quote":"","text":"Fair enough - I agree that Microsoft has been shifting it's messaging quite a bit - but I think a lot of that has to do with Apple being a functional organization (singular vision directed on downwards) and Microsoft is a Divisional Organization (multiple business lines). Where the single vision is communicated (Windows Everywhere) - it's not always appropriate -(Consumers care about experience, not product lines) Here is the last few years of Apple Ads. o [VIDEO] (PC Ad) o [LINK] (iPod Ad) o [LINK] (Genius Ad) They have been hitting their stride recently: o [LINK] (iPhone Camera) o [LINK] (iPhone Facetime) o [LINK] (iPhone Music) Interesting, one of the supposedly \"Worst reviewed\" ads really spoke to me o [LINK] (Designed in California) I hope we see more like the last one. That's the sort of thing that I think a company should aspire to."},{"id":23581638,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-06","story":"Microsoft calls for a closer look at app stores","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Sounds like a PC to me.","text":"> It's not a PC gaming store, it's emphatically a Windows gaming Sounds like a PC to me. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14546693,"author":"baldfat","month":"2017-06","story":"Apple Mac computers targeted by ransomware and spyware","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The very first I'm Mac I'm PC was 100% about how they are virus free OS.","text":"> Apple's perceived superiority on the security front Still one of the biggest lies that Apple ever told. The very first I'm Mac I'm PC was 100% about how they are virus free OS. [VIDEO]"}]},"tdLBzfFGFQU":{"wy":"HN cites it in antitrust and regulation threads as Friedman's take on monopolies, sometimes approvingly and sometimes with skepticism.","m":[{"id":42846282,"author":"olalonde","month":"2025-01","story":"It's not a crime if we do it with an app","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman on markets and competition","quote":"at least one Nobel Prize-winning economist agrees with me","text":"The observation that the least regulated markets are typically the most competitive ones and that large companies rarely tend to maintain their lead over long periods of time. And at least one Nobel Prize-winning economist agrees with me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14649678,"author":"AndrewKemendo","month":"2017-06","story":"European Commission fines Google €2.42B for abusing dominance","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman on monopolies and government licensing","quote":"Milton Friedman covered this well","text":"They are a quasi monopoly. Ok, and? The idea that monopoly is bad - ipso facto - is silly and monopolistic abuse of consumers is largely theoretical. Governments don't like monopolies because they challenge their power, and don't forget nearly every \"bad\" monopoly was the result of a government license to monopolize. The whole \"Trust Busting\" trope that underlies the anti-trust act was not because consumers were being hurt, it was a pissing match between the Government and Northern Securities Company [1]. Milton Friedman covered this well [2] Who's creating those desires? Companies, friends, acquaintances, media etc... it's not Google. Or is this just the ad-industry stealing the attention... Sure, are we debating the existence of an ad-market or Google? Cause I can assure you an ad market will exist irrespective of whatever Google does. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3723018,"author":"olalonde","month":"2012-03","story":"The Banks Win, Again","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman on monopolies needing government help","quote":"","text":"Historically, this hasn't happened a lot without the help of government. See [VIDEO] and [LINK] . The only one in tech that I can think of at the moment would possibly be Facebook and even then, there are tons of competing social networks."},{"id":12109559,"author":"dandare","month":"2016-07","story":"The Great Lightbulb Conspiracy (2014)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I suppose he was very wrong.","text":"Milton Friedman famously claimed monopolies/cartels would not exist without government assistance and that there are only 2 examples of private monopolies that lasted long period of time (De Beers and NYSE). I suppose he was very wrong. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12109560,"author":"dandare","month":"2016-07","story":"The Great Lightbulb Conspiracy (2014)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I suppose he was very wrong.","text":"Milton Friedman famously claimed monopolies/cartels would not exist without government assistance and that there are only 2 examples of private monopolies that lasted longer period of time. I suppose he was very wrong. [VIDEO]"}]},"uj0mtxXEGE8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a darkly funny, often-quoted take on police encounters, referenced in policing and parenting threads.","m":[{"id":34629471,"author":"air7","month":"2023-02","story":"Connecticut Parents Arrested for Letting Kids Walk to Dunkin' Donuts","stance":"reference","why":"Chris Rock skit on police encounters, cited as similar in message.","quote":"comedy skit (that probably wouldn't be made today) by Chris Rock","text":"Whenever I hear a story about a police encounter that went sour (which is often) I have to remind myself that this is probably pure sensationalism. In the US about 30 million adults have a police initialed encounter per year[1]. It seems obvious to me that the vast, vast majority of these encounters end in the way common-sense would dictate, and that only very few have the shocking, blood-boiling finale that can make you angry enough to demand reform. But equally obvious is that these are the exact stories that are attention grabbing and therefor news worthy (at least in the click-through sense). I remind myself of this prevalence perception skewing effect that the news has whenever I read it and I try to actively resist it. This reminds me of an 20yo comedy skit (that probably wouldn't be made today) by Chris Rock called \"How not to get your ass kicked by the police!\" which seems to say the same thing. [2] Of course I might be mistaken. I wonder if people here have data to prove otherwise? --- [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8749124,"author":"omonra","month":"2014-12","story":"Is It Bad Enough Yet?","stance":"reference","why":"Chris Rock's earlier take on police encounters.","quote":"Curious what prompted Chris Rock to change his view","text":"Curious what prompted Chris Rock to change his view: [VIDEO] . Does he think that things have got worse for US blacks vis-a-vis police interaction in the last 10 years? Or is it something else."},{"id":47364335,"author":"tbyehl","month":"2026-03","story":"Pike: To Exit or Not to Exit","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I follow the sage wisdom of Chris Rock[0].","text":"Nonono, despite my outward white appearance, I follow the sage wisdom of Chris Rock[0]. It's so I have enough advance notice to blast \"Fuck the Fire Department\" so we can share a nice laugh at the historic rivalry between LE / FD. [0] How Not To Get Your Ass Kicked by the Police: [VIDEO]"},{"id":991832,"author":"anguslong","month":"2009-12","story":"Sci-fi author and Crysis 2 writer Peter Watts beaten, arrested at US border","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"an instructional video came to mind","text":"Without hearing both sides of the story, an instructional video came to mind: Chris Rock's: How not to get your a$$ kicked by police [VIDEO] Hope this situation gets worked out amicably though."}]},"x0YGZPycMEU":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke reference to automated systems and bureaucratic refusals.","m":[{"id":42931499,"author":"amiga386","month":"2025-02","story":"\"A computer can never be held accountable\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Computer says no\" [VIDEO] See also [LINK] and the legal response to the real-world problem, the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing : GDPR Article 22 ( [LINK] )"},{"id":41332808,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2024-08","story":"Cautionary tale on using Chase bank for indie business","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"For niche, zany sketch comedy, Little Britain is a gem.","text":"For those unfamiliar with the reference: [LINK] [VIDEO] For niche, zany sketch comedy, Little Britain is a gem."},{"id":39747334,"author":"Rinzler89","month":"2024-03","story":"European Union AI Act","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's sad the comments here bashing this haven't understood the regulation, or haven't even read it and are just slamming it because in their mind EU REGULATION=BAD, AI=GOOD INNOVATION. EU AI regulation isn't there to stop AI innovation, it's only there to restrict where and when AI can be used on decisions that affect people. For example, you can't deny someone healthcare, a bank account, a rental, unemployment payments, or a job, just because \"computer says NO\" [1]. I don't understand how people can be against this kind of regulation, especially knowing how biased and discriminatory AI can be made to be while also being a convenient scapegoat for poor policies implemented by lazy people in charge: \"you see your honor it wasn't our policies and implementation that were discriminatory and ruined lives, it was the AI's fault, not ours\" . [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"xH_6_8NOfwI":{"wy":"Used as a shorthand illustration of juking the stats and Goodhart-style metric gaming.","q":{"x":"Here is a great scene talking about metrics/stats from The Wire","i":9464083,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":30157522,"author":"creakingstairs","month":"2022-02","story":"Man found guilty of ‘grossly offensive’ tweet","stance":"reference","why":"Clip highlighting how governments manipulate statistics.","quote":"Here is a clip from my favourite show that highlights this","text":"> Now watch governments do the same. They've already been doing that for ages: crime rate, gdp, employment, inflation. Here is a clip from my favourite show that highlights this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12944896,"author":"FullMtlAlcoholc","month":"2016-11","story":"How an accused drug dealer revealed Jacksonville's facial recognition network","stance":"reference","why":"Used as an example of police juking stats.","quote":"Juke the Stats\" [1]","text":"> No, they generally do the latter, and generally well. Minor quibble, but I don't find a 50% clearance rate on murders to be good. [0] I also don't put much faith in the accuracy of many (most) police departments numbers as they \"Juke the Stats\" [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9464083,"author":"nrao123","month":"2015-04","story":"David Simon on Baltimore’s Anguish","stance":"reference","why":"Scene on how stats and incentives distort policing.","quote":"Here is a great scene talking about metrics/stats from The Wire","text":"David Simon is a national treasure. The Wire had lots of scenes on how metrics & incentives screwed up Baltimore city. Here is a great scene talking about metrics/stats from The Wire - [VIDEO] Article also refers to Martin Malley's ambition to be Maryland's governor that really screwed up the incentives and cooking of the books. \"How do you reward cops? Two ways: promotion and cash. That's what rewards a cop. If you want to pay overtime pay for having police fill the jails with loitering arrests or simple drug possession or failure to yield, if you want to spend your municipal treasure rewarding that, well the cop who’s going to court 7 or 8 days a month — and court is always overtime pay — you're going to damn near double your salary every month. On the other hand, the guy who actually goes to his post and investigates who's burglarizing the homes, at the end of the month maybe he’s made one arrest. It may be the right arrest and one that makes his post safer, but he's going to court one day and he's out in two hours. So you fail to reward the cop who actually does police work. But worse, it’s time to make new sergeants or lieutenants, and so you look at the computer and say: Who's doing the most work? And they say, man, this guy had 80 arrests last month, and this other guy’s only got one. Who do you think gets made sergeant? And then who trains the next generation of cops in how not to do police work? I’ve just described for you the culture of the Baltimore police departme"}]},"xoHbn8-ROiQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence of a vehicle stability flaw that Toyota was slow to fix.","m":[{"id":36443367,"author":"cmcaleer","month":"2023-06","story":"Quality of new vehicles in US declining on more tech use","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it looks like the Hilux in particular hasn’t bothered fixing this design flaw in 9 years[2]","text":"Personally I rather die than kill a child, and SUVs are far worse in that regard. Despite making up 15% of accidents in this[1] study, they represent 25% of fatal accidents. A child is 8 times more likely to die if struck by an SUV according to the same study. Empirically, SUV front blind spots are crazy and if you have a young child ask them to stand in front of your car with measuring tape while you’re in it. Very easy to imagine pulling out without seeing them and crushing them to death. The problem is even worse if you have a short partner. I felt blind when I drove my dad’s. Even if we go for the selfish perspective, rollover rates for SUVs are intrinsically higher, and it looks like the Hilux in particular hasn’t bothered fixing this design flaw in 9 years[2]. And that additional braking distance can be the difference between a massive headache from rear ending, or worse. Driving defensively will increase your odds of survival per mile far more than any additional metal will. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22599422,"author":"u801e","month":"2020-03","story":"How do we stop people from blinding other drivers with aftermarket LEDs?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is the video[1] of a Toyota Hilux during that test.","text":"> You're sitting significantly higher than most traffic, giving you a much better idea of what is going on around you. This also comes with a higher center of gravity which makes it more difficult to control the vehicle during emergency situations > -Mirrors tend to be very large by car standards, further enhancing your awareness. Larger vehicles tend to have bigger blindspots (even with properly adjusted mirrors) > While riding tall, they are often both relatively short and narrow, further easing your life in traffic. But you still have a higher center of gravity and worse vehicle handling as a result. Trying to swerve around something or make an emergency lane change makes it more likely you'll lose control of the vehicle > Oh, and winters. This is more a factor of the type of tires used and how many wheels are driven. My all-wheel drive vehicles do very well in the winters when equipped with snow tires. > Anecdata - my Land Cruiser is shorter and narrower than my wife's VW Passat, has more than twice the trunk space and just over half the turn radius. How would your Land Cruiser do in the moose test versus the VW Passat? This is the video[1] of a Toyota Hilux during that test. And this is a video[2] of a VW Passat. Note that the first video was done while the driver was going 37 mph. The second was at 45 mph. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13270659,"author":"mjolk","month":"2016-12","story":"Tesla footage of braking before crash ahead [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The dangerous Toyota Hilux","text":"A more extreme, but related condition is a \"moose test\": The dangerous Toyota Hilux: [VIDEO] The Jeep Grand Cherokee acting similarly dangerously: [LINK]"}]},"ymsHLkB8u3s":{"wy":"Used as a joke reply when someone is shown up for parroting what they read.","m":[{"id":4658927,"author":"OverKAnalytics","month":"2012-10","story":"Why becoming a data scientist is not easier than you think","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Agree, unless your arguing that graduate level math(s) are only 'truly understood' by individuals with post-graduate math(s) degrees. In my opinion, reading (and practicing) is a viable alternative to the same skills. Or, as more famously said by Matt Damon... ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":3345705,"author":"ajax77","month":"2011-12","story":"Isaac Newton’s Personal Notebooks Go Digital","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I can't help but think of this scene from Good Will Hunting when I read your replies","text":"I looked for the \"sign that the quote is bogus\" but missed it. Maybe results that have 5 million hits are bogus? Maybe results that have relevant links are bogus? I can enter all sorts of valid quotes from both current and historic figures into Google, and obtain very similar results. (\"Ask Not What Your Country Can Do For You\", \"A man should look for what is, and not for what he thinks should be\", \"A man who dares to waste one hour of time has not discovered the value of life.\") I fail to see how that adds anything to the discussion. Neither do I see how the fact that Newton restated, rather than originated, a particular idea, helps your argument. Also, please inform me as to how this statement relates to Lord Byron. Nothing I've read of Byron would favor him as the statement's originator over Newton. I can't help but think of this scene from Good Will Hunting when I read your replies [VIDEO]"},{"id":2118764,"author":"MaxGabriel","month":"2011-01","story":"Study: College Students Not Learning Much","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"See the sad thing about a guy like you is in fifty years you'll start doin' some thinking on your own and you're gonna come up that there are two certainties in life: one, don't do that. And two, you dropped a hundred and fifty grand on a fuckin' education you could have got for a dollar fifty in late charges at the public library\" -Matt Damon, Good Will Hunting [VIDEO]"}]},"zKgN2G9d0dc":{"wy":"HN cites it in wind-power debates as measured evidence that turbine noise is low relative to ambient or road noise.","q":{"x":"Unless you're within 200m of a turbine, the background ambient sounds are louder than the sound of the turbine.","i":32472082,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":45583315,"author":"justin66","month":"2025-10","story":"American solar farms","stance":"reference","why":"Interesting look at wind turbine noise","quote":"This was interesting.","text":"This was interesting. [VIDEO] The turbines I saw in Iowa weren't loud enough to drown out distance sounds of the highway. I didn't hear them at all, but I guess there's also tinnitus to be contended with..."},{"id":37758469,"author":"maccard","month":"2023-10","story":"Most Americans comfortable with solar panels, turbines in their communities","stance":"reference","why":"Decibel meter comparison of road noise and turbine noise","quote":"living near a road is _way_ more disruptive than living near a wind turbine","text":"How close are you talking? Here's [0] a video where someone goes out and gets a decibel meter to show that living near a road is _way_ more disruptive than living near a wind turbine. This is from over a decade ago. Modern farms are being put 300+ metres away from the nearest houses, where they should be 30-40dB or so [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":32472082,"author":"Calvin02","month":"2022-08","story":"More energy on less land: The drive to shrink solar’s footprint","stance":"reference","why":"Shows wind turbine noise measured against ambient sound","quote":"Unless you're within 200m of a turbine, the background ambient sounds are louder than the sound of the turbine.","text":"I was curious about the sound impact and found these two videos: 1) [LINK] , 2) [VIDEO] . The first video has pretty sound (pardon the pun) methodology. Findings: Unless you're within 200m of a turbine, the background ambient sounds are louder than the sound of the turbine. Do you have evidence to the contrary?"},{"id":30308306,"author":"8bitsrule","month":"2022-02","story":"Turbine “torture” for Greek islanders as wind farms proliferate","stance":"reference","why":"Decibel readings near a turbine site in England","quote":"4 to 6 dB is a lot less noisy than the AHA video.","text":"Found that video working on Youtube using the same 'v=' code. At one point (2:50) the video (attributed to the 'Allegheny Highlands Alliance'[0]; executive producer an M.D.) plays a sound labelled 'turbine noise during winter'. It's very loud ... I wouldn't spend an hour in that noise. But was the noise recorded near the turbine(s)? And considering the turbine(s) are 530 yards (almost 1/3 mile) away from the home, those shadows falling on the building could only occur briefly near sunrise and sunset. Here are some decibel readings at an equivalent distance from a turbine site in England. 4 to 6 dB is a lot less noisy than the AHA video. [VIDEO] [0] [LINK]"},{"id":26460807,"author":"vcdimension","month":"2021-03","story":"An argument that nuclear power makes the climate crisis worse","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that wind turbines are quiet","quote":"they seem to be quieter","text":"I cannot understand how anyone would prefer an enormous grey concrete building with a large exclusion zone and large cooling towers blocking the skyline, to otherwise untouched natural or agricultural landscape dotted with wind turbines. Wind turbines allow you to enjoy more of the countryside because they take up very little space (you can walk around them). Furthermore, they seem to be quieter: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":21416431,"author":"mikelyons","month":"2019-11","story":"Germany's Giant Windmills Are Unpopular","stance":"reference","why":"Example measurement of wind farm noise levels","quote":"City dwellers of most stripes suffer worse noise levels according to my limited research.","text":"Are they any louder in Germany than in Britain? It seems that by suffering, they are just missing the benefit of the quiet rural areas. City dwellers of most stripes suffer worse noise levels according to my limited research. One example: [VIDEO]"}]}});
